B.apl=s([B.uk,B.DM,B.ul],A.aC("J<Cz>"))
B.aSs=s(["Invention","Observation","Survey count","Evidence-based choice"],t.s)
B.aGU=s(["An invention is a made solution to a problem. Filipino scientist Ramon Barba developed a way to help mango trees flower on a more useful schedule. Children must not copy chemical treatments.","An observation records what was actually seen or measured. A photo of litter beside a bin is evidence of that place and time, not every place.","A survey count gathers answers or observations using the same question or rule. Counting plastic, paper, and food scraps separately can reveal a waste pattern.","An evidence-based choice uses findings to select a solution. If most measured waste is food scraps, reducing food waste may help more than adding a glass bin."],t.s)
B.aye=s(["0|Which label fits a newly made device that solves a problem?","1|Which label fits recording litter visible in one photograph?","2|Which label fits totals collected using the same waste categories?","3|Which label fits choosing a paper reuse box because paper dominates the count?","0|Which label fits Barba developing a useful mango-flowering method?","1|Which label fits writing only what was actually seen rather than guessing?","2|Which label fits asking every household the same waste question?","3|Which label fits comparing before-and-after findings to decide whether to keep a solution?","2|Two groups count waste, but one includes the playground and one does not. Which part needs a fair shared rule?","3|A sample has far more food scraps than glass. Which model step chooses a solution based on that finding?","1|A learner says one litter photo proves all streets are dirty. Which model part should be described more narrowly?"],t.s)
B.c8g=new A.bO(1,"Materials","Inventions Solve Problems with Evidence","Connect an invention to a need|Distinguish observations from guesses|Use fair survey data for a local problem",B.aSs,B.aGU,"A class counts 12 paper scraps and 3 bottles in a safe photo survey. Paper is four times as common in this sample. A paper reuse box addresses the larger counted group; a second survey can check whether it helps.","Can a single photo prove every street has the same waste problem?","No. It records one place and time. More observations using the same rule are needed before extending the claim.","A famous inventor or a confident claim is not evidence that every proposed solution works.","Science-based solutions connect a real need, useful evidence, and a testable choice.",B.aye)
B.b1F=s(["Burning","Rusting","Biodegrading","Reducing harmful waste"],t.s)
B.aCt=s(["Burning changes fuel into new substances, including gases and ash. It needs oxygen and can release harmful smoke. Study pictures; never burn waste.","Rusting changes iron when oxygen and water act on it. Rust has different properties from the original metal. Keeping iron dry helps slow rusting.","Biodegrading happens when living decomposers break down materials such as old leaves. Plastic is not broken down quickly in the same way.","Reducing harmful waste means using less, reusing safely, and sorting waste properly. Do not mix cleaners or unknown chemicals, because dangerous reactions may occur."],t.s)
B.b2F=s(["0|Which change makes ash and smoke from fuel?","1|Which change produces reddish-brown material on wet iron?","2|Which change involves decomposers breaking down fallen leaves?","3|Which choice includes refusing to mix unknown household cleaners?","0|Which change is unsafe to test by setting rubbish alight?","1|Which change is slowed by protecting iron from water?","2|Which change returns material from dead leaves through decomposers?","3|Which choice includes reusing a safe container instead of discarding it?","1|A metal gate becomes flaky after rain and air exposure. Which process best fits?","3|A plan removes rubbish but creates harmful smoke. Which model goal should replace that plan?","2|Leaves break down in suitable compost while a plastic wrapper remains. Which process explains the leaf change?"],t.s)
B.c8l=new A.bO(1,"Materials","Materials Can React and Become Waste","Recognize chemical changes from evidence|Compare degradable materials|Choose safe ways to reduce harmful changes",B.b1F,B.aCt,"An iron gate develops reddish-brown flakes after repeated rain. Water and air reached the iron. The new flaky material is rust, not just dirt that dried on the gate.","A family wants to remove plastic rubbish by setting it on fire. Is that a good material-care choice?","No. Burning can release harmful smoke. Reduce and sort the waste and follow local collection rules instead.","A change in appearance alone does not always prove a new substance. Melting ice changes appearance but remains water.","Chemical properties affect how materials change and how we should handle their waste.",B.b2F)
B.aD_=s(["Support and movement","Digestion","Breathing and circulation","Root and shoot systems"],t.s)
B.aSN=s(["The skeleton supports the body and protects organs. Muscles pull on bones to move joints. Bones do not move themselves.","Digestion breaks food into substances the body can absorb. The stomach and intestines help; much absorption happens in the small intestine.","Breathing brings air to lungs. Oxygen enters blood there, and the heart pumps blood around the body. The systems work together to supply cells.","A plant has roots and shoots. Roots anchor it and absorb water; stems carry water and leaves make food using light. A shoot includes stems and leaves."],t.s)
B.aQE=s(["0|Which model part explains muscles pulling bones at a joint?","1|Which model part explains breaking food down for absorption?","2|Which model part explains oxygen entering blood in lungs?","3|Which model part explains water moving from roots to leaves?","0|Which model part explains protection of the brain by the skull?","1|Which model part includes much nutrient absorption in the small intestine?","2|Which model part includes the heart pumping oxygen-carrying blood?","3|Which model part includes leaves making food using light?","2|A learner says lungs pump blood around the body. Which connected model part distinguishes lung and heart jobs?","0|An arm bends because muscle pulls bone, not because bone contracts. Which model part explains this?","3|Roots take up water but a stem is damaged. Which model part explains why leaves may receive less water?"],t.s)
B.c7G=new A.bO(2,"Living Things","Body and Plant Systems Work Together","Identify jobs of body systems|Trace oxygen delivery|Compare plant roots and shoots",B.aD_,B.aSN,"After walking, body cells need oxygen. Air enters the lungs. Oxygen moves into blood. The heart pumps blood to the active body parts. Lungs and heart have different connected jobs.","Can a heart pump alone replace the lungs?","No. The heart moves blood, but lungs allow oxygen from air to enter that blood.","Food does not become energy simply by reaching the stomach. It is digested and absorbed, then used by cells.","Systems have different parts whose connected jobs keep plants and animals alive.",B.aQE)
B.ayB=s(["Life cycle","Habitat","Herbivore","Carnivore or omnivore"],t.s)
B.axd=s(["A life cycle shows development and reproduction across generations. A butterfly passes through egg, larva, pupa, and adult. A chicken has no pupa stage.","A habitat supplies needs. A mangrove swamp and a garden support different organisms because water, shelter, and food conditions differ.","A herbivore eats plants. In grass \u2192 grasshopper \u2192 bird, the grasshopper is a herbivore. The arrow shows food and energy moving to the eater.","A carnivore eats animals; an omnivore eats both plants and animals. A chicken eating seeds and worms is an omnivore. Its diet, not its size, decides the group."],t.s)
B.aVa=s(["0|Which model part includes a butterfly pupa?","1|Which model part explains why mangrove organisms need suitable water conditions?","2|Which label fits a grasshopper eating rice leaves?","3|Which label fits a frog eating insects?","0|Which model part compares egg-to-adult stages in a chicken and butterfly?","1|Which label fits the place supplying a fish with its needs?","2|Which label fits a goat feeding on grass?","3|Which label fits a chicken eating both seeds and worms?","2|In grass \u2192 grasshopper \u2192 frog, which model label describes the first animal?","0|A learner adds a pupa to a chicken diagram. Which model part needs correction?","3|A bird eats fruit and insects. Which combined model group includes its correct diet type?"],t.s)
B.c8j=new A.bO(2,"Living Things","Life Cycles Habitats and Food Chains","Compare life-cycle stages|Read a feeding arrow|Classify consumers by food",B.ayB,B.axd,"In rice plant \u2192 grasshopper \u2192 frog, the grasshopper eats the plant and the frog eats the grasshopper. Energy moves in the arrow direction, from food to eater.","A chicken eats seeds in the morning and worms later. Is it a herbivore only?","No. It eats plant and animal food, so it is an omnivore.","Food-chain arrows do not mean the eater points toward its food. They show transfer from food to eater.","Organisms develop, live in suitable habitats, and depend on feeding relationships.",B.aVa)
B.azG=s(["Distance","Time","Speed","Force"],t.s)
B.aSV=s(["Distance is how far something travels. Measure with equal units, such as meters, along its route.","Time is how long motion lasts. Seconds are useful for short toy trips. A fair comparison must consider both distance and time.","Speed compares distance with time. A toy covering 6 meters in 3 seconds averages 2 meters each second. On a distance-time graph a steeper straight line means greater constant speed.","A force is a push or pull. It can change speed, direction, or shape. A gentle sideways push can turn a toy without stopping it first."],t.s)
B.aDq=s(["0|Which quantity is measured in meters along a toy route?","1|Which quantity is measured in seconds?","2|Which quantity compares distance with time?","3|Which model part can turn a moving toy?","0|A toy travels from the zero mark to the four-meter mark. What route quantity is recorded?","1|A stopwatch reads five seconds at the end of a trip. What was measured?","2|A distance-time line gets steeper. Which motion quantity increases?","3|Which model part can flatten a soft sponge?","2|A toy covers 6 meters in 3 seconds. Which quantity equals 2 meters per second?","1|Two toys travel equally far. Which measurement must also be compared to decide which is faster?","3|A toy changes direction while still moving. Which model part explains the cause of the change?"],t.s)
B.c7I=new A.bO(3,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Measuring Motion and Changing It","Compare motion using distance and time|Interpret a distance-time record|Predict a force effect",B.azG,B.aSV,"Toy A travels 8 meters in 4 seconds. Divide 8 by 4: 2 meters per second. Toy B travels 4 meters in the same 4 seconds, so B averages 1 meter per second. A is faster.","A distance-time record stays at 3 meters for several seconds. What is happening?","The object stays at the same recorded distance. Its speed is zero during that interval; a horizontal distance-time line shows no additional distance.","Greater distance alone does not prove greater speed unless time is considered.","Measure distance and time, compare speed, and use forces to explain changes.",B.aDq)
B.aI5=s(["Magnetic interaction","Light energy","Sound energy","Heat transfer"],t.s)
B.b26=s(["Magnets can act without touching. Opposite poles attract and like poles repel. Some iron and steel objects are attracted, but not every metal is magnetic.","Light energy helps us see and supports plant food making. Very bright light can harm eyes. A torch can light an object without touching it.","Vibrating objects make sound. Sound carries energy through matter. Loud sound can harm ears even without anything touching them.","Heat moves from warmer to cooler things. Sunlight can warm a surface. Never use your skin to test whether an unknown object is hot."],t.s)
B.aWO=s(["0|Which interaction explains two north poles moving apart?","1|Which model item lets a torch illuminate a book?","2|Which model item comes from a vibrating bell?","3|Which model item explains a warm object heating a cooler one?","0|Which interaction can attract a steel clip across a small gap?","1|Which model item helps green leaves make food?","2|Which model item can harm ears when too intense?","3|Which model item explains warming of a surface in sunlight?","0|A magnet attracts steel but not a wooden stick. Which model part explains why material matters?","2|A loudspeaker hurts ears without touching them. Which energy effect explains this?","1|A lamp makes objects visible from a distance. Which useful energy effect is involved?"],t.s)
B.c8i=new A.bO(3,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Magnets and Energy We Can Observe","Predict attraction and repulsion|Identify noncontact effects|Match energy with useful and harmful effects",B.aI5,B.b26,"Two north poles face each other and move apart. They repel. Turning one magnet so its south pole faces the north changes the interaction to attraction.","A steel paper clip moves toward a magnet before they touch. What does that show?","A magnetic interaction can act across a gap. It does not show that every material will be attracted.","A shiny object is not automatically magnetic. Material matters, not just appearance.","Magnetic forces and energy can produce changes without direct contact.",B.aWO)
B.atH=s(["Sandy soil","Clay-rich soil","Silt-rich soil","Loam"],t.s)
B.aOq=s(["Sandy soil has many large particles with larger gaps. Water often drains through it quickly, so it may dry quickly.","Clay-rich soil has very small particles. It often holds much water and drains slowly. Too much water can leave roots with too little air.","Silt-rich soil has particles between sand and clay in size. It can feel smooth and hold more water than very sandy soil.","Loam is a useful mixture of sand, silt, clay, and organic matter. It often balances water holding and air spaces, but the best soil depends on the plant."],t.s)
B.azE=s(["0|Which soil often drains quickly because of larger particles?","1|Which soil often drains slowly because of very small particles?","2|Which soil has particles between sand and clay in size?","3|Which soil is a balanced mixture of particle sizes and organic matter?","0|Which sample may dry quickest after equal watering?","1|Which sample is most likely to stay waterlogged if drainage is poor?","2|Which soil label fits a sample dominated by smooth medium-sized particles?","3|Which soil label fits a mixture often useful for garden plants?","0|In a fair test one sample lets much more water through quickly. Which model soil is most consistent with that result?","1|A sample holds water so long that roots lack air. Which soil type is most consistent with slow drainage?","3|A gardener wants water holding and air spaces rather than either extreme. Which model soil is a reasonable starting choice?"],t.s)
B.c8o=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space","Soil Holds Water for Plants","Compare soil particle patterns|Interpret a fair drainage record|Explain why roots need water and air",B.atH,B.aOq,"Equal cups of soil receive equal water. After equal time, more water has drained from the sandy sample. The sand held less of the added water in this test. Keep soil amount and cups the same.","Why is keeping the amount of water the same important in a soil comparison?","Otherwise extra drainage could come from adding extra water, not from the soil type. Change the soil type while keeping other conditions similar.","The soil holding the most water is not always best. Roots also need air, and plants differ.","Soil particle sizes affect drainage and the water available to roots.",B.azE)
B.aCq=s(["Thermometer","Rain gauge","Wind vane","Sun and shadow model"],t.s)
B.aWl=s(["A thermometer measures temperature. Read the scale at eye level in suitable shade for an air-temperature record, not pressed against a hot wall.","A rain gauge collects rainfall to measure its depth. Place it safely in a suitable open location with adult help, not under a roof that blocks rain.","A wind vane indicates the direction wind comes from. Wind speed is a different quantity, measured with an anemometer.","The Sun is a huge hot star giving light and warmth. A shadow forms where an object blocks light. Its direction is opposite the Sun and its length changes as the Sun appears higher or lower."],t.s)
B.axj=s(["0|Which instrument measures air temperature?","1|Which instrument measures rainfall depth?","2|Which instrument indicates where wind comes from?","3|Which model explains a stick blocking sunlight?","0|Which instrument might have a scale in degrees Celsius?","1|Which instrument should not be sheltered under a roof during a rain measurement?","2|Which instrument answers north or south rather than fast or slow?","3|Which model explains why shadows are often shorter near midday?","1|An observer wants to compare rain on two days using collected depth. Which instrument fits?","3|A fixed stick has a longer shadow when the Sun appears lower. Which model explains the record?","0|A learner records how warm the air is in consistent shade. Which instrument supplies that measurement?"],t.s)
B.c7t=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space","Weather Instruments Sunlight and Shadows","Match weather instruments to quantities|Read changes in a shadow record|Explain sunlight and safety",B.aCq,B.aWl,"A stick has a long morning shadow and a shorter shadow near midday. With the same upright stick, the shorter shadow indicates a higher apparent Sun. Observe shadows, never the Sun directly.","Why would a rain gauge under a roof give a misleading rainfall record?","The roof blocks much of the rain. The gauge would not collect the same rainfall as the open ground being studied.","A wind vane shows direction, not speed. Keep instrument jobs separate.","Weather measurements and shadow records reveal patterns when instruments are used consistently.",B.axj)
