B.aww=s(["Homogeneous mixture","Heterogeneous mixture","Solute","Solvent"],t.s)
B.aum=s(["A homogeneous mixture has a uniform composition at the scale being observed. Fully dissolved salt water looks uniform; different spoonfuls can contain the same ratio of salt and water.","A heterogeneous mixture is not uniform throughout. Oil and water form layers, and a vegetable salad has visibly different parts.","A solute is a substance dissolved in a solution. Salt remains in salt water even when the crystals cannot be seen.","A solvent does the dissolving. Water is the solvent in a simple salt-water solution. Not every substance dissolves in water."],t.s)
B.aTA=s(["0|Which label fits fully dissolved salt water with uniform composition?","1|Which label fits oil and water in separate layers?","2|Which role belongs to salt dissolved in water?","3|Which role belongs to water doing the dissolving?","0|Which mixture type has the same observed composition throughout?","1|Which mixture type includes a salad with distinct pieces?","2|Sugar dissolved in water has which role?","3|Water in a simple sugar solution has which role?","0|A clear sample contains salt and water uniformly distributed. Which label fits the whole sample rather than either ingredient?","2|Water evaporates from salt water and crystals remain. Which role did the crystals have before evaporation?","1|Two spoonfuls from different parts of a mixture have obviously different ingredients. Which mixture label is most appropriate?"],t.s)
B.c8b=new A.bO(1,"Matter","Mixtures Can Look Uniform or Uneven","Compare homogeneous and heterogeneous mixtures|Identify solute and solvent|Explain why dissolved matter remains present",B.aww,B.aum,"Stir a pictured spoon of salt into water until it is fully dissolved. Salt is the solute; water is the solvent. The result is a homogeneous mixture, not pure water.","Does clear-looking salt water contain only one substance?","No. It can contain dissolved salt and water. Appearance alone cannot prove purity.","Dissolving is not disappearing or melting. The salt is distributed through the water and remains present.","Mixtures contain more than one substance; uniform appearance does not mean pure.",B.aTA)
B.b_c=s(["Sieving","Filtration","Decantation","Evaporation"],t.s)
B.b1_=s(["Sieving separates solid particles by size. A mesh can let fine sand through while retaining larger pebbles.","Filtration separates an insoluble solid from liquid using pores. Filter paper can retain sand while water passes. It does not reliably remove dissolved salts or all germs.","Decantation pours off a liquid after a denser solid settles, or separates suitable layers carefully. It may leave some material mixed, so it is not always complete.","Evaporation removes a liquid as vapor and leaves a dissolved solid. Salt can remain after water evaporates. Study diagrams only; do not heat samples."],t.s)
B.aJj=s(["0|Which method separates pebbles from fine dry sand by particle size?","1|Which method traps insoluble sand while water passes through paper?","2|Which method gently pours clear liquid off settled mud?","3|Which method can recover dissolved salt by removing water as vapor?","0|Which method uses a mesh with openings between two particle sizes?","1|Which method cannot normally remove dissolved salt with ordinary paper?","2|Which method depends on allowing solid particles to settle before pouring?","3|Which method leaves nonvolatile dissolved solids behind as liquid leaves?","1|A mixture has sand and dissolved salt in water. Which method should remove the sand first?","3|After sand is removed, dissolved salt remains in the liquid. Which method can recover that salt?","0|A dry mixture has two sizes of grain and no liquid. Which model method best matches the difference?"],t.s)
B.c83=new A.bO(1,"Matter","Choose Separation by a Physical Property","Select a method for a mixture|Explain the property used|Distinguish clarity from drinking-water safety",B.b_c,B.b1_,"A mixture contains sand and dissolved salt in water. First filter out the sand. The salt goes through with the water. Then evaporation can leave salt behind. Different properties require different steps.","Can ordinary filter paper remove dissolved salt from water?","No. Dissolved salt passes through ordinary filter paper with water. A method such as evaporation is needed to recover the salt.","Clear filtered water is not necessarily safe to drink. Germs and dissolved substances may remain.","A separation method works because mixture components differ in size, settling, or evaporation behavior.",B.aJj)
B.aL8=s(["Muscles bones and skin","Digestive system","Respiratory and circulatory systems","Nervous system"],t.s)
B.axy=s(["Muscles pull on bones to move the body. Skin forms a protective barrier and helps regulate temperature. Bones support and protect internal organs.","The digestive system breaks food down and absorbs useful nutrients, mostly in the small intestine. Blood then carries absorbed nutrients to cells.","The lungs exchange gases and the heart circulates blood. Oxygen moves from lungs into blood and reaches working cells; carbon dioxide travels back to lungs.","The nervous system receives information and coordinates responses. The brain, spinal cord, and nerves help muscles respond to changing conditions."],t.s)
B.aTt=s(["0|Which model group directly moves a joint by pulling bones?","1|Which system absorbs nutrients in the small intestine?","2|Which systems deliver oxygen from air to cells?","3|Which system coordinates messages to muscles?","0|Which model group includes a protective outer barrier?","1|Which system breaks food into absorbable substances?","2|Which model group returns carbon dioxide to lungs for removal?","3|Which system includes brain, spinal cord, and nerves?","2|A learner says a strong heart means lungs are unnecessary. Which connected model group explains why that is wrong?","3|A hand moves away after a warning signal is processed. Which model system coordinates the response?","1|Cells receive oxygen but also need nutrients from a meal. Which system prepares and absorbs those nutrients?"],t.s)
B.c7J=new A.bO(2,"Living Things","Human Systems Coordinate Every Step","Trace oxygen and nutrient delivery|Explain support and protection|Describe nervous-system coordination",B.aL8,B.axy,"During a safe walk, nerves coordinate leg muscles. Muscles pull bones. Breathing and circulation bring oxygen to cells, and digestion has supplied nutrients. No one system does all the work.","Why can stronger muscles alone not replace the respiratory system?","Muscles need oxygen delivery. Lungs exchange gases, and blood carries oxygen to muscle cells. Different systems have different necessary jobs.","The heart does not clean air or digest food. It pumps blood within an interacting system.","Body systems exchange materials and information so the body can function as a whole.",B.aTt)
B.ats=s(["Vertebrates","Invertebrates","Feeding relationships","Habitat protection"],t.s)
B.aND=s(["Vertebrates have a backbone. Fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, and mammals belong to this group despite their different habitats.","Invertebrates lack a backbone. Insects, snails, worms, and corals are examples. A hard outer shell is not a backbone.","Feeding relationships move energy from food to eater. In mangroves, dead leaves support decomposers and small animals that become food for larger animals.","Protecting rainforests, coral reefs, and mangroves preserves habitat and many interacting species. Reducing pollution and avoiding damage protect more than one organism."],t.s)
B.b2R=s(["0|Which group includes animals with backbones?","1|Which group includes a crab and a snail?","2|Which model part describes energy moving from food to eater?","3|Which model part includes preventing damage to mangrove roots?","0|Which group includes fish, birds, and mammals?","1|Which group includes corals despite their hard structures?","2|Which model part connects small animals to the larger animals eating them?","3|Which choice includes reducing pollution near a coral reef?","1|An animal has a shell but no backbone. Which structural group fits?","3|Young fish lose shelter after mangroves are removed. Which model action addresses the cause?","2|Dead mangrove leaves support small organisms and then larger consumers. Which model part explains the connection?"],t.s)
B.c89=new A.bO(2,"Living Things","Animal Groups and Ecosystem Connections","Compare vertebrate and invertebrate groups|Identify habitat interactions|Explain a conservation action",B.ats,B.aND,"A fish and a crab both live in water. The fish has a backbone; the crab does not. Habitat alone is not the rule for classifying them.","Why can removing mangrove trees harm fish even if nobody catches the fish?","Mangroves provide shelter and support food relationships. Losing them changes the habitat and resources that fish depend on.","Corals are animals, not rocks or flowering plants. A hard structure does not decide the group by itself.","Body structures classify animals; habitat and feeding links connect them.",B.b2R)
B.aY_=s(["Gravity","Friction","Lever","Inclined plane"],t.s)
B.aDd=s(["Gravity is attraction between masses. Near Earth it pulls objects toward Earth. Air resistance can affect how objects fall.","Friction opposes sliding or attempted sliding between touching surfaces. It provides grip for walking and can convert motion energy into thermal energy.","A lever turns about a fulcrum. A longer effort arm can reduce the effort force needed for a load, but the effort moves farther.","An inclined plane is a ramp. It can reduce the force needed to raise a load by increasing the travel distance. Friction means real ramps also waste some energy as heat."],t.s)
B.b0P=s(["0|Which force pulls a dropped object toward Earth?","1|Which force provides grip between shoes and ground?","2|Which machine turns about a fulcrum?","3|Which machine raises a load along a sloping path?","0|Which force keeps a ball from remaining suspended after release?","1|Which force slows a sliding box and can warm surfaces?","2|Which machine can use a longer effort arm to reduce force?","3|Which machine trades a longer path for less lifting force?","1|A wet smooth path makes walking harder because grip is reduced. Which interaction matters?","3|A cart reaches a platform by a longer gentle slope. Which machine is used?","2|A rigid bar rotates about a support to lift a load. Which model machine fits?"],t.s)
B.c7S=new A.bO(3,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Gravity Friction and Simple Machines","Predict effects of gravity and friction|Compare levers and ramps|Explain why machines do not create energy",B.aY_,B.aDd,"A load needs lifting to a platform. A gentle ramp lets it reach the same height with less force over a longer distance. The ramp does not remove the need for energy.","Why do shoe soles need friction rather than being perfectly slippery?","Friction provides grip against the ground. Without enough grip, a foot slides instead of pushing effectively.","Simple machines reduce required force in some arrangements, not the total ideal work for the same lift.","Forces affect motion; machines trade force for distance rather than creating energy.",B.b0P)
B.aCv=s(["Electrical to light","Electrical to motion","Light to electrical","Motion to thermal"],t.s)
B.aAo=s(["A lamp transforms electrical energy into light and thermal energy. An efficient lamp provides the needed light while wasting less energy as heat.","An electric fan transforms electrical energy into motion of blades and air. Sound and heating are additional outputs; not all input becomes useful motion.","A solar cell transforms light energy into electrical energy. It needs light, not fuel burning, but output depends on conditions.","Friction transforms some motion energy into thermal energy. Bicycle brakes slow a wheel while parts warm. Energy changes form rather than disappearing."],t.s)
B.aZ9=s(["0|Which transformation is the main useful one in a lamp?","1|Which transformation drives a fan?","2|Which transformation occurs in a solar cell?","3|Which transformation is caused by friction at brakes?","0|Which model transformation produces the light needed for reading from electricity?","1|Which model transformation makes electric motor blades turn?","2|Which model transformation begins with sunlight and supplies current?","3|Which model transformation explains surfaces warming as a box slides?","0|Two lamps give equal light but one uses less electricity. Which useful transformation is being compared?","3|A spinning wheel slows without its energy disappearing. Which model pathway explains much of the change?","2|A device supplies electricity in sunlight without burning fuel. Which model transformation fits?"],t.s)
B.c84=new A.bO(3,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Energy Changes in Useful Devices","Trace energy inputs and outputs|Identify useful and unwanted outputs|Explain why efficient devices waste less",B.aCv,B.aAo,"Compare two lamps giving the same useful light. If one needs less electrical energy, it is more efficient for lighting. It still produces some heat; efficiency does not mean no losses.","A fan blade stops after the power is off. Has its moving energy vanished?","No. Friction and air resistance transfer its energy mainly to thermal energy and some sound in the surroundings.","Devices do not make energy from nothing. Track both useful outputs and less useful outputs.","Energy is transformed and transferred; efficiency describes how much input becomes the useful output.",B.aZ9)
B.b_0=s(["Earthquake","Volcanic eruption","Seasonal weather pattern","Preparedness plan"],t.s)
B.aYI=s(["An earthquake is shaking caused by sudden movement within Earth, commonly along a fault. During shaking, use Drop, Cover, and Hold On where appropriate and follow local safety guidance.","A volcanic eruption releases magma, ash, or gases from a volcano. Ash can harm breathing and structures. Follow exclusion zones and evacuation instructions; do not visit to inspect activity.","Many Philippine places have wetter and drier parts of the year, but timing differs by location. Seasonal patterns come from many observations, not one afternoon.","A preparedness plan identifies trusted warnings, safe places, contacts, and supplies with adults. Never enter damaged buildings or cross floodwater to investigate."],t.s)
B.ayR=s(["0|Which event involves sudden shaking from movement within Earth?","1|Which event can release ash and volcanic gases?","2|Which model part describes wetter months repeated over many years?","3|Which model part lists safe contacts and evacuation instructions?","0|Which event calls for appropriate shaking protection such as Drop, Cover, and Hold On?","1|Which event makes volcano exclusion zones important?","2|Which model part needs more evidence than one rainy day?","3|Which model part includes not returning to a damaged building without clearance?","2|A chart compares rainfall for many years by month. Which model idea can it support?","3|A warning is issued and a family checks where to go with needed supplies. Which model part is being used?","1|Fine ash falls near an active volcano. Which event best matches the material source?"],t.s)
B.c7u=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space","Earth Hazards and Philippine Weather Patterns","Distinguish earthquakes from eruptions|Explain weather and season records|Identify safer hazard responses",B.b_0,B.aYI,"One rainy afternoon is a weather event. A chart showing many years with more rain in certain months is a seasonal pattern. Use long records for the pattern and current warnings for today.","A family sees ash falling. Should they approach the volcano to judge whether it is dangerous?","No. Use official warnings and the local preparedness plan. Dangerous gases and further eruptions may occur without a safe visible sign.","A forecast or hazard map cannot promise a particular home is safe in every event. Local conditions and official instructions matter.","Earth hazards need prepared responses; seasonal weather claims need long records.",B.ayR)
B.aV1=s(["Earth rotation","Earth revolution and tilt","Rocky inner planets","Outer giant planets"],t.s)
B.aEX=s(["Earth rotates on its axis about once a day. The side facing the Sun has daylight, while the other side has night.","Earth revolves around the Sun in about a year. Its axis stays tilted relative to its orbital plane. Changing sunlight angle and day length cause seasons, not a simple nearer-or-farther rule.","Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars are the four rocky inner planets. Their order from the Sun matters; Earth is third.","Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune are outer giant planets. Jupiter and Saturn are gas giants; Uranus and Neptune are ice giants. A small model cannot show both sizes and distances well unless scales are stated."],t.s)
B.aEH=s(["0|Which motion explains the daily change between daylight and night?","1|Which model part explains changing day length across a year?","2|Which group includes Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars?","3|Which group includes Jupiter through Neptune?","0|Which motion takes about one day?","1|Which model part combines a yearly orbit with a tilted axis?","2|Which group contains the third planet from the Sun?","3|Which group includes gas giants and ice giants?","0|A place moves into and out of sunlight while Earth turns. Which motion is responsible?","1|A learner claims seasons only depend on Earth being nearer the Sun. Which model part corrects the explanation?","3|A model shows four large planets outside Mars. Which group do they represent?"],t.s)
B.c85=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space","Earth Motions and the Planet Family","Distinguish rotation and revolution|Explain seasons using tilt|Compare rocky and giant planets",B.aV1,B.aEX,"Turn a pictured globe while keeping one light source fixed. A marked place moves into light and later into darkness: rotation explains day and night. Moving the globe around the light models a different motion.","Why is a line of equally spaced same-sized planet beads a limited model?","Actual planets differ greatly in size and spacing. Such a model can show order, but not true relative sizes and distances.","Earth rotation and revolution are not interchangeable. One explains a day; the other contributes to the yearly pattern.","Earth motions explain daily and yearly patterns; planet models need clearly stated scales.",B.aEH)
