B.aDG=s(["Solid particles","Liquid particles","Gas particles","Energy transfer"],t.s)
B.aGw=s(["In a solid, particles are closely arranged and vibrate about positions. They are not motionless. Strong attractions help maintain the solid shape.","In a liquid, particles remain close but move past one another. A liquid flows while retaining nearly constant volume under ordinary conditions.","In a gas, particles are much farther apart and move freely between collisions. A gas spreads to fill its container and is much easier to compress than a liquid.","Heating usually increases particle motion; at a state change, transferred energy can change particle arrangement instead of raising temperature. Cooling removes energy. Drawn circles represent particles and are not actual magnified photographs."],t.s)
B.b39=s(["0|Which model has particles vibrating about fixed positions?","1|Which model has close particles sliding past one another?","2|Which model has widely spaced freely moving particles?","3|Which model part explains a change in arrangement during melting?","0|Which particle model explains a solid keeping its own shape?","1|Which model explains water flowing without filling all the air space above it?","2|Which particle model explains easy compression into a smaller volume?","3|Which model part explains cooling by energy leaving a substance?","2|A sample fills a container and is easily compressed. Which arrangement best fits the evidence?","0|A learner draws particles in a solid as perfectly motionless. Which model needs vibration added?","3|During melting, energy enters but temperature can remain steady. Which model part explains where the energy goes?"],t.s)
B.c7N=new A.bO(1,"Science of Materials","Particle Models Explain States and Changes","Compare particle arrangements|Explain state changes with energy|State a limitation of a particle drawing",B.aDG,B.aGw,"Compare water and water vapor. The particles do not become larger when water evaporates. They become more widely separated and move in a gaseous arrangement. The material is still water.","Why does a gas compress more easily than a liquid?","A gas has much larger spaces between particles. Compression reduces those spaces; it does not mainly shrink the particles themselves.","Particle circles are not to scale and do not imply that actual particles have the drawn colors or sizes.","Particle spacing, movement, attractions, and energy changes explain states of matter.",B.b39)
B.aLx=s(["Solute","Solvent","Concentration","Controlled comparison"],t.s)
B.aUq=s(["A solute is dissolved in a solution. In salt water the salt remains present as distributed particles even when crystals cannot be seen.","A solvent dissolves the solute. Water is the solvent in a simple salt-water solution. Acids and bases are classified using appropriate indicators, not tasting; acids turn blue litmus red and bases turn red litmus blue.","Concentration describes how much solute is present in a stated amount of solution or solvent. State the basis: 10 grams of salt per 100 milliliters of water is different from 10 grams per 100 milliliters of final solution.","A controlled comparison changes one intended variable and measures an outcome while keeping relevant conditions alike. A warming test of solubility needs the same solute, water amount, method, and measurement units. Stirring affects dissolving rate, not necessarily the final solubility."],t.s)
B.awd=s(["0|Which role belongs to dissolved salt in salt water?","1|Which role belongs to water in a simple salt solution?","2|Which quantity compares solute amount on a stated volume basis?","3|Which model part requires keeping stirring the same in a temperature test?","0|Which role belongs to dissolved sugar in sugar water?","1|Which role belongs to the dissolving medium rather than the dissolved substance?","2|Which model quantity distinguishes 5 grams from 10 grams per equal 100 milliliters of water?","3|Which model part requires an outcome recorded with units?","2|Two samples use the same water volume but one has twice the dissolved salt. Which property is greater in that sample?","3|Temperature and stirring both change between samples. Which model requirement has not been met?","0|A clear solution yields salt when its water is removed. Which role had the recovered material played?"],t.s)
B.c7m=new A.bO(1,"Science of Materials","Solutions Concentration and a Fair Investigation","Identify solute and solvent|Compare concentration using stated quantities|Plan a test with controlled variables",B.aLx,B.aUq,"Sample A has 5 grams of salt per 100 milliliters of water; B has 10 grams per 100 milliliters of water. B has twice the salt for the same water basis. This comparison does not require tasting either sample.","A learner compares dissolving in warm water stirred quickly with cold water not stirred. Can temperature alone explain the result?","No. Stirring also changed. Control stirring and water amount, record temperature and dissolved mass, then compare.","Faster dissolving does not necessarily mean more can dissolve at equilibrium. Rate and solubility are different.","Identify solution roles, state the concentration basis, and control variables before drawing a conclusion.",B.awd)
B.axT=s(["Microscope magnification","Cell boundary and nucleus","Mitochondria and ribosomes","Plant chloroplasts and wall"],t.s)
B.b_4=s(["A compound microscope uses an eyepiece and objective. Total magnification is their product: a 10\xd7 eyepiece and 4\xd7 objective give 40\xd7. Start with low power and carry the instrument with supported hands under teacher guidance.","The cell membrane regulates exchange with surroundings. The nucleus of a typical plant or animal cell contains genetic information. Cytoplasm surrounds internal structures. Not every cell has a nucleus; bacteria do not.","Mitochondria support aerobic cellular respiration, releasing usable energy from food. Ribosomes build proteins. Many organelles cannot be resolved clearly with an ordinary classroom light microscope, so diagrams supplement observations.","Many green plant cells have chloroplasts for photosynthesis and a rigid cell wall outside the membrane. Animal cells lack chloroplasts and cell walls. Not every plant cell contains chloroplasts; root cells are a useful counterexample."],t.s)
B.aMM=s(["0|Which model part gives 100\xd7 from a 10\xd7 eyepiece and 10\xd7 objective?","1|Which model part includes regulation of exchange at the cell membrane?","2|Which model part includes protein building?","3|Which model part distinguishes many green plant cells from animal cells?","0|Which model part explains starting observation with a low-power objective?","1|Which model part includes genetic information in a typical cell nucleus?","2|Which model part connects mitochondria with aerobic respiration?","3|Which model part includes a rigid layer outside the membrane?","0|A learner adds 10 and 4 to get 14\xd7. Which model calculation corrects the result?","3|A root cell lacks chloroplasts but has a wall. Which model part explains why this can still be a plant cell?","2|A cell needs to manufacture proteins. Which model group includes the responsible structures?"],t.s)
B.c7f=new A.bO(2,"Life Science","Microscopes Reveal Cells and Their Jobs","Calculate total microscope magnification|Compare plant and animal cells|Connect organelles to functions",B.axT,B.b_4,"A microscope has a 10\xd7 eyepiece and 10\xd7 objective. Multiply 10 by 10 to obtain 100\xd7. If a plant-cell diagram shows a wall and chloroplasts, use those as structural clues, not the diagram color alone.","Does a plant root cell without chloroplasts become an animal cell?","No. Plant cells differ by function. A root cell may not photosynthesize but still has plant structures such as a cell wall.","Magnification is a product, not a sum. Larger magnification also does not guarantee more useful detail.","Cells have specialized structures, and careful microscopy separates observations from model interpretations.",B.aMM)
B.aZh=s(["Mitosis","Meiosis and fertilization","Biological organization","Trophic energy transfer"],t.s)
B.aXx=s(["Mitosis divides a nucleus so resulting cells usually receive the same chromosome number and genetic information. It supports growth and repair. Asexual reproduction can produce genetically very similar offspring from one parent.","Meiosis produces cells with half the usual chromosome number for sexual reproduction. Fertilization joins reproductive cells, combining information from two parents and restoring the usual number.","Cells form tissues, tissues form organs, and organs work in systems within organisms. Organisms belong to populations, communities, and ecosystems; the biosphere includes all Earth ecosystems.","Trophic levels identify feeding positions. Producers support consumers. Less energy is available at successive levels because organisms use energy and release much as heat; energy does not cycle like matter."],t.s)
B.atB=s(["0|Which process mainly supports growth and repair with the usual chromosome number?","1|Which model part halves chromosome number then combines parental information?","2|Which model part orders cell, tissue, organ, and organism?","3|Which model part explains less available energy at higher feeding levels?","0|Which division is used when many body cells replace damaged cells?","1|Which model part connects sexual reproduction with variation from two parents?","2|Which model part includes populations within communities?","3|Which model part represents producers at the base of an energy pyramid?","1|A reproductive cell must have half the chromosome number before fusion. Which model process provides it?","3|Only a fraction of plant energy reaches top predators. Which model part explains the decrease?","2|Several populations interact with nonliving surroundings. Which model framework identifies this as an ecosystem level?"],t.s)
B.c87=new A.bO(2,"Life Science","Cell Division Links Organisms and Ecosystems","Distinguish mitosis and meiosis|Trace levels of organization|Explain decreasing energy across trophic levels",B.aZh,B.aXx,"In a simple model, plants capture 1000 energy units and herbivores receive 100. If the next transfer is also 10%, carnivores receive 10. The 10% values are illustrative, not a rule for every ecosystem.","Why can a food pyramid usually support fewer top predators than herbivores?","Less energy reaches higher trophic levels. Much is used in life processes and dispersed as heat at earlier levels.","Meiosis is not growth division, and energy pyramids are not simply diagrams of animal body sizes.","Cell processes sustain organisms; organization and feeding links connect them to the biosphere.",B.atB)
B.aYK=s(["Balanced forces","Unbalanced forces","Distance and displacement","Speed velocity and graphs"],t.s)
B.aAV=s(["Balanced forces have zero vector sum. They do not change velocity: an object may remain at rest or continue at constant velocity. Draw force arrows with direction and relative size.","Unbalanced forces have a nonzero sum and cause acceleration. An 8-newton rightward force opposed by 3 newtons leftward gives a net 5 newtons rightward.","Distance is total path length; displacement is change from start to finish with direction. Walking 3 meters east and 3 meters west gives 6 meters distance but zero displacement.","Speed uses distance per time; velocity uses displacement per time and direction. A straight rising distance-time line indicates constant speed; its slope is distance change divided by time change."],t.s)
B.aIa=s(["0|Which force condition can maintain constant velocity?","1|Which force condition gives a nonzero vector sum?","2|Which model part distinguishes a whole route from start-to-finish change?","3|Which model part uses the slope of a distance-time graph?","0|Equal opposite forces act on a resting box. Which condition fits?","1|Opposing forces are 8 newtons and 3 newtons. Which condition fits the net force?","2|Which model part explains a return trip having zero change in position but nonzero path length?","3|Which model part distinguishes 2 meters per second from 2 meters per second east?","0|A cart moves straight at constant velocity. Which force condition is consistent with that motion?","2|A walker goes east then returns to the start. Which model part explains why displacement is zero?","3|A straight graph rises 6 meters over 3 seconds. Which model part gives a speed of 2 meters per second?"],t.s)
B.c7r=new A.bO(3,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Forces Vectors and Motion Graphs","Find a simple net force|Distinguish distance from displacement|Read uniform motion on a graph",B.aYK,B.aAV,"A walker goes 4 meters east, then 4 meters west in 8 seconds. Distance is 8 meters, average speed 1 meter per second, displacement zero, and average velocity zero.","Must a moving object have an unbalanced force on it?","No. If velocity is constant, forces can balance. A nonzero net force is needed to change velocity, not to sustain ideal constant velocity.","Zero average velocity does not mean no movement occurred. A return trip can have nonzero distance and speed.","Net force changes velocity; vector quantities require direction as well as size.",B.aIa)
B.b02=s(["Temperature versus heat","Conduction","Convection","Radiation and conversion"],t.s)
B.aYP=s(["Temperature indicates thermal state and relates to average particle motion. Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. A large warm bath can transfer more energy than a tiny hotter drop.","Conduction transfers energy through interactions between neighboring particles without bulk flow. Metals often conduct well; insulation slows the transfer.","Convection transfers energy through bulk motion of fluids. Warmer less-dense fluid can rise while cooler denser fluid sinks, setting up circulation.","Radiation transfers energy by electromagnetic waves and can cross empty space. Solar radiation warms Earth. In some power systems heat produces moving steam, which drives a turbine and generator; conversion is not perfectly efficient."],t.s)
B.aIS=s(["0|Which model part distinguishes degrees Celsius from transferred energy?","1|Which mechanism warms a spoon by contact with a drink?","2|Which mechanism carries energy through circulating warm water?","3|Which model part explains energy arriving across space from the Sun?","0|Which model part explains why hotter does not always mean more total transferable energy?","1|Which mechanism is reduced by a suitable insulating handle?","2|Which mechanism involves bulk movement of warmer and cooler fluid?","3|Which model part includes heat driving a turbine and generator?","2|Warm air rises and cooler air replaces it. Which mechanism describes this circulation?","1|A pan transfers energy along its metal handle without the handle material flowing. Which mechanism fits?","3|A solar collector receives energy through a near-vacuum path. Which model part explains the transfer?"],t.s)
B.c7W=new A.bO(3,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Heat Temperature and Energy Technologies","Distinguish temperature and heat|Explain three heat-transfer mechanisms|Trace heat-to-electricity conversion",B.b02,B.aYP,"A warm drink heats a metal spoon mainly by conduction. Circulating fluid moves energy by convection. Energy arriving from the Sun is radiation; it does not require air between Sun and Earth.","Why is sunlight reaching Earth not conduction through space?","Conduction requires material interactions along the path. Solar energy crosses the near-vacuum as radiation.","Heat and temperature are not interchangeable. Heat describes transferred energy, not a substance stored like a liquid.","Particles and fluid motion explain heat transfer; radiation can cross space, and heat can be converted into useful work.",B.aIS)
B.b2k=s(["Normal fault","Reverse fault","Strike-slip fault","Earthquake source and effects"],t.s)
B.awV=s(["On a normal fault, the block above an inclined fault plane moves down relative to the other block. Extension pulls the crust apart.","On a reverse fault, the block above the inclined plane moves up. Compression squeezes the crust. A low-angle reverse fault is a thrust fault.","On a strike-slip fault, blocks move mainly sideways past one another. A map view is useful for showing the horizontal displacement.","The focus is the underground rupture source and the epicenter lies above it at the surface. Magnitude describes event size; intensity describes effects at a location. Distance, ground conditions, and buildings affect damage. Some undersea ruptures displace the seabed and generate tsunamis; not every earthquake does."],t.s)
B.aFP=s(["0|Which fault has its upper block moving down under extension?","1|Which fault has its upper block moving up under compression?","2|Which fault moves mainly sideways?","3|Which model part distinguishes focus from epicenter?","0|Which fault type is consistent with crust being pulled apart?","1|Which fault family includes a thrust fault?","2|Which fault type is best shown with opposite horizontal map arrows?","3|Which model part distinguishes one event magnitude from local intensity?","1|An inclined fault is compressed and its upper block rises. Which fault type fits?","2|A road is shifted sideways across a fault with little vertical change. Which type fits?","3|Two towns experience different damage from the same earthquake. Which model part explains why?"],t.s)
B.c7w=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space Science","Fault Motion Earthquake Evidence and Safety","Compare normal reverse and strike-slip motion|Distinguish focus from epicenter|Explain why shaking effects vary",B.b2k,B.awV,"One earthquake can have one reported magnitude but different intensities in two towns. A town closer to rupture or on softer ground may shake more strongly. Magnitude and local effects answer different questions.","Does every undersea earthquake necessarily create a destructive tsunami?","No. Significant water displacement is needed. Some fault motions do not lift or lower enough seabed. Follow local warnings and move to prescribed high ground when instructed or natural tsunami warning signs occur.","Do not interpret a map as predicting an exact earthquake date. Use hazard information for preparedness, not date predictions.","Fault direction explains rupture style; source, distance, and local conditions explain earthquake effects.",B.aFP)
B.b0e=s(["Rotation","Axial tilt and revolution","Land and sea breezes","Monsoons and ITCZ"],t.s)
B.b34=s(["Earth rotates about its axis, producing day and night. The Sun appears to move daily because the observing place turns with Earth.","Earth revolves with an axis tilted about 23.5 degrees from the perpendicular to its orbit. Changing sunlight angle and day length cause seasonal heating differences. Opposite hemispheres have opposite seasons.","Land and sea heat at different rates. In a typical daytime sea breeze, warmer land air rises and cooler surface air moves from sea toward land. At night, surface flow can reverse as land cools faster.","Monsoons are seasonal shifts in prevailing winds linked to large-scale heating differences. Near the ITCZ, converging winds and rising moist air favor clouds and rain. These systems influence Philippine weather, with local variation."],t.s)
B.aQd=s(["0|Which motion explains the daily alternation of daylight and darkness?","1|Which model part explains opposite seasons in the two hemispheres?","2|Which model part explains a daytime surface wind from sea to shore?","3|Which model part explains seasonal shifts in prevailing winds?","0|Which motion makes the Sun appear to cross the sky each day?","1|Which model part changes sunlight angle and day length over a year?","2|Which model part uses the faster heating and cooling of land?","3|Which model part includes converging winds and rising moist air near the ITCZ?","2|Land warms, air rises, and cooler surface air arrives from the sea. Which local system is described?","1|A learner says Earth-Sun distance alone causes summer everywhere. Which model part corrects the claim?","3|A long seasonal change in prevailing wind differs from one afternoon shower. Which model part makes that distinction?"],t.s)
B.c7H=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space Science","Sunlight Drives Daily and Seasonal Systems","Explain rotation and tilt effects|Compare land and sea breezes|Connect heating to large weather patterns",B.b0e,B.b34,"On a sunny coast, land warms faster than water. Air over land warms and rises. Surface air from the sea replaces it, so the daytime breeze blows sea to land. Rising air is only one part of the circulation.","Why do seasons not occur at the same time in both hemispheres?","Axial tilt directs more concentrated sunlight and longer days toward one hemisphere while the other gets less. Distance to the Sun cannot alone explain opposite seasons.","Monsoon does not mean any heavy shower. It describes a seasonal wind pattern that can influence rainfall.","Uneven solar heating, Earth rotation, and axial tilt organize daily and seasonal patterns.",B.aQd)
B.aE8=s([B.c7N,B.c7m,B.c7f,B.c87,B.c7r,B.c7W,B.c7w,B.c7H],t.EM)
