B.b6b=new A.ao(B.bjp,["Nine plus sixteen equals twenty-five.","b\xb2 = 169 \u2212 25 = 144 \u2192 b = 12","4 + 9 \u2260 16, so not right."],t.w)
B.azg=s(["Right-triangle legs three and four give hypotenuse what?|5|7;1;25|Square sum is twenty-five, whose positive root is five.","Where is the hypotenuse?|Opposite the right angle|Always horizontal;Opposite the smallest angle;Any chosen side|It is the longest side of a right triangle.","Hypotenuse thirteen and leg five give other leg what?|12|18;8;144|Subtract twenty-five from 169 and take the positive root.","Rectangle sides six and eight give diagonal what?|10|14;2;100|The diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right triangle.","Does 2\xb2 + 3\xb2 equal 4\xb2?|No|Yes;Only for centimeters;Only for rotated triangles|Thirteen is not sixteen.","Which formula is correct for right-triangle legs a and b?|a\xb2 + b\xb2 = c\xb2|a + b = c;a\xb2 \u2212 b\xb2 = c\xb2;ab = c|The theorem compares the squared side lengths.","Why use the positive square root for a length?|Lengths are nonnegative|All roots are negative;Both signs give different physical lengths;Squaring changes units to time|A side length is not a signed coordinate.","Legs five and twelve give hypotenuse what?|13|17;7;169|Twenty-five plus 144 equals 169.","Hypotenuse seventeen and leg eight give other leg what?|15|9;25;225|Square difference is 225, giving fifteen.","Legs nine and twelve give hypotenuse what?|15|21;3;225|Eighty-one plus 144 is 225.","Can the theorem be used directly for every triangle?|No, a right angle is required|Yes;Only for equal sides;Only for small triangles|The squared-side relation is specific to right triangles."],t.s)
B.a98=new A.cs("Pythagorean Theorem",B.auz,"In a right triangle, the squares of the shorter sides sum to the hypotenuse square.","For perpendicular legs a and b and hypotenuse c, a\xb2 + b\xb2 = c\xb2. The hypotenuse lies opposite the right angle and is longest. Legs three and four give c\xb2 = 9 + 16 = 25, hence c = 5. To find a missing leg, subtract the known leg square from c\xb2. The relation is for right triangles; do not apply it to an arbitrary triangle without checking.",B.b6b,"Find the diagonal of a six-by-eight rectangle. Step 1: its sides form perpendicular legs. Step 2: square to get thirty-six and sixty-four. Step 3: diagonal square is one hundred. Step 4: the positive diagonal length is ten.","A right triangle has hypotenuse seventeen and leg eight. Find the other leg.","The missing square is 289 \u2212 64 = 225, giving the positive length fifteen.","Do not add side lengths instead of squares or choose a negative length. Identify the hypotenuse before rearranging the formula.","Use the right angle to choose legs and hypotenuse, then square, add or subtract, and take a positive root.",B.azg)
