B.ot=s([B.aqu,B.aqt,B.aqs,B.aqv,B.aqz,B.aqy,B.aqx,B.aqw,B.aqB,B.aqA,B.arp,B.aro,B.aqp,B.ars,B.arr,B.ati,B.aqq,B.art,B.aCT,B.aBg,B.arw,B.atl,B.atk,B.atj,B.arx,B.atm,B.aMv,B.aID,B.arm,B.aNL,B.aVg,B.aYF,B.arn,B.arl,B.aKk,B.aWC,B.aOs,B.aAY,B.b2a,B.aOn,B.atg,B.aOO,B.aEJ,B.aQL,B.aVx,B.ayM,B.aG9,B.azn,B.ath,B.b_w,B.b0J,B.b33,B.aJt,B.avw,B.aSt,B.aIE,B.aLz,B.aNN,B.aDU,B.apY,B.aBF,B.aSS,B.aJ0,B.aFb,B.aLi,B.apy,B.aPW,B.aAH,B.aNg,B.aG1,B.aKD,B.aTO,B.aET,B.b1T,B.ay4,B.as1,B.aAz,B.aBO,B.aYQ,B.b17,B.ar6,B.aq7,B.axi,B.aJY,B.aIm,B.aq8,B.axo,B.arS,B.aOt,B.aQl,B.aFO,B.apM,B.b_Q,B.aDL,B.ar7,B.aLs,B.aBz,B.at4,B.aXq,B.axx,B.b_V,B.aKR,B.aA4,B.aN_,B.azB,B.aNZ,B.aWA,B.aDg,B.ayO,B.aYl,B.aK7,B.aTN,B.aMU,B.b35,B.aL0,B.aJD,B.b2M,B.aFl,B.aXX,B.aWB,B.b2Z,B.aSL,B.avp,B.aB0,B.aq6,B.aTU,B.atb,B.aQ7,B.aM3,B.b3y,B.aBP,B.aSM,B.aB_,B.aT3,B.aRh,B.aSe,B.aQY,B.aCM,B.awe,B.aRi,B.aFk,B.aqj,B.aS5,B.aDE,B.b0p,B.avg,B.aPV,B.aYY,B.aMy,B.aNT,B.aCW,B.b37,B.b1n,B.b_f,B.b_H,B.aOA,B.azH,B.aGK,B.aB9,B.aFo],t.Zb)
B.aGc=s(["Divergent boundary","Convergent boundary","Transform boundary","Lithosphere and asthenosphere"],t.s)
B.aIe=s(["At a divergent boundary, plates move apart. Rising mantle material can partially melt, forming new oceanic crust at ridges. Continental extension can form rifts.","At a convergent boundary, plates move toward each other. Subduction of oceanic lithosphere can form trenches and volcanic arcs; continental collision can build high mountains without a typical oceanic subduction arc.","At a transform boundary, plates slide past. Earthquakes are common, but crust is not systematically created or destroyed along an ideal transform segment.","The lithosphere includes crust and the rigid uppermost mantle. It moves over a hotter weaker asthenosphere that is mostly solid but can flow slowly. Plates are not floating on a global ocean of liquid magma."],t.s)
B.aUl=s(["0|Which boundary forms new oceanic crust as plates separate?","1|Which boundary can form a trench and volcanic arc by subduction?","2|Which boundary mainly slides plates sideways?","3|Which model part defines a plate as more than crust alone?","0|Which boundary type includes many mid-ocean ridges?","1|Which boundary type includes continental collision mountain building?","2|Which ideal boundary neither creates nor consumes crust systematically?","3|Which model part corrects the idea of plates floating on a fully liquid mantle?","1|A trench, volcanic arc, and deepening earthquake zone occur together. Which boundary is best supported?","0|Two plates pull apart and new basaltic crust forms between them. Which boundary fits?","2|Earthquakes occur along a sideways offset with no typical new-crust ridge. Which boundary motion fits?"],t.s)
B.c81=new A.bO(1,"Earth and Space","Plate Boundaries Build and Destroy Crust","Compare three boundary types|Relate boundaries to landforms|Distinguish lithosphere from the underlying mantle",B.aGc,B.aIe,"A map shows a deep trench beside a volcanic island arc and earthquakes extending deeper inland. Together these clues support subduction at a convergent boundary, not a ridge where plates move apart.","Why is a volcanic arc alone not enough to label every nearby boundary type?","Use multiple evidence: plate motions, trenches, earthquake depths, and the regional setting. Different features can overlap or have other origins.","Do not call every collision subduction in the same form. Oceanic subduction and continental collision have different outcomes.","Relative plate motion and plate material explain characteristic landforms and earthquake patterns.",B.aUl)
B.aUT=s(["Continental-fit evidence","Seafloor-spreading evidence","Earthquake and volcano patterns","Plate-driving mechanisms"],t.s)
B.aJJ=s(["Matching fossils, rock sequences, and past-climate clues across separated continents support past connections. Coastline fit alone is suggestive but incomplete because coasts change.","Ocean crust is generally youngest near spreading ridges and older away from them. Roughly symmetric magnetic stripes record field reversals as new crust forms and moves outward.","Earthquake epicenters, depths, volcanoes, and mountain belts outline many plate interactions. Not all volcanoes occur at plate boundaries; hotspots are an important exception.","Earth internal heat and gravity contribute to plate motion. Sinking dense slabs can pull plates, and elevated ridges contribute gravitational spreading. Mantle convection interacts with plates; a simple conveyor-belt picture has limits."],t.s)
B.aCx=s(["0|Which evidence includes similar fossils across now-separated continents?","1|Which evidence includes symmetric magnetic stripes and crust ages?","2|Which evidence uses mapped epicenters and volcanic belts?","3|Which model part includes slab pull and ridge-related gravity effects?","0|Which evidence becomes stronger when matching rock sequences accompany apparent coast fit?","1|Which evidence shows young crust near a ridge and older crust farther away?","2|Which model part warns that hotspot volcanoes can lie away from boundaries?","3|Which model part links internal heat and gravity with motion rather than describing distribution only?","1|A model must explain both ridge-centered age patterns and matching reversal records. Which evidence set is central?","0|A learner relies only on continent shapes. Which evidence set can add fossils and rock matches?","3|A dense descending slab helps move its attached plate. Which model part explains the force mechanism?"],t.s)
B.c7h=new A.bO(1,"Earth and Space","Evidence and Mechanisms of Moving Plates","Evaluate multiple lines of plate evidence|Read seafloor age and magnetic patterns|Explain forces driving plate motion",B.aUT,B.aJJ,"A ridge has matching magnetic stripe sequences on either side, with older crust farther away. New material forming at the center and moving outward explains both age and magnetic evidence at once.","Why is one matching shoreline weaker evidence than matching shorelines plus fossils and rocks?","Different independent observations reduce alternative explanations. A strong model accounts for several patterns, not only a visual resemblance.","A magnetic stripe is not an alternating strip of ordinary paint or north-south pieces glued to the sea floor. It records mineral magnetization acquired as rock cooled.","Plate tectonics is supported by converging evidence and mechanisms involving internal heat and gravity.",B.aCx)
B.ard=s(["Radio and microwaves","Infrared and visible","Ultraviolet","X-rays and gamma rays"],t.s)
B.b1w=s(["Radio waves and microwaves have relatively long wavelengths and low frequencies. They support communications; microwaves can transfer energy to food. Devices still require safe use, and a microwave oven must never be tampered with.","Infrared is associated with thermal emission; visible light is the small spectral range detected by human eyes. All electromagnetic waves travel at the same speed in vacuum, with c = frequency \xd7 wavelength.","Ultraviolet lies above visible violet in frequency. It can damage skin and eyes, and some ultraviolet is ionizing. Shade and appropriate protection reduce exposure.","X-rays and gamma rays have very high frequencies and short wavelengths. Their photons can ionize matter. Medical and industrial use requires justified exposure and shielding, not casual demonstrations."],t.s)
B.aJM=s(["0|Which model group includes long-wavelength communication waves?","1|Which model group includes the wavelengths detected by human eyes?","2|Which model region lies just above visible violet?","3|Which model group includes highly penetrating ionizing photons used in controlled imaging?","0|Which model group includes microwave cooking and radio broadcasting?","1|Which model group includes thermal infrared emission?","2|Which model region makes skin and eye protection from sunlight important?","3|Which model group has the shortest wavelengths among the four groups?","3|A radiation type has much higher photon energy than visible light and requires specialized shielding. Which group best fits?","1|A sensor detects infrared rather than the part our eyes see. Which combined model group includes both?","2|Sunlight contains a region beyond violet that can damage skin. Which model region is that?"],t.s)
B.c7B=new A.bO(2,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Electromagnetic Waves Across the Spectrum","Relate frequency wavelength and energy|Order spectrum regions|Connect uses and risks to wave properties",B.ard,B.b1w,"In vacuum, doubling an electromagnetic wave frequency halves its wavelength because their product remains c. Higher frequency also means greater energy per photon, not a faster vacuum speed.","Does a gamma ray travel faster through vacuum than a radio wave?","No. Both travel at c in vacuum. They differ in wavelength, frequency, and photon energy.","Non-ionizing does not mean harmless at every intensity. Heating and exposure conditions still matter.","Spectrum regions differ in frequency and photon energy, with uses and hazards tied to those properties.",B.aJM)
B.aSA=s(["Reflection in mirrors","Refraction in lenses","Electric motor","Electric generator"],t.s)
B.b_C=s(["Reflection returns light from a surface with incidence angle equal to reflection angle, measured from the normal. A plane mirror forms an upright virtual image the same size, as far behind the mirror as the object is in front.","Refraction changes light direction at many boundaries because wave speed changes. A convex lens converges parallel rays; a concave lens diverges them. A convex lens image depends on object position relative to its focal length.","A motor uses current in a magnetic field to produce a force and rotation. It converts electrical energy into mechanical energy, with thermal and sound losses.","A generator uses changing magnetic flux through a coil to induce an emf. Mechanical motion can produce electrical energy. A stationary magnet beside an unchanging coil is not enough for continuous generation."],t.s)
B.avL=s(["0|Which model part uses equal angles measured from the normal?","1|Which model part explains a convex lens converging parallel rays?","2|Which machine mainly converts electrical energy into rotation?","3|Which machine mainly converts mechanical input into electrical output?","0|Which model part gives a virtual plane-mirror image behind the surface?","1|Which model part changes light direction because speed changes between media?","2|Which machine uses current experiencing a magnetic force?","3|Which machine requires changing magnetic flux for induced emf?","0|An object is 30 centimeters before a plane mirror and its image appears 30 centimeters behind. Which model explains the 60-centimeter separation?","3|A coil and magnet stay fixed and no continuous emf is induced. Which machine principle requires flux change?","2|A powered fan rotates because current interacts with a magnetic field. Which machine model fits?"],t.s)
B.c7F=new A.bO(2,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Optical Images and Electromagnetic Machines","Predict simple mirror and lens effects|Distinguish reflection from refraction|Compare motors and generators",B.aSA,B.b_C,"For a plane mirror, an object 30 centimeters in front has a virtual image 30 centimeters behind. The object-image separation is 60 centimeters. The image is not physically located on the mirror surface.","Why does turning a generator handle produce electricity but holding everything still does not continuously do so?","Turning changes magnetic flux through the coil. The changing flux induces an emf; motion supplies the energy.","A convex lens does not always give the same image type. Object distance matters. Never focus sunlight to test lenses.","Light direction explains images; electromagnetic forces and induction connect electrical and mechanical energy.",B.avL)
B.aWF=s(["Nervous signaling","Endocrine signaling","Negative feedback","Reproductive coordination"],t.s)
B.b2B=s(["The nervous system sends electrical signals along neurons and chemical signals across many synapses. It supports rapid, targeted coordination, including sensing changes and activating muscles.","Endocrine glands release hormones into blood. Target cells respond through matching receptors. Hormonal responses often act over longer times than a rapid nerve signal, but timing varies.","Negative feedback counteracts a deviation from a regulated range. When blood glucose rises, insulin helps cells take up glucose and promotes storage, tending to lower the level. It is not a guarantee of perfect regulation.","Reproduction involves nervous and endocrine coordination. FSH and LH influence ovarian activity; estrogen and progesterone influence the uterine lining. Falling hormone levels can lead to menstruation when pregnancy does not occur. Ovulation timing varies, so a simple day chart is not reliable contraception."],t.s)
B.aTn=s(["0|Which model part carries rapid signals along neurons?","1|Which model part sends hormones through blood to receptor-bearing targets?","2|Which model part opposes a deviation from a regulated range?","3|Which model part connects ovarian activity and uterine lining changes?","0|Which model part includes chemical communication at many synapses?","1|Which model part explains why not every cell responds to the same hormone?","2|Which model part explains insulin tending to reduce a glucose rise?","3|Which model part includes FSH, LH, estrogen, and progesterone?","2|A change triggers responses tending to reverse that change. Which control model fits?","1|A hormone circulates widely but only certain cells respond. Which model part explains target specificity?","3|A learner assumes every cycle has ovulation on one fixed day. Which model part explains why the simple chart is limited?"],t.s)
B.c8n=new A.bO(3,"Living Things","Feedback Coordinates Body and Reproduction","Compare nervous and hormonal signals|Explain negative feedback|Trace a simplified reproductive cycle",B.aWF,B.b2B,"After a meal, glucose rises. The pancreas releases insulin. Cells take up more glucose and the level tends toward its regulated range. A response opposing the original rise is negative feedback.","Does negative feedback mean the response is harmful or emotionally negative?","No. Negative means it opposes the direction of the initial change. It is a control relationship.","Hormones do not affect every cell equally. Receptors and target-cell responses matter. This lesson is not personal medical advice.","Signals and feedback coordinate organs; reproductive changes involve interacting hormones rather than one isolated organ.",B.aTn)
B.arN=s(["DNA to protein","Mutation and variation","Natural selection","Carrying capacity"],t.s)
B.aEG=s(["DNA stores sequence information. During gene expression, information is transcribed into RNA and translated into a protein sequence. Proteins influence structures and processes; not every gene directly specifies a visible trait.","Mutation changes DNA sequence and can affect a protein, regulation, or neither. Heritable variation also comes from recombination. Mutations do not appear because an organism knows which change it needs.","Natural selection occurs when heritable differences affect survival and reproduction in a particular environment. Over generations, allele frequencies can change. Individuals do not evolve simply by trying harder.","Carrying capacity is the population size an environment can sustain under given conditions. Food, water, space, and interactions constrain growth. It can change with seasons, disturbance, and resource supply; biodiversity does not guarantee immunity to collapse."],t.s)
B.aZs=s(["0|Which model part orders transcription before translation?","1|Which model part includes a DNA sequence change?","2|Which model part changes trait frequencies through differential reproductive success?","3|Which model part limits population growth through resources?","0|Which model part links sequence information with a protein product?","1|Which model part can have neutral, harmful, or beneficial effects?","2|Which model part acts across generations rather than by individual effort?","3|Which model part can change when habitat food supply changes?","2|An inherited variant becomes more common because its carriers leave more offspring. Which model process fits?","3|A population without predators still stops growing as food becomes scarce. Which model concept explains the limit?","1|A learner says every DNA change happens to meet a need. Which model part corrects that purposeful-change claim?"],t.s)
B.c7s=new A.bO(3,"Living Things","DNA Evolution and Ecosystem Limits","Trace information from DNA to protein|Explain population change by selection|Relate resources to carrying capacity",B.arN,B.aEG,"A population varies in an inherited trait. After a habitat change, some variants leave more offspring. Across generations those variants can become more common. Selection acts on existing variation, not a planned response by every individual.","Can a population grow without limit if it has no predators?","No. Food, space, disease, and other constraints can still limit growth. Carrying capacity depends on resources and conditions, not predators alone.","A DNA change is not automatically harmful or beneficial. Effects depend on the sequence, function, and environment.","DNA variation enables evolutionary change, while ecological resources limit population growth.",B.aZs)
B.aZ3=s(["Boyle relationship","Charles relationship","Pressure-temperature relationship","Ideal gas model"],t.s)
B.aTL=s(["For fixed gas amount at constant temperature, pressure times volume is constant. Halving volume doubles pressure in the idealized Boyle relationship. More frequent wall collisions explain the pressure rise.","At constant pressure and gas amount, volume is proportional to absolute temperature. Use kelvin, approximately Celsius plus 273. A gas at 300 K expanding to 600 K doubles its ideal volume.","At constant volume and gas amount, pressure is proportional to kelvin temperature. Heating increases particle motion and wall-collision effects. Never test this by heating a sealed container.","The ideal gas equation PV = nRT connects pressure, volume, amount, and absolute temperature with consistent units. It is an approximation that works best away from condensation and extreme pressure; real particles have size and attractions."],t.s)
B.aV0=s(["0|Which relationship doubles pressure when volume halves at fixed temperature and amount?","1|Which relationship doubles volume when kelvin temperature doubles at fixed pressure?","2|Which relationship increases pressure with kelvin temperature in a fixed volume?","3|Which model uses PV = nRT?","0|Which relationship uses P1V1 = P2V2?","1|Which relationship requires constant pressure while volume changes with temperature?","2|Which relationship explains a pressure rise in a rigid sealed gas container as it warms?","3|Which model has limitations from real particle size and attractions?","0|A fixed gas changes from 4 liters to 2 liters at constant temperature. Which relationship predicts pressure rising from 100 to 200 kilopascals?","1|A balloon model remains at constant pressure while temperature changes from 300 K to 600 K. Which relationship predicts double volume?","2|A gas stays at fixed volume and amount while absolute temperature doubles. Which relationship predicts double pressure?"],t.s)
B.c7q=new A.bO(4,"Matter","Gas Laws Require Conditions and Kelvin","Use inverse pressure-volume reasoning|Use absolute temperature in a gas law|State model conditions and limitations",B.aZ3,B.aTL,"A fixed gas occupies 4 liters at 100 kilopascals and constant temperature. Compressing to 2 liters gives P2 = 100 \xd7 4 \xf7 2 = 200 kilopascals. The temperature condition is essential.","Why is changing 20 degrees Celsius to 40 degrees Celsius not a doubling of absolute temperature?","Those values are approximately 293 K and 313 K. Their ratio is about 1.07, not 2. Gas-law ratios need absolute temperature.","Do not apply a two-variable rule when gas escapes or another required condition changes. Use provided data only.","Gas-law predictions depend on fixed conditions, consistent units, and absolute temperature.",B.aV0)
B.aDR=s(["Carbohydrates and lipids","Proteins and nucleic acids","Balanced chemical equation","Reaction rate and catalysts"],t.s)
B.aBM=s(["Carbohydrates include sugars and starches used in energy supply and structure. Lipids include fats and membrane components. Food labels describe nutrients per stated serving, so compare serving sizes before totals.","Proteins are built from amino acids and serve structural, transport, and enzyme roles. Nucleic acids such as DNA store information. A food label protein mass is not a direct measure of all biological value.","A balanced equation conserves atoms of every element. In 2H2 + O2 \u2192 2H2O, each side has four hydrogen atoms and two oxygen atoms. Change coefficients, not chemical subscripts, to balance an equation.","Reaction rate depends on effective particle collisions and can change with temperature, concentration, surface area, or catalysts. A catalyst provides a lower-activation-energy pathway and is regenerated overall; it does not supply missing atoms."],t.s)
B.b1U=s(["0|Which model group includes sugars, starches, and fats?","1|Which model group includes amino-acid chains and DNA?","2|Which model part checks atom counts on both sides?","3|Which model part includes lower activation energy through a catalyst?","0|Which model group requires matching serving sizes when comparing carbohydrate and fat labels?","1|Which model group includes many biological enzymes and genetic information molecules?","2|Which model part changes coefficients rather than water chemical subscripts?","3|Which model part predicts increased rate from more effective collisions?","2|A learner changes H2O into H2O2 to balance a water-forming reaction. Which model part corrects this?","3|An enzyme speeds a reaction but is regenerated overall. Which model explains why it is not used as a bulk reactant?","0|Two food labels report different serving masses. Which model group requires a fair equal-serving comparison before judging sugar or fat amounts?"],t.s)
B.c7M=new A.bO(4,"Matter","Biomolecules Reactions and Conservation","Connect biomolecule groups to functions|Balance a simple reaction|Explain factors affecting reaction rate",B.aDR,B.aBM,"To balance hydrogen plus oxygen forming water, begin H2 + O2 \u2192 H2O. Put 2 before water to balance oxygen, then 2 before H2 to balance hydrogen. Check four H and two O on each side. This is a paper model, not an experiment.","Why can an enzyme speed a reaction without being a source of the product atoms?","It is a catalyst: it provides a pathway with lower activation energy and is regenerated overall. Reactant atoms still supply the product atoms.","Changing H2O to H2O2 to balance oxygen changes the substance. Balance with coefficients instead.","Molecular structure supports life functions; chemical reactions rearrange conserved atoms at condition-dependent rates.",B.b1U)
