B.aOw=s(["Read ordered pairs.","Plot points in the first quadrant.","Find horizontal and vertical distances."],t.s)
B.bhN={"(3, 5)":0,"(5, 3)":1,"(2, 4) to (7, 4)":2}
B.b6u=new A.ao(B.bhN,["Three right, then five up from the origin.","Five right, then three up: a different position.","Horizontal distance five units."],t.w)
B.b10=s(["In (3, 5), the x-coordinate is what?|3|5;8;0|The first number gives horizontal position.","Where is the origin?|(0, 0)|(1, 1);(0, 1);(1, 0)|Both coordinate axes start together at zero.","Which point lies on the x-axis?|(4, 0)|(0, 4);(4, 4);(1, 4)|An x-axis point has zero y-coordinate.","Which lies on the y-axis?|(0, 6)|(6, 0);(6, 6);(1, 6)|A y-axis point has zero x-coordinate.","Are (3, 5) and (5, 3) the same point?|No|Yes, order never matters;Only if both are positive;Only on any graph|Swapping coordinates changes the position.","Distance from (2, 4) to (7, 4)?|5 units|9 units;3 units;4 units|They share y, so subtract x-values.","From the origin, six right and two up gives what?|(6, 2)|(2, 6);(8, 0);(0, 8)|Record horizontal movement first.","What stays fixed in a vertical move?|The x-coordinate|The y-coordinate always;Both coordinates;Neither coordinate|Vertical movement changes only y.","Distance from (3, 1) to (3, 7)?|6 units|8 units;4 units;3 units|They share x, so subtract y-values.","Which point is four right and eight up?|(4, 8)|(8, 4);(12, 0);(0, 12)|The ordered pair is horizontal then vertical.","Distance from (1, 2) to (6, 2)?|5 units|7 units;4 units;6 units|The shared y makes this a horizontal distance."],t.s)
B.a8W=new A.cs("Coordinate Plane",B.aOw,"An ordered pair gives a position using two perpendicular number lines.","The origin is (0, 0). In (3, 5), move three units along the horizontal x-axis, then five upward parallel to the y-axis. Order matters: (5, 3) is a different point. This lesson uses nonnegative first-quadrant positions. Points on the x-axis have y = 0; points on the y-axis have x = 0. For points sharing y, horizontal distance is the difference between x-values; for points sharing x, use y-values.",B.b6u,"Locate (6, 2). Step 1: start at the origin. Step 2: move six right. Step 3: move two up. Step 4: check the x-coordinate is six and y-coordinate two.","Find the distance from (3, 1) to (3, 7). Explain which coordinate stays fixed.","The x-coordinate remains three, so movement is vertical. Distance is 7 \u2212 1 = 6 units.","Do not reverse coordinate order or start at an arbitrary point. Distance is a nonnegative amount, not a signed coordinate.","Read x first and y second; use shared-coordinate differences for axis-aligned distances.",B.b10)
B.Gs=s([B.Ch,B.C7,B.Cd,B.a8W,B.Cr],t.LQ)
B.aPZ=s(["Respiratory exchange","Circulatory transport","Photosynthesis","Cellular respiration"],t.s)
B.azA=s(["Alveoli provide a large thin moist surface for gas exchange. Oxygen diffuses into nearby blood and carbon dioxide diffuses out. Smoking damages respiratory structures and can reduce effective exchange.","Blood transports gases and nutrients. The right side of the heart sends blood to lungs; the left sends it to the body. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, not necessarily oxygen-rich blood.","Photosynthesis in chloroplasts uses light energy to form sugars from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen. Carbon in plant sugars comes mainly from carbon dioxide, not from soil.","Cellular respiration releases usable energy from food. Aerobic respiration uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and water; mitochondria are important sites. Plants respire as well as photosynthesize."],t.s)
B.aX8=s(["0|Which model part includes diffusion across alveoli?","1|Which model part carries oxygen from lungs toward body cells?","2|Which process uses light to build sugars from carbon dioxide?","3|Which process releases usable energy from food using oxygen?","0|Which model part depends on a large thin moist lung surface?","1|Which model part defines arteries by flow away from the heart?","2|Which process supplies much of the oxygen released by green leaves?","3|Which process occurs in plants during darkness as well as in animals?","2|An investigation asks where the carbon in plant sugar originated. Which process draws it from carbon dioxide?","1|Blood needs to reach body cells after gaining oxygen in lungs. Which model part supplies that transport?","3|A learner claims plants never use sugars for energy. Which model process disproves that claim?"],t.s)
B.c7n=new A.bO(1,"Living Things","Gas Exchange Photosynthesis and Respiration","Trace oxygen through body systems|Compare photosynthesis and respiration|Explain a gas-exchange adaptation",B.aPZ,B.azA,"Trace an oxygen molecule: inhaled air \u2192 alveolus \u2192 blood \u2192 body cell. The cell uses oxygen in aerobic respiration. The heart transports it but does not itself take it directly from the air.","Do green plants stop needing respiration because they can photosynthesize?","No. Plant cells need usable energy from respiration day and night. Photosynthesis supplies sugars when suitable light is available.","Breathing is air movement; cellular respiration is a chemical process in cells. They are connected but different.","Gas-exchange surfaces and transport systems connect organisms to energy-releasing and food-making processes.",B.aX8)
B.aAe=s(["Incomplete dominance","Multiple alleles and codominance","Sex-linked inheritance","Environmental change and extinction"],t.s)
B.aTJ=s(["In incomplete dominance, a heterozygote has an intermediate phenotype in a simplified model. Red and white flower alleles can yield pink heterozygotes without the alleles permanently blending.","A gene may have more than two alleles in a population, but one person usually carries two copies. In the ABO model, IA and IB are codominant, so IAIB gives blood type AB; i is recessive.","A sex-linked gene lies on a sex chromosome. In a simple X-linked recessive model, an individual with one X can express a recessive allele on that X because a matching dominant allele is absent.","Environmental change can reduce food, habitat, or breeding success. Populations with low numbers and limited variation may be vulnerable. Extinction can have natural and human causes; individuals cannot choose inherited traits to meet a sudden need."],t.s)
B.aKm=s(["0|Which pattern gives an intermediate heterozygote phenotype?","1|Which model includes IAIB producing type AB blood?","2|Which model places a gene on a sex chromosome?","3|Which model part connects habitat loss with extinction risk?","0|Which pattern can produce pink flowers from red and white alleles in a simple model?","1|Which model distinguishes population allele variety from two copies in one person?","2|Which model explains expression of a recessive allele on a single X?","3|Which model part explains why a sudden loss of food can reduce a population?","0|Red and white offspring reappear after two pink heterozygotes are crossed. Which model explains why the alleles were not permanently blended?","1|A learner thinks three ABO alleles in the population means three copies in each person. Which model distinction corrects that?","3|A small isolated population loses its breeding habitat. Which model part addresses the resulting threat?"],t.s)
B.c7V=new A.bO(1,"Living Things","Inheritance Variation and Extinction Risk","Distinguish inheritance patterns|Interpret an allele combination|Explain why environmental change can threaten populations",B.aAe,B.aTJ,"In a simplified flower cross, RR red \xd7 WW white produces RW pink. Crossing RW \xd7 RW gives RR, RW, RW, WW. The original red and white outcomes can reappear, so the alleles were not erased by mixing.","Can a person carry all three common ABO alleles just because the population has three?","Usually no. Each person has two gene copies, one inherited from each parent. Multiple alleles describes population diversity.","An inherited trait is not a measure of worth. Avoid using human trait examples to rank people or predict complex abilities.","Inheritance patterns produce variation; rapid habitat change can still threaten diverse populations.",B.aKm)
B.aJe=s(["Ionic bonding","Covalent bonding","Metallic bonding","Intermolecular attraction"],t.s)
B.b1R=s(["Ionic bonding is electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions after electron transfer. Solid salts have fixed ions; molten or dissolved ions can move and carry current.","Covalent bonding involves atoms sharing electron pairs. Molecular substances contain covalent bonds within molecules, but a covalent substance can also have an extended network.","Metallic bonding involves positive metal centers and delocalized electrons. Mobile electrons explain why many metals conduct electricity as solids and why layers can shift without destroying all bonding.","Intermolecular attractions act between molecules, not as the covalent bonds within them. Boiling a molecular liquid usually separates molecules rather than splitting each molecule into atoms."],t.s)
B.aQF=s(["0|Which bond model uses attraction between oppositely charged ions?","1|Which bond model shares electron pairs between atoms?","2|Which bond model includes delocalized mobile electrons?","3|Which attraction model acts between molecules?","0|Which model explains a salt conducting when its ions become mobile?","1|Which model describes bonds within a water molecule?","2|Which model explains solid copper conducting electricity?","3|Which model mainly explains molecules separating when a molecular liquid boils?","0|A material conducts when molten but not as a solid because ions can move only in the melt. Which model fits?","3|A learner claims boiling water breaks every H2O into atoms. Which model level corrects that?","2|A solid conducts with moving electrons rather than migrating whole ions. Which bonding model best fits?"],t.s)
B.c7y=new A.bO(2,"Matter","Bonding Explains Material Properties","Compare ionic covalent and metallic bonding|Connect structure to conductivity|Separate bond type from intermolecular attraction",B.aJe,B.b1R,"A salt does not conduct well as a dry solid but does when molten. Its ions are charged in both states; the change is that ions can move in the melt. Mobility, not the first appearance of charge, explains the difference.","When liquid water boils, are its hydrogen and oxygen atoms normally separated from each other?","No. Water molecules separate farther from one another; the covalent bonds within ordinary H2O molecules remain.","Electron transfer creates ions, but the ionic bond itself is the attraction between ions. Do not equate every conductivity pattern with the same structure.","Bonding and particle mobility explain properties better than memorizing material labels alone.",B.aQF)
B.b2e=s(["Carbon bonding","Organic functional groups","Mole and molar mass","Percentage composition"],t.s)
B.b2Q=s(["Carbon often forms four covalent bonds and can bond to other carbon atoms in chains, branches, and rings. Different structures can produce different properties even with the same formula.","Functional groups help explain properties of organic compounds. Alcohols have an -OH group; hydrocarbons contain only carbon and hydrogen. Not every carbon-containing substance is classed as organic.","One mole contains about 6.02 \xd7 10^23 specified entities. Molar mass connects grams and moles: moles = mass divided by molar mass. Water has an approximate molar mass of 18 grams per mole.","Mass percentage is component mass divided by total mass times 100. Using H = 1 and O = 16, water contains 16/18 \xd7 100, or about 88.9% oxygen by mass, not by atom count."],t.s)
B.aMf=s(["0|Which model part explains carbon forming chains and rings?","1|Which model part identifies an alcohol by an -OH group?","2|Which model part converts grams using grams per mole?","3|Which model part uses component mass divided by total mass times 100?","0|Which model part explains different structures with the same molecular formula?","1|Which model part distinguishes hydrocarbons from compounds containing oxygen groups?","2|Which model part gives 2 moles from 36 grams of water at 18 grams per mole?","3|Which model part gives about 88.9% oxygen by mass in water?","2|A learner assumes one mole of every substance weighs 18 grams. Which model part corrects that?","3|A compound has two light atoms for every heavy atom, yet the heavy atom supplies most mass. Which calculation is needed rather than atom-count percentage?","0|Two compounds share a formula but have different arrangements and properties. Which model part explains the structural possibility?"],t.s)
B.c7E=new A.bO(2,"Matter","Carbon Compounds Moles and Composition","Explain carbon structural variety|Convert simple mass to moles|Calculate a mass percentage",B.b2e,B.b2Q,"A 36-gram water sample has 36 \xf7 18 = 2 moles. Each mole contains one mole of oxygen atoms and two moles of hydrogen atoms. Oxygen mass is 32 grams, which is 32/36 \xd7 100 \u2248 88.9%.","Why is oxygen most of the mass in water even though there are twice as many hydrogen atoms?","Each oxygen atom has about 16 times a hydrogen atom mass. Atom count and mass contribution are different comparisons.","A mole is a counting unit, not a fixed mass for every substance. Equal moles of different substances can have different masses.","Carbon structure explains variety; the mole links particle counts to measurable mass.",B.aMf)
B.azi=s(["Magma and eruption","Geothermal energy","Climate controls","Enhanced greenhouse effect"],t.s)
B.aEr=s(["Magma viscosity and gas content influence eruption behavior. More viscous magma can trap gas and favor explosive activity. Volcano shape, past deposits, and monitoring help assess hazards; do not approach active vents.","Geothermal systems use heat from within Earth. In some plants hot water or steam drives a turbine and generator. The energy source is internal heat, not sunlight reflected by a volcano.","Climate is a long-term pattern of weather. Latitude, elevation, distance from large water bodies, currents, and terrain influence it. Higher elevations are often cooler, but local conditions matter.","Greenhouse gases absorb and emit infrared radiation, slowing energy loss to space. Human increases in greenhouse gases change Earth energy balance. This is different from ozone depletion and from one hot afternoon."],t.s)
B.azZ=s(["0|Which model part links trapped gases and high viscosity with explosive behavior?","1|Which model part uses internal Earth heat to help generate electricity?","2|Which model part includes elevation and distance from water?","3|Which model part involves infrared absorption by added greenhouse gases?","0|Which model part distinguishes subsurface magma from erupted lava?","1|Which model part can include steam turning a turbine near a hot reservoir?","2|Which model part requires long-term records rather than one afternoon?","3|Which model part explains a change in energy balance rather than ozone-layer loss?","2|Two sites at similar latitude have different elevations and long-term temperatures. Which model part helps explain the difference?","3|A claim confuses added carbon dioxide warming with a hole in the ozone layer. Which model part supplies the correct warming mechanism?","0|A viscous magma traps much gas before eruption. Which model part connects those properties to the hazard?"],t.s)
B.c7D=new A.bO(3,"Earth and Space","Volcanoes Climate and Energy from Earth","Relate magma properties to eruption behavior|Explain a geothermal energy pathway|Distinguish climate controls from daily weather",B.azi,B.aEr,"Compare two locations at similar latitude, one near sea level and one high in mountains. Elevation helps explain the cooler mountain climate. A single day could differ, so use long records before comparing climate.","Does one unusually cool day disprove long-term global warming?","No. Weather varies from day to day. Climate conclusions rely on patterns over many places and years, together with physical evidence.","Magma is below the surface; material erupted at the surface is called lava. Do not treat all volcanoes as having the same hazard style.","Internal heat drives volcanic and geothermal processes; long-term energy balance and geography shape climate.",B.azZ)
B.aqK=s(["Stellar temperature","Apparent brightness","Constellation pattern","Earth motions and sky view"],t.s)
B.aTM=s(["For ordinary stars, color is related to surface temperature. Blue stars are generally hotter than red stars. This reverses an everyday hot-red cold-blue color convention.","Apparent brightness depends on luminosity and distance, with other effects such as intervening dust. A faint star need not produce little light; it may be far away.","A constellation is a recognized sky pattern or region as viewed from Earth. Its stars may lie at very different distances and need not form a physically close group.","Earth rotation explains the apparent movement of stars during a night. Earth revolution changes which night-sky directions are visible over a year. These motions do not make a constellation predict individual destinies."],t.s)
B.aL4=s(["0|Which model part explains blue stars generally being hotter than red stars?","1|Which model part depends on both luminosity and distance?","2|Which model part can join stars that are far apart in actual space?","3|Which model part explains hourly and seasonal changes in sky view?","0|Which model property can be estimated from a star color?","1|Which model part explains a luminous star looking faint when far away?","2|Which model part describes a recognized pattern rather than necessarily a close physical cluster?","3|Which model part connects yearly visibility with Earth revolution?","1|Two equally luminous stars have different distances. Which observed property is likely to differ?","2|A flat star map makes three stars look neighboring, but their distances differ greatly. Which model limitation applies?","3|A star appears to cross the sky over several hours. Which model part attributes the pattern mainly to Earth rotation?"],t.s)
B.c7R=new A.bO(3,"Earth and Space","Stars Constellations and Apparent Motion","Use color as a temperature clue|Distinguish brightness from distance|Explain daily and yearly sky patterns",B.aqK,B.aTM,"Two stars have the same luminosity but one is farther away. The farther one generally appears fainter. Brightness alone cannot rank their actual energy output without distance information.","Why can a constellation seen during one season be difficult to see at the same evening time months later?","Earth has moved along its orbit, so the night side faces different directions in space. This yearly change differs from the rotation causing hourly movement.","Stars looking close on a flat sky map may be far apart in three-dimensional space. Astrology is not established by recognizing a star pattern.","Stellar properties require evidence, while Earth motions explain changing views of sky patterns.",B.aL4)
B.b31=s(["Projectile components","Momentum","Impulse","Momentum conservation"],t.s)
B.b1c=s(["Ignoring air resistance, a projectile has constant horizontal velocity and downward vertical acceleration near Earth. Horizontal and vertical components share time but follow different motion rules.","Momentum is mass times velocity: p = mv. It has direction. A 2-kilogram cart moving at 3 meters per second east has momentum 6 kilogram meters per second east.","Impulse is net force times time and equals momentum change. For the same stopping change, increasing stopping time reduces average force. Protective padding uses this idea, but no model makes a dangerous collision safe to try.","Total momentum is conserved for an isolated system with negligible external impulse. Two carts sticking together can conserve momentum even though kinetic energy is transformed into other forms."],t.s)
B.aM2=s(["0|Which model part separates horizontal constant velocity from downward acceleration?","1|Which quantity is mass times velocity?","2|Which quantity equals force times time and momentum change?","3|Which model part applies when external impulse on a system is negligible?","0|Which model part explains a projectile still accelerating at its highest point?","1|Which quantity gives 6 kilogram meters per second for 2 kilograms moving at 3 meters per second?","2|Which model part explains reduced average force when stopping time increases?","3|Which model part can still hold when two carts stick and lose kinetic energy?","3|A moving cart joins an equal resting cart and both continue at half the original speed. Which model principle explains the combined motion?","2|Two stops have the same momentum change, but one takes twice as long. Which model part predicts half the average force?","0|A learner says a horizontally launched object has no vertical acceleration. Which model part corrects that?"],t.s)
B.c7x=new A.bO(4,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Projectiles Momentum and Collision Reasoning","Separate horizontal and vertical motion|Calculate simple momentum|Relate impulse and stopping time",B.b31,B.b1c,"A 2-kilogram cart at 3 meters per second meets an equal cart at rest and they stick. Initial momentum is 6. Combined mass is 4 kilograms, so final speed is 6 \xf7 4 = 1.5 meters per second in the original direction.","Why can padding lower average force for the same stopping momentum change?","It lengthens the stopping time. Since impulse is unchanged, force multiplied by time stays equal to the momentum change.","Conserved momentum does not mean conserved kinetic energy in every collision. Define the system and check external forces.","Components explain projectile motion; momentum and impulse explain interactions over time.",B.aM2)
B.aUV=s(["Mechanical energy conservation","Thermal energy transfer","Engine efficiency","Electrical transmission"],t.s)
B.aCA=s(["With negligible nonconservative effects, kinetic plus potential energy remains constant. A falling object loses gravitational potential energy and gains kinetic energy. Friction transfers some mechanical energy to surroundings.","Heat flows due to temperature difference. Some transferred energy can do work, but thermal processes also disperse energy. Cooling an object is not destroying energy.","Efficiency is useful output energy divided by input energy, multiplied by 100%. A heat engine taking 1000 joules and producing 300 joules of useful work has 30% efficiency; the remaining energy is transferred elsewhere.","For a given power, higher transmission voltage permits lower current. Wire heating scales with I\xb2R, so lower current reduces losses. Transformers change alternating voltage. Keep away from power lines and use diagrams only."],t.s)
B.aEj=s(["0|Which model part tracks kinetic plus potential energy in an ideal falling system?","1|Which model part describes energy transfer due to temperature difference?","2|Which model part calculates useful output divided by input?","3|Which model part reduces wire losses by using lower current at higher voltage?","0|Which model part explains a falling object gaining speed while losing height?","1|Which model part explains surroundings warming as an object cools?","2|Which model part gives 30% from 300 joules useful output for 1000 joules input?","3|Which model part uses transformers in alternating-current systems?","2|A machine receives 2000 joules and gives 500 joules useful output. Which model calculation gives 25%?","3|An engineer keeps delivered power the same while lowering current to reduce I\xb2R losses. Which model part explains this?","0|A learner includes friction but assumes mechanical energy must remain unchanged. Which model principle requires its ideal-condition limitation checked?"],t.s)
B.c7c=new A.bO(4,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Mechanical Energy Heat Engines and Power Lines","Track potential and kinetic energy|Calculate a simple efficiency|Explain why power transmission uses high voltage",B.aUV,B.aCA,"A system receives 2000 joules and delivers 500 joules of useful output. Efficiency is 500 \xf7 2000 \xd7 100 = 25%. The other 1500 joules are not missing; they transfer through less useful pathways.","Why use high voltage for long-distance transmission rather than large current at low voltage?","For the same power, lower current reduces resistive heating losses in wires. Voltage is later reduced for distribution and use.","Conservation of energy does not imply 100% useful efficiency. Energy remains, but much can be less available for useful work.","Track all energy pathways and distinguish conservation from useful efficiency.",B.aEj)
B.aXg=s([B.c7n,B.c7V,B.c7y,B.c7E,B.c7D,B.c7R,B.c7x,B.c7c],t.EM)
B.aXr=s(["Wood","Metal","Plastic","Glass","Paper","Fabric","Rubber"],t.s)
