B.aU1=s(["Absorbent cloth","Waterproof cover","Reusable container","Hazardous substance"],t.s)
B.aqP=s(["Absorbent cloth takes liquid into small spaces among fibers. It is useful for wiping water but not for keeping rain out.","A waterproof cover resists water passing through. It can protect a bag from rain but cannot soak up a spill like cloth.","A reusable container can reduce waste if it is clean, suitable, and not damaged. A container that held chemicals must not be used for food.","A hazardous substance can harm people or the environment. Cleaning liquids may be useful when adults follow labels, but mixing them can create dangerous gases."],t.s)
B.aMD=s(["0|Which material is best suited to soaking up a small clean-water spill?","1|Which material is best suited to blocking rain from a schoolbag?","2|Which model item can reduce packaging waste when clean and food-safe?","3|Which model label includes a cleaner that can harm when misused?","0|Which material draws water into spaces among fibers?","1|Which cover resists water passing through rather than absorbing it?","2|Which choice requires checking what was previously stored inside?","3|Which model item must not be mixed with other unknown cleaners?","1|A cloth becomes soaked in rain. Which alternative property is needed for a rain cover?","3|A child thinks two cleaners must work better together. Which model label highlights the danger?","2|A family selects a clean undamaged food tub to use again. Which waste-reducing model choice fits?"],t.s)
B.c8h=new A.bO(1,"Matter","Material Properties Help Us Choose Safely","Connect material properties to uses|Distinguish useful from harmful handling|Choose safer local materials",B.aU1,B.aqP,"Choose a material for wiping a water spill. A waterproof sheet moves water around; absorbent cloth takes it up. Select by the property needed for the job.","Can every empty container be reused for drinking water?","No. The previous contents and material matter. Never reuse chemical containers for food or water.","Useful does not mean safe in every amount or every use. Follow adult guidance and labels.","Choose materials by their properties, purpose, and safe handling.",B.aMD)
B.aFr=s(["Rusting","Burning","Melting","Waste reduction"],t.s)
B.aHg=s(["Iron rusts when oxygen and water react with it. Rust is a new material. Keeping iron dry and protected slows this change.","Burning forms new substances and releases energy. Fuel needs oxygen to keep burning. Smoke can harm lungs, so never burn waste or test flames.","Melting is a physical state change caused by heating a solid enough. Melted ice remains water and can freeze again.","Reduce, reuse, repair, and recycle keep useful materials in use. Repairing a safe broken bag can prevent buying and discarding another one."],t.s)
B.aZU=s(["0|Which change needs water and oxygen acting on iron?","1|Which change forms smoke and ash from fuel?","2|Which change turns ice into water without a new substance?","3|Which model choice includes repairing a safe reusable bag?","0|Which change is slowed by keeping iron tools dry?","1|Which change needs oxygen to keep a fuel flame going?","2|Which change can be reversed by cooling water enough to freeze?","3|Which model choice includes using less packaging?","2|A solid becomes liquid but is still the same substance. Which model change fits?","0|A wet iron nail develops flaky brown material. Which change best explains it?","3|A worn bag can be safely mended rather than thrown out. Which model choice fits?"],t.s)
B.c7U=new A.bO(1,"Matter","Oxygen Heat and Responsible Waste Choices","Explain rusting and burning safely|Distinguish a state change from a chemical change|Choose reduce reuse or repair",B.aFr,B.aHg,"A metal roof rusts after water and air reach it. A plastic ice tray contains melted ice. The roof has formed a new material; the water has only changed state.","Why does drying a wet iron tool help protect it?","It removes one condition needed for rusting: water. Oxygen may still be present, but reducing water exposure slows the reaction.","Not every change caused by heat is chemical. Ice melting is physical; fuel burning forms new substances.","Use evidence of new substances to compare changes, and prevent unnecessary waste.",B.aZU)
B.aUp=s(["Testes","Ovaries","Uterus","Puberty and care"],t.s)
B.aVK=s(["Testes are reproductive organs that produce sperm after puberty begins. They also produce hormones involved in development.","Ovaries contain egg cells and produce hormones. As the body matures, an egg is usually released during a menstrual cycle.","The uterus is an organ where a pregnancy can develop. When pregnancy does not occur, its lining may leave the body during menstruation.","Puberty is a period of body change with different timing for different people. Washing the outer body, using clean menstrual products when needed, and seeking a trusted adult or health worker help maintain health."],t.s)
B.ara=s(["0|Which organs produce sperm after puberty begins?","1|Which organs contain egg cells?","2|Which organ has a lining involved in menstruation?","3|Which model topic explains body changes beginning at different ages?","0|Which model organs produce hormones and sperm?","1|From which organs is an egg usually released during a cycle?","2|In which organ can a pregnancy develop?","3|Which topic includes using clean menstrual products and asking for help?","2|A learner asks where the lining shed during menstruation comes from. Which organ answers that question?","3|Two classmates develop at different times. Which model topic explains why teasing is not scientifically justified?","1|A cycle diagram shows an egg being released before possible fertilization. Which organs should be labeled at the release site?"],t.s)
B.c7O=new A.bO(2,"Living Things","Puberty and Human Reproductive Systems","Identify reproductive organ functions|Explain puberty as normal development|Describe respectful hygiene and help seeking",B.aUp,B.aVK,"A learner notices body hair and a changing voice. These may be normal puberty changes caused by hormones. People develop at different times, so comparing or teasing is unhelpful.","Does menstruation mean a person is dirty or ill?","No. It is a normal body process involving the uterine lining. Clean products and ordinary hygiene support comfort and health.","Not everyone starts puberty at the same age. Ask a trusted adult or health worker about pain or worries; do not share private body information publicly.","Reproductive organs have distinct jobs, and puberty needs accurate knowledge, privacy, and respectful care.",B.ara)
B.aVt=s(["Flowering-plant seeds","Spores and vegetative growth","Animal reproduction","Coastal interactions"],t.s)
B.aVC=s(["In flowering plants, pollen from an anther can reach a stigma. After fertilization, ovules can develop into seeds. Fruits develop from flower parts, usually the ovary.","Ferns reproduce using spores, not flowers. Some plants can also grow from parts such as stems; a new plant need not always begin as a seed.","Many animals reproduce when sperm fertilizes an egg. Butterflies and frogs lay eggs; cats give birth to live young. Young animals develop through species-specific stages.","Estuaries mix river water and seawater. Intertidal zones are covered and uncovered by tides. Organisms interact with water, mud, food, and shelter; protecting habitat supports survival."],t.s)
B.b0R=s(["0|Which model part includes pollen moving from anther to stigma?","1|Which model part includes fern reproduction without flowers?","2|Which model part compares egg-laying frogs with cats giving birth?","3|Which model part includes freshwater mixing with seawater?","0|Which model part explains ovules developing into seeds?","1|Which model part includes a new plant growing from a stem cutting?","2|Which model part includes a butterfly developing from an egg?","3|Which model part explains why tidal water levels affect shore organisms?","1|A learner claims every new plant starts from a seed. Which model part gives a counterexample?","0|A diagram confuses pollen transfer with a seed already formed. Which model process needs stages added?","3|A shoreline loses shelter and food after damage. Which model part connects those losses to survival?"],t.s)
B.c7l=new A.bO(2,"Living Things","Reproduction and Life in Coastal Habitats","Compare plant and animal reproduction|Identify flower functions|Explain one coastal habitat interaction",B.aVt,B.aVC,"A butterfly lays eggs, then larvae feed on leaves. A cat gives birth to live young. Both produce offspring, but their young start life in different ways and have different stages.","A fern has no flowers. Does that mean it cannot reproduce?","No. Ferns produce spores. Flowers and seeds are not the only way plants produce new individuals.","Pollination is pollen transfer, not fertilization itself. Fertilization occurs later when reproductive cells join.","Living things reproduce in different ways and need suitable habitats for their young to survive.",B.b0R)
B.aFI=s(["Speed","Light transmission","Light absorption","Heat conduction"],t.s)
B.aSb=s(["Average speed is total distance divided by time. Traveling 12 meters in 4 seconds gives 3 meters per second. This average does not prove the speed never changed.","Transmission means light passes through a material. Clear glass transmits much visible light; a solid wall does not.","Absorption means a material takes in light energy. Under the same conditions, dark surfaces often absorb more sunlight and warm more than light surfaces.","Conduction transfers heat through contact, especially well in many metals. A metal spoon can warm from a warm drink. Use supplied examples, not hot-object experiments."],t.s)
B.aV7=s(["0|Which quantity is distance divided by time?","1|Which process lets much light pass through clear glass?","2|Which process helps a dark surface warm in sunlight?","3|Which process transfers heat through a metal spoon by contact?","0|Which quantity equals 3 meters per second for 12 meters in 4 seconds?","1|Which property is useful for a clear window?","2|Which process takes in rather than passes on light energy?","3|Which process makes a metal pan handle warm by contact with the pan?","0|A toy travels 18 meters in 6 seconds. Which model quantity has the value 3 meters per second?","1|A classroom needs daylight through a material. Which light property is needed?","2|Two similar covers receive equal sunlight; the darker one warms more. Which process is a likely explanation?"],t.s)
B.c7Z=new A.bO(3,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Tracking Motion and Energy Effects","Calculate simple average speed|Compare materials with light|Explain heat conduction",B.aFI,B.aSb,"A toy covers 15 meters in 5 seconds. Divide 15 by 5 to obtain 3 meters per second. Another toy covering 10 meters in 5 seconds averages 2 meters per second.","Two similar surfaces receive the same sunlight. Why might the darker one become warmer?","It often absorbs more incoming light energy. Keep material, size, exposure time, and conditions comparable before making that conclusion.","A material that transmits light is not necessarily a good heat insulator. These are different properties.","Distance and time quantify motion; transmission, absorption, and conduction explain energy effects.",B.aV7)
B.b13=s(["Complete circuit","Open circuit","Electrical insulator","Electromagnet"],t.s)
B.aGy=s(["A complete circuit provides a continuous conducting path from one cell terminal through components to the other. A suitable bulb can light when current flows.","An open circuit has a break. Opening a switch stops current in that path. Current does not jump safely through ordinary gaps.","An electrical insulator resists current flow. Plastic covering on wires helps prevent contact, but damaged wires are still dangerous. Never test wall electricity.","An electromagnet uses current in a coil to produce magnetism, often strengthened by an iron core. Switching current off greatly reduces the temporary magnetic effect."],t.s)
B.aMj=s(["0|Which circuit state allows current around an unbroken loop?","1|Which circuit state has a break at an open switch?","2|Which model item describes protective plastic around a wire?","3|Which device uses current in a coil for magnetism?","0|A suitable cell and bulb are connected in an unbroken conducting path. Which label fits?","1|A wire is disconnected and the bulb goes out. Which label fits?","2|Which property helps prevent current passing through a dry plastic cover?","3|Which device can have its magnetic strength controlled by current?","1|A bulb does not light because the switch leaves a gap. Which state explains this?","3|A crane needs to attract iron, then release it by switching off. Which device fits?","0|A learner traces a path from one terminal through a bulb back to the other terminal with no gap. Which circuit state is shown?"],t.s)
B.c78=new A.bO(3,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Circuits and Controllable Magnets","Trace a complete circuit|Distinguish conductor and insulator|Explain how an electromagnet can be switched",B.b13,B.aGy,"A diagram shows a cell, switch, and bulb in one loop. Closing the switch completes the path, allowing the bulb to light. Opening it breaks the path.","Why is an electromagnet useful for a device that must pick up iron and then release it?","Its magnetism is controlled by current. Turning the current off greatly reduces the attraction, allowing release.","Do not connect a cell directly across a wire with no suitable load. It can overheat. Work from diagrams, not homemade electrical tests.","A continuous path allows current; a controlled current can create a controllable magnet.",B.aMj)
B.aOv=s(["Weathering","Erosion","Plant protection","Storm readiness"],t.s)
B.avb=s(["Weathering breaks rocks down where they are. Water entering cracks and roots growing in cracks can help break rock over long times.","Erosion transports loosened material by water, wind, or gravity. Soil washed into a stream has moved, not merely broken apart.","Plant cover and roots can reduce soil loss. Bare slopes often lose more soil when heavy rain strikes.","Storm readiness uses current official weather warnings and adult plans. Prepare safe water, necessary medicines with an adult, a light, and a way to receive updates. Never cross floodwater."],t.s)
B.aVZ=s(["0|Which process breaks rock into smaller pieces in place?","1|Which process carries loose soil downhill?","2|Which model part helps hold soil with roots?","3|Which model part uses official warnings and an emergency plan?","0|Roots widen a crack in rock. Which process is occurring?","1|Wind carries dry loose particles away. Which process is occurring?","2|Keeping grass on a suitable slope helps through which model part?","3|Which model part includes refusing to cross a flooded road?","1|Rock fragments are already loose and rain now moves them to a stream. Which process describes the movement?","0|A rock splits but its pieces remain beside it. Which process is shown without transport?","3|A storm approaches and the family checks local evacuation advice. Which model choice reduces immediate risk?"],t.s)
B.c8k=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space","Weathering Erosion and Storm Readiness","Distinguish rock breakdown from transport|Explain erosion prevention|Use official storm information safely",B.aOv,B.avb,"A rock cracks, then rain carries pieces downhill. Cracking is weathering. Transport is erosion. They are connected stages, not two names for the same action.","Why should a family act on a current storm warning instead of going outside to inspect rising water?","Warnings support earlier safe action. Floodwater may be deep, fast, contaminated, or hide electrical hazards. Follow local instructions and adults.","Do not use old storm-signal descriptions as a current forecast. Follow current PAGASA and local authority information.","Rocks break down, loose material moves, and informed preparation reduces storm risk.",B.aVZ)
B.aWD=s(["New Moon","First quarter","Full Moon","Last quarter"],t.s)
B.aWa=s(["At new Moon, the Moon is roughly toward the Sun from Earth. Its sunlit half faces mostly away from us. The Moon still exists even when we cannot see it easily.","At first quarter, about half the visible disk is sunlit and the Moon is waxing. It has completed about a quarter of its phase cycle after new Moon.","At full Moon, the visible side is nearly fully sunlit. Earth is roughly between the directions of Sun and Moon, but their alignment usually does not cause an eclipse.","At last quarter, half the visible disk is sunlit while the Moon wanes toward new Moon. The full phase cycle lasts about 29.5 days."],t.s)
B.axZ=s(["0|Which main phase has the visible side mostly unlit?","1|Which main phase shows a half-lit disk while waxing?","2|Which main phase shows a nearly fully lit disk?","3|Which main phase shows a half-lit disk while waning?","0|Which phase begins the usual new-to-new sequence?","1|Which main phase comes after new Moon and before full Moon?","2|Which phase comes between the waxing and waning halves of the cycle?","3|Which main phase comes after full Moon and before the next new Moon?","1|A half-lit Moon is followed days later by a fuller lit shape. Which quarter phase was observed first?","3|A half-lit Moon is followed days later by a thinner crescent. Which quarter phase was observed first?","2|A diary records the greatest visible lit area before it begins shrinking. Which main phase fits?"],t.s)
B.c7z=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space","Moon Phases Repeat in a Pattern","Explain reflected moonlight|Order main lunar phases|Distinguish phases from eclipses",B.aWD,B.aWa,"A diary shows new Moon, then first quarter, then full Moon. The visible lit part grows, so it is waxing. Later it shrinks through last quarter toward new Moon.","Does Earth cast the ordinary dark part of a crescent Moon?","No. Phases result from seeing different portions of the Moon sunlit by the Sun as the Moon orbits Earth. Earth shadow causes a lunar eclipse, a different event.","Quarter refers to progress around the phase cycle, not one quarter of the visible disk being bright.","Moon phases are changing views of a sunlit sphere in an approximately monthly cycle.",B.axZ)
