B.b0q=s(["audio/music/synthwave_4k.mp3","audio/music/vaporware.mp3","audio/music/lifewave_2k.mp3","audio/music/synthwave_15k.mp3","audio/music/synthwave_421k.mp3"],t.s)
B.b0v=s([0.001,0.001,0.001,0.001,1,0.001,0.001,0.001,0,0,0.001,0.001,0.001,0.001,0,0,1,0,0,0,0.1],t.n)
B.b2s=s(["Inertia","Net force and acceleration","Interaction pair","Circular acceleration"],t.s)
B.b_A=s(["Inertia is resistance to a change in velocity. With zero net force an object remains at rest or moves at constant velocity. Greater mass means greater inertia.","For constant mass, net force equals mass times acceleration: F = ma. A 6-newton net force on 2 kilograms gives 3 meters per second squared. Use the net force, not just one force.","Interaction forces are equal and opposite but act on different objects. A hand pushes a wall and the wall pushes the hand. They do not cancel on a single-object force diagram.","Circular motion changes velocity direction even at constant speed. An inward net force is needed for centripetal acceleration. Without it, an object initially continues along a tangent."],t.s)
B.aQx=s(["0|Which model idea explains resistance to a change in velocity?","1|Which model part uses F = ma?","2|Which model part involves equal opposite forces on different objects?","3|Which model part explains changing velocity at constant speed around a circle?","0|Which property increases when mass increases?","1|Which model part gives 3 meters per second squared from 6 newtons on 2 kilograms?","2|Which model part describes a wall pushing back on the hand pushing it?","3|Which model part requires an inward net force?","1|A cart has opposing forces of 12 and 3 newtons and mass 3 kilograms. Which model part gives its acceleration?","2|A learner cancels a foot-on-ground force with ground-on-foot force on one diagram. Which model distinction corrects that?","3|An object leaves a curved path and initially moves tangentially. Which model part explains the previous need for inward force?"],t.s)
B.c80=new A.bO(1,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Newton Laws Predict Changes in Motion","Relate net force mass and acceleration|Identify inertia|Distinguish an interaction pair from balanced forces",B.b2s,B.b_A,"A 3-kilogram cart has 12 newtons rightward and 3 newtons leftward. Net force is 9 newtons rightward. Divide by 3 kilograms: acceleration is 3 meters per second squared rightward.","A ball moves in a circle at constant speed. Is its acceleration zero?","No. Its velocity direction changes. An inward acceleration is required even though speed stays constant.","An action-reaction pair is not two balanced forces on the same object. Always name the object each force acts on.","Use a defined object, net force, and velocity direction to apply Newton laws.",B.aQx)
B.aYq=s(["Work","Power","Mechanical energy","Current voltage and resistance"],t.s)
B.aE7=s(["Work by a constant force along a displacement is force times distance: W = Fd. A 5-newton force moving a load 2 meters in its direction does 10 joules of work. Holding still has zero mechanical work on the unmoving load.","Power is work done per time: P = W/t. Doing 20 joules in 4 seconds means 5 watts. The same work in less time means greater average power.","Kinetic energy is associated with motion and equals half of mass times speed squared. Gravitational potential energy near Earth is mgh. Doubling speed quadruples kinetic energy for the same mass.","For an ohmic component at stable temperature, V = IR. A 6-volt supply across 3 ohms gives 2 amperes. Current is charge flow; voltage is energy per unit charge. Use circuit diagrams, not mains experiments."],t.s)
B.auy=s(["0|Which quantity is a force times displacement in the force direction?","1|Which quantity is work divided by time?","2|Which model part includes mgh and half of mass times speed squared?","3|Which model part uses V = IR for an ohmic component?","0|Which quantity equals 10 joules for 5 newtons acting along 2 meters?","1|Which quantity is measured in watts?","2|Which model part explains quadrupled kinetic energy when speed doubles?","3|Which model part gives 2 amperes from 6 volts across 3 ohms?","1|Two machines do 30 joules of work but one finishes sooner. Which quantity distinguishes their work rates?","0|A person holds a load still. Which model quantity is zero on the stationary load despite effort?","2|A raised object falls and gains speed. Which model part connects decreasing height with increasing motion energy?"],t.s)
B.c7j=new A.bO(1,"Force, Motion, and Energy","Work Power Energy and Circuit Quantities","Calculate simple work and power|Compare kinetic and potential energy|Apply a simple voltage-current relationship",B.aYq,B.aE7,"A force of 10 newtons moves a load 3 meters in its direction. Work is 30 joules. If this takes 5 seconds, power is 6 watts. Joules and watts describe different quantities.","Two lifts do the same work but one takes half the time. Which has greater power?","The faster lift has twice the power because power is work divided by time.","Force alone is not work, and voltage alone is not current. Include the relevant distance, time, or resistance.","Work transfers energy, power measures its rate, and circuit quantities have distinct roles.",B.auy)
B.aQc=s(["Focus and epicenter","Magnitude and intensity","Fault movement","Tsunami preparedness"],t.s)
B.aZo=s(["The focus is where rupture begins within Earth. The epicenter is directly above the focus on the surface; the two are not the same place.","Magnitude estimates earthquake size from measurements. Intensity describes shaking effects at a location. One event can produce different intensities because distance, ground, and structures differ.","Sudden fault slip releases stored elastic energy. Active faults have evidence of recent movement in geological terms. A hazard map supports planning but does not predict an exact date.","A tsunami can be generated when a large volume of water is displaced, including by some undersea earthquakes. Strong or long coastal shaking, sudden unusual sea change, and official warnings require following the local route to safe high ground."],t.s)
B.aX4=s(["0|Which model part distinguishes an underground source from the surface point above it?","1|Which model part distinguishes event size from local shaking effects?","2|Which model part explains release of stored energy along a fracture?","3|Which model part includes moving along a prescribed coastal evacuation route?","0|Which model part identifies the source below the epicenter?","1|Which model part explains different reported effects in two towns?","2|Which model part uses active-fault evidence without predicting an exact date?","3|Which model part treats a sudden unusual sea change as a possible natural warning?","1|A learner thinks one earthquake must have identical shaking everywhere. Which model distinction corrects this?","3|Strong long shaking occurs near the coast. Which model part supports prompt local safety action?","2|A map shows a nearby active fault. Which model part explains why it informs planning rather than an exact forecast?"],t.s)
B.c7d=new A.bO(2,"Earth and Space","Faults Tsunamis and Local Hazard Decisions","Locate focus and epicenter|Distinguish magnitude and intensity|Use fault and tsunami information responsibly",B.aQc,B.aZo,"A map marks the epicenter at the coast but the focus is beneath the ground. Town A and Town B may report different intensities although they experienced the same event magnitude.","Should people wait to watch a wave after a strong coastal earthquake?","No. Follow local tsunami procedures and natural warning guidance promptly. A receding sea is not a sightseeing opportunity.","Not every undersea earthquake creates a tsunami, but people should not delay safety action while trying to calculate it themselves.","Separate earthquake source measurements from local effects and act on reliable hazard guidance.",B.aX4)
B.aM_=s(["Tropical cyclone","Comet","Asteroid","Meteor"],t.s)
B.aO0=s(["A tropical cyclone draws energy from warm ocean water and moist rising air. Rotation and suitable atmospheric conditions support development. Land and cooler water can weaken it, but flooding risk may continue; use current PAGASA warnings.","A comet is an icy small body orbiting the Sun. Near the Sun, released gas and dust can form tails that generally point away from the Sun, not simply behind its travel.","An asteroid is a mainly rocky or metallic small body orbiting the Sun. Many orbit between Mars and Jupiter. An asteroid is not a star.","A meteor is the visible streak when a meteoroid enters the atmosphere. A fragment reaching the ground is called a meteorite. The streak is not a star falling from the sky."],t.s)
B.aJl=s(["0|Which model item gains energy from warm ocean water and moist air?","1|Which model body can release gas and dust from ices near the Sun?","2|Which model body is mainly rocky or metallic and orbits the Sun?","3|Which model item is a visible streak from atmospheric entry?","0|Which model item needs current weather-track warnings for community decisions?","1|Which model item may have a tail pointing generally away from the Sun?","2|Which model group has many members between Mars and Jupiter?","3|Which model item is wrongly called a falling star?","0|A storm moves inland and winds weaken, but heavy rain remains. Which model explains why warnings still matter?","3|A short bright streak ends before reaching the ground. Which visible event was observed?","1|An orbiting icy body develops a tail as it nears the Sun. Which body is it?"],t.s)
B.c7A=new A.bO(2,"Earth and Space","Typhoons and Small Solar System Bodies","Explain conditions supporting tropical cyclones|Use storm-track information safely|Distinguish comets meteors and asteroids",B.aM_,B.aO0,"A forecast track shows a cyclone moving inland. It may lose its warm-water energy source, yet heavy rain can still cause flooding. A weaker wind label is not a promise that all hazards ended.","A bright brief streak appears at night. Is it a distant star falling?","No. The streak is a meteor produced by a small body entering the atmosphere, much nearer than a star.","A comet tail is shaped by solar effects, so it need not point backward along the comet orbit.","Cyclones are atmospheric systems; comets, asteroids, and meteors have different origins and behavior.",B.aJl)
B.aAm=s(["Element","Compound","Mixture","Molecule"],t.s)
B.av5=s(["An element contains atoms with one atomic number. Oxygen gas contains oxygen atoms only, even though its common molecules contain two atoms each.","A compound contains different elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio. Water contains hydrogen and oxygen in a two-to-one atom ratio. Separating it into elements requires chemical change.","A mixture contains substances physically combined with variable composition. Air contains several gases; its parts are not all bonded into one air molecule.","A molecule is a group of atoms chemically bonded together. O2 is a molecule of an element; H2O is a molecule of a compound. Some compounds, such as ionic salts, are extended structures rather than separate molecules."],t.s)
B.aIw=s(["0|Which substance class contains just one atomic-number type?","1|Which substance class has different elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio?","2|Which substance class includes air with variable proportions of gases?","3|Which particle term includes both O2 and H2O?","0|Which class fits pure oxygen even when its particles are O2?","1|Which class fits water as hydrogen and oxygen bonded in a fixed ratio?","2|Which class can often be separated using differences in physical properties?","3|Which term names a discrete bonded group of atoms rather than a whole substance class?","0|A pure sample contains only pairs of identical oxygen atoms. Which substance class fits?","2|Two gases share a container without becoming one chemically bonded substance. Which class fits the sample?","1|A substance always contains two hydrogen atoms per oxygen atom and requires chemical change to separate its elements. Which class fits?"],t.s)
B.c7k=new A.bO(3,"Matter","Atoms Molecules Elements and Compounds","Distinguish elements compounds and mixtures|Use particle diagrams|Explain why a molecule need not be a compound",B.aAm,B.av5,"A diagram with only identical O2 pairs represents a pure element and also contains molecules. A diagram of H2O particles represents a compound. Different atom types joined in a fixed ratio are the key compound clue.","Does having two atoms automatically make a particle a compound?","No. Two identical oxygen atoms form O2, a molecule of an element. A compound requires different elements.","A mixture is not defined by looking dirty or cloudy. Clear air and clear salt water are mixtures.","Atom type, bonding, and composition distinguish elements, compounds, mixtures, and molecules.",B.aIw)
B.aNA=s(["Protons and atomic number","Neutrons and isotopes","Electrons and ions","Groups and periods"],t.s)
B.aH_=s(["Protons have positive charge in the nucleus. Atomic number equals proton number and determines element identity. An atom with six protons is carbon regardless of its neutron count.","Neutrons are uncharged nuclear particles. Mass number is protons plus neutrons. Isotopes of an element differ in neutron number, not proton number.","Electrons have negative charge outside the nucleus. A neutral atom has equal electron and proton counts. Losing electrons produces a positive ion; gaining them produces a negative ion.","The periodic table orders elements by atomic number. Vertical groups often share valence patterns and related chemical behavior; horizontal rows are periods. Similarity is a pattern with exceptions, not identical behavior."],t.s)
B.apE=s(["0|Which model part determines element identity?","1|Which model part explains different masses of the same element?","2|Which model part explains charge after an atom loses an electron?","3|Which model part organizes vertical families and horizontal rows?","0|Which model part gives atomic number from a nuclear count?","1|Which model part contributes with protons to mass number?","2|Which model part explains why equal proton and electron counts give neutrality?","3|Which model part predicts related behavior from table position?","2|An atom keeps 11 protons but changes from 11 to 10 electrons. Which model part explains its new +1 charge?","1|Two atoms both have six protons but one has an extra neutron. Which model concept distinguishes them?","0|An unknown atom has eight protons. Which model count, rather than total particle count, identifies the element?"],t.s)
B.c7e=new A.bO(3,"Matter","Atomic Structure Organizes the Periodic Table","Identify proton neutron and electron roles|Read atomic and mass numbers|Use periodic-table organization",B.aNA,B.aH_,"An atom has 11 protons, 12 neutrons, and 11 electrons. Atomic number is 11, mass number is 23, and charge is zero. Losing one electron leaves 10 electrons and gives a +1 ion without changing the element.","If two carbon atoms have different masses, must they be different elements?","No. Both have six protons. Different neutron numbers make isotopes of carbon.","Do not use neutron or electron count to define element identity. Proton number is the defining count.","Protons define identity, neutrons distinguish isotopes, electrons determine ion charge, and table position reveals patterns.",B.apE)
B.aUr=s(["Digestion and absorption","Mitosis","Meiosis","Mendelian inheritance"],t.s)
B.aR7=s(["Digestion breaks food mechanically and chemically. Enzymes help break molecules down; the small intestine absorbs many nutrients. Absorption is entry into the body transport system, not just chewing.","Mitosis distributes copied chromosomes into nuclei, usually preserving chromosome number. It supports growth and repair. Cell division follows DNA copying; cells do not simply halve an unprepared set.","Meiosis reduces chromosome number and produces genetically varied reproductive cells. Fertilization restores the usual paired chromosome number in the offspring.","In a simple dominant-recessive model, an individual receives one allele from each parent. Crossing Aa with Aa gives AA, Aa, Aa, aa: a one-in-four chance of aa for each offspring. Real traits can follow more complex patterns."],t.s)
B.aFf=s(["0|Which model part includes nutrient uptake through the small intestine?","1|Which division mainly supports growth while retaining chromosome number?","2|Which division reduces chromosome number for reproduction?","3|Which model part uses an Aa \xd7 Aa probability grid?","0|Which model part distinguishes chewing from absorption?","1|Which model part includes distributing copied chromosomes during body-cell replacement?","2|Which model part helps produce varied reproductive cells?","3|Which model part describes one allele received from each parent?","3|Four grid outcomes include one aa, but a small family need not match that exact ratio. Which model explains probability rather than a schedule?","2|Fertilization would double chromosome number each generation without a preceding reduction. Which division provides that reduction?","0|Food is broken down but nutrients still need to enter blood. Which model part includes this next step?"],t.s)
B.c7g=new A.bO(4,"Living Things","Digestion Cell Division and Inherited Traits","Trace digestion and absorption|Compare mitosis and meiosis|Use a simple inheritance model",B.aUr,B.aR7,"For Aa \xd7 Aa, write A and a from one parent across a grid and A and a from the other down it. Combine entries: AA, Aa, Aa, aa. Three have the dominant phenotype in this simplified model.","Does a one-in-four probability require exactly one recessive offspring in every four?","No. Each offspring is a new chance. A probability predicts long-run patterns, not a fixed sequence.","Dominant does not mean common, stronger, better, or guaranteed to appear in every child.","Digestion supplies cells; cell division and allele inheritance explain growth and reproduction.",B.aFf)
B.aQC=s(["Classification","Food-web energy flow","Material cycles","Biodiversity protection"],t.s)
B.aG3=s(["Classification groups organisms by shared features and evolutionary relationships. Species fit within genera and wider groups. Similar habitat alone does not establish close relationship.","Energy enters most food webs through producers capturing sunlight. Consumers receive only part of the previous level energy; much is used and dispersed as heat. Energy flows rather than cycling back unchanged.","Water, carbon, oxygen, and nitrogen move through living and nonliving parts of ecosystems. Decomposers return nutrients to the environment. Nitrogen-fixing microbes help convert atmospheric nitrogen into forms usable in food webs.","Biodiversity includes variety of organisms and supports ecosystem functions. Habitat loss, pollution, and unsustainable harvest can remove species and disrupt interactions. Protection must preserve habitat and relationships, not only individual animals."],t.s)
B.aso=s(["0|Which model part places related species into genera?","1|Which model part explains decreasing available energy at successive trophic levels?","2|Which model part includes decomposers returning nutrients?","3|Which model part addresses habitat loss threatening species?","0|Which model part warns that shared habitat does not prove close relationship?","1|Which model part begins with producers capturing energy?","2|Which model part includes nitrogen-fixing microbes?","3|Which model part includes sustainable use and pollution prevention?","2|Carbon moves from air to plants and later back to air. Which model part describes this return pathway?","1|A learner draws energy returning unchanged from predators to sunlight. Which model part corrects the cycle claim?","3|Saving one species while destroying its feeding and breeding habitat is insufficient. Which model idea explains why?"],t.s)
B.c7P=new A.bO(4,"Living Things","Biodiversity Energy Flow and Material Cycles","Use classification levels|Compare energy flow with matter cycling|Explain effects of habitat change",B.aQC,B.aG3,"In a food web, a plant takes carbon dioxide from air and a caterpillar eats plant material. Carbon can later return through respiration and decomposition. Energy is also transferred, but much disperses as heat rather than being recycled like carbon.","Why are decomposers important even though they are not large visible predators?","They break down dead material and return nutrients, supporting material cycles and future plant growth.","An energy pyramid is not a closed energy cycle. Matter cycles; usable energy requires continuing input.","Classification describes diversity; webs and cycles explain interactions that conservation must protect.",B.aso)
B.b0K=s([B.c80,B.c7j,B.c7d,B.c7A,B.c7k,B.c7e,B.c7g,B.c7P],t.EM)
B.aec=new A.n1(0,"totp")
B.aed=new A.n1(1,"phone")
B.aee=new A.n1(2,"webauthn")
B.b0Z=s([B.aec,B.aed,B.aee,B.DL],A.aC("J<n1>"))
B.b19=s(["pointerdown","pointermove","pointerleave","pointerup","pointercancel","touchstart","touchend","touchmove","touchcancel","mousedown","mousemove","mouseleave","mouseup","wheel"],t.s)
B.aJG=s(["Solid","Liquid","Gas","Container"],t.s)
B.arA=s(["A solid has its own shape and takes up space. A stone stays the same shape when moved between cups.","A liquid has a fixed amount but takes the shape of the part of its container it fills. Water can be poured.","A gas spreads to fill available space. Air has mass and takes space even when we cannot see it.","A container holds matter. Moving water to a wider container changes its shape, not the amount if none spills."],t.s)
B.aBq=s(["0|Which state fits a stone that keeps its shape?","1|Which state fits water poured into a cup?","2|Which state fits air inside a balloon?","3|Which item holds water but is not the water itself?","0|Which state has its own shape like a wooden block?","1|Which state takes the shape of the lower part of a bowl?","2|Which state spreads through all available room in a closed box?","3|What changes from cup to bowl when the same water is transferred?","2|A balloon grows when invisible matter enters. Which state explains the space it occupies?","1|Matter changes shape when poured but keeps its amount. Which state fits?","0|A small object moves from a wide cup to a narrow cup without changing shape. Which state fits?"],t.s)
B.c7K=new A.bO(1,"Matter","Solids Liquids and the Air We Cannot See","Classify common matter|Compare shape and space|Explain why air is matter",B.aJG,B.arA,"Pour a pictured cup of water into a pictured wide bowl without spilling. The water spreads wider. Its amount stays the same because nothing was added or removed.","A toy balloon grows bigger when air enters. What does that show about air?","Air takes up space. It is a gas, not empty nothing.","Invisible does not mean absent. Air is matter even though clean air is usually not visible.","Solids keep shape, liquids flow, and gases fill available space.",B.aBq)
B.aLn=s(["Melting","Freezing","Evaporation","Condensation"],t.s)
B.aXj=s(["Melting changes solid ice into liquid water when it gains enough heat. The water has not become a new substance.","Freezing changes liquid water into solid ice when it loses enough heat. A freezer removes heat from water.","Evaporation changes liquid water at a surface into water vapor. It can happen without boiling, as wet clothes dry.","Condensation changes water vapor into liquid drops when the vapor cools. Drops outside a cold cup come from the surrounding air."],t.s)
B.aXL=s(["0|Which change turns an ice cube into liquid?","1|Which change turns water into ice?","2|Which change helps wet clothes dry?","3|Which change forms drops outside a cold cup?","0|A piece of ice becomes smaller in a room and leaves water. Which change happened?","1|Liquid water loses enough heat in a freezer. Which change follows?","2|A puddle gets smaller without water flowing away. Which change explains this?","3|Water vapor cools into tiny drops on a cool surface. Which change is that?","3|A sealed cold bottle has drops outside. Which process, rather than a leak, can explain them?","2|A child says a drying towel loses water forever. Which process explains where the water goes?","0|An ice pop softens into liquid while kept away from the freezer. Which change should be recorded?"],t.s)
B.c7o=new A.bO(1,"Matter","Water Changes State but Stays Water","Describe changes caused by warming or cooling|Connect changes to everyday water|Distinguish a state change from disappearance",B.aLn,B.aXj,"A cold cup stands on a table. Water vapor in nearby air cools at the cup and forms drops outside. The drops are not proof that the cup leaked.","A wet path dries on a warm day. Did the water stop existing?","No. Water changed to vapor and entered the air by evaporation.","The white cloud above warm water is tiny liquid drops; water vapor itself is invisible. Do not investigate using hot water.","Water gains or loses heat and changes state. It does not vanish.",B.aXL)
B.aAv=s(["Eyes","Ears","Nose","Skin"],t.s)
B.aLo=s(["Eyes detect light and help us see color and shape. Good light and keeping objects away from eyes help protect them.","Ears detect sound. Very loud sound can damage hearing, so lower the sound or move away.","The nose detects smells. Smell is a clue, not a safe test for an unknown substance.","Skin detects touch and helps protect the body. Do not use touch to test hot objects; ask an adult."],t.s)
B.axE=s(["0|Which organs detect the shape of a road sign?","1|Which organs detect a warning bell?","2|Which organ detects a familiar food smell?","3|Which organ detects the feel of a clean smooth cloth?","0|Which organs need protection from very bright light?","1|Which organs need protection from very loud sound?","2|Which organ gives a smell clue that still cannot prove food safety?","3|Which organ covers and helps protect most of the outside of the body?","1|A child moves away from a painfully loud speaker. Which organs does that action mainly protect?","0|A class compares leaf colors without touching plants. Which organs supply the needed clue?","3|A child feels a soft clean towel but refuses to touch a hot pan. Which organ is being used safely?"],t.s)
B.c8a=new A.bO(2,"Living Things","Body Parts Help Us Sense and Respond","Match organs to sensory clues|Explain why senses need care|Use more than one clue safely",B.aAv,B.aLo,"A bicycle approaches. You see it with your eyes and hear its bell with your ears. Two clues help you notice it, but you still follow safe crossing rules.","Can a pleasant smell prove that an unknown liquid is safe?","No. The nose detects smell, not whether a substance is safe. Do not taste or sniff unknown substances.","Do not place anything inside an ear to clean it. Ask a trusted adult about ear care.","Sense organs provide clues; safe habits protect them.",B.axE)
B.aq9=s(["Roots take in water","Leaves make food","Animals obtain food","Habitats meet needs"],t.s)
B.apk=s(["Roots anchor a plant and absorb water from soil. Roots do not eat pieces of soil as food.","Green leaves use light, water, and air to make food. A plant needs suitable light as well as water.","Animals get food by eating plants, other animals, or both. Different animals need different foods.","A habitat supplies needs such as water, air, food, and space. Removing all plants can remove food and shelter for animals."],t.s)
B.aNi=s(["0|Which model statement explains water entering a plant from soil?","1|Which model statement explains why a green plant needs light?","2|Which model statement fits a goat eating grass?","3|Which model statement fits a pond supplying a fish with a place to live?","0|Which process is harmed if a seedling loses most of its roots?","1|Which process is harmed when a green plant stays in darkness?","2|Which model statement fits a bird catching an insect?","3|Which model statement explains why clearing a garden can affect insects and birds?","1|A child adds water daily but keeps a plant in a dark cupboard. Which model process is still limited?","3|A pond is drained. Fish lose more than a sleeping place. Which model idea explains the effect?","2|A caterpillar cannot make its own food from sunlight. Which model statement explains its need for leaves?"],t.s)
B.c8m=new A.bO(2,"Living Things","Living Things Need Their Habitats","Match structures to survival|Explain basic plant needs|Describe care for a habitat",B.aq9,B.apk,"In a garden, leaves use light to make food. A caterpillar eats leaves. A bird may eat the caterpillar. Removing all leaves affects more than just the plant.","A watered plant is left in darkness for many days. Which need for food making is missing?","Light is missing. Leaves cannot make food normally without it; water alone is not enough.","A habitat is not just a sleeping place. It must help meet several life needs.","Plant parts and animal feeding connect living things to their habitats.",B.aNi)
B.aCr=s(["Reference point","Push or pull","Distance moved","Time taken"],t.s)
B.aGv=s(["A reference point is a place used for comparison, such as a tree. Saying a toy is near the tree describes its position.","A push or pull is a force. A force can start, stop, or turn a toy, but some pushes are too small to move it.","Distance moved tells how far an object travels. Use equal units and the same route when comparing travel.","Time taken tells how long a movement lasts. To compare how fast two toys move, compare distance in equal times."],t.s)
B.b_n=s(["0|Which model part is the tree in the phrase beside the tree?","1|Which model part is used to set a resting toy moving?","2|Which quantity tells how far a toy travels?","3|Which quantity tells how long the trip lasts?","0|Which model part makes the position behind the chair meaningful?","1|Which model part can make a moving toy turn?","2|What is counted when a toy crosses six equal floor squares?","3|What does a clock measure during a toy trip?","3|Two toys travel equally far, but one finishes sooner. Which measurement differs?","2|Two toys move for the same time. Which measurement lets you compare how far they went?","0|A learner says the toy moved farther away but names no landmark. Which model part should be added?"],t.s)
B.c8d=new A.bO(3,"Force and Motion","Position and Movement Need a Reference","Describe position relative to a landmark|Compare motion over equal times|Identify a force that changes motion",B.aCr,B.aGv,"Toy A travels four floor squares in two seconds. Toy B travels two squares in the same time. A travels farther in equal time, so A is faster.","A toy is described only as being near. What information is missing?","A reference point: near what? Naming a chair or tree makes the position clear.","Farther does not always mean faster. Compare the times too.","Describe movement with a reference point, distance, and time.",B.b_n)
B.aF_=s(["Light","Sound","Heat","Electricity"],t.s)
B.b_1=s(["Light lets us see objects when it reaches our eyes. The Sun and a lamp are sources. Never stare at very bright sources.","Sound comes from vibrating things. A ringing bell sends sound through air. Loud sound can harm ears.","Heat is energy moving from warmer things to cooler things. A warm meal cools as heat moves to its surroundings.","Electricity can power lamps and fans in complete circuits. Wall sockets and damaged wires are dangerous; do not touch them."],t.s)
B.aCO=s(["0|Which model item helps us see a book?","1|Which model item comes from a vibrating bell?","2|Which model item moves from a warm meal to cooler air?","3|Which model item powers a plug-in fan?","0|Which model item comes from the Sun and reaches our eyes?","1|Which model item can harm ears if very loud?","2|Which model item explains warming near sunlight?","3|Which model item makes damaged wall wires dangerous?","0|A lamp uses electricity. Which useful output lets a child read?","2|A cup feels warmer after holding a warm drink. Which transfer explains that change?","1|A drum stops vibrating and its main signal fades. Which model item fades?"],t.s)
B.c8c=new A.bO(3,"Force and Motion","Useful Energy and Its Safe Sources","Match energy with an observed effect|Identify everyday sources|Choose safe behavior around energy",B.aF_,B.b_1,"A working electric lamp uses electricity and gives light. It may also become warm. One device can have more than one energy effect.","Which energy transfer explains a warm meal cooling on the table?","Heat moves from the warmer meal to cooler surroundings. The meal does not create cold.","Electricity and light are not identical. A fan uses electricity but its main useful effect is movement.","Energy produces useful changes, but bright light, loud sound, heat, and electricity need safe handling.",B.aCO)
B.atG=s(["Rock pieces","Soil","Water flow","Plant cover"],t.s)
B.axQ=s(["Rocks can break into smaller pieces over time. Rock pieces are part of many soils, but soil also contains other material.","Soil contains rock particles, decayed material, air, and water. Many plants spread roots through it.","Flowing water can carry loose soil away. Muddy water after rain may contain soil moved from bare ground.","Plant cover helps hold soil with roots and softens the impact of rain. Keeping suitable plants can reduce soil loss."],t.s)
B.aOo=s(["0|Which material forms when larger rocks break?","1|Which material contains rock particles and decayed matter?","2|Which process can carry loose soil downhill?","3|Which model part can hold soil with roots?","0|A stone cracks into smaller hard bits. Which model label fits those bits?","1|Which material usually surrounds garden roots?","2|Rain runs down a path carrying mud. Which model part is active?","3|Which choice helps reduce bare ground on a suitable garden slope?","3|A bare slope loses more mud than a grassy slope. Which model part best explains the difference?","2|Loose soil ends up in a puddle lower down after rain. Which model process moved it?","1|A sample has tiny stones, old leaf material, water, and air spaces. Which label best fits the whole sample?"],t.s)
B.c79=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space","Soil Water and Care for Our Surroundings","Identify parts of the local environment|Explain how water moves soil|Choose an action that protects a habitat",B.atG,B.axQ,"Two pictured slopes receive rain. One is bare; one has grass. Roots hold soil on the grassy slope, so less loose soil may wash away.","Why is keeping plants on a slope often better than clearing it bare?","Roots help hold soil and leaves reduce rain impact. Plant cover can reduce soil carried away.","Soil is not only dirt to throw away. It supports plant growth and is difficult to replace quickly.","Rocks, soil, water, and plants interact in our surroundings.",B.aOo)
B.b_T=s(["Weather today","Sun","Moon","Clouds"],t.s)
B.aHV=s(["Weather describes conditions now or over a short time: rain, wind, and how warm the air is. Record observations from a safe place.","The Sun is a star that gives Earth light and warmth. It appears to cross the sky during a day. Never look straight at it.","The Moon reflects sunlight. It can be seen in daytime or at night, depending on its position.","Clouds contain tiny water drops or ice crystals. Cloud cover can change quickly; not every cloud brings rain."],t.s)
B.aCI=s(["0|Which label fits a record of rain and wind this morning?","1|Which object provides most daylight?","2|Which object reflects sunlight and goes around Earth?","3|Which objects contain tiny drops or ice?","0|Which label fits air-temperature and rainfall observations for one day?","1|Which sky object should never be stared at?","2|Which rocky sky object can sometimes appear in a daytime sky?","3|Which model item can cover part of the sky without rain falling?","0|Two days have different rain symbols. Which model idea is being compared?","2|A child says every daytime bright object makes its own light. Which object shows reflected sunlight instead?","3|A white shape changes quickly in the sky and is made of water drops. Which model label fits?"],t.s)
B.c8_=new A.bO(4,"Earth and Space","Daily Weather and Our Sky","Describe weather with evidence|Distinguish clouds from sky objects|Use weather clues safely",B.b_T,B.aHV,"A chart says Monday was rainy and Tuesday was sunny. These are daily weather observations. They do not prove every Monday will be rainy.","Can a bright Moon seen in daytime be real?","Yes. The Moon is not only a night object. We see its sunlit part when its position and sky brightness allow.","Do not treat one dark cloud as a certain forecast. Use adult advice and official warnings for safety.","Weather changes over short times. Sun, Moon, and clouds have different properties.",B.aCI)
B.b1h=s([B.c7K,B.c7o,B.c8a,B.c8m,B.c8d,B.c8c,B.c79,B.c8_],t.EM)
