B.b5U=new A.ao(B.wj,["Mathematics in the Modern World","Science, Technology and Society","Purposive Communication"],t.w)
B.LC=new A.ao(B.wj,["General Mathematics","General Science","Effective Communication"],t.w)
B.b2f=s(["Describe a numerical pattern using a rule.","Distinguish a conjecture from a justified conclusion.","Use a mathematical model while stating its limits."],t.s)
B.bjn={"Observed terms: 4, 7, 10, 13":0,"Assumption: the difference stays 3":1,"Prediction: a(5) = 16":2}
B.b6j=new A.ao(B.bjn,["Successive differences are 3, 3, and 3.","The model is a(n) = 3n + 1 for positive whole-number n.","This follows from the model, not from four observations alone."],t.w)
B.aEi=s(["Under constant addition, what follows 4, 7, 10, 13?","16","14","15","17","Add the common difference 3 to 13."],t.s)
B.asu=s(["What is the common difference of 5, 8, 11?","3","2","5","6","Both 8 - 5 and 11 - 8 equal 3."],t.s)
B.ayo=s(["For a(n) = 3n + 1, what is a(4)?","13","12","16","4","Substitute n = 4 to get 12 + 1."],t.s)
B.aqQ=s(["Which statement is a model assumption?","The increase remains 3 each step","The first observed term is 4","The third observed term is 10","Four terms were recorded","The continuation rule goes beyond the observed data."],t.s)
B.aRq=s(["For T(n) = 5 + 3(n - 1), how many tiles are in design 6?","20","18","23","15","There are five increases of 3 after the initial 5."],t.s)
B.aPU=s(["Do four matching examples prove a rule for every positive integer?","No","Yes, always","Only if written in a table","Only if drawn in color","Finite observations alone do not prove an unrestricted claim."],t.s)
B.aKH=s(["A model ignores withdrawals. Which situation violates that assumption?","Money is taken from the savings account","The planned amount is deposited","Weeks are counted in order","The first deposit is recorded","A withdrawal changes the balance beyond the model."],t.s)
B.aUh=s(["For S(n) = 20 + 10(n - 1), what is S(5)?","PHP 60","PHP 50","PHP 70","PHP 100","Start with 20, then add four amounts of 10."],t.s)
B.aRf=s(["Under constant multiplication, what follows 3, 6, 12, 24?","48","27","36","30","Each term doubles."],t.s)
B.b_d=s(["A fare model uses an outdated fare schedule. What should be done?","Update the assumptions before using its predictions","Treat its answer as legally exact","Ignore the date of the schedule","Change only the passenger name","A useful model must match the applicable conditions."],t.s)
B.aUJ=s(["Which best separates evidence from inference?","Recorded terms are evidence; the continuation rule is an inference","A guessed rule is always an observation","Any diagram is a proof","A prediction cannot be checked","The observed values and the proposed rule have different roles."],t.s)
B.aMK=s([B.aEi,B.asu,B.ayo,B.aqQ,B.aRq,B.aPU,B.aKH,B.aUh,B.aRf,B.b_d,B.aUJ],t.E)
B.bJb=new A.he("Patterns and Mathematics in Our World",B.b2f,"Mathematics describes relationships in objects, quantities, and change. A pattern may suggest a rule, but a few examples do not prove that the rule always holds. For the sequence 4, 7, 10, 13, a constant increase of 3 suggests the arithmetic rule a(n) = 4 + 3(n - 1). The rule predicts a fifth term of 16. A different rule could agree with the first four terms and disagree later, so state the assumed model. A jeepney fare model might use a base amount and an additional amount per kilometer. Its predictions apply only to the route, distance rules, and fare schedule assumed. Models help us reason; they are not exact copies of reality.",B.b6j,"A row of tiles has 5 tiles in design 1, 8 in design 2, and 11 in design 3. 1. Compare counts: each new design adds 3. 2. State the constant-growth assumption. 3. Write T(n) = 5 + 3(n - 1). 4. For design 6, T(6) = 5 + 15 = 20 tiles.","A savings plan begins with PHP 20 and adds PHP 10 each week. Counting the first deposit as week 1, what is the amount at week 5?","Under the stated plan, S(n) = 20 + 10(n - 1). Thus S(5) = 60 pesos. This is the accumulated balance only if each week adds PHP 10 and no money is withdrawn.","Do not confuse the term number with the term value, or call a pattern a proof. A leaf arrangement can inspire mathematics without every plant following an exact sequence.","Observe, propose a rule, state assumptions, and check predictions against the situation.",B.aMK)
B.aRj=s(["Translate everyday claims into precise mathematical statements.","Use union, intersection, and complements in a stated universe.","Test conditional claims using counterexamples."],t.s)
B.bh7={"Bus only: 12; both: 6; walk only: 4; neither: 8":0,"Bus or walk: 12 + 6 + 4 = 22":1,"If divisible by 4, then even":2}
B.b6g=new A.ao(B.bh7,["The four disjoint regions total 30 learners.","Count the overlap only once.","The converse fails for 6."],t.w)
B.axA=s(["Which is a statement with a truth value?","8 is even","Close the door","What is your name?","Please count","The assertion about 8 can be judged true or false."],t.s)
B.azu=s(["If |A| = 18, |B| = 10, and the overlap is 6, what is the union size?","22","28","34","12","Subtract the overlap once from 18 + 10."],t.s)
B.aDQ=s(["In a universe of 30, a union contains 22 people. How many are outside it?","8","22","52","16","The complement has 30 - 22 people."],t.s)
B.aGM=s(["What does A intersection B contain?","Members belonging to both sets","Only members outside both sets","Every member of the universe","Only members in A but not B","Intersection means simultaneous membership."],t.s)
B.aSz=s(['Which disproves the converse of "divisible by 4 implies even"?',"6","8","12","16","6 is even but not divisible by 4."],t.s)
B.aIF=s(["What is needed to interpret the complement of a set?","A specified universal set","A bar graph only","A positive mean","A large sample","Complement is relative to the universe being considered."],t.s)
B.aZD=s(["Tea: 9, coffee: 8, both: 3. How many like at least one?","14","17","20","6","9 + 8 - 3 = 14."],t.s)
B.aZE=s(["In a universe of 20 with 14 in a union, how many are in neither set?","6","14","34","3","Subtract the union size from the universe size."],t.s)
B.aJu=s(['What disproves "Every prime number is odd"?',"2 is prime and even","3 is prime and odd","9 is odd and composite","10 is composite and even","A single prime that is not odd defeats the universal claim."],t.s)
B.aCV=s(['Which is the negation of "All learners attended"?',"At least one learner did not attend","No learner attended","All teachers attended","Most learners attended","Not all means at least one exception, not necessarily none."],t.s)
B.aUu=s(["A learner belongs to both A and B. How often is the learner counted in the union?","Once","Twice","Never","Four times","A set contains each member once."],t.s)
B.atz=s([B.axA,B.azu,B.aDQ,B.aGM,B.aSz,B.aIF,B.aZD,B.aZE,B.aJu,B.aCV,B.aUu],t.E)
B.bJ8=new A.he("Mathematical Language, Sets, and Logic",B.aRj,'Mathematical language makes assumptions and relationships explicit. Let U be all 30 learners in a class. Let A be learners who use a bus, and B learners who walk part of the trip. A union B includes learners in either group or both; A intersection B includes learners in both. The complement of A contains class members not in A, relative to U. A statement has a truth value; a question or command does not. The conditional "If a whole number is divisible by 4, then it is even" is true. Its converse is false: 6 is even but not divisible by 4. One counterexample disproves a universal claim. Definitions and quantifiers such as "all" and "some" matter.',B.b6g,"In a class of 30, |A| = 18, |B| = 10, and |A intersection B| = 6. 1. Add 18 + 10. 2. Subtract the overlap 6. 3. The union has 22 learners. 4. The number in neither is 30 - 22 = 8.","In a universe of 20 people, 9 like tea, 8 like coffee, and 3 like both. How many like at least one? How many like neither?","The union is 9 + 8 - 3 = 14. The complement of that union has 20 - 14 = 6 people.",'In mathematics, "or" normally includes both. Do not reverse a conditional without checking it, or use a complement without specifying the universe.',"Define the universe, distinguish and/or, and test general claims with precise reasoning.",B.atz)
B.aJo=s(["Represent a practical problem with variables and equations.","Solve a linear model and check units and constraints.","Compare alternatives rather than accepting an unchecked answer."],t.s)
B.bjC={"10 pages":0,"30 pages":1,"50 pages":2}
B.b7u=new A.ao(B.bjC,["A = PHP 60; B = PHP 40.","A = B = PHP 100.","A = PHP 140; B = PHP 160."],t.w)
B.aTo=s(["What does n represent in A(n) = 40 + 2n?","Number of pages","Cost per page","Setup fee","Number of printers","The variable counts pages."],t.s)
B.aRp=s(["What is A(10) for A(n) = 40 + 2n?","PHP 60","PHP 42","PHP 400","PHP 20","40 + 2(10) = 60."],t.s)
B.ash=s(["At how many pages do 40 + 2n and 10 + 3n cost the same?","30","10","40","50","Subtracting 2n and 10 gives n = 30."],t.s)
B.asn=s(["Which shop is cheaper for 50 pages?","A: 40 + 2n","B: 10 + 3n","They cost the same","Neither can be calculated","A costs 140 and B costs 160."],t.s)
B.aKZ=s(["What is the trip cost 900 + 60n for 15 learners?","PHP 1,800","PHP 960","PHP 13,500","PHP 1,500","The variable part is 900, added to the fixed 900."],t.s)
B.aNP=s(["Under a PHP 2,100 budget, what is the largest n with 900 + 60n <= 2,100?","20","21","35","15","After the fixed fee, 1,200 remains; 1,200/60 = 20."],t.s)
B.aRd=s(["Why is n = -2 inadmissible for a page count?","A count of printed pages cannot be negative","Negative numbers never exist","Every equation has only positive roots","A setup fee is zero","The practical domain restricts the mathematical variable."],t.s)
B.aQs=s(["For 45 pages, how much cheaper is A than B?","PHP 15","PHP 30","PHP 45","PHP 130","145 - 130 = 15."],t.s)
B.b1M=s(["An algebraic model gives 20.6 as the maximum affordable people. What count is feasible?","20","21","20.6 people","206","A maximum whole-person count must be rounded down."],t.s)
B.avF=s(["Which extra information could change the shop comparison?","A bulk discount on orders above 40 pages","The variable is named n","The equation is written on paper","Addition is commutative","A bulk discount changes the cost rule."],t.s)
B.aLN=s(["What is the final check after solving a word problem?","Check units, constraints, and the original question","Erase all assumptions","Assume every root is feasible","Choose the largest numeral","A solution must answer the actual situation, not only the equation."],t.s)
B.aCP=s([B.aTo,B.aRp,B.ash,B.asn,B.aKZ,B.aNP,B.aRd,B.aQs,B.b1M,B.avF,B.aLN],t.E)
B.bJ2=new A.he("Problem Solving and Mathematical Models",B.aJo,"A mathematical solution starts with understanding the question, selecting a representation, carrying out a plan, and checking the result in context. Suppose printing shop A charges PHP 40 setup plus PHP 2 per page. Shop B charges PHP 10 setup plus PHP 3 per page. Let n be a nonnegative whole-number page count. Costs are A(n) = 40 + 2n and B(n) = 10 + 3n. Equal cost occurs when 40 + 2n = 10 + 3n, or n = 30. Below 30 pages, B is cheaper; above 30, A is cheaper. The model excludes discounts, taxes, and paper differences unless explicitly included. An algebraic value is not a finished answer until its unit and feasibility have been checked.",B.b7u,"Compare shops for 45 pages. 1. Set n = 45. 2. A costs 40 + 2(45) = 130. 3. B costs 10 + 3(45) = 145. 4. A saves PHP 15, assuming equivalent service.","A school trip costs PHP 900 fixed plus PHP 60 per learner. What is the cost for 15 learners? If the budget is PHP 2,100, how many learners can the model fund?","C(15) = 900 + 60(15) = PHP 1,800. For the budget, 900 + 60n <= 2,100 gives n <= 20. The largest feasible whole-number count is 20.","Do not add a per-page price only once, accept a negative passenger count, or round a maximum affordable count upward beyond the budget.","Define variables and units, solve, compare, and verify the result against real constraints.",B.aCP)
B.b1Q=s(["Compute and compare mean, median, and range.","Distinguish a population, sample, and biased sampling method.","Interpret association without claiming unsupported causation."],t.s)
B.bio={"Sorted times: 10, 15, 15, 20, 90 minutes":0,"Mean 30; median 15":1,"Club volunteers versus all students":2}
B.b57=new A.ao(B.bio,["Sum 150; five observations.","The unusually long journey pulls the mean upward.","The sampling frame can exclude important groups."],t.w)
B.b05=s(["What is the mean of 10, 15, 15, 20, 90?","30","15","20","90","Their sum is 150 and there are five observations."],t.s)
B.aG8=s(["What is the median of 10, 15, 15, 20, 90?","15","30","10","80","The third sorted observation is 15."],t.s)
B.aRZ=s(["What is the range of 10, 15, 15, 20, 90?","80","90","30","15","Range is maximum minus minimum: 90 - 10."],t.s)
B.ask=s(["Which is the population in a study of all students at a university?","All students at that university","Only the surveyed class","Only volunteers","Only graduates","The population is the entire stated group of interest."],t.s)
B.b2V=s(["What is a risk of surveying only one sports club about all students?","Selection bias","Guaranteed representativeness","A census of all students","No possible measurement error","Club members may differ from other students."],t.s)
B.b_X=s(["What is the mean of 4, 6, 6, 8?","6","4","8","24","The sum is 24 divided by 4."],t.s)
B.aYz=s(["What is the median of 2, 3, 3, 4, 18?","3","6","18","4","The middle of the five sorted values is 3."],t.s)
B.aS2=s(["A study finds umbrella use associated with wet roads. What can be concluded from association alone?","The two occur together; causation is not established","Umbrellas cause rain","Wet roads manufacture umbrellas","No further study can help","Rain can be a common cause of both observations."],t.s)
B.awA=s(["Which feature is essential on a travel-time graph?","A clearly labeled time unit","An unexplained truncated scale","Only decorative colors","A missing title and axis labels","Readers must know what quantity is measured."],t.s)
B.aQ2=s(["Why can the median better describe typical travel time when one trip is extremely long?","It is less affected by an extreme value","It always equals the largest value","It ignores the order of the data","It proves every journey is short","The median depends on the middle position rather than the sum."],t.s)
B.av9=s(["Ten of 20 students pass in A and 90 of 100 pass in B. What is the combined pass rate?","100/120, about 83.3%","70%","140%","50%","Combine pass counts and total counts; do not take the unweighted average of rates."],t.s)
B.azL=s([B.b05,B.aG8,B.aRZ,B.ask,B.b2V,B.b_X,B.aYz,B.aS2,B.awA,B.aQ2,B.av9],t.E)
B.bJc=new A.he("Data, Statistics, and Responsible Interpretation",B.b1Q,"Data management involves asking a question, collecting suitable data, summarizing it, and interpreting limitations. A population is the whole group of interest; a sample is the subset observed. A survey of volunteers from one club may not represent every student. For daily travel times 10, 15, 15, 20, and 90 minutes, the mean is 150/5 = 30 minutes. The median is 15, and the range is 90 - 10 = 80. The long journey raises the mean; the median describes a typical middle observation more robustly here. A graph must state units and use a fair scale. Two variables changing together does not prove that one causes the other; a third factor or reverse direction may explain an association.",B.b57,"Summarize scores 4, 6, 6, 8. 1. Sort the values. 2. Mean = 24/4 = 6. 3. Median = (6 + 6)/2 = 6. 4. Range = 8 - 4 = 4; include the score scale before interpreting.","Find the mean and median of 2, 3, 3, 4, 18. Which is more resistant to the extreme value?","The sum is 30, so the mean is 6. The middle sorted value is 3. The median is more resistant to the unusually high 18.","Do not average percentages from unequal group sizes without weighting, or describe a correlation from a survey as proof of causation.","Use suitable summaries, explain the sample, label graphs, and separate association from causal evidence.",B.azL)
B.avu=s(["Compute simple and compound interest with matched time units.","Compare percentage changes and real purchasing power.","Identify assumptions and costs omitted from a financial model."],t.s)
B.bi4={"Simple, year 2":0,"Compounded annually, year 2":1,"Price 100, down 20%, then up 20%":2}
B.b60=new A.ao(B.bi4,["10,000 + 10,000(0.05)(2) = PHP 11,000.","10,000(1.05)^2 = PHP 11,025 before fees and taxes.","100 x 0.8 x 1.2 = 96."],t.w)
B.aBB=s(["What decimal represents 5%?","0.05","5","0.5","50","Percent means per hundred."],t.s)
B.arE=s(["What is simple interest on PHP 10,000 at 5% per year for 2 years?","PHP 1,000","PHP 500","PHP 11,000","PHP 25","I = 10,000(0.05)(2)."],t.s)
B.aqc=s(["What is the annual-compound balance of PHP 10,000 at 5% for 2 years?","PHP 11,025","PHP 11,000","PHP 10,500","PHP 12,000","10,000(1.05)^2 = 11,025."],t.s)
B.aAM=s(["Six months expressed in years equals what?","0.5","6","12","2","Six of twelve months is half a year."],t.s)
B.at9=s(["What is simple interest on PHP 4,000 at 6% annually for half a year?","PHP 120","PHP 240","PHP 1,440","PHP 24","4,000(0.06)(0.5) = 120."],t.s)
B.aw5=s(["A PHP 100 item is discounted 20%. What is its sale price?","PHP 80","PHP 120","PHP 20","PHP 96","Multiply the original price by 0.8."],t.s)
B.aXv=s(["After PHP 100 falls 20% then rises 20%, what is the new amount?","PHP 96","PHP 100","PHP 120","PHP 80","The second percentage applies to 80, not 100."],t.s)
B.aPX=s(["What makes compound interest differ from simple interest?","Interest can earn further interest","The principal must be negative","Time has no effect","Fees are automatically included","Compounding applies interest to accumulated balances."],t.s)
B.aXK=s(["What is PHP 5,000 compounded annually at 4% for 2 years?","PHP 5,408","PHP 5,400","PHP 5,200","PHP 5,040","5,000 x 1.04 x 1.04 = 5,408."],t.s)
B.asp=s(["Savings grow 2% while prices rise 5%. What happens to approximate purchasing power?","It decreases","It necessarily increases 7%","It stays exactly unchanged","It doubles","Prices grow faster than the nominal balance."],t.s)
B.aOJ=s(["Two loans quote rates but only one discloses fees. What is needed for a fair comparison?","Comparable total costs over the same period","The shorter advertisement","The larger printed percentage","Ignoring repayment dates","Fees, timing, and interest all affect the cost."],t.s)
B.ar4=s([B.aBB,B.arE,B.aqc,B.aAM,B.at9,B.aw5,B.aXv,B.aPX,B.aXK,B.asp,B.aOJ],t.E)
B.bJ6=new A.he("Financial Mathematics and Informed Choices",B.avu,"Financial mathematics supports decisions, not guaranteed profit predictions. Simple interest is I = Prt, where P is principal, r the rate per time period, and t the number of those periods. At 5% per year for 2 years, PHP 10,000 earns PHP 1,000 simple interest. With annual compounding the balance is P(1 + r)^t = 10,000(1.05)^2 = PHP 11,025. Compounding earns interest on earlier interest. Fees, taxes, inflation, and risk can change the outcome. A 20% discount followed by a 20% increase does not return the original price: 100 becomes 80, then 96. Compare alternatives using the same period and disclose the complete cash flows.",B.b60,"A PHP 4,000 loan has simple interest of 6% per year for 6 months. 1. Convert 6 months to 0.5 year. 2. Use r = 0.06. 3. I = 4,000(0.06)(0.5) = 120. 4. Principal plus interest is PHP 4,120, excluding any fee.","Find the balance on PHP 5,000 compounded annually at 4% for 2 years, before taxes and fees.","A = 5,000(1.04)^2 = 5,408 pesos. Interest is 408 pesos. The rate and period are both annual.","Do not enter 5 instead of 0.05 for 5%, mix months with an annual rate, or treat a quoted yield as a risk-free guarantee.","Match rates and time units, distinguish interest models, and account for fees, inflation, and risk.",B.ar4)
B.aRl=s(["Represent a network using vertices and edges.","Distinguish a path through locations from a route through every edge.","Evaluate a decision using constraints and a stated fairness rule."],t.s)
B.biU={"A-B: 4 km; B-C: 3 km; A-C: 10 km":0,"Triangle A-B-C-A":1,"Group sizes 10, 20, 30; fund PHP 1,200":2}
B.b58=new A.ao(B.biU,["A to B to C is 7 km, shorter than the direct 10 km edge.","Each vertex has degree 2; an Euler circuit is possible.","Proportional shares are PHP 200, 400, and 600."],t.w)
B.arz=s(["What represents a location in a road-network graph?","A vertex","A percentage","A median","An interest rate","Vertices model locations; edges model connections."],t.s)
B.aQm=s(["What represents a road between two modeled locations?","An edge","A sample mean","A universal quantifier","A discount","An edge connects modeled vertices."],t.s)
B.aM8=s(["A-B is 4 km and B-C is 3 km. What is the distance A-B-C?","7 km","12 km","1 km","10 km","Add the two edge lengths."],t.s)
B.axb=s(["A-C is 10 km and A-B-C is 7 km. Which minimizes distance?","A-B-C","A-C","Both are 10 km","The path with fewer letters","7 is less than 10."],t.s)
B.asd=s(["What is the degree of each vertex in a triangle graph?","2","1","3","6","Two edges meet at each vertex."],t.s)
B.b3l=s(["A connected undirected graph has exactly two odd-degree vertices. What is possible?","An Euler trail with different start and end vertices","No route along any edge","An Euler circuit starting anywhere","A proof that every distance is equal","An Euler trail starts and ends at the two odd vertices."],t.s)
B.aWw=s(["PHP 1,200 divided equally among 3 groups gives each how much?","PHP 400","PHP 200","PHP 600","PHP 300","1,200/3 = 400."],t.s)
B.aVS=s(["For sizes 10, 20, 30 sharing PHP 1,200 proportionally, what does the smallest group receive?","PHP 200","PHP 400","PHP 600","PHP 120","10/60 of 1,200 equals 200."],t.s)
B.aJy=s(["For sizes 5, 10, 15 sharing PHP 600 proportionally, what does the largest group receive?","PHP 300","PHP 200","PHP 100","PHP 600","15/30 of the fund equals half, or 300."],t.s)
B.aVV=s(["Why may a shortest-distance route not be the best real route?","Safety or closures may impose additional constraints","Distance cannot be added","All roads always have equal risk","Vertices are not allowed in routes","The objective and constraints must reflect the actual situation."],t.s)
B.apU=s(["Before calling a fund allocation fair, what should be stated?","The fairness rule and its purpose","Only the largest share","Only the number of banknotes","A claim that all rules are identical","Equal shares and proportional shares serve different goals."],t.s)
B.aYf=s([B.arz,B.aQm,B.aM8,B.axb,B.asd,B.b3l,B.aWw,B.aVS,B.aJy,B.aVV,B.apU],t.E)
B.bJf=new A.he("Graphs, Networks, and Fair Decisions",B.aRl,"A mathematical graph models connections, not necessarily a chart of measured values. Vertices can represent barangays and edges roads. Edge weights may represent distances or costs. The shortest route minimizes the sum of weights, not the number of roads alone. A route visiting each road exactly once is an Euler trail; in a connected undirected graph it exists when exactly zero or two vertices have odd degree. Visiting each location once is a different problem. Decision mathematics also makes fairness rules explicit. Sharing PHP 1,200 equally among 3 groups gives PHP 400 each; sharing in proportion to sizes 10, 20, 30 gives PHP 200, 400, 600. Neither rule can be called uniquely fair without explaining the intended goal.",B.b58,"Find a route from A to C. 1. List direct A-C = 10 km. 2. List A-B-C = 4 + 3 = 7 km. 3. Compare 7 with 10. 4. Choose A-B-C under a distance-only objective; road safety or closures could change the decision.","Three groups have 5, 10, and 15 members. Share PHP 600 in proportion to group size.","There are 30 members. Each member corresponds to PHP 20. Group shares are 100, 200, and 300 pesos; their sum is 600.","Do not confuse an edge with a vertex, the fewest edges with the lowest total weight, or equal shares with proportional shares.","Define the network, objective, and constraints; state the fairness criterion before comparing decisions.",B.aYf)
B.aPM=s([B.bJb,B.bJ8,B.bJ2,B.bJc,B.bJ6,B.bJf],t.Qy)
B.azP=s(["Distinguish scientific explanation from technological design.","Explain how social priorities shape research and invention.","Evaluate a technology using benefits, costs, and affected groups."],t.s)
B.bhp={"Science: explain flood risk":0,"Technology: design an alert":1,"Society: decide who is protected":2}
B.b55=new A.ao(B.bhp,["Measure rainfall and river levels, then test relationships and predictions.","Combine sensors, power, messages, and accessible warning devices.","Include remote households, language access, maintenance, and evacuation support."],t.w)
B.aMG=s(["Which activity primarily develops a scientific explanation?","Testing how rainfall relates to river rise","Choosing a phone color","Printing a sales slogan","Naming a product","The activity investigates an evidence-based relationship."],t.s)
B.aG4=s(["Which is a technological design decision in a flood system?","Selecting how alerts reach residents","Proving all floods identical","Ignoring maintenance","Assuming every home has a phone","Design selects tools and processes to meet a need."],t.s)
B.aKF=s(["Why may text-only alerts exclude some residents?","Not everyone has reliable phone access or signal","All residents prefer identical devices","Texts eliminate every access barrier","Rainfall cannot be measured","Access depends on infrastructure and user circumstances."],t.s)
B.azR=s(["What should a technology evaluation include?","Benefits, costs, risks, and affected groups","Only novelty","Only the advertisement","Only the purchase price","A social evaluation examines more than initial cost."],t.s)
B.atK=s(["How should local knowledge be treated?","With attribution, consent, and evidence-sensitive respect","As automatically false","As automatically scientifically proven","As free of any ownership concerns","Respect for knowledge holders and evaluation of claims can coexist."],t.s)
B.aHq=s(["Which is an ongoing cost of a warning system?","Sensor maintenance and repairs","Only the first purchase","The product name","The color of a poster","Systems require continued upkeep to remain reliable."],t.s)
B.aMO=s(["Who should help evaluate an evacuation alert?","Residents including people with access needs","Only the vendor","Only phone manufacturers","No actual users","Affected users reveal requirements that designers can miss."],t.s)
B.aNp=s(["Which is evidence of successful alert delivery?","A drill showing residents received and understood the warning","A large advertising budget","A bright logo","A claim that testing is unnecessary","A drill tests the actual communication objective."],t.s)
B.atE=s(["A solar pump has low fuel costs but high repair costs. What comparison is needed?","Total costs and benefits over its useful life","Only fuel cost","Only its first-day output","Only its brand popularity","Lifetime evaluation includes operating and repair expenses."],t.s)
B.aEM=s(["Can more accurate sensors alone guarantee safe evacuation?","No; communication and accessible response also matter","Yes, in all communities","Yes, even with no messages","Only if no one maintains them","Risk reduction is a whole-system problem."],t.s)
B.avy=s(["What is an unintended effect worth investigating?","Water access becoming less affordable for poorer households","The stated purpose on the box","The spelling of the device name","A diagram title","Distributional harms can occur even when the device works technically."],t.s)
B.aue=s([B.aMG,B.aG4,B.aKF,B.azR,B.atK,B.aHq,B.aMO,B.aNp,B.atE,B.aEM,B.avy],t.E)
B.bJ1=new A.he("Science, Technology, and Philippine Society",B.azP,"Science develops explanations supported by evidence; technology applies knowledge and practical skills to design processes and tools. Society influences which questions receive funding, which tools are built, and who can use them. A flood-warning system combines rainfall measurements, scientific models, communication equipment, and local decisions. More accurate sensors alone cannot ensure safety if messages are inaccessible or evacuation routes exclude people with limited mobility. Philippine local and Indigenous knowledge includes careful observations of environments and useful practices. Such knowledge deserves attribution, consent, and respectful evaluation; calling a practice traditional neither proves nor disproves a scientific claim. A responsible analysis considers evidence, history, cultural context, distribution of benefits, and unintended effects.",B.b55,"A barangay proposes text-only flood alerts. 1. Identify the aim: timely evacuation. 2. Identify constraints: mobile signal, phone access, language, and disability access. 3. Compare text alerts with radio, sirens, and trained local messengers. 4. Recommend a tested combination and a maintenance plan rather than assuming one device reaches everyone.","A solar-powered water pump reduces fuel use but requires expensive repairs. What evidence should a community examine before purchasing it?","Compare lifetime costs, seasonal water demand, repair skills, spare parts, environmental effects, and access for different households. Consult users and test whether the design meets the actual need.","Newer does not automatically mean better, and a technology is not socially neutral merely because it has a scientific component.","Analyze science, design, and social choices together; include people who receive fewer benefits or bear larger risks.",B.aue)
B.aC1=s(["Explain how new evidence can challenge established explanations.","Distinguish a scientific model from an untested assertion.","Use examples of intellectual change without treating science as infallible."],t.s)
B.bje={"Established explanation":0,"New observations and criticism":1,"Revised explanation":2}
B.b5E=new A.ao(B.bje,["A model organizes observations but can contain errors.","Compare predictions, methods, and competing explanations.","Retain what evidence supports; revise what evidence contradicts."],t.w)
B.aIO=s(["What did the Copernican model challenge?","An Earth-centered arrangement of planetary motion","The existence of measurements","The use of mathematical models","All forms of observation","It placed Earth among planets orbiting the Sun."],t.s)
B.aP5=s(["Which later improvement is associated with Kepler?","Elliptical planetary orbits","Every orbit being a perfect square","Planets having no motion","The Sun orbiting every insect","Elliptical orbits improved planetary predictions."],t.s)
B.b2u=s(["What is required for natural selection to change a population?","Heritable variation related to reproductive success","Every organism deciding to change","No variation among organisms","A single individual becoming a new population","Selection changes the frequency of heritable traits across generations."],t.s)
B.aI0=s(["In the insect example, when can resistance variation already exist?","Before insecticide exposure","Only after every insect dies","Only after a conscious decision","Only after the insect is named","Selection can act on pre-existing heritable variation."],t.s)
B.aA0=s(["Does natural selection require organisms to want a trait?","No","Yes, always","Only in plants","Only in bacteria","It depends on variation and differential reproduction, not intention."],t.s)
B.b21=s(["What should be done with an unexpected measurement?","Check methods and compare explanations","Erase it because it is surprising","Accept it without checking","Judge it only by popularity","Reliable revision requires scrutiny of both evidence and models."],t.s)
B.aNM=s(["What distinguishes historical influence from current scientific support?","Influence concerns impact; support depends on evidence for particular claims","They are exactly identical","Every old idea is false","Every famous idea is untestable","A theory can influence history without all of its claims remaining accepted."],t.s)
B.aYr=s(["What is a useful scientific model expected to do?","Explain evidence and produce testable predictions within limits","Avoid every possible test","Guarantee complete certainty forever","Replace observation with authority","Models can be evaluated against observations."],t.s)
B.aCY=s(["Resistance becomes more common across many insect generations. What has changed?","The population distribution of traits","Every survivor's conscious goal","The date of exposure only","The definition of a planet","Evolution concerns heritable changes in populations."],t.s)
B.aGE=s(["Why does science revise explanations?","New reliable evidence or better models can reveal limitations","Revision proves evidence is useless","Only fashion determines facts","All old measurements must be discarded","Revisability is a strength of evidence-based inquiry."],t.s)
B.aB4=s(["Which statement avoids a misconception about evolution?","Traits advantageous in one environment can be costly in another","Every trait is perfect","All evolution has a fixed goal","Individuals evolve because they try hard","Fitness depends on context and trade-offs."],t.s)
B.aDv=s([B.aIO,B.aP5,B.b2u,B.aI0,B.aA0,B.b21,B.aNM,B.aYr,B.aCY,B.aGE,B.aB4],t.E)
B.bIZ=new A.he("Scientific Revolutions and Evidence",B.aC1,"Scientific knowledge changes through evidence, criticism, and improved methods. The Copernican model placed Earth and other planets in motion around the Sun, challenging an Earth-centered arrangement. Later observations and models, including Kepler's elliptical orbits, improved the account; Copernicus did not already possess every detail of modern astronomy. Darwin and Wallace developed explanations of evolution by natural selection: heritable variation can affect survival and reproduction, changing populations across generations. Evolution does not say that an individual changes because it needs to. Intellectual movements in psychology also changed discussion of human behavior, but historical influence is not the same as present empirical support for every claim. Evaluate each claim with appropriate evidence, alternatives, testability, and limitations.",B.b5E,"A population of insects varies in resistance before exposure to an insecticide. 1. Some variation is heritable. 2. Susceptible insects are more likely to die during exposure. 3. Resistant survivors can leave more offspring. 4. Resistance becomes more common over generations; the insects did not consciously choose to evolve.","A new observation disagrees with a familiar astronomical model. Should a researcher discard the observation immediately or examine the measurement and competing explanations?","Check the instrument, methods, uncertainty, and reproducibility. If the discrepancy persists, compare revised models and their predictions. Neither popularity nor novelty alone decides the issue.","Do not describe natural selection as purposeful improvement toward perfection, or assume an influential historical idea is beyond further testing.","Scientific change is evidence-guided revision, not a contest between famous names.",B.aDv)
B.aBN=s(["Compare benefits and burdens of a proposed technology.","Apply autonomy, fairness, and harm prevention to a case.","Distinguish informed consent from merely obtaining a signature."],t.s)
B.biL={Benefit:0,Burden:1,"Ethical response":2}
B.b61=new A.ao(B.biL,["An online service saves travel time for many users.","People without internet access may lose service access.","Keep accessible alternatives and involve affected users in evaluation."],t.w)
B.aQj=s(["Which question belongs in an ethical technology assessment?","Who benefits and who bears the risks?","Only how new the device is","Only how expensive the logo is","Only whether it can be built","Ethical evaluation considers affected people and consequences."],t.s)
B.aQM=s(["What is required for informed consent?","Understandable information and voluntary choice","Only a signature","Pressure from a supervisor","No opportunity for questions","Consent requires understanding and freedom to decide."],t.s)
B.b28=s(["A participant cannot read the consent form. What should happen?","Provide an understandable explanation and check understanding","Assume the signature proves understanding","Hide the risks","Prevent questions","Accessibility is part of meaningful informed consent."],t.s)
B.aIt=s(["What is a less intrusive alternative to facial recognition attendance?","A suitable non-biometric attendance process","Collecting more unrelated biometric data","Removing every appeal route","Publishing all face records","Compare effective methods that collect less sensitive data."],t.s)
B.aqk=s(["Why examine error rates across user groups?","Errors may burden groups unequally","All systems always fail equally","Fairness excludes measurement","Only average accuracy can matter","An overall average may hide unequal harms."],t.s)
B.aLX=s(["Which supports participation?","Consulting affected users before and during implementation","Only consulting the vendor","Banning complaints","Declaring all concerns irrational","Users can identify harms and needs otherwise overlooked."],t.s)
B.aAd=s(["What does human flourishing include beyond efficiency?","Dignity, health, relationships, and participation","Only device ownership","Only screen time","Only corporate income","Well-being has multiple social and personal dimensions."],t.s)
B.aYO=s(["Does technical feasibility alone establish ethical acceptability?","No","Yes, always","Only if the device is expensive","Only if an advertisement says so","Being able to build a system does not settle whether it should be used."],t.s)
B.awH=s(["Which feature helps people harmed by an automated error?","An accessible appeal and correction process","No human review","Permanent uncorrectable records","Hidden decision rules with no contact route","People need a meaningful way to challenge and correct errors."],t.s)
B.aS4=s(["What should be said about important uncertainty?","Disclose it and explain how risks will be monitored","Promise perfect safety","Suppress all discussion","Treat uncertainty as proof of no risk","Responsible decisions acknowledge evidence limits."],t.s)
B.aEa=s(["When might identical treatment be unfair?","When differing access needs require different support","Whenever everyone receives water","Only when nobody uses technology","Never under any circumstances","Equal dignity can require accommodations rather than identical procedures."],t.s)
B.apK=s([B.aQj,B.aQM,B.b28,B.aIt,B.aqk,B.aLX,B.aAd,B.aYO,B.awH,B.aS4,B.aEa],t.E)
B.bJa=new A.he("Human Flourishing, Ethics, and Technology",B.aBN,"Human flourishing involves more than efficiency or the number of devices owned. Technologies should be evaluated in relation to dignity, health, participation, relationships, and environmental conditions. An ethical assessment asks who benefits, who bears risk, whether alternatives exist, and whether affected people have a meaningful voice. In research, informed consent requires understandable information, voluntary choice, and the ability to decline or withdraw under the stated conditions. A signature alone does not establish understanding or freedom from pressure. Fairness may require additional support for excluded groups rather than identical treatment regardless of need. Risk cannot always be reduced to zero, but uncertainty must be disclosed rather than hidden behind claims that a technology is perfectly safe.",B.b61,"A university wants mandatory facial recognition for attendance. 1. Identify the goal and less intrusive alternatives. 2. Examine data security, error rates, and unequal effects. 3. Consult students and staff, including those harmed by errors. 4. Decide whether necessity and benefits justify the risks, with appeal and deletion procedures.","A research participant signs a form they cannot understand because it is written in an unfamiliar language. Is the signature enough?","No. The researcher must provide understandable information, check understanding, and ensure the choice is voluntary. Translation or an appropriate explanation may be needed.","Do not equate consent with a signature, fairness with ignoring different needs, or technical feasibility with ethical acceptability.","Assess dignity, consent, distribution of harms, meaningful participation, and less harmful alternatives.",B.apK)
B.az7=s(["Distinguish genetic, species, and ecosystem diversity.","Explain how biodiversity supports human communities.","Evaluate a biotechnology claim case by case using evidence and trade-offs."],t.s)
B.bia={"Genetic diversity":0,"Species diversity":1,"Ecosystem diversity":2}
B.b7g=new A.ao(B.bia,["Different inherited variants within a crop species.","Different species in a mangrove community.","Mangroves, seagrass beds, reefs, and forests across a landscape."],t.w)
B.aWb=s(["Variation among inherited traits within one crop species is what?","Genetic diversity","Only ecosystem diversity","Only species richness","No biological diversity","Genetic diversity concerns inherited variation within a species."],t.s)
B.aGO=s(["Mangroves, reefs, and forests in a region illustrate what?","Ecosystem diversity","One genetic variant","A single organism","An interest rate","They are different ecological systems."],t.s)
B.b2q=s(["Which is a possible benefit of mangroves?","Nursery habitat for some fish and coastal wave reduction","Guaranteed prevention of every storm impact","Unlimited protection without habitat health","Elimination of all sea-level rise","Benefits are real but depend on conditions and have limits."],t.s)
B.avO=s(["Which can threaten biodiversity?","Habitat loss","Protecting diverse habitats","Monitoring species recovery","Reducing pollution","Removing habitats can eliminate resources and populations."],t.s)
B.b3c=s(["Which is biotechnology but not necessarily genetic engineering?","Fermentation","Drawing a weather map","Measuring a road","Calculating a fare","Fermentation uses biological processes."],t.s)
B.apS=s(["What is needed to evaluate a genetically engineered crop?","Evidence about its particular trait and context","Only the word modified","Only a slogan","Only the package color","Different traits and environments create different benefits and risks."],t.s)
B.b29=s(["Why is seedling count alone a weak restoration measure?","It does not show survival or ecosystem recovery","Every planted seedling always survives","Species never matter","Hydrology has no effect","Restoration requires sustained ecological function, not just planting."],t.s)
B.aMg=s(["Does natural automatically mean safe?","No","Yes, without exception","Only if expensive","Only if unnamed","Natural substances and organisms can also cause harm."],t.s)
B.aVo=s(["Who should be included in coastal restoration decisions?","Local communities and relevant ecological specialists","Only a marketing team","No resource users","Only people living far away","Decisions affect livelihoods and ecosystems."],t.s)
B.aUK=s(["Why may a mixed-species restoration be preferable to monoculture?","Different species can support different functions and resilience","Every species has identical needs","Monoculture always contains more species","Diversity prevents every possible loss","Diversity can support complementary functions without guaranteeing immunity to harm."],t.s)
B.aZu=s(["Which evaluation is most defensible?","Compare measured benefits, risks, alternatives, and affected livelihoods","Choose only by whether a method sounds modern","Assume all crop traits have identical risks","Ignore monitoring after approval","Public decisions need context-specific evidence and follow-up."],t.s)
B.awr=s([B.aWb,B.aGO,B.b2q,B.avO,B.b3c,B.apS,B.b29,B.aMg,B.aVo,B.aUK,B.aZu],t.E)
B.bJ_=new A.he("Biodiversity, Biotechnology, and Public Decisions",B.az7,'Biodiversity includes variation within species, among species, and among ecosystems. A mangrove area can support fish nurseries, store carbon, and reduce some coastal wave impacts, but it is not an unlimited shield against every storm. Loss of habitat, overuse, invasive species, pollution, and climate change can reduce biodiversity. Conservation decisions involve local livelihoods as well as ecological evidence. Biotechnology uses organisms or biological processes; examples include fermentation, tissue culture, and genetic engineering. These methods are not interchangeable. A genetically engineered crop should be evaluated by its particular trait, environmental setting, food-safety evidence, monitoring, and socioeconomic effects. The labels "natural" and "modified" alone do not establish safety, harm, or suitability.',B.b7g,"A coastal area proposes replacing mixed mangroves with a single fast-growing species. 1. Identify the goal and current species. 2. Examine habitat, survival, hydrology, and local uses. 3. Compare mixed restoration with monoculture. 4. Monitor outcomes rather than judging success only by seedlings planted.",'A crop advertisement says "genetically modified, therefore always safe." What information is missing?',"The exact trait and use, relevant safety studies, environmental exposure, alternatives, regulatory assessment, and impacts on farmers are needed. A method label is not a complete risk assessment.","Do not count planted seedlings as surviving forest, confuse all biotechnology with genetic engineering, or assume natural products are automatically harmless.","Specify the level of diversity and the actual intervention; assess ecological and social consequences with evidence.",B.awr)
B.aU8=s(["Explain the greenhouse effect and distinguish weather from climate.","Compare mitigation with adaptation using Philippine examples.","Evaluate energy choices using emissions, reliability, and access."],t.s)
B.bi3={Mitigation:0,Adaptation:1,"Just transition":2}
B.b6i=new A.ao(B.bi3,["Reduce emissions through efficiency and lower-emission energy.","Improve heat plans, flood resilience, and water management.","Consider workers, affordability, access, and affected communities."],t.w)
B.aUg=s(["Which describes climate rather than a single weather event?","Long-term patterns of temperature and rainfall","Rain at noon today","This morning's wind gust","One afternoon thunderstorm","Climate concerns distributions and trends over longer periods."],t.s)
B.aK6=s(["What radiation do greenhouse gases absorb and emit?","Infrared radiation","Only sound waves","Only mechanical vibration","No radiation","Their infrared interactions affect Earth's energy balance."],t.s)
B.apI=s(["Which activity increases atmospheric carbon dioxide?","Burning fossil fuels","Measuring temperature","Drawing a graph","Sorting survey answers","Combustion transfers fossil carbon to the atmosphere."],t.s)
B.azd=s(["Which is mitigation?","Reducing emissions from energy use","Only building a flood shelter","Only distributing emergency water","Only relocating a heat clinic","Mitigation addresses the causes of climate change."],t.s)
B.aAq=s(["Which is adaptation?","Improving heat-health response plans","Only calculating annual emissions","Only changing a graph title","Ignoring flood exposure","Adaptation reduces vulnerability to impacts."],t.s)
B.aA6=s(["Does one cool week disprove long-term global warming?","No","Yes, conclusively","Only if it rains","Only if it is in November","Short local variability and long-term global trends are different scales."],t.s)
B.aAh=s(["What challenge must solar electricity planning consider?","Variation with sunlight and system needs","Sunlight being identical all hours","No need for electrical safety","Guaranteed output at night without storage","Output varies; system design must manage supply and demand."],t.s)
B.b_D=s(["Which is relevant to a just energy transition?","Worker livelihoods and affordable access","Only the color of panels","Only a slogan","Excluding nearby communities","Fairness includes how costs and benefits are distributed."],t.s)
B.aNG=s(["Why examine lifecycle impacts?","Manufacturing, operation, and disposal can all affect the environment","Only the first second of use matters","All materials have no impacts","A label removes environmental costs","A complete comparison follows the whole system."],t.s)
B.aK3=s(["Which evidence best evaluates a warming trend?","Quality-controlled measurements over many years and locations","One photograph of a cloudy day","One person feeling cold","A single uncalibrated reading","Trends require reliable temporal and spatial evidence."],t.s)
B.aWJ=s(["Can mitigation and adaptation be used together?","Yes; they address different but related needs","No; only one is logically possible","Only if all weather stops","Only outside the Philippines","Reducing causes and reducing vulnerability are complementary."],t.s)
B.aKK=s([B.aUg,B.aK6,B.apI,B.azd,B.aAq,B.aA6,B.aAh,B.b_D,B.aNG,B.aK3,B.aWJ],t.E)
B.bJ9=new A.he("Climate Change, Energy, and Shared Responsibility",B.aU8,"Weather describes conditions over short periods; climate describes patterns over longer periods. Greenhouse gases absorb and emit infrared radiation, affecting Earth's energy balance. Human activities, including burning fossil fuels and changing land use, increase greenhouse-gas concentrations and drive current long-term warming. A single cool day does not overturn a long-term trend. Mitigation reduces the causes, such as emissions; adaptation reduces exposure or vulnerability to impacts. Insulating a building may reduce energy demand, while improving flood drainage can reduce flood risk. Solar and wind generation have low operational emissions but vary with conditions; planning can combine storage, transmission, demand management, and other resources. An energy comparison should consider lifecycle impacts, affordability, reliability, and who bears environmental burdens.",B.b6i,"A campus proposes rooftop solar and a heat-response plan. 1. Solar can reduce fossil-fuel electricity demand when it displaces it. 2. The heat plan reduces health risk during extreme heat. 3. These address mitigation and adaptation respectively. 4. Measure energy output and access to cooling support instead of claiming all climate risk is removed.","A week is unusually cool. Does this disprove long-term warming? Explain which evidence is relevant.","No. A short local weather event cannot establish or reverse a global long-term trend. Compare long-term measurements, spatial coverage, and uncertainty.","Do not confuse ozone depletion with the greenhouse effect, treat one storm as the entire climate record, or claim any energy source has zero lifecycle impact.","Use long-term evidence, distinguish mitigation from adaptation, and compare energy systems across environmental and social criteria.",B.aKK)
B.aW6=s(["Assess a scientific claim using sources, methods, and uncertainty.","Distinguish personal data, consent, and responsible data handling.","Evaluate benefits and limitations of AI and other emerging technologies."],t.s)
B.bh5={Claim:0,"Evidence questions":1,"Governance questions":2}
B.b6v=new A.ao(B.bh5,["A post says an AI system is accurate for every patient.","Which population, comparison, error types, and independent validation?","Who is accountable, which data are needed, and how can errors be corrected?"],t.w)
B.aVT=s(["What is a first step when checking a viral scientific claim?","Trace it to an original study or reliable official record","Count emojis only","Trust the longest headline","Assume every repost independently verifies it","Original evidence helps assess what was actually studied."],t.s)
B.aOQ=s(["Three satisfied buyers are what kind of evidence?","A small set of testimonials","A guaranteed randomized trial","A complete population census","Proof for all users","Testimonials have selection and comparison limitations."],t.s)
B.aVO=s(["Can AI generate a nonexistent citation?","Yes","No, never","Only when offline","Only if the article is old","Generated text can include plausible but fabricated references."],t.s)
B.aSn=s(["What should be done before citing an AI-provided article?","Verify it in reliable publication records","Cite it because it sounds formal","Invent a page number","Assume the author approved it","Citation details must correspond to an actual source."],t.s)
B.aAA=s(["What is data minimization?","Collecting only data necessary for a stated legitimate purpose","Collecting all available data forever","Removing all security controls","Publishing everyone's records","Minimization limits unnecessary collection and exposure."],t.s)
B.aEC=s(["Why examine a study's comparison group?","It helps distinguish an intervention effect from alternatives","It replaces measurement","It guarantees no bias of any kind","It makes consent unnecessary","A comparison can reveal changes that would happen without the intervention."],t.s)
B.aUZ=s(["What is a conflict of interest worth checking?","The study sponsor profits from a positive result","The study has a title","The report uses punctuation","The data have units","Financial interests can affect study design or interpretation and should be disclosed."],t.s)
B.aJR=s(["Who remains accountable for using an AI recommendation?","The responsible people and institutions","No one","Only the keyboard","Only a fictional author","Using a tool does not remove human and institutional responsibility."],t.s)
B.aTI=s(["Does public visibility of personal data automatically justify every reuse?","No","Yes, always","Only if copied quickly","Only if many people repost it","Purpose, rights, and applicable law still matter."],t.s)
B.avq=s(["An AI system has high average accuracy. What else should be examined?","Error types, affected groups, and deployment conditions","Only its logo","Only its number of users","Nothing else","An average can hide harmful errors or poor performance in some contexts."],t.s)
B.aSD=s(["Which supports a responsible conclusion?","State what the evidence supports and what remains uncertain","Promise certainty from weak evidence","Hide contradictory results","Replace methods with testimonials","Transparent limitations improve scientific and public reasoning."],t.s)
B.aSf=s([B.aVT,B.aOQ,B.aVO,B.aSn,B.aAA,B.aEC,B.aUZ,B.aJR,B.aTI,B.avq,B.aSD],t.E)
B.bJ0=new A.he("Information, Emerging Technology, and Public Evidence",B.aW6,"The information age changes how knowledge is produced, shared, and contested. A scientific claim should be traced to its original study or official record where possible. Ask who made it, what evidence supports it, how the study was conducted, and whether independent evidence agrees. A testimonial is not equivalent to a controlled comparison. Artificial intelligence can identify patterns and generate useful drafts, but outputs can contain errors, biased associations, or fabricated citations. Human accountability and verification remain necessary. Digital systems also collect personal information. Responsible practice uses a legitimate purpose, appropriate consent or other lawful basis, minimal necessary data, secure storage, and clear retention rules. Access to information is valuable, but public availability does not automatically justify every reuse of a person's data.",B.b6v,"An online post claims a device improves memory because 3 buyers liked it. 1. Identify the evidence as a small testimonial sample. 2. Check for a comparison group, measurement method, and conflicts of interest. 3. Seek stronger independent studies. 4. Report uncertainty rather than treating popularity as proof.","An AI response lists a journal article that cannot be found. What should a student do before citing it?","Verify the title, authors, journal, and identifier against reliable records. If unverified, do not cite it as a real source; seek an authentic source and disclose relevant tool use.","Do not assume fluent writing proves truth, an official-looking logo proves authenticity, or publicly visible personal information is unrestricted.","Trace evidence, check methods, protect data, and retain human responsibility for technological decisions.",B.aSf)
B.b15=s([B.bJ1,B.bIZ,B.bJa,B.bJ_,B.bJ9,B.bJ0],t.Qy)
B.aXp=s(["Identify purpose, audience, channel, and feedback in a message.","Revise communication to remove ambiguity and access barriers.","Apply respect and accuracy when communicating across differences."],t.s)
B.biN={Purpose:0,"Audience and access":1,"Action and time":2}
B.b7B=new A.ao(B.biN,["Explain a temporary closure and available renewal options.","Borrowers include students with and without internet access.","Renew before 4 p.m. on Friday; normal hours resume Saturday."],t.w)
B.aHW=s(["Campus notice: The library will close at 4 p.m. on Friday for electrical maintenance. Borrowers may renew books through the portal before 4 p.m. Students without internet access may ask the service desk to renew books before closing. Normal hours resume on Saturday.\n\nWhat is the notice's main purpose?","Explain a temporary closure and renewal options","Advertise a permanent closure","Cancel all borrowing","Announce a new degree program","The notice gives closure timing and practical options."],t.s)
B.aNB=s(["Campus notice: The library will close at 4 p.m. on Friday for electrical maintenance. Borrowers may renew books through the portal before 4 p.m. Students without internet access may ask the service desk to renew books before closing. Normal hours resume on Saturday.\n\nWhen does the library close on Friday?","4 p.m.","4 a.m.","Midnight","Saturday morning","The first sentence explicitly states 4 p.m. Friday."],t.s)
B.aHw=s(["Campus notice: The library will close at 4 p.m. on Friday for electrical maintenance. Borrowers may renew books through the portal before 4 p.m. Students without internet access may ask the service desk to renew books before closing. Normal hours resume on Saturday.\n\nWhich option serves borrowers without internet?","Ask the service desk before closing","Use only the portal after closing","Wait until every book is overdue","Contact an unnamed seller","The notice includes a non-internet alternative."],t.s)
B.aCJ=s(["What is feedback in a communication process?","A response that helps check understanding","Only the original sender's message","A broken microphone","An unrelated poster color","Feedback returns information about receipt or interpretation."],t.s)
B.aTG=s(["What is semantic noise?","An unfamiliar term that obstructs understanding","Only a loud engine","Only a lost internet cable","Only a dark room","Semantic noise concerns meaning rather than physical sound alone."],t.s)
B.aTi=s(["Which deadline is clearest?","Before 4 p.m. on Friday","Soon","Later","Whenever possible without a date","A specific day and time guide action."],t.s)
B.aHY=s(["Which practice supports intercultural communication?","Clarify meaning respectfully instead of relying on stereotypes","Assume accent determines intelligence","Dismiss unfamiliar expressions without asking","Use insults to demand agreement","Clarification and respect reduce misunderstanding."],t.s)
B.aGF=s(["Which is ethical source use?","Acknowledge borrowed information and represent it accurately","Invent a supporting quotation","Hide the source to appear original","Change findings to suit a claim","Attribution and accurate representation support trust."],t.s)
B.aKw=s(["Campus notice: The library will close at 4 p.m. on Friday for electrical maintenance. Borrowers may renew books through the portal before 4 p.m. Students without internet access may ask the service desk to renew books before closing. Normal hours resume on Saturday.\n\nWhen do normal hours resume?","Saturday","Friday at noon","Monday only","Never","The final sentence states Saturday."],t.s)
B.aCX=s(['Which revision best replaces "Do it online soon"?',"Renew books through the portal before 4 p.m. Friday","Deal with things sometime","Click anything","Ignore the message","The revision specifies action, channel, and deadline."],t.s)
B.aO4=s(["What would best check whether a notice is understood?","Ask readers to describe the required action in their own words","Count only the printed copies","Assume sending guarantees understanding","Remove every contact option","A comprehension check tests interpretation rather than delivery alone."],t.s)
B.aKB=s([B.aHW,B.aNB,B.aHw,B.aCJ,B.aTG,B.aTi,B.aHY,B.aGF,B.aKw,B.aCX,B.aO4],t.E)
B.bJe=new A.he("Purpose, Audience, and Ethical Communication",B.aXp,"Purposive communication selects language and media for a particular audience, situation, and goal. A message is not successful merely because it was sent: receivers must be able to access and interpret it. Feedback allows clarification. Noise can be physical, technical, or semantic, such as an unfamiliar term. Ethical communicators avoid deception, acknowledge sources, and represent others without stereotypes. In multilingual Philippine settings, plain language, translation where appropriate, and multiple accessible channels can support participation. Accent differences do not by themselves establish competence or credibility. Consider this original notice:\n\nCampus notice: The library will close at 4 p.m. on Friday for electrical maintenance. Borrowers may renew books through the portal before 4 p.m. Students without internet access may ask the service desk to renew books before closing. Normal hours resume on Saturday.",B.b7B,'Revise "The library closes early; do it online." 1. Identify missing information: date, time, action, and alternative access. 2. Replace vague "it" with "renew books." 3. Give Friday, 4 p.m., and the service-desk alternative. 4. Ask a reader to explain the required action to check understanding.',"Write a two-sentence reminder for borrowers who cannot use the portal. Include the action and deadline.","If you cannot access the portal, ask the library service desk to renew your books before 4 p.m. on Friday. Normal library hours resume on Saturday.",'Do not assume everyone has internet access, use an unspecified "soon" as a deadline, or infer ability from accent or language background.',"A clear, ethical message states who should do what, when, why, and through an accessible channel.",B.aKB)
B.aIR=s(["Distinguish a source's claim, evidence, and limitation.","Separate what a report states from unsupported inference.","Summarize quantitative evidence accurately for an audience."],t.s)
B.bhb={"Claim actually supported":0,Qualification:1,"Claim not established":2}
B.b7n=new A.ao(B.bhb,["48 of 80 respondents preferred printed notes.","One class, voluntary participation; preference only.","Printed notes improve every student's test score."],t.w)
B.aP8=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nWhat was measured?","Reported preference for notes","A controlled change in exam scores","Every university student's income","Printer electricity consumption","The report explicitly concerns preference."],t.s)
B.b3i=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nHow many respondents preferred printed notes?","48","80","32","60","The report states 48 of 80."],t.s)
B.aTZ=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nWhat percentage preferred printed notes?","60%","48%","80%","32%","48/80 x 100 = 60%."],t.s)
B.azU=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nWhat limits campus-wide generalization?","The survey used volunteers from one class","The report has sentences","The number 80 is even","The title uses English","The sampling process may exclude different student experiences."],t.s)
B.aFF=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nWhich claim is unsupported?","Printed notes improve every student's grades","48 respondents preferred printed notes","Participation was voluntary","The authors suggest surveying more classes","No grade or performance effect was measured."],t.s)
B.aQf=s(["During a talk, which note-taking method supports accuracy?","Separate the speaker's claim from your interpretation","Write your opinion as a direct quotation","Ignore all qualifiers","Record only applause","Distinguishing source content from response prevents misattribution."],t.s)
B.aC2=s(["Which is a focused clarification question for the practice report?","How were survey volunteers recruited?","Why is every student identical?","Can you guarantee all paper is perfect?","What color is your pen?","Recruitment affects interpretation of the sample."],t.s)
B.aCQ=s(['What is the role of a qualifier such as "in one class"?',"It limits the scope of the claim","It proves every campus agrees","It removes the need for evidence","It changes preference into test scores","Qualifiers define the population or conditions."],t.s)
B.aJ8=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nWhich summary is most accurate?","Most respondents in one class preferred print; broader research is needed","All students learn only from paper","The report banned digital notes","Printed notes doubled exam scores","The summary preserves both finding and limitation."],t.s)
B.atX=s(["Does a claim become independently verified when the same report is reposted many times?","No","Yes, automatically","Only after ten shares","Only if a headline is capitalized","Repeated distribution is not new independent evidence."],t.s)
B.az3=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nWhat comparison would address learning performance more directly?","A suitable study measuring learning outcomes under specified conditions","Only another preference slogan","Only counting reposts","Changing preferred to proven","Performance requires actual outcome measurements and an appropriate design."],t.s)
B.aDJ=s([B.aP8,B.b3i,B.aTZ,B.azU,B.aFF,B.aQf,B.aC2,B.aCQ,B.aJ8,B.atX,B.az3],t.E)
B.bJ5=new A.he("Critical Reading and Evidence-Based Listening",B.aIR,'Critical reading and listening involve identifying a claim, asking what evidence supports it, and checking the limits of that evidence. Listen or read for qualifiers such as "in one class," "may," and "before recommending." A preference survey does not automatically measure learning performance. A faithful summary preserves the population, result, and qualification rather than selecting an impressive number in isolation. During a talk, record the speaker\'s claim separately from your response; ask focused questions rather than attributing words the speaker did not use. Use the following original report for practice:\n\nPractice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.',B.b7n,'Evaluate "The survey proves paper improves grades." 1. Identify the measured variable: preference. 2. Identify the sample: volunteers from one class. 3. Note that grades were not measured. 4. Revise: "In this survey, 60% preferred printed notes; the result does not establish a performance effect."',"Write a one-sentence summary including the result and an important limit. Then suggest one clarification question.","In a voluntary survey of 80 students from one class, 48 preferred printed notes, but the result does not establish learning performance or represent the entire campus. A useful question is: How were respondents recruited?",'Do not replace "preferred" with "performed better," omit the sample limits, or equate repetition of a claim with independent evidence.',"Identify what was measured, whom it describes, and what cannot responsibly be concluded.",B.aDJ)
B.aJ1=s(["Distinguish summary, paraphrase, and direct quotation.","Integrate a source without changing its meaning.","Use attribution and verifiable references to avoid plagiarism."],t.s)
B.biF={Summary:0,Paraphrase:1,Attribution:2}
B.b5S=new A.ao(B.biF,["A limited class survey favored printed notes but did not measure performance.","Of the 80 volunteers, 48 said they would rather use printed notes.","Identify the practice report; do not invent a real journal citation."],t.w)
B.aX3=s(["What does a summary normally do?","Condenses a source's main ideas","Copies every sentence verbatim","Invents a stronger result","Hides all source limitations","Summary selects central ideas while preserving meaning."],t.s)
B.atc=s(["What distinguishes a direct quotation?","It reproduces exact wording and marks it as quoted","It never needs attribution","It always changes every word","It invents page numbers","Readers must be able to distinguish exact borrowed words."],t.s)
B.azq=s(["Does a paraphrase of a borrowed idea need attribution?","Yes","No, never","Only if printed","Only if it contains numbers","Borrowed ideas require acknowledgment even with new wording."],t.s)
B.az_=s(["Which is an inadequate paraphrase technique?","Changing a few words while keeping the source structure","Understanding the idea first","Writing from understanding","Checking that meaning is preserved","Synonym swapping can remain too close to the source."],t.s)
B.aZR=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nWhich paraphrase preserves the measured variable?","Respondents reported which note format they liked","Respondents proved paper raised grades","The report measured every student's intelligence","The study recorded printer speed","Preference concerns liking, not performance or unrelated outcomes."],t.s)
B.apB=s(["What should a student do if a source page number is unknown?","Locate it when required rather than inventing one","Guess a plausible number","Use the current year as a page","Copy a number from another source","Citation details must be genuine and verifiable."],t.s)
B.azF=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nWhich summary retains a key limit?","A voluntary one-class survey favored print among its respondents","All students everywhere need paper","Digital notes never work","Every respondent chose print","It preserves both finding and scope."],t.s)
B.asf=s(["How should this original classroom passage be identified?","As the practice report provided in the lesson","As a fabricated international journal article","As a quotation from an invented professor","As a national census without evidence","A source should be described truthfully."],t.s)
B.axw=s(["Which action is plagiarism?","Presenting another person's work as your own without acknowledgment","Quoting with proper attribution","Summarizing and crediting the source","Citing a real verified source","Plagiarism misrepresents authorship or source use."],t.s)
B.aUY=s(["What is a useful final paraphrase check?","Compare accuracy and wording with the source","Remove all attribution","Make the result more dramatic","Delete every limitation","A paraphrase must be both meaning-preserving and independently worded."],t.s)
B.aTR=s(["Practice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.\n\nWhich recommendation is faithful?","Gather evidence from more classes before campus-wide changes","Immediately prohibit all digital notes","Stop all research permanently","Treat the class as the entire country","The report recommends broader evidence before policy changes."],t.s)
B.aJ5=s([B.aX3,B.atc,B.azq,B.az_,B.aZR,B.apB,B.azF,B.asf,B.axw,B.aUY,B.aTR],t.E)
B.bJ4=new A.he("Academic Summary, Paraphrase, and Attribution",B.aJ1,"A summary condenses the main ideas of a longer source. A paraphrase expresses a particular idea in substantially new wording and sentence structure while preserving meaning. A direct quotation reproduces exact words and marks them as quoted. All three require source acknowledgment when the idea or wording is borrowed. Replacing a few words with synonyms is not a sufficient paraphrase. Record the author or organization, date, title, and location needed by the required citation style. Never invent authors, page numbers, quotations, or study results. When a practice passage has no external author, label it as a classroom passage rather than fabricating a journal. Use this original classroom passage:\n\nPractice report: In a survey of 80 first-year students from one class, 48 said they preferred printed notes. Participation was voluntary. The report describes preference, not test performance. The authors recommend surveying more classes before changing campus-wide services.",B.b5S,'Paraphrase "The report describes preference, not test performance." 1. Understand the distinction. 2. Set the sentence aside. 3. Write: "The survey concerns which format respondents liked, not how well they scored." 4. Compare meanings and attribute the practice report.',"Paraphrase the recommendation to survey more classes before changing services. Keep its cautious meaning.","The practice report advises gathering responses from additional classes before making campus-wide service changes. This retains the recommendation without claiming that a change has already been justified.","Do not remove qualifications, copy sentence structure with only synonym swaps, or assume paraphrased borrowed ideas need no attribution.","Understand first, rewrite independently, preserve the claim's limits, and credit the real source.",B.aJ5)
B.aDu=s(["Write a concise message with a clear request and relevant context.","Adapt tone and organization for a professional audience.","Record meeting decisions with actions, owners, and deadlines."],t.s)
B.biE={Subject:0,"Request and alternative":1,"Action record":2}
B.b6F=new A.ao(B.biE,["Request to reschedule consultation on 12 August.","Move 2 p.m. to 3 p.m., or use another available office hour.","Mika will confirm the agreed time with the group after the professor replies."],t.w)
B.az1=s(["Subject: Request to reschedule consultation on 12 August\nDear Professor Santos,\nOur group has a required laboratory session during our 2 p.m. consultation on 12 August. May we meet at 3 p.m. instead? We can also attend during your next available office hour. Please let us know which option is suitable. Thank you.\nRespectfully,\nMika Cruz, Group 2\n\nWhat is the main request?","Move the consultation to 3 p.m. or another suitable office hour","Cancel the degree program","Request a final grade change","Reserve a library book","The message asks to reschedule a consultation."],t.s)
B.aZt=s(["Subject: Request to reschedule consultation on 12 August\nDear Professor Santos,\nOur group has a required laboratory session during our 2 p.m. consultation on 12 August. May we meet at 3 p.m. instead? We can also attend during your next available office hour. Please let us know which option is suitable. Thank you.\nRespectfully,\nMika Cruz, Group 2\n\nWhy does the group request a change?","A required laboratory session conflicts with the original time","The professor already canceled every class","The library closed permanently","The group has no coursework","The message states a laboratory conflict."],t.s)
B.aAZ=s(["Subject: Request to reschedule consultation on 12 August\nDear Professor Santos,\nOur group has a required laboratory session during our 2 p.m. consultation on 12 August. May we meet at 3 p.m. instead? We can also attend during your next available office hour. Please let us know which option is suitable. Thank you.\nRespectfully,\nMika Cruz, Group 2\n\nHas the professor approved 3 p.m. in this message?","No; the group is requesting approval","Yes, explicitly","Yes, because the subject names a date","Approval is unnecessary in every situation","A request does not establish agreement."],t.s)
B.azD=s(["Which subject line is most informative?","Request to reschedule consultation on 12 August","Hello","Important things","Read now!!!","It identifies the action and event date."],t.s)
B.aEw=s(["What belongs in an actionable meeting record?","Task, responsible person, and deadline","Only a list of jokes","Every unrelated comment","Only the room color","An action record supports follow-through."],t.s)
B.aSm=s(["Which is the clearest action item?","Ben: Send the draft to Ana by 5 p.m. Thursday","Someone should do it soon","Draft stuff","Things will happen","It specifies owner, task, recipient, and time."],t.s)
B.aGP=s(["What should an application document claim?","Genuine relevant skills and experience","Invented degrees","Unverified awards as facts","Another person's work as your own","Professional persuasion must remain truthful."],t.s)
B.b2G=s(["Which tone is appropriately professional?","Clear, respectful, and specific","Threatening without reason","Deliberately ambiguous","Insulting unfamiliar readers","Clarity and respect support cooperation."],t.s)
B.aYv=s(["Subject: Request to reschedule consultation on 12 August\nDear Professor Santos,\nOur group has a required laboratory session during our 2 p.m. consultation on 12 August. May we meet at 3 p.m. instead? We can also attend during your next available office hour. Please let us know which option is suitable. Thank you.\nRespectfully,\nMika Cruz, Group 2\n\nWho sends the example email?","Mika Cruz, Group 2","Professor Santos","The library vendor","An unnamed dean","The closing identifies the sender."],t.s)
B.apN=s(["Why limit unrelated personal details in a work email?","They can distract and disclose unnecessary private information","Every detail must always be public","Privacy never matters in email","More unrelated detail always improves clarity","Include information relevant to the professional purpose."],t.s)
B.aFj=s(["After a consultation change is approved, what is a useful next step?","Confirm the agreed date and time with all affected group members","Assume nobody needs the update","Delete the only record immediately","Announce an unapproved different time","Shared confirmation prevents coordination errors."],t.s)
B.aWP=s([B.az1,B.aZt,B.aAZ,B.azD,B.aEw,B.aSm,B.aGP,B.b2G,B.aYv,B.apN,B.aFj],t.E)
B.bJd=new A.he("Professional Correspondence and Collaborative Work",B.aDu,"Professional correspondence helps people act on reliable information. A useful email has a meaningful subject, an appropriate greeting, concise context, a specific request, and a courteous closing. Respectful language need not be vague or excessively long. Include dates and times, but disclose only personal details necessary for the purpose. Collaboration also requires clear records: minutes summarize decisions and responsibilities rather than every spoken word. An action item should say what will be done, by whom, and by when. Application documents should describe genuine skills and experience; inventing a credential is not persuasive professional communication. Read this original example:\n\nSubject: Request to reschedule consultation on 12 August\nDear Professor Santos,\nOur group has a required laboratory session during our 2 p.m. consultation on 12 August. May we meet at 3 p.m. instead? We can also attend during your next available office hour. Please let us know which option is suitable. Thank you.\nRespectfully,\nMika Cruz, Group 2",B.b6F,'Improve "Can we move it? We are busy." 1. Name the event and date. 2. Briefly explain the schedule conflict. 3. Suggest a specific alternative without assuming approval. 4. Ask for confirmation and close respectfully.',"A group agrees that Ben will send the draft to Ana by 5 p.m. on Thursday. Write one action item for the minutes.","Ben: Send the draft to Ana by 5 p.m. Thursday. This records the owner, action, recipient, and deadline.","Do not treat a request as approval, omit the date, bury the action under unrelated personal details, or invent qualifications in an application.","State context, action, owner, and time; use an audience-appropriate tone and accurate information.",B.aWP)
B.b1V=s(["Organize a presentation around a claim, evidence, and action.","Use visuals that clarify rather than distort information.","Provide accessible spoken and visual explanations."],t.s)
B.bhk={"Problem and evidence":0,Recommendation:1,Evaluation:2}
B.b6y=new A.ao(B.bhk,["120 bottles were counted in selected bins during one week.","Test two accessible refill stations in a four-week pilot.","Track use, costs, bottle counts, and water quality rather than promise guaranteed success."],t.w)
B.aEc=s(["Proposal: Install labeled refill stations near two busy classrooms. A one-week audit recorded 120 disposable bottles in the nearby bins. The audit covered these bins only. A four-week pilot will track refill use, maintenance costs, and bottle counts. Stations must be accessible and water quality must be checked before use.\n\nWhat does the audit count establish?","120 bottles were recorded in the selected bins during one week","Every campus bin contains exactly 120 bottles daily","Refill stations already eliminated all waste","All students oppose drinking water","The evidence has a specific place and time scope."],t.s)
B.azO=s(["Proposal: Install labeled refill stations near two busy classrooms. A one-week audit recorded 120 disposable bottles in the nearby bins. The audit covered these bins only. A four-week pilot will track refill use, maintenance costs, and bottle counts. Stations must be accessible and water quality must be checked before use.\n\nHow long is the proposed pilot?","Four weeks","One day","One year","Ten years","The proposal explicitly specifies four weeks."],t.s)
B.aYU=s(["Which presentation structure is most coherent?","Problem, evidence and limits, recommendation","Unrelated images followed by a hidden request","Only a conclusion with no support","Only background music","A logical sequence connects the reason to the proposed action."],t.s)
B.aQ_=s(["Which helps a listener who cannot see a graph?","Describe the important data aloud",'Say only "as you can see"',"Remove all labels","Use color without text","Spoken description makes visual evidence accessible."],t.s)
B.aIr=s(["What should a graph include?","Labeled variables, units, and a non-misleading scale","Only decorative effects","No source or context","A hidden axis","Readers need a fair representation of the data."],t.s)
B.aRm=s(["Why are video captions useful?","They support access when audio cannot be heard or understood","They prove every claim is true","They replace all source checking","They make context unnecessary","Captions improve access to spoken information."],t.s)
B.aT_=s(["Proposal: Install labeled refill stations near two busy classrooms. A one-week audit recorded 120 disposable bottles in the nearby bins. The audit covered these bins only. A four-week pilot will track refill use, maintenance costs, and bottle counts. Stations must be accessible and water quality must be checked before use.\n\nWhich condition is required before station use?","Check water quality","Skip maintenance planning","Assume any water is safe","Ignore accessibility","The proposal explicitly requires water-quality checks."],t.s)
B.aQJ=s(["Which slide title is most accurate?","120 bottles counted in selected bins in one week","Our campus wastes exactly 120 bottles every day forever","Pilot already proves success","All waste has ended","The title retains the measured scope."],t.s)
B.aCp=s(["Why should a presenter rehearse with a listener?","To check timing, clarity, and comprehension","To avoid all questions permanently","To replace evidence with confidence","To guarantee unanimous agreement","A listener can reveal confusing transitions or explanations."],t.s)
B.aLT=s(["Proposal: Install labeled refill stations near two busy classrooms. A one-week audit recorded 120 disposable bottles in the nearby bins. The audit covered these bins only. A four-week pilot will track refill use, maintenance costs, and bottle counts. Stations must be accessible and water quality must be checked before use.\n\nWhich is an appropriate final request?","Approve a monitored four-week pilot and review its results","Declare permanent success without testing","Stop checking water quality","Remove all maintenance funding","The recommendation matches the proposed evidence-gathering plan."],t.s)
B.b2X=s(["What does multimodal coherence mean?","Words and visuals support the same purposeful message","Every slide has unrelated animation","Sound contradicts every chart","Decorations replace explanations","Modes should work together to improve understanding."],t.s)
B.ax9=s([B.aEc,B.azO,B.aYU,B.aQ_,B.aIr,B.aRm,B.aT_,B.aQJ,B.aCp,B.aLT,B.b2X],t.E)
B.bJ7=new A.he("Evidence-Based Presentations and Multimodal Messages",B.b1V,"A purposeful presentation connects a claim to evidence and a realistic action. State the problem, explain the relevant evidence and its limits, consider alternatives, then give a concrete recommendation. Multimodal communication combines words, images, sound, or movement. Each mode should help the audience understand the same message rather than compete for attention. Label graphs, show fair scales, describe important visuals aloud, provide captions for video, and avoid using color as the only signal. Rehearsal should test timing and comprehension, not only memorization. Read the proposal:\n\nProposal: Install labeled refill stations near two busy classrooms. A one-week audit recorded 120 disposable bottles in the nearby bins. The audit covered these bins only. A four-week pilot will track refill use, maintenance costs, and bottle counts. Stations must be accessible and water quality must be checked before use.",B.b6y,"Build a three-part presentation from the proposal. 1. Open with the local bottle-count problem. 2. Explain the limited audit and pilot plan using a labeled count chart. 3. Request approval for the pilot and a review date. Describe the chart aloud and retain the audit limitation.","Write a slide title that states the evidence without exaggeration, then give one accessibility improvement.",'Title: "120 disposable bottles counted in selected bins during one week." Add a clear text description of the graph and do not rely only on red and green bars.',"Do not turn a local bin count into a campus-wide yearly claim, use an unlabeled truncated scale, or place essential information only in an image.","Unify claim, evidence, visual design, accessibility, and a feasible request.",B.ax9)
B.aEL=s(["Formulate a focused question and proportionate recommendation.","Integrate evidence, counterarguments, and uncertainty.","Plan an ethical evaluation with measurable outcomes."],t.s)
B.bim={"Focused question":0,Counterargument:1,"Evaluation response":2}
B.b7w=new A.ao(B.bim,["Does the four-week pilot reduce bottle counts in the same selected bins?","Maintenance may cost more than expected.","Record costs and use alongside comparable bottle counts, and report limitations."],t.w)
B.axC=s(["Which is a focused research question for the case?","How do bottle counts in the selected bins change during the four-week pilot?","Is everything good?","Why is all technology perfect?","Can one station solve every environmental problem?","It specifies an outcome, place, and period."],t.s)
B.aCa=s(["Proposal: Install labeled refill stations near two busy classrooms. A one-week audit recorded 120 disposable bottles in the nearby bins. The audit covered these bins only. A four-week pilot will track refill use, maintenance costs, and bottle counts. Stations must be accessible and water quality must be checked before use.\n\nWhich outcome should be measured?","Refill use, costs, and comparable bottle counts","Only the poster color","Only the number of slogans","Only the presenter's confidence","The proposal names relevant practical outcomes."],t.s)
B.aIc=s(["Which is a fair counterargument?","Maintenance costs may be higher than expected","Everyone who disagrees is foolish","Evidence should be banned","Water quality can never matter","A cost concern addresses the proposal rather than attacking people."],t.s)
B.azS=s(["What is cherry-picking?","Selecting favorable evidence while hiding relevant contrary evidence","Reporting all relevant findings","Stating limitations","Checking a source","Cherry-picking distorts the overall evidential picture."],t.s)
B.aPn=s(["Which recommendation is proportionate to the available evidence?","Run and evaluate a limited pilot","Declare universal success immediately","Guarantee zero waste nationwide","Claim the audit measured every school","A small local audit supports investigation, not sweeping certainty."],t.s)
B.b0g=s(["Why define success criteria before collecting results?","To reduce selective reinterpretation after seeing the data","To guarantee a favorable result","To avoid measuring anything","To suppress unexpected findings","Predefined outcomes support transparent evaluation."],t.s)
B.aAb=s(["Which practice protects research participants?","Collect only necessary information with appropriate permissions","Publish names without need","Hide the data purpose","Demand unrelated private records","Ethical data use limits collection and protects privacy."],t.s)
B.aQD=s(["Does a simple before-and-after decrease prove the station alone caused it?","No; other changes may contribute","Yes, always","Only if the chart is colorful","Only if no limitations are reported","Timing alone does not rule out alternative explanations."],t.s)
B.ayK=s(["Proposal: Install labeled refill stations near two busy classrooms. A one-week audit recorded 120 disposable bottles in the nearby bins. The audit covered these bins only. A four-week pilot will track refill use, maintenance costs, and bottle counts. Stations must be accessible and water quality must be checked before use.\n\nWhich evidence limitation should be disclosed?","The baseline audit covered selected bins for one week","The audit covered the entire country","The pilot already lasted decades","Every cost is known with certainty","The original evidence has a limited sampling frame and duration."],t.s)
B.aNC=s(["Which response best addresses a critic asking about access?","Explain the accessibility plan and invite practical feedback","Insult the critic","Remove the access requirement","Claim criticism proves failure","Evidence-based advocacy engages relevant concerns."],t.s)
B.aEv=s(["What should a final evaluation report include?","Methods, results, limitations, and a justified next step","Only positive slogans","Only names of supporters","Fabricated improvement percentages","A responsible report allows readers to judge the evidence."],t.s)
B.aHI=s([B.axC,B.aCa,B.aIc,B.azS,B.aPn,B.b0g,B.aAb,B.aQD,B.ayK,B.aNC,B.aEv],t.E)
B.bJ3=new A.he("Research Communication and Responsible Advocacy",B.aEL,"Research communication helps an audience judge a question, evidence, and recommended action. A focused question specifies the setting, issue, and outcome; a broad slogan does not. An advocacy message can support change while remaining honest about uncertainty. Distinguish a current finding from a prediction, and a proposed pilot from a proven intervention. Address strong counterarguments such as cost, access, and maintenance rather than attacking people who disagree. Collect only necessary data, obtain appropriate permission, and protect participants. An evaluation should define what success would look like before results are known. Use this original case:\n\nProposal: Install labeled refill stations near two busy classrooms. A one-week audit recorded 120 disposable bottles in the nearby bins. The audit covered these bins only. A four-week pilot will track refill use, maintenance costs, and bottle counts. Stations must be accessible and water quality must be checked before use.",B.b7w,'Revise "Refill stations will solve all waste." 1. Narrow the claim to the selected location and pilot period. 2. State the current evidence and its limited scope. 3. Add cost, access, and water-quality criteria. 4. Recommend a monitored trial, not an unmeasured guarantee.',"Write a focused evaluation question and one counterargument for the refill-station proposal.","Question: Over four weeks, how do bottle counts in the selected bins change compared with a comparable baseline? Counterargument: Maintenance and water-testing costs may be substantial. Record those costs rather than dismissing the concern.","Do not cherry-pick only favorable data, attack critics instead of their reasoning, or claim a before-and-after change proves causation without considering other factors.","Advocate with a focused question, honest evidence, fair counterarguments, and a measurable evaluation plan.",B.aHI)
B.auw=s([B.bJe,B.bJ5,B.bJ4,B.bJd,B.bJ7,B.bJ3],t.Qy)
B.oI=new A.ao(B.wj,[B.aPM,B.b15,B.auw],A.aC("ao<j,V<he>>"))
