B.c8D=new A.H2(2,"up")
B.c2G=new A.nM(B.c8D)
B.c8E=new A.H2(3,"down")
B.c2H=new A.nM(B.c8E)
B.b5X=new A.cn([B.i1,B.c2G,B.i0,B.c2H,B.hZ,B.Vz,B.i_,B.Vy],t.Fp)
B.wi={g1:0,g2:1,g3:2,g4:3,g5:4,g6:5,g7:6,g8:7,g9:8,g10:9}
B.aZg=s(["Say a greeting.","Ask to join politely.","Name who is speaking."],t.s)
B.b3k=s(["Greeting|Hello starts Lina\u2019s talk.","Request|May I sit here? asks Ben.","Thanks|Thank you follows Ben\u2019s yes."],t.s)
B.aO5=s(["Who says Hello first?|Lina|Ben|A dog|A bird|The first line names Lina.","Which words tell Lina\u2019s name?|I am Lina.|Please sit.|Thank you.|Yes.|I am Lina names the speaker.","What does Lina ask to do?|Sit here|Run home|Get a cup|Read a book|Her question is May I sit here?","Who says Yes?|Ben|Lina|Mum|Dad|Ben answers Lina\u2019s question.","Which word asks kindly?|Please|Ben|Here|Am|Ben uses Please with sit.","Which line gives thanks?|Thank you.|I am Ben.|Hello!|May I sit here?|Lina thanks Ben after his help.","What should Ben do while Lina asks?|Listen|Shout over her|Walk off at once|Hide her seat|Taking turns means listening first.","Which mark ends Lina\u2019s question?|Question mark (?)|Full stop (.)|Comma (,)|Dash (-)|May I sit here? ends with a question mark.","You join this talk. Which start is kind?|Hello. I am Sam.|Go away, Ben!|Give me that seat!|I will not listen.|The first choice greets and names the new speaker.","Ben has just said Please sit. What fits next?|Thank you.|What is a fish?|Do not sit, Ben.|I am a tree.|Thanks fits the help Ben has given.","Which pair shows taking turns?|Lina asks; Ben answers.|Both shout at once.|Ben never listens.|Lina hides from Ben.|Their turns are a question and its answer."],t.s)
B.aai=new A.cg("Lina Says Hello","Q1 \xb7 self and family; oral interaction","Lina: Hello! I am Lina.\nBen: Hello, Lina. I am Ben.\nLina: May I sit here?\nBen: Yes. Please sit.\nLina: Thank you.",B.aZg,"A greeting starts a talk. Hello is a greeting. I am tells your name. Listen while your friend talks. Then take your turn. May I sit here? asks before sitting. Thank you shows care after help. You can first try these ideas in the language you use at home.","Who asks to sit? First find the question mark. Read the name before that line: Lina. So Lina asks. Ben answers Yes.","Take turns with a helper. Say your name. Ask to sit. What can you say after the helper says yes?","One talk is: Hello. I am Ana. May I sit here? The helper says yes. Ana says Thank you. Use your own name.","Do not talk over your friend. Please asks kindly. Thank you comes after help. They do not mean the same thing.","Say hello. Say your name. Ask kindly. Listen. Take turns.",B.b3k,B.aO5)
B.aDf=s(["Name a familiar thing.","Tell a thing\u2019s color.","Use my and we in a short talk."],t.s)
B.aOT=s(["Thing|Bag names what the speaker carries.","Color|Red tells about the speaker\u2019s bag.","Group|We includes the speaker and Mum."],t.s)
B.aNb=s(["What color is the speaker\u2019s bag?|Red|Blue|Green|Black|My bag is red tells its color.","What is in that bag?|A cup|A cat|A shoe|A ball|The third sentence puts a cup in the bag.","Who has a blue bag?|Mum|A dog|Ben|A bird|Mum has a blue bag.","Which word names a thing?|Cup|Red|Blue|My|A cup is a thing; red and blue are colors.","Which word tells belonging?|My|Is|Red|In|My bag belongs to the speaker.","Where do they take the bags?|Home|To the moon|To sea|Up a tree|The last line says home.","What does we mean here?|The speaker and Mum|Only the cup|Only Mum|No one|We includes the speaker with Mum.","Which sentence tells a color?|My bag is red.|We take our bags home.|This is my bag.|A cup is in my bag.|Red is the color word.","Which thing\u2019s color is not told?|The cup|The speaker\u2019s bag|Mum\u2019s bag|Both bags|The cup is named, but its color is not given.","Use the text. Which pair is right?|Mum \u2014 blue bag|Mum \u2014 red cup|Speaker \u2014 blue bag|Cup \u2014 green|Only Mum and blue bag match the stated words.","You hold your own green bag. Which line fits?|My bag is green.|My cup is Mum.|We is red.|A home is in my bag.|My shows your bag; green tells its color."],t.s)
B.aap=new A.cg("Our Red Bag","Q1 \xb7 familiar words and oral description","This is my bag. My bag is red. A cup is in my bag. Mum has a blue bag. We take our bags home.",B.aDf,"Bag and cup name things. Red and blue tell colors. My tells that a thing belongs to the speaker. We means the speaker and someone else. Say My bag is red. Point to a bag if you have one. Tell its real color. Your bag need not be red.","What is in the bag? First hear cup. Then hear in my bag. Put the words together: a cup is in the speaker\u2019s bag.","Find the two colors. Then tell a helper: Mum has a ___ bag.","Red and blue are the colors. Mum has a blue bag. Blue comes right before bag in the line about Mum.","Do not say the cup is red. The text tells the bag\u2019s color, not the cup\u2019s color. We is not just one person.","Name a thing. Add a color. Use my for belonging and we for a group.",B.aOT,B.aNb)
B.aq4=s(["Tell what a school sign asks.","Follow a short spoken direction.","Put three actions in order."],t.s)
B.axL=s(["Message|WASH HANDS tells an action.","Next|Then rinse comes after wash.","Last|Ana dries her hands after rinsing."],t.s)
B.aPT=s(["Where is the sign?|At the tap|On a kite|Under a bed|In a tree|The first line puts the sign at the tap.","What does the sign ask?|Wash hands|Read books|Feed cats|Shut bags|The sign says WASH HANDS.","Who asks for soap?|Ana|Jo|A cook|A guard|Ana\u2019s line asks May I use the soap?","What comes after wash in Jo\u2019s words?|Rinse|Sleep|Run|Draw|Jo says Wash, then rinse.","What does Ana do last?|Dry her hands|Get in bed|Wash a cup|Put on a hat|The final line says she dries her hands.","Which word tells the next step?|Then|May|At|The|Then points to the step after washing.","What helps take soap off hands?|Clean water for rinsing|A book|A red bag|A pencil|Rinse means use water to take off soap.","Which line is a question?|May I use the soap?|WASH HANDS.|Ana washes.|She rinses.|May I use the soap? asks for an answer.","Which order matches Ana\u2019s steps?|Wash, rinse, dry|Dry, wash, rinse|Rinse, dry, wash|Dry, rinse, wash|The final three sentences give wash, rinse, dry.","Jo has just said Yes. What may Ana use?|The soap|Jo\u2019s hat|A kite|A bell|Yes answers Ana\u2019s request for soap.","Which new sign would mean the same thing?|Clean your hands.|Eat your lunch.|Find your bag.|Close the door.|Clean your hands gives the same main action."],t.s)
B.aal=new A.cg("A Sign at School","Q2 \xb7 school; images, symbols and requests","At the tap is a sign: WASH HANDS.\nAna: May I use the soap?\nJo: Yes. Wash, then rinse.\nAna washes. She rinses. She dries her hands.",B.aq4,"A sign can give a message. WASH HANDS tells us what to do. Wash means clean with soap and water. Rinse means use clean water to take off soap. Then tells what comes next. A helper can read the sign aloud. Try saying each action as you point to it.","What comes after wash? Jo says Wash, then rinse. The word then links the steps. So rinse comes after wash.","Tell a helper the three things Ana does. Start with First.","First Ana washes. Next she rinses. Last she dries her hands. The last three short sentences show this order.","The sign does not say drink the soap. Rinse is not dry. Follow all the steps, not just the first one.","A sign has a message. Listen for action words and do steps in order.",B.axL,B.aPT)
B.b_7=s(["Name the people in a story.","Tell a problem and its end.","Say where a thing is found."],t.s)
B.aur=s(["Problem|Our ball is gone.","Place|Under the chair is where Tom finds it.","End|Tom gives the ball to Nia; she smiles."],t.s)
B.azY=s(["What is lost?|A ball|A cup|A hat|A book|The first sentence says the ball is gone.","Who looks by the desk?|Nia|Tom|Mum|Jo|Nia looks by the desk.","Does Nia see a ball there?|No|Yes, two balls|Yes, a red ball|Yes, a tiny ball|No ball! tells us she does not.","Where does Tom look?|Under the chair|On the roof|In a cup|By a tree|Tom looks under the chair.","Who finds the ball?|Tom|Nia|A bird|A cat|There is the ball follows Tom\u2019s look.","Who gets the ball from Tom?|Nia|Jo|Dad|Ben|Tom gives it to Nia.","What does Nia do at the end?|Smile|Cry|Sleep|Run away|The last line says Nia smiles.","Which word tells where?|Under|Smiles|Gives|Gone|Under tells the ball\u2019s place below the chair.","Which tells the story in order?|Lost, found, given back|Found, lost, eaten|Given back, lost, found|Lost, broken, thrown|The ball goes missing, Tom finds it, and returns it.","Why may Nia smile?|She has the ball back.|She has lost the desk.|Tom took her chair.|The ball is still gone.|The return of the ball gives a reason for her smile.","Which is a full short sentence about the end?|Nia smiles.|Under the.|The ball by.|Tom to.|Nia smiles tells who and what she does."],t.s)
B.aaX=new A.cg("The Lost Class Ball","Q2 \xb7 school stories and simple sentences","Our ball is gone. Nia looks by the desk. No ball! Tom looks under the chair. There is the ball. Tom gives it to Nia. Nia smiles.",B.b_7,"A story tells what happens. People in a story are its characters. Nia and Tom are the people here. A problem is something that needs help. The ball is gone. Under tells where the ball is. A full idea can be short: Nia smiles.","Did Nia find the ball by the desk? Read No ball! after her look. That means no. Next Tom looks under the chair and finds it.","Say what was wrong. Then say how the story ends.","The ball was lost. Tom found it under the chair. He gave it to Nia, and she smiled. The problem is fixed.","Do not put the ball on the chair. Under means below it. Nia looks first, but Tom finds the ball.","Name who is there. Tell what goes wrong. Tell how it is fixed.",B.aur,B.azY)
B.aI1=s(["Tell where people go.","Retell a short event.","Use a kind request in a community place."],t.s)
B.aY6=s(["Where|A fruit stall is where Lolo and the child go.","Request|May we have two? asks for bananas.","End|We walk home follows their thanks."],t.s)
B.aAs=s(["Who goes with the child?|Lolo|A teacher|A guard|A baby|The first line says Lolo and I.","Where do they go?|A fruit stall|A bus|A pond|A bedroom|They go to a fruit stall.","What fruit does the child see?|Bananas|Apples|Grapes|Mangoes|The child sees small bananas.","How many do they ask for?|Two|One|Five|Ten|Lolo asks May we have two?","Who gives the fruit?|The seller|A dog|The child|A driver|The seller gives the bananas.","What do they say after getting fruit?|Thank you.|Go away.|No books.|I am asleep.|The text gives their thanks.","How do they get home?|They walk.|They fly.|They swim.|They ride a boat.|The last line says walk home.","Which word tells about the bananas?|Small|Home|Seller|Go|Small describes the bananas.","Which request fits Lolo\u2019s turn?|May we have two bananas?|Give me all your hats!|Where is my fish?|I do not see a stall.|The polite request matches the fruit and number.","Which happens before they walk home?|They say Thank you.|They sail away.|They lose a bag.|They plant rice.|Thanks comes before the last walk.","What does us mean in gives us two bananas?|Lolo and the child|Only the seller|All the fruit|The stall|Us refers to the two people receiving fruit."],t.s)
B.aaU=new A.cg("At the Fruit Stall","Q3 \xb7 community; retelling and social language",'Lolo and I go to a fruit stall. I see small bananas. Lolo asks, "May we have two?" The seller gives us two bananas. We say, "Thank you." We walk home.',B.aI1,"A stall is a small place that sells things. A seller gives things people buy. Lolo asks with May we have two? This is a kind request. We includes Lolo and the child. First, next and last help us retell. Tell what happened, not a new event.","How many bananas do they get? First hear Lolo ask for two. Then hear the seller give two. Both lines agree: two bananas.","Retell with First we go to ___. Next we get ___. Last we ___.","First we go to a fruit stall. Next we get two bananas. Last we walk home. These are events from the text.","Lolo is a family name used here for a grandfather. A child may use a different name at home. Do not change bananas to apples when retelling this story.","Use kind words in the community. Retell where, what and the end.",B.aY6,B.aAs)
B.azt=s(["Tell what the words show.","Use a clue to make a sensible guess.","Say how someone helps."],t.s)
B.b0b=s(["Clue|Rain taps the roof.","Clue|The path is wet.","Reasoned guess|The umbrella helps both children stay dry."],t.s)
B.aMh=s(["What taps the roof?|Rain|A cat|A ball|A leaf|The first words are Rain taps the roof.","What is wet?|The path|A book|A cup|A bed|The text says The path is wet.","Where does Mila stand?|By the gate|In a boat|On a bed|In a tree|Mila stands by the gate.","What does Mila have?|An umbrella|A kite|A drum|A bowl|She has a big umbrella.","Who does she call?|Her little brother|A seller|A teacher|A driver|She tells her little brother Come under.","What does big tell about the umbrella?|Its size|Its color|Its price|Its name|Big describes size.","What does it mean in under it?|The umbrella|The roof\u2019s sound|The gate\u2019s lock|The path|They walk under the umbrella just mentioned.","How do the children go home?|They walk.|They swim.|They fly.|They crawl in a pipe.|The last line says they walk home.","Why does Mila share her umbrella?|To help keep her brother dry|To make the rain louder|To hide a fruit stall|To wet his book|Rain and wet ground support the reason of staying dry.","Which detail is not given?|The umbrella\u2019s color|The wet path|The rain|The brother|No color is named.","Which kind action matches Mila\u2019s words?|Making room under her umbrella|Pushing her brother into rain|Taking his shoes|Leaving him alone|Come under invites her brother to share the cover."],t.s)
B.aan=new A.cg("Rain at the Gate","Q4 \xb7 environment; observation and inference",'Rain taps the roof. The path is wet. Mila stands by the gate. She has a big umbrella. "Come under," she tells her little brother. They walk home under it.',B.azt,"Some words tell us a fact in the story: the path is wet. We can also think with clues. Rain and umbrella help us guess why Mila says Come under. She wants to keep her brother dry. A good guess fits the words. It is not a wild new story.","Why use an umbrella? First find rain. Next find the wet path. Rain can make people wet. An umbrella covers them, so they use it to help stay dry.","How does Mila help her brother? Say one line from the text that helps you know.","She shares the umbrella. Come under invites him to share its cover. They both walk under it.","Wet does not mean hot. The text does not say the umbrella\u2019s color. Do not guess its color from the rain.","Hear the words. Find a clue. Make a guess that fits. Tell the clue.",B.b0b,B.aMh)
B.auA=s([B.aai,B.aap,B.aal,B.aaX,B.aaU,B.aan],t.PF)
B.aP0=s(["Blend sounds in short words.","Hear words that rhyme.","Connect a word to a story detail."],t.s)
B.awM=s(["Blend|m + a + p makes map, the guide Pat holds.","Change|Map to tap changes the first sound.","Rhyme|Pat and hat share /at/."],t.s)
B.aEe=s(["What does Pat have?|A map|A mat|A mug|A mop|The first sentence says map.","Which letter starts map?|m|p|t|h|Map starts with m.","Which letter ends map?|p|m|a|t|The order m-a-p ends with p.","Which word rhymes with Pat?|Hat|Hut|Dot|Map|Pat and hat share the /at/ ending.","Which word is m-a-p?|Map|Tap|Hat|Dot|Blending m-a-p makes map.","What marks the hut?|A red dot|A blue hat|A big tree|A black cup|A red dot marks the hut.","What does Pat tap?|The dot|His hat|A cup|A bell|Pat taps the dot.","Which word has a different middle sound from hat?|Hut|Pat|Mat|Sat|Hut has short u, not short a.","Change the first sound of map to /t/. What word do you get?|Tap|Hat|Dot|Hut|Keeping a-p and adding t makes tap.","Which pair changes only its middle letter?|Hat and hut|Map and dot|Pat and map|Hut and red|Hat and hut share h and t.","Why is a map useful to Pat?|It shows a place to go.|It keeps rain off.|It holds water.|It is a shoe.|The dot shows the hut as their destination."],t.s)
B.aaO=new A.cg("Pat and the Map","Q1 \xb7 oracy, sounds and word recognition",'Pat has a map. A red dot marks a hut. Pat taps the dot. "We can go to the hut," Pat says. His sister puts on her hat.',B.aP0,"Say map slowly: /m/ /a/ /p/. Join the sounds to read map. Change m to t: tap. Change p to t in map: mat. Hat and Pat end with the same /at/ sound. They rhyme. Hut has a different middle sound. Have a helper say the words with you.","Read hat. First say /h/. Add the short a sound. End with /t/. Blend: hat. Pat rhymes with hat because both end in /at/.","Say map and tap. Which sound changes? Find the word for something Pat uses to find a place.","The first sound changes from /m/ to /t/. Map names the thing that shows where the hut is.","Do not read only the first letter. Hat and hut have the same first and last letters but different middle sounds.","Read every sound. Blend in order. Rhyming words share an ending sound.",B.awM,B.aEe)
B.aXD=s(["Use a polite request.","Listen for an instruction.","Tell who a pronoun means."],t.s)
B.apR=s(["Need|Ria forgot her spoon.","Request|May I borrow a clean one?","Direction|Wash it; put it in the tray when clean."],t.s)
B.aH9=s(["What did Ria forget?|Her spoon|Her bag|Her hat|Her book|Ria says she forgot her spoon.","Who lends a spoon?|The cook|Ria\u2019s dog|A bus driver|A guard|The cook says Here is a spoon.","What does borrow mean here?|Use and give back|Keep for good|Throw away|Break in half|Borrowing includes returning the thing.","Which words show thanks?|Thank you|Forgot my|After lunch|This tray|Thank you is Ria\u2019s response to help.","When will Ria wash the spoon?|After lunch|Before waking|Next year|Before borrowing it|Ria says after lunch.","Where should the clean spoon go?|In the tray|Under a bed|In a shoe|On the floor|The cook names the tray.","What does it mean in wash it?|The spoon|The lunch room|The cook|The school|The spoon is the thing Ria borrowed.","Which word makes the cook\u2019s direction polite?|Please|Tray|When|Clean|Please introduces the direction kindly.","Which plan follows all the directions?|Eat, wash the spoon, place it in the tray|Eat, keep the spoon|Eat, put the dirty spoon away|Eat, hide the tray|The spoon must be clean before it goes in the tray.","Which request could replace Ria\u2019s first line politely?|Could I borrow a clean spoon, please?|Give me every spoon!|I will keep your tray.|Do not speak to me.|The replacement keeps a clear, kind request.","Why is one used in a clean one?|It avoids saying spoon again.|It means one tray of food.|It names the cook.|It means no spoon.|One stands for a spoon already named."],t.s)
B.aae=new A.cg("A Kind Turn at Lunch","Q1 \xb7 oral language and listening comprehension","Ria: I forgot my spoon. May I borrow a clean one?\nCook: Yes. Here is a spoon.\nRia: Thank you. I will wash it after lunch.\nCook: Please put it in this tray when it is clean.",B.aXD,"May I borrow asks to use a thing and give it back. Thank you shows thanks. Please makes the cook\u2019s direction kind. It can stand for a thing named before. In I will wash it, it means the spoon. Read both speakers\u2019 words to follow the talk.","Where should the spoon go? First find the cook\u2019s last line. It says this tray. Check when: when it is clean. So wash it first.","Say Ria\u2019s plan in two steps. Use first and then.","First she will wash the spoon after lunch. Then she will put it in the tray. Both lines help tell the full plan.","Borrow does not mean keep forever. Do not put a dirty spoon in the clean tray; the cook says when it is clean.","Ask kindly. Hear the whole direction. Use nearby words to explain it.",B.apR,B.aH9)
B.aIP=s(["Name the character and place.","Tell a story problem and solution.","Retell events in order."],t.s)
B.arK=s(["Character|Pip, a small cat.","Problem|Rain makes his box wet.","Solution|Ada puts a dry basket indoors."],t.s)
B.aH3=s(["What animal is Pip?|A cat|A dog|A hen|A goat|The first sentence names Pip as a cat.","What falls in the yard?|Rain|Snow|Rice|Sand|Rain falls in the yard.","Where does Pip first hide?|Under a box|In a basket|On a roof|By a stove|He first hides under a box.","What goes wrong with the box?|It gets wet.|It flies away.|It becomes a boat.|It fills with books.|Rain makes the box wet.","Who helps Pip?|Ada|Ben|Lolo|A cook|Ada sees Pip and makes a dry place.","Where is the basket?|Inside the house|In a tree|At a stall|Under the rain|Ada puts it inside the house.","What does Pip do at the end?|Curl up|Swim|Chase Ada|Run into rain|Pip curls up in the basket.","Which pair tells the change in shelter?|Wet box to dry basket|Dry basket to wet box|Basket to roof|Yard to river|The two shelters occur in that order.","Why does the basket help?|It gives Pip a dry place.|It makes more rain.|It makes the box bigger.|It keeps Ada outside.|The basket solves the wet-shelter problem.","Which sentence best retells the story?|Ada helps a wet cat find a dry place.|A cat teaches Ada to swim.|Ada loses a house in the sun.|A box eats a basket.|The retelling keeps the problem and solution.","What does it mean in curls up in it?|The basket|The yard|The rain|Ada\u2019s shoe|Basket is the shelter named just before."],t.s)
B.aaG=new A.cg("Pip Finds a Home","Q2 \xb7 story elements and retelling","Pip is a small cat. Rain falls in the yard. Pip hides under a box, but the box gets wet. Ada sees Pip. She puts a dry basket inside the house. Pip curls up in it.",B.aIP,"A character is a person or animal in a story. A setting is where and when it happens. A problem makes life hard for a character. Here rain wets Pip\u2019s box. A solution helps solve the problem. Ada offers a dry basket. Retell only the main events.","Find the problem. Pip hides, but the box gets wet. That means his hiding place fails. Ada\u2019s dry basket gives him a safe place.","Finish: Pip\u2019s box was ___. Ada gave him a dry ___.","The box was wet. Ada gave him a dry basket inside the house. The contrast wet and dry shows how she helped.","Pip is a cat, not Ada\u2019s brother. The text does not say who owns the box. Do not add a new ending when retelling.","Tell who, where, the problem and how it is solved.",B.arK,B.aH3)
B.azo=s(["Find naming and action words.","Use one and more-than-one nouns.","Describe a thing with a clear word."],t.s)
B.aLl=s(["Noun|Pots names the two plant holders.","Action|Wipes tells what the brother does.","Description|Green tells the leaves\u2019 color."],t.s)
B.awJ=s(["How many pots are there?|Two|One|Three|Ten|The first words say Two pots.","Where are the pots?|By the window|Under a bed|In a lake|On a bus|The text puts them by the window.","Which word names a thing?|Shelf|Gives|Wipes|Green|Shelf is a noun naming a thing.","Which word tells an action?|Wipes|Bean|Pot|Window|Wipes tells what the brother does.","Who gives water?|Tess|Her brother|A seller|A cat|Tess gives the beans water.","Which word tells a color?|Green|Two|Light|Sit|Green is the leaves\u2019 color.","Choose the plural of pot.|Pots|Potes|Potting|Potted|Adding s makes pots.","What do the leaves face?|The light|The floor|A cup|A door mat|The last line says the light.","Which sentence tells the brother\u2019s action?|Her brother wipes the shelf.|The shelf wipes her brother.|Two green wipe pots.|The bean is a brother.|The chosen sentence names the correct doer and action.","Which pair means one and more than one?|Leaf \u2014 leaves|Leaf \u2014 water|Pot \u2014 light|Bean \u2014 green|Leaves is the plural form of leaf.","Add a describing word from the text: The ___ leaves face the light.|Green|Wipes|Brother|Gives|Green can describe leaves; the other options cannot fill that role."],t.s)
B.aay=new A.cg("Plants by the Window","Q2 \xb7 vocabulary, naming and action words","Two pots sit by the window. A bean grows in each pot. Tess gives the beans water. Her brother wipes the shelf. The green leaves face the light.",B.azo,"A noun names a person, place, animal or thing. Pot, bean and shelf are nouns. Many nouns add s for more than one: pot, pots. An action word tells what someone does: gives and wipes. Green describes the leaves. Use each word\u2019s job in its sentence.","In Her brother wipes the shelf, who acts? Brother. What does he do? Wipes. What does he wipe? Shelf. Brother and shelf name things or people; wipes tells an action.","Change one pot to two ___. Find the color word in the last line.","Two pots adds s. Green is the color word. Leaves is a special plural: one leaf, many leaves.","Do not write two pot. Use pots for two. Leaves is not leafs here. Green tells a color, not an action.","Nouns name. Action words tell doing. Describing words add detail.",B.aLl,B.awJ)
B.aM5=s(["Find who a note is for.","Find a request and its details.","Write a short complete message."],t.s)
B.aTz=s(["Reader|Dear Dad names the person receiving the note.","Message|Send one small pot for Friday\u2019s planting.","Writer|Mia signs at the end."],t.s)
B.aVM=s(["Who is the note for?|Dad|Mia\u2019s cat|A cook|A driver|Dear Dad names the reader.","Who wrote the note?|Mia|Dad|Ben|Tess|Mia\u2019s signature names the writer.","What will the class plant?|Beans|Mango trees|Rice|Roses|The class will plant beans.","When will they plant?|Friday|Monday|Sunday|Tuesday|Friday is stated in the first sentence.","What should Dad send?|One small pot|Two big bags|A red cap|A toy car|The request says one small pot.","Where will Mia bring it?|To school|To the sea|To a stall|To the zoo|She says she will bring it to school.","Which word starts with a capital because it is a day\u2019s name?|Friday|Small|Pot|Bring|Friday is the name of a day.","Which line asks for help?|Please send one small pot.|Love, Mia|Dear Dad|Our class will plant beans on Friday.|Please send makes a request.","Which reply shows Dad understood?|I will send one small pot.|I will send three cats.|I will plant the school.|I will bring a huge boat.|It keeps the item and both size and number details.","What does it refer to in bring it?|The pot|Friday|The whole class|Love|The pot is the item Mia will carry.","Which ending helps Dad know who wrote?|Love, Mia|A bare full stop|Please send|On Friday|The signature identifies the writer."],t.s)
B.aav=new A.cg("A Note for Dad","Q3 \xb7 reading and writing simple messages","Dear Dad,\nOur class will plant beans on Friday. Please send one small pot. I will bring it to school.\nLove,\nMia",B.aM5,"A note sends a short message. Dear Dad names the reader. Mia signs her name at the end so Dad knows who wrote. The middle tells what happens and asks for a pot. A sentence starts with a capital and ends with a mark. Friday has a capital as a day\u2019s name.","What should Dad send? Find Please send. Read the next words: one small pot. Keep both details, one and small.","Tell who wrote the note and when the class will plant.","Mia wrote it; her name is at the end. The class will plant on Friday; the first sentence gives the day.","Dad is the reader, not the writer. Mia asks for a pot, not a bag of beans. Do not leave out Friday in a retelling.","A useful note names the reader, gives a clear message and names the writer.",B.aTz,B.aVM)
B.aX9=s(["Tell an informational text\u2019s main message.","Connect a cause and an effect.","Make a clear sentence for a sign."],t.s)
B.aKL=s(["Fact|Fish live in the pond.","Risk|Bags and cups can hurt fish.","Action|Kai puts his cup in a bin."],t.s)
B.aOp=s(["What lives in the pond?|Fish|Cats|Goats|Hens|The first line names fish.","What can hurt the fish?|Bags and cups|Clean air|A kind word|A school bell|The second line names bags and cups.","What does the sign say?|KEEP LITTER OUT|FEED THE CATS|OPEN THE GATE|READ A BOOK|Those are the sign\u2019s words.","Where does Kai put his cup?|In a bin|In the pond|On a fish|Under a desk|Kai uses a bin.","What does empty mean for the cup?|Nothing is left inside.|It is full of soup.|It is made of fish.|It is a bag.|An empty cup has no drink left.","Who hears Kai\u2019s request?|His friend|His teacher|A seller|His doctor|He tells his friend to use the bin.","What does them mean in hurt them?|Fish|Bins|Friends|Signs|Fish are the living things mentioned before.","Which word makes Kai\u2019s request kind?|Please|Bin|Use|The|Please makes the request polite.","Why does Kai\u2019s action fit the sign?|He keeps litter out of the pond.|He puts a cup in the water.|He takes the sign away.|He scares all fish.|Using a bin follows KEEP LITTER OUT.","Which new sign keeps the same message?|Put litter in the bin.|Throw cups in the pond.|Hide every fish.|Drink pond water.|It directs people to the right place for litter.","Which statement is supported, not too strong?|Litter can hurt fish.|Every fish is dead.|All cups are in the pond.|Kai owns every bin.|The text says can hurt, not that harm always happened."],t.s)
B.aaZ=new A.cg("Keep the Pond Clean","Q4 \xb7 informational text, signs and cause",'Fish live in our pond. Bags and cups can hurt them. A sign says KEEP LITTER OUT. Kai puts his empty cup in a bin. He tells his friend, "Use the bin, please."',B.aX9,"Information tells about the world. The first two lines tell why litter matters. A cause makes something happen. Bags and cups can cause harm to fish. The sign gives a short rule. Kai follows it by using a bin. A good sign is short and clear.","Why use the bin? Find can hurt them. Them means fish. Keeping cups out of the pond can help protect the fish.","Say the sign\u2019s message in a full sentence. Tell how Kai follows it.","Do not put litter in the pond. Kai puts his empty cup in a bin, not in the water.","The text says litter can hurt fish. It does not say every cup has already hurt a fish. Keep the claim the right size.","Find the main message. Use facts to tell why an action matters.",B.aKL,B.aOp)
B.aKX=s([B.aaO,B.aae,B.aaG,B.aay,B.aav,B.aaZ],t.PF)
B.aKY=s(["Read sh and th in words.","Use an opposite as a word clue.","Describe an object using text evidence."],t.s)
B.avD=s(["Letter team|Sh begins shell and shore.","Meaning clue|Smooth, not rough gives an opposite clue.","Place|Beside a thin stick tells where the shell goes."],t.s)
B.ayZ=s(["What does Shan find?|A shell|A cup|A ring|A hat|The first sentence names a shell.","Which letters begin shell?|sh|th|ch|wh|Shell starts with the letter team sh.","Which word starts with the same sound as shell?|Shore|Thin|Crab|Past|Shore also begins with sh.","Which word starts with th?|Thin|Stick|Shan|Shell|Thin starts with t and h together.","What does smooth mean here?|Not bumpy|Very loud|Very sharp|Full of holes|Not rough is a clue to an even surface.","Where is the shell put?|Beside a stick|Under a bed|In a bag|On a crab|The text says beside a thin stick.","Which animal runs past?|A crab|A bird|A dog|A fish|A crab runs past.","Does Shan take the shell home?|No, she leaves it there.|Yes, in a box.|Yes, in her hat.|Yes, with the crab.|The last line says she leaves it where she found it.","Which pair gives opposite surface words?|Smooth and rough|Shell and shore|Thin and stick|Finds and puts|Smooth and rough describe contrasting surfaces.","Which word means next to in the passage?|Beside|Past|Found|Smooth|Beside places the shell next to the stick.","Which description stays true to the text?|A smooth shell near a thin stick|A rough shell in a bag|A blue crab in a cup|A thick stick on a roof|Both smooth and thin match the stated details."],t.s)
B.aa0=new A.cg("The Shell on the Shore","Q1 \xb7 word reading, vocabulary and description","Shan finds a shell on the shore. It is smooth, not rough. She puts it beside a thin stick. A crab runs past. Shan leaves the shell where she found it.",B.aKY,"Sometimes two letters make one sound. Sh begins shell and shore. Th begins thin. Say the words with a helper; keep your voice gentle for th in thin. Smooth and rough are opposites here. Not rough helps us know smooth means even, not bumpy. Beside means next to.","Read shell. Notice sh working together. Add the short e sound, then l. Blend shell. To explain smooth, read the clue not rough: the shell feels even.","Find two words starting with sh. Where does Shan put the shell?","Shell and shore start with sh. Shan puts the shell beside a thin stick. Beside tells its place.","Do not say a separate s and h at the start of shell. Thin and tin are different words. Shan does not take the shell home.","Look for letter teams. Use nearby words to check meaning.",B.avD,B.ayZ)
B.aR4=s(["Match pronouns to their nouns.","Use action words with one or many doers.","Read a complete sentence for meaning."],t.s)
B.aug=s(["Group|The children becomes They.","One person|Rico becomes He.","Agreement|Children move; Rico wipes."],t.s)
B.aVQ=s(["What do the children move?|A bench|A cupboard|A bed|A cart|They move a small bench.","Where do they put it?|In the shade|In a river|On the roof|Under a bus|The second sentence says in the shade.","What does They mean?|The children|The leaf|The bench|The shade|They replaces the children.","Who wipes the seat?|Rico|His sister|A cook|A guard|Rico wipes the seat.","What does He mean?|Rico|The leaf|The bench|All children|He refers to Rico in the previous sentence.","What does Rico see?|A loose leaf|A lost shoe|A red cup|A tiny bell|He sees a loose leaf.","Who picks up the leaf?|His sister|Rico\u2019s dog|A driver|A seller|His sister picks it up.","Choose the correct action: Rico ___ the seat.|Wipes|Wipe|Wiping only|Were wipe|Rico is one doer, so wipes fits this present-tense sentence.","Choose the correct sentence about the group.|The children move the bench.|The children moves the bench.|The children moving the bench.|The children is move the bench.|The plural children takes move.","Which is a complete sentence?|The bench is ready.|In the shade.|A loose.|Picks up the.|The bench is ready gives a full thought.","What does it mean in They put it in the shade?|The bench|The children|The sister|The leaf|The bench is the object moved and put in the shade."],t.s)
B.aab=new A.cg("Who Moved the Bench","Q1 \xb7 sentences, pronouns and comprehension","The children move a small bench. They put it in the shade. Rico wipes the seat. He sees a loose leaf on it. His sister picks up the leaf. The bench is ready.",B.aR4,"They can replace a group: the children. He replaces Rico. It replaces the bench or seat, so check the nearby words. The children move uses move for many. Rico wipes uses wipes for one. A full sentence tells who or what and gives a full idea.","Who does They mean? Read the sentence before it: The children move a small bench. They means the children, not the bench.","Finish: Rico ___ the seat. Then explain He in the next line.","Rico wipes the seat. He means Rico because Rico is the person named just before. Wipes agrees with one person.","Do not change They to He when it means all the children. Read the whole sentence before deciding what it refers to.","Match a pronoun to its noun. Match the action word to the doer.",B.aug,B.aVQ)
B.aUf=s(["Sequence story events.","Infer a feeling from an action.","Support an answer with a clue."],t.s)
B.azm=s(["Problem|The kite stays stuck despite Leo\u2019s pull.","Help|Ate uses a long pole from the ground.","Feeling clue|A frown becomes a smile after the kite is freed."],t.s)
B.awK=s(["What lifts the kite?|A gust|A bird|A child|A truck|A gust is the wind that lifts it.","Where is the kite stuck?|In a low tree|On a roof|In a pond|Under a bench|The first line names a low tree.","What does Leo try first?|Pulling the string|Cutting the tree|Using a ladder|Hiding the kite|Leo first pulls the string.","Who suggests asking Ate?|May|Leo|A guard|A seller|May says Let us ask Ate.","What does Ate use?|A long pole|A net|A chair|A spoon|Ate uses a long pole.","Where does Ate stand?|On the ground|On the branch|On the roof|In the tree|The passage states on the ground.","Which expression comes last?|A smile|A tight frown|Closed eyes|A yawn|Leo\u2019s frown turns into a smile.","What does gust mean?|A quick strong wind|A long rope|A kind word|A wet leaf|The wind\u2019s action on the kite explains gust.","Why is Leo likely relieved?|His stuck kite is free.|He has lost his kite.|The tree has fallen.|Ate has gone away.|The solved problem and smile support relief.","Which event comes just before the smile?|The kite comes free.|The gust first blows.|Leo buys string.|May climbs a roof.|The freed kite is followed by the smile.","Which claim adds something the text never says?|Ate climbs the tree.|May asks for help.|Leo pulls the string.|The kite gets stuck.|The text places Ate on the ground, not in the tree."],t.s)
B.aaL=new A.cg("The Kite in the Tree","Q2 \xb7 narrative, feelings and inference",'A gust lifts Leo\u2019s kite into a low tree. Leo pulls the string, but it stays stuck. "Let us ask Ate," says May. Ate stands on the ground and uses a long pole. The kite comes free. Leo\u2019s tight frown turns into a smile.',B.aUf,"A gust is a quick strong wind. A story may show a feeling without naming it. A tight frown can show worry or upset. A smile after help can show relief. Use the event with the expression; a smile alone can mean many things.","How does Leo feel at the end? First note that his kite comes free. Next note his smile. Together they suggest relief and happiness because his problem is solved.","Why does May ask Ate? Find the failed action before her words.","Leo pulls the string but the kite stays stuck. May asks Ate because Leo\u2019s first plan does not work.","Ate does not climb the tree. The text says she stays on the ground. A useful inference must fit that detail.","Use events and expressions together to explain feelings.",B.azm,B.awK)
B.aVp=s(["Follow a written procedure.","Use sequence words.","Explain why a step matters."],t.s)
B.aJa=s(["Shape|Fold the sheet in half.","Close sides|Tape two short sides; leave the top open.","Fill and label|Add dry seeds, fold the top, write the name."],t.s)
B.aZb=s(["What is the pocket for?|Dry seeds|Hot soup|Live fish|Drinking water|The opening sentence gives its purpose.","What shape is the sheet at the start?|Square|Round|A star|A ring|The first step says a square sheet.","What is the first action?|Fold the sheet in half|Add seeds|Write a name|Close the top|First introduces folding.","How many sides are taped?|Two short sides|All four sides|One long side|No sides|The second step names two short sides.","What must stay open before filling?|The top|A desk|A bag of rice|The floor|Leave the top open allows seeds in.","What goes inside?|Dry seeds|Wet sand|A cup|A pencil case|Put dry seeds inside is explicit.","What should you write?|The seed name|Your lunch order|A bus number|A weather report|The last step says the seed name.","Which word signals the final step?|Last|To|Dry|Inside|Last marks the final action group.","Why not close the top before adding seeds?|You need an opening for the seeds.|The paper will turn red.|The seeds will become rocks.|Tape only works at night.|An open top allows filling.","Which order matches the procedure?|Fold, tape sides, add seeds, close top|Add seeds, close top, get paper|Close all sides, add seeds|Write on seeds, unfold, throw away|The chosen order follows the printed steps.","Why label the pocket?|To know which seeds are inside|To make the seeds wet|To replace all the tape|To open the bottom|A seed name identifies its contents."],t.s)
B.aat=new A.cg("Make a Paper Seed Pocket","Q2 \xb7 procedural reading and sequence","To keep dry seeds, make a paper pocket.\nFirst, fold a square sheet in half. Next, tape the two short sides. Leave the top open. Put dry seeds inside. Last, fold the top down and write the seed name on it.",B.aVp,"A procedure tells how to do something. Action words start steps: fold, tape, leave, put and write. First, next and last show order. Read the whole task before starting. If you close the top too soon, you cannot put the seeds inside.","Why tape the sides? Folding makes a pocket shape, but the sides are still open. Tape closes those gaps so seeds can stay inside. Keep the top open until filling.","Tell the step before adding seeds. What is done after adding them?","Leave the top open before adding seeds. After adding them, fold the top down and write the seed name.","Use dry seeds, not wet ones. Do not tape every opening before filling. The name belongs on the pocket, not on each seed.","Follow actions in order. Ask what each step helps you do.",B.aJa,B.aZb)
B.ayW=s(["Find a likeness and a difference.","Choose details for a main idea.","Write connected comparison sentences."],t.s)
B.aEo=s(["Shared place|Both are near books.","Mat|Soft; holds four children.","Bench|Hard; holds two children."],t.s)
B.aQZ=s(["What two places are compared?|A mat and a bench|A bus and a car|A pond and a well|A desk and a bed|The first sentence names the two reading places.","Where are both places?|Near the books|Outside the gate|On the roof|By a river|Both are near the books.","Which place is soft?|The mat|The bench|Both|Neither|The text calls the mat soft.","How many children can the mat hold?|Four|One|Two|Ten|The mat holds four children.","How many can the bench hold?|Two|Four|Five|Six|The bench holds two.","Which word introduces a likeness?|Both|Hard|Four|Friend|Both signals a shared detail.","Which place is chosen for a small group?|The mat|The roof|The road|The gate|We choose the mat for a small group.","Which is the main topic?|Two reading places|How to cook rice|A lost bus|Ways to wash cups|All sentences compare reading places.","Where can the speaker and one friend fit according to the text?|On the bench|On a one-person stool|Inside a book|On a single shelf|The two-person bench fits the speaker and one friend.","Which comparison is correct?|The mat is soft, but the bench is hard.|Both places hold four.|The bench holds more than the mat.|Neither is near books.|The correct choice uses the stated contrasting surfaces.","Which extra sentence belongs in this paragraph?|We put our books beside the mat.|Fish have fins.|My uncle repairs buses.|Rain falls from clouds.|A sentence about reading at the mat stays on the paragraph\u2019s topic."],t.s)
B.aax=new A.cg("Two Places to Read","Q3 \xb7 comparison, paragraphs and information","Our room has a reading mat and a reading bench. Both are near the books. The mat is soft and holds four children. The bench is hard and holds two. We choose the mat for a small group. I choose the bench when I read with one friend.",B.ayW,"Compare means find how things are alike and different. Both introduces a likeness. But can join a difference: The mat is soft, but the bench is hard. A paragraph keeps its sentences on one main idea. Here the idea is two reading places.","Which holds more children? Find four for the mat and two for the bench. Four is more than two. So the mat holds more.","Say one way the places are alike. Then use but to tell a difference.","Both are near the books. The mat is soft, but the bench is hard. These details compare the same two places.","Both means two things share a detail. Do not say both hold four; only the mat does. Keep a paragraph on its topic.","Name both things. Tell a shared feature. Give matching differences.",B.aEo,B.aQZ)
B.aPG=s(["Summarize a factual recount.","Recognize past action words.","Edit a sentence\u2019s capital and end mark."],t.s)
B.aEz=s(["When|On Tuesday places the event in time.","Actions|Picked paper, pulled weeds, put tools away.","Result|The garden looked neat."],t.s)
B.b0N=s(["When did the class clean?|Tuesday|Friday|Sunday|Monday|The first line says On Tuesday.","What did they pick up first?|Paper|Flowers|Tools|Fruit|First introduces picking up paper.","Who helped with the weeds?|Their teacher|A driver|A doctor|A seller|They pulled weeds with their teacher.","What did they leave growing?|Flowers|Paper|Tools|Cups|They left the flowers growing.","What did they put away?|Their tools|Their garden|Their teacher|Their flowers|At the end they put tools away.","How did the garden look at the end?|Neat|Frozen|Broken|Full of paper|The final line says neat.","Which word is a past action?|Cleaned|Clean|Cleaning|Will clean|Cleaned tells an action already done.","Which word is past but has no ed ending?|Left|Looked|Pulled|Picked|Left is the irregular past form of leave.","Which summary keeps the main event and result?|The class cleaned the garden and made it neat.|A teacher bought flowers.|The class lost all its tools.|Tuesday is a kind of flower.|The summary includes the cleaning and its result.","Which sentence is correctly edited?|On Tuesday we cleaned the garden.|on tuesday we cleaned the garden|On tuesday we cleaned the garden!|on Tuesday we cleaned the garden?|The first choice has both capitals and a full stop.","Which event happened after pulling weeds?|Putting tools away|Picking up paper first|Arriving on Monday|Buying a garden|The text places putting tools away at the end."],t.s)
B.aa1=new A.cg("The Class Garden Report","Q4 \xb7 factual recount, main idea and editing","On Tuesday our class cleaned the garden. First, we picked up paper. Then we pulled small weeds with our teacher. We left the flowers growing. At the end, we put our tools away. The garden looked neat.",B.aPG,"A recount tells events that already happened. Cleaned, picked, pulled and looked are past action words. Left is also past, but it does not end in ed. A short summary keeps the main event and result. Start sentences and Tuesday with capitals.","Summarize the text. Ask what the class did: cleaned the garden. Ask the result: it looked neat. Combine: The class cleaned the garden and left it neat.","Fix this sentence: on tuesday we cleaned the garden","On Tuesday we cleaned the garden. On starts the sentence. Tuesday names a day. A full stop ends the statement.","Do not say the class pulled out flowers. They left flowers growing. Left is the past form here, not leaved.","A recount tells past events in order. A summary keeps the main event and result.",B.aEz,B.b0N)
B.aXh=s([B.aa0,B.aab,B.aaL,B.aat,B.aax,B.aa1],t.PF)
B.ayA=s(["Find a story\u2019s conflict and solution.","Infer a feeling from evidence.","Retell important events in order."],t.s)
B.aDo=s(["Conflict|Wind tears Neri\u2019s lantern.","Response|Pao offers help; they tape the tear.","Resolution|Neri carries the lantern onto the stage."],t.s)
B.b3g=s(["What is the lantern for?|The class play|A boat trip|A market sale|A cooking task|The opening sentence gives the purpose.","What tears the lantern?|Wind|Pao|A dog|The curtain|The text names wind as the cause.","Where does Neri stop?|At the gate|At the beach|Inside a shop|On the stage|She stops at the school gate.","How does Pao respond?|He offers to mend it.|He tears it again.|He walks away laughing.|He hides all the tape.|We can mend it offers help.","What fixes the tear?|Paper and tape|Water and soap|Sand and stones|A new bag|They tape paper over the tear.","Which event comes last?|Neri carries the lantern onstage.|Wind tears the side.|Pao offers help.|Neri hides the lantern.|The stage action ends the passage.","Which is a stated fact?|Neri holds the lantern behind her bag.|Neri hates every play.|Pao is always brave.|The teacher is angry.|Only the bag action is directly stated.","What does mend mean here?|Repair|Destroy|Sell|Paint blue|Taping the tear shows that mend means repair.","Which feeling is reasonably suggested by hiding the damage?|Worry about the torn lantern|Joy at losing the play|Hunger for lunch|Anger at the sea|Hiding damage supports worry, though it is not directly named.","Which summary keeps the main plot?|Friends repair a torn lantern so Neri can use it in a play.|Neri sells bags at a gate.|A curtain tears in strong rain.|Pao builds a boat alone.|The chosen summary links problem, help and result.","Which detail best supports a theme of cooperation?|They tape paper over the tear together.|The lantern is made of paper.|There is a school gate.|The curtain opens.|Their shared repair demonstrates cooperation."],t.s)
B.aaw=new A.cg("The Lantern for the Play","Q1 \xb7 literary text, sequence and inference",'Neri made a paper lantern for the class play. On the way to school, wind tore one side. Neri stopped at the gate and held the lantern behind her bag. "We can mend it," said Pao. They taped paper over the tear. When the curtain opened, Neri carried the lantern onto the stage.',B.ayA,"A plot links events around a problem. The torn lantern is the problem. A character\u2019s actions can hint at feelings. Hiding the lantern may show worry or shame, but the narrator does not name the feeling. Say may and give your clue. A summary keeps the problem, response and result.","Was the lantern ready at the end? First find They taped paper over the tear. Then find Neri carrying it onstage. The repair and use support the idea that it was ready.","Why might Neri hide the lantern? Give one action as evidence.","She may worry that people will see the damage. Holding it behind her bag supports this. Other feelings need evidence too.","Do not claim Pao tore it. Wind caused the tear. Do not treat a guessed feeling as a stated fact.","Link problem, response and result. Support inferred feelings with actions.",B.aDo,B.b3g)
B.aFi=s(["Infer word meaning from a clue.","Explain a simple comparison.","Join ideas with a suitable connector."],t.s)
B.apv=s(["Clue|Only at the ankles explains shallow.","Comparison|Like silver needles describes the fish.","Reason|Slippery stones explain staying on the path."],t.s)
B.b_E=s(["What does shallow mean here?|Not deep|Very noisy|Very wide|Completely dry|Ankle-high water is a clue to little depth.","Which detail explains shallow?|Water reached only Lito\u2019s ankles.|His sister sketched.|Fish moved.|The path was dry.|The water level directly explains the depth.","What is compared to needles?|Small fish|Stones|Ankles|The path|The fish darted like silver needles.","What does the comparison suggest?|Thin shining shapes|Fish made of metal|Needles that can breathe|A sewing class|Silver needles describe appearance, not literal material.","Who sketched?|Lito\u2019s sister|Lito|A fisher|A teacher|His sister sketched the bank.","Which word joins two related actions?|And|Only|Silver|Dry|And joins watching and sketching.","Why did they stay on the path?|The stones were slippery.|The path was under water.|The fish called them.|They lost a boat.|Because introduces slippery stones as the reason.","What does quietly tell?|How Lito watched|What the creek was called|How deep the water was|Who made the path|Quietly describes the manner of watching.","Choose a complete reason-and-action sentence.|Because the stones were slippery, they stayed on the path.|Because the stones were slippery.|And because slippery.|The path because stones.|Only the first joins a reason to a full main idea.","Which claim takes the comparison too literally?|The fish were actual sewing needles.|The fish looked thin.|The fish looked bright.|The fish moved quickly.|Like signals a comparison, not a change into needles.","Which connector fits: Lito watched, ___ his sister sketched?|And|Because of|Under|Only|And joins the two complete related ideas."],t.s)
B.aa7=new A.cg("Words beside the Creek","Q1 \xb7 vocabulary, sentences and description","The creek was shallow; the water reached only Lito\u2019s ankles. Small fish darted like silver needles. Lito watched quietly, and his sister sketched the bank. Because the stones were slippery, they stayed on the dry path.",B.aFi,"A context clue helps explain a word. Water reaching only ankles shows shallow means not deep. Like can compare two unlike things. Fish like silver needles suggests thin, shining shapes. And joins related ideas. Because tells a reason. Read a sentence\u2019s meaning before choosing a connector.","Why stay on the path? Find Because the stones were slippery. That clause gives a reason. The main action is they stayed on the dry path.","Explain shallow using the passage. Then join: Lito watched. His sister sketched. Use and.","Shallow means not deep, shown by water only at the ankles. Lito watched, and his sister sketched.","Fish are not real needles. The comparison describes their look. Because gives a reason; and adds an idea.","Use context for meaning. Explain comparisons. Choose connectors by their job.",B.apv,B.b_E)
B.b_k=s(["Find a main idea and supporting details.","Recognize enumeration-description.","Plan a short factual paragraph."],t.s)
B.aOl=s(["Bees|Move pollen between flowers.","Earthworms|Make tunnels in soil.","Lady beetles|Eat some tiny leaf pests."],t.s)
B.aW9=s(["How many kinds of visitors are named?|Three|Two|Four|Five|Bees, earthworms and lady beetles make three.","Which visitor moves pollen?|Bees|Earthworms|Lady beetles|Snails|The bee sentence gives that job.","Where do worms make tunnels?|In soil|In cups|In roofs|In books|The text says in the soil.","What do lady beetles eat in the passage?|Some tiny pests|Every garden leaf|All bees|Paper|The stated food is some tiny pests.","What is the main idea?|Different visitors help the garden.|Only bees matter.|All insects are harmful.|The garden has no soil.|This idea includes all three examples.","How is the text organized?|A list with descriptions|A recipe|A timed race|A lost-and-found dialogue|It enumerates visitors and describes their jobs.","Which detail supports the main idea?|Worms make small soil tunnels.|My shoes are blue.|The bus is late.|The bell rings.|The worm\u2019s job is one way a visitor helps.","How do the children watch?|Without grabbing the visitors|By catching every insect|By pulling worms apart|By covering all flowers|The last line specifies observation without grabbing.","Which heading is too narrow for the whole text?|Bees Move Pollen|Helpful Garden Visitors|Three Garden Helpers|Visitors and Their Jobs|The bee-only heading leaves out two visitors.","Which plan matches an enumeration paragraph?|Name three visitors and explain each job.|Give only a date.|List steps for folding paper.|Write a greeting and signature only.|Enumeration lists items under one topic with details.","Which claim is too strong for the text?|Lady beetles remove every pest.|Worms make tunnels.|Bees move pollen.|Visitors help in different ways.|Some pests does not support every pest."],t.s)
B.aar=new A.cg("Three Helpful Garden Visitors","Q2 \xb7 enumeration-description and main idea","Our garden has three helpful visitors. Bees move pollen between flowers. Earthworms make small tunnels in the soil. Lady beetles eat some tiny pests on leaves. Each visitor helps in a different way. We watch them without grabbing them.",B.b_k,"Enumeration lists parts or examples of a topic. This paragraph names three garden visitors and describes one job for each. The main idea includes them all: visitors help the garden in different ways. A detail about just bees is too narrow for the whole paragraph.","Choose a heading. Bees in Our Garden leaves out worms and beetles. Three Helpful Garden Visitors covers all three and their useful jobs, so it fits better.","Make a three-row list. Pair each visitor with its job.","Bees \u2014 move pollen. Earthworms \u2014 make tunnels. Lady beetles \u2014 eat some pests. Keep each job with the right visitor.","The passage says some pests, not every pest. Worms do not move pollen in this text. Do not swap the details.","A main idea covers the whole list. Details explain each item.",B.aOl,B.aW9)
B.b1E=s(["Sequence steps in a procedure.","Explain precise direction words.","Write a clear instruction."],t.s)
B.aMp=s(["Set up|Ruler upright, zero at inside bottom level.","Place|Level ground in an open area.","Read and record|Eye height; write day and height before emptying."],t.s)
B.aDc=s(["What holds the rain?|A clean jar|A paper bag|A flat leaf|A closed book|The teacher gives a jar.","Where is the ruler taped?|Outside the jar|Inside a bag|Under the soil|On a roof|The ruler is taped upright outside it.","Where is zero placed?|At the inside bottom level|At the jar\u2019s top|Above the ruler|At a window|The passage gives the zero position.","Where should the jar stand?|Level ground in an open place|Under a roof|On a sloping branch|Inside a cupboard|The placement must be level and open.","When is the water read?|After rain|Before any rain only|While the jar is empty only|Once a year only|After rain introduces the reading.","At what height should eyes be?|At the water level|High above the jar|Below the ground|Facing away|Eye-height reading means eyes level with the water mark.","What is recorded?|The day and water height|Only the jar\u2019s color|Only a pupil\u2019s name|The ruler\u2019s price|Both day and height are requested.","What happens after recording?|The jar is emptied.|The ruler is eaten.|The ground is tilted.|The notes are erased.|Then empty the jar follows recording.","Why keep the jar in an open place?|To let rain enter without a roof blocking it|To keep all rain out|To hide the ruler|To make the jar taller|The purpose of collecting rain explains open placement.","Which instruction is most precise?|Read the water level at eye height.|Look at it sometime.|Do the thing.|Read something.|The first choice names what to read and how.","Which claim is accurate about this model?|It is a simple classroom gauge.|It is a perfect official weather station.|It needs no recording.|It measures wind speed.|The lesson explicitly limits this rough model\u2019s precision."],t.s)
B.aaz=new A.cg("A Rain Gauge for Our Class","Q2 \xb7 procedural order and clear directions","Our teacher gives us a clean, straight-sided jar. First, we tape a ruler upright outside it, with zero at the inside bottom level. Next, we set the jar on level ground in an open place. After rain, we read the water level at eye height. We record the day and height, then empty the jar.",B.b1E,"A procedure needs action, order and useful detail. Upright tells how to place the ruler. At eye height tells how to read it. First and next order setup steps; after rain tells when to measure. This is a simple classroom model, not a calibrated weather-station instrument.","Should we empty the jar before recording? Read record the day and height, then empty. Then places emptying after recording, so the result is not lost.","Write the reading step with its important height detail.","After rain, read the water level at eye height. Leaving out eye height makes the instruction less precise.","Do not put the jar under a roof that blocks rain. Do not call this rough classroom reading an official weather record.","Order actions and include where, when and how details that affect the task.",B.aMp,B.aDc)
B.aQv=s(["Identify a recount\u2019s time order.","Use past-tense verbs.","Separate an event from its reason."],t.s)
B.b2E=s(["Before|Books placed on three tables.","During|Each child chose one book.","After|The class thanked helpers."],t.s)
B.ayb=s(["When was the swap?|Last Thursday|Next Monday|Every Sunday|Tomorrow|The opening time phrase states Last Thursday.","How many tables held books?|Three|One|Two|Ten|The class used three tables.","When were books set out?|Before break|After the helpers left|At midnight|Next week|Before break introduces the setup.","How many books did each child choose?|One|Three|Five|None|Each child chose one book.","Why did the narrator return the first book?|The story was already familiar.|The book was wet.|The teacher took it.|It had no pages.|The narrator already knew the story.","What did the narrator choose next?|A new story|A spoon|A chair|A ruler|The narrator chose a new one, meaning a different book.","What happened after the swap?|They thanked the helpers.|They opened school for the first time.|They bought three tables.|They tore the books.|The final sentence gives the thanks.","Which is the past form of choose used here?|Chose|Chooses|Choosing|Will choose|Chose is the irregular past form.","Which connector shows a result in the passage?|So|Before|At|Last|So links knowing the story to returning it.","Which opening would suit another past recount?|Last Friday we visited the library.|Visit the library tomorrow!|What is a library?|Libraries are places for books.|The dated past event starts a recount.","Which three-event summary has the right order?|Set out books, choose books, thank helpers|Thank helpers, choose books, set out books|Choose books, thank helpers, set out books|Return home, build tables, buy books|The first order matches before, at and after."],t.s)
B.aaV=new A.cg("A Recount of the Book Swap","Q3 \xb7 recount, chronology and reported events","Last Thursday our class held a book swap. Before break, we placed books on three tables. At break, each child chose one book. I first picked a story I already knew, so I put it back and chose a new one. After the swap, we thanked the helpers.",B.aQv,"A recount retells a real or imagined past experience in time order. Last Thursday sets the time. Before, at and after organize events. Held, chose and thanked are past verbs. So connects a reason to a result: knowing the first story leads the narrator to choose another.","Why change books? Read I already knew, then so I put it back. The first phrase gives the reason; putting it back and choosing a new one are the results.","Retell three events using Before break, At break and After the swap.","Before break, the class set out books. At break, children chose books. After the swap, they thanked helpers.","Do not say the swap happened tomorrow. The verbs and Last Thursday place it in the past. Chose is the past of choose.","Set the time. Retell past events in order. Explain reasons when needed.",B.b2E,B.ayb)
B.avR=s(["Combine a visual description with printed words.","Distinguish an invitation from a recount.","Check details when reporting an event."],t.s)
B.aza=s(["Poster words|Saturday, 8 a.m., school gate gives meeting details.","Visual cue|Gloved hands support Bring gloves.","Recount evidence|Twelve pupils cleaned; the path was clear by 9 a.m."],t.s)
B.b1b=s(["What does the poster announce?|Cleanup Day|A book swap|A sports final|A cooking show|CLEANUP DAY is the heading.","When should people meet?|Saturday at 8 a.m.|Friday at noon|Sunday at 9 p.m.|Monday at 6 a.m.|The poster gives Saturday and 8 a.m.","Where is the meeting point?|School gate|A beach|A library desk|A shop|School gate appears in the poster.","What should people bring?|Gloves|Kites|Lunch trays|Paint|Bring gloves is the reminder.","What is in the described picture?|Gloved hands beside a bin|A clock above a boat|Twelve named pupils|A teacher holding a book|The visual description gives hands and a bin.","How many pupils came according to the report?|Twelve|Eight|Nine|Twenty|The report states twelve pupils.","By what time was the path clear?|9 a.m.|8 p.m.|Noon|6 a.m.|By 9 a.m. appears in the report.","What did they do with filled bags?|Put them beside the bin for collection|Throw them into a creek|Take them into class to open|Leave them on the path|The last sentence gives the placement and purpose.","Which fact cannot be learned from the poster alone?|How many pupils actually attended|The planned day|The planned meeting place|What to bring|Attendance is reported afterward, not guaranteed by an invitation.","How does the visual support the poster?|It reinforces the glove reminder.|It proves twelve attended.|It gives a full cleanup report.|It changes Saturday to Friday.|The gloves in the picture match the instruction.","Which sentence is a recount rather than an invitation?|On Saturday, twelve pupils met at the gate.|Join us on Saturday!|Please bring gloves.|Meet at 8 a.m.|The past-tense sentence reports an event that happened."],t.s)
B.aaR=new A.cg("The Cleanup Poster and Report","Q4 \xb7 recount, visual text and audience","Poster: CLEANUP DAY \u2014 Saturday, 8 a.m., school gate. Bring gloves. Picture: a pair of gloved hands beside a bin.\nReport: On Saturday, twelve pupils met at the gate. They wore gloves and picked up litter. By 9 a.m., the path was clear. They put the filled bags beside the bin for collection.",B.avR,"A poster can invite people to act. Large words give the event; smaller details tell when and where. A picture should support the words. The described gloved hands match Bring gloves. A report tells what happened afterward. Check names, times and numbers before retelling it.","Does the picture tell the meeting time? No. The printed 8 a.m. gives that detail. The hands support the glove reminder. Use each part for the information it actually gives.","Tell one shared detail and one detail found only in the report.","Both name Saturday and the gate. Only the report says twelve pupils came and the path was clear by 9 a.m.","The poster invites; it does not prove attendance. Twelve pupils is a report detail, not a number in the picture.","Read words and visuals together. Keep plans separate from reported results.",B.aza,B.b1b)
B.asq=s([B.aaw,B.aa7,B.aar,B.aaz,B.aaV,B.aaR],t.PF)
B.aIC=s(["Identify a narrator\u2019s point of view.","Explain a change in a character.","Make a prediction using evidence."],t.s)
B.aSR=s(["Before|The spare seems a waste of space.","Turning event|The old paddle cracks; the spare helps.","After|The narrator checks the spare on the next trip."],t.s)
B.b_G=s(["Which word signals first-person narration?|I|Uncle|Lake|Spare|I names the speaker telling the experience.","What does Uncle pack?|A spare paddle|A new sail|A fishing net|A glass jar|The first sentence names the spare.","What is the lake like?|Calm|Frozen|Stormy|Dry|The lake is described as calm.","What happens to the old paddle?|It cracks.|It becomes a sail.|It sinks before launch.|It is sold.|The old paddle cracks halfway across.","How does Uncle respond?|He reaches for the spare.|He shouts for a bus.|He throws away the boat.|He jumps ashore.|He uses the item he packed.","What does the narrator do on the next trip?|Checks the spare|Hides the spare|Refuses to help|Breaks the boat|The final line describes checking it.","Whose private opening thought do we hear?|The narrator\u2019s|Uncle\u2019s|A stranger\u2019s|Every person\u2019s|The I narrator shares the thought about wasted space.","What does spare mean here?|An extra kept for use if needed|A broken main paddle|A kind of lake|A boat\u2019s color|The extra paddle replaces the cracked one.","Which theme is best supported?|Preparation can solve an unexpected problem.|Calm lakes break every boat.|Extra items are always useless.|Children must never paddle.|The spare solves the problem caused by the crack.","Which prediction fits the narrator\u2019s change?|The narrator will value the spare on another trip.|The narrator will always hide all paddles.|The narrator will forget boats exist.|The narrator will ask for no equipment.|Checking the spare supports valuing it.","Which claim goes beyond the narrator\u2019s knowledge in this text?|Uncle secretly felt angry the whole time.|The narrator once doubted the spare.|The paddle cracked.|They reached shore.|Uncle\u2019s private feelings are not stated."],t.s)
B.aa4=new A.cg("The Spare Paddle","Q1 \xb7 narrative, prediction and point of view","I thought Uncle was wasting space when he packed a spare paddle. Halfway across the calm lake, our old paddle cracked. Uncle reached for the spare without a word. I took it and helped paddle to shore. Next trip, I checked the spare before Uncle could ask.",B.aIC,"A first-person narrator uses I to tell an experience. We learn this narrator\u2019s thoughts directly, but not every thought Uncle has. A character can change after an event. Compare the opening thought with the last action. Predictions should fit that change, not add an unrelated adventure.","How does the narrator change? At first, the spare seems a waste. It helps after the crack. Checking it next trip shows the narrator now values being prepared.","Predict whether the narrator will object to a spare next time. Use the final line as a clue.","The narrator probably will not object. Checking the spare without being asked shows a new appreciation for it.","First person does not mean the writer is the real child. This is an original fictional narrator. Do not invent Uncle\u2019s thoughts.","Compare beginning and ending evidence to explain a narrator\u2019s change.",B.aSR,B.b_G)
B.aMY=s(["Use common prefixes and suffixes.","Confirm a word\u2019s meaning through context.","Explain how a word describes an action."],t.s)
B.ary=s(["re + use|Reused means used again for library cards.","un + read + able|Unreadable explains the old label\u2019s problem.","Context check|Readable from the door supports clear."],t.s)
B.au_=s(["What is the box used for?|Library cards|Wet fish|Cooking rice|Flower soil|The opening sentence gives library cards.","What does re- mean in reused?|Again|Not|Before|Without|The box is used again for a new purpose.","What was wrong with the old label?|It was unreadable.|It was too cold.|It was full of water.|It was a living plant.|Unreadable states the problem.","What does -able contribute in readable?|Able to be|Never|A person who|Many times|Readable means able to be read.","Why did Mina rewrite the label?|People could not read the old one.|The cards were wet.|The box had no bottom.|She disliked all books.|Unreadable gives the reason.","What kind of letters did she use?|Large|Invisible|Tiny|Upside-down|She rewrote it in large letters.","Which word tells how she placed the box?|Carefully|Wooden|Library|Door|Carefully modifies the placing action.","Where could people read the new label from?|The door|Another town|Inside a closed bag|Under the floor|The last line says from the door.","Which meaning of clear fits this passage?|Easy to read|Empty of clouds|Made of glass|Free of people|The label can be read from the door.","If a page is reread, what happens?|It is read again.|It is never read.|It loses all words.|It becomes a reader.|Re- plus read gives read again.","Which method gives the best word meaning?|Use word parts and check the surrounding sentence.|Guess from the first letter only.|Ignore the sentence.|Choose the longest dictionary entry.|Both parts and context constrain the meaning."],t.s)
B.aaE=new A.cg("The Unlocked Word Box","Q1 \xb7 vocabulary, context and word parts","The class reused a wooden box for library cards. Its label was unreadable, so Mina rewrote it in large letters. She carefully placed the box beside the books. The new label was clear: everyone could read it from the door.",B.aMY,"Re- often means again: reused means used again. Un- can mean not: unreadable means not readable. The ending -able can mean able to be. The nearby large letters and everyone could read it confirm clear means easy to read here. Carefully tells how Mina placed the box.","Explain unreadable. Start with read. Add -able: able to be read. Add un-: not able to be read. Mina rewrites the label because it was hard to read.","Use word parts to explain reused. Then find the clue for clear.","Reused means used again. Everyone could read it from the door shows the new writing is easy to see and read.","Do not assume every word beginning re has this prefix. Check the base word and context. Clear here is about the label, not empty sky or transparent glass.","Try word parts, then check the meaning against the sentence.",B.ary,B.au_)
B.aqf=s(["Locate key facts in a news report.","Distinguish opinion from checkable information.","Keep a claim within the evidence."],t.s)
B.ar8=s(["Fact|Opened Monday beside the gate.","Count|The parent group donated six benches.","Opinion and limit|Ella says nicest; use numbers are not yet known."],t.s)
B.aFm=s(["What opened?|A covered waiting area|A swimming pool|A new library|A fruit stall|The first news sentence names the area.","When did it open?|Monday|Tuesday|Friday|Sunday|Monday is the stated day.","Where is it?|Beside the school gate|On the roof|Across a river|Inside the kitchen|The report gives the school gate.","Who donated benches?|The parent group|Ella alone|A bus company|A class pet|The parent group is named as donor.","How many benches were donated?|Six|Two|Eight|Twelve|The count is six.","When may pupils wait there?|After dismissal|Only at midnight|Before sunrise only|Never|The principal gives after dismissal.","Which phrase is an opinion?|The nicest place in school|Opened on Monday|Six benches|Beside the school gate|Nicest is a personal judgment.","Who calls it nicest?|Ella|Every pupil|The news writer|The entire parent group|The quotation is attributed to Ella.","Which claim is not supported?|Most pupils already use the area.|The area is covered.|Benches were donated.|It opened Monday.|There is no use count to support most pupils.","Which detail could be checked by counting?|Six benches|Nicest place|Most beautiful roof|Best seat ever|A bench count can verify six.","Which summary preserves the evidence?|A covered area opened Monday with six donated benches.|Everyone agrees it is the best place.|Six pupils built the gate.|Most pupils have already used it.|Only the first keeps the report\u2019s known facts."],t.s)
B.aa2=new A.cg("News from the School Gate","Q2 \xb7 news, fact and opinion",'SCHOOL NEWS: A new covered waiting area opened on Monday beside the school gate. The parent group donated six benches. Principal Ramos said pupils may wait there after dismissal. One pupil, Ella, said, "It is the nicest place in school." No waiting-area use count has been collected yet.',B.aqf,"News should make important facts easy to find: what, when, where and who. A fact can be checked against records or observation. An opinion expresses a judgment, such as nicest. A quotation names who said something; quoting an opinion does not make the judgment a proved fact.","Is nicest a fact about the building? Compare it with six benches. Six can be counted. Nicest depends on personal judgment, so it is Ella\u2019s opinion.","Find the opening day and the donor. Can we say most pupils already use the area?","It opened Monday. The parent group donated benches. We cannot claim most pupils use it: no count has been collected.","Six benches does not mean six pupils attended. Ella\u2019s view is one pupil\u2019s, not necessarily everyone\u2019s.","Find checkable details. Attribute opinions. Do not invent missing data.",B.ar8,B.aFm)
B.apJ=s(["Choose verbs that agree with the subject.","Use time clues for tense.","Track pronoun reference clearly."],t.s)
B.aOi=s(["Past|Yesterday: Mara found; she gave.","Present agreement|The box sits; pupils ask.","Reference|Them points to two keys, not Mr. Uy."],t.s)
B.aRn=s(["What did Mara find?|Two keys|One wallet|Three books|A watch|The opening sentence names two keys.","Where did she find them?|Near the office|In a river|On a roof|At a market|Near the office gives the place.","Who received the keys?|Mr. Uy|Ella|A driver|Mara\u2019s brother|She gave them to Mr. Uy.","What does She refer to?|Mara|The box|The keys|The office|Mara is the person named before She.","What does them refer to?|The two keys|Two pupils|The office|Mr. Uy|Them replaces the plural keys.","Which word signals past time?|Yesterday|Today|Small|Desk|Yesterday places the event in past time.","Choose the correct present verb: The box ___ on his desk.|Sits|Sit|Sitting only|Were sit|The singular box takes sits.","Choose the correct present verb: Pupils ___ at the office.|Ask|Asks|Asking only|Was ask|Plural pupils takes ask.","Which past sentence is correct?|Yesterday Mara found two keys.|Yesterday Mara finds two keys.|Yesterday Mara finding two keys.|Yesterday Mara will find two keys.|Found matches yesterday.","Who does he refer to in Today, he keeps them?|Mr. Uy|Mara|A pupil not named|The box|Mr. Uy is the male person who received the keys.","Which rewrite keeps the meaning clear?|Mr. Uy keeps the two keys in a box.|The keys keep Mr. Uy in a box.|Mara is two keys.|The desk asks at the office.|Naming both person and object preserves the pronoun links."],t.s)
B.aaS=new A.cg("Messages about the Lost Keys","Q2 \xb7 agreement, tense and pronoun reference","Yesterday, Mara found two keys near the office. She gave them to Mr. Uy. Today, he keeps them in a small box. The box sits on his desk. Pupils ask at the office if they have lost their keys.",B.apJ,"Yesterday places found and gave in the past. Today places keeps and sits in the present. One box sits; several pupils ask. A pronoun must clearly match its noun. She means Mara, he means Mr. Uy, and them means the two keys. Read nearby sentences to track these links.","Why The box sits? Box is one thing. In this present-tense sentence, sits agrees with it. Pupils ask has no s on ask because pupils is plural.","Explain them in She gave them to Mr. Uy. Change Yesterday Mara finds to a past sentence.","Them means the two keys. Yesterday Mara found two keys uses found, the past form of find.","Do not write two keys sits. Keys is plural. Do not change them to him when referring to the keys.","Use the time clue, match subject and verb, and keep each pronoun clear.",B.aOi,B.aRn)
B.aXo=s(["Explain a metaphor.","Identify sensory language.","Read a poem with meaningful phrasing."],t.s)
B.aKQ=s(["Metaphor|Morning as painter makes sunlight like brushwork.","Sight|Gold across the wall gives a light-and-color image.","Sound|The rooster\u2019s rough alarm suggests a harsh call."],t.s)
B.aWN=s(["What is morning compared to?|A painter|A driver|A fisherman|A cook|The first line names a painter.","Which word names the light\u2019s color?|Gold|Rough|Quiet|Hall|Gold describes the light on the wall.","Which animal appears?|A rooster|A dog|A cat|A goat|A rooster rings its rough alarm.","Which phrase appeals most to hearing?|Rough alarm|Gold across the wall|Soft shadows|Along the hall|Alarm points to the rooster\u2019s sound.","Which phrase appeals most to sight?|Gold across the wall|Rough alarm|Quiet voice|A whispered word|Gold and wall form a visual image.","What happens to the shadows?|They shrink.|They grow into trees.|They sing.|They fall as rain.|The last line states shrink.","Why is the first line a metaphor?|It compares morning to a painter without like or as.|It counts painters.|It gives a date.|It asks a question.|The line directly identifies one thing with another.","Is there a real painter stated in the poem?|No, painter is an imagined comparison.|Yes, a named worker.|Yes, the rooster paints.|Yes, the hall paints.|The metaphor describes morning light.","What best explains Brushing gold?|Sunlight spreads warm color on the wall.|Someone buys a gold brush.|The wall becomes solid gold.|A rooster cleans the floor.|The image connects sunlight with paint.","Which reading suits quiet morning and a rough call?|Read gently, then emphasize the rooster\u2019s call.|Shout every word equally.|Ignore all meaning.|Stop after each letter.|Changing expression can reflect the contrast.","Which explanation stays anchored to the poem?|Growing morning light makes shadows smaller.|The poem reports a night storm.|The rooster owns a clock shop.|Gold coins cover the floor.|Morning light and shrinking shadows support the first explanation."],t.s)
B.aaj=new A.cg("Morning Is a Quiet Painter","Q3 \xb7 poetry, imagery and figurative language","Morning is a quiet painter,\nBrushing gold across the wall.\nA rooster rings its rough alarm;\nSoft shadows shrink along the hall.",B.aXo,"A metaphor compares without like or as. Morning is a quiet painter imagines sunlight as paint. It does not say a real person paints the wall. Imagery helps us imagine sight or sound. Gold suggests warm light. Rough alarm suggests the rooster\u2019s harsh call. Pause where meaning needs it, not automatically after every word.","Why call morning a painter? Link Brushing gold with the wall. Sunlight spreads color over a surface like a painter\u2019s brush. That explains the comparison.","Find one sound image and one sight image. Explain each.","Rough alarm suggests the rooster\u2019s sound. Soft shadows shrink suggests the changing shapes we see as light grows.","Do not claim the rooster holds an alarm clock. A poem\u2019s comparison is not a literal news report.","Name a figure of speech, then explain what its words help us imagine.",B.aKQ,B.aWN)
B.aCu=s(["Identify information a reader needs.","Revise vague references.","Organize a clear informational notice."],t.s)
B.b2d=s(["Audience|Grade 5 pupils returning borrowed books.","Event details|Thursday, 3 p.m., library.","Action|Bring the class book and give it to Ms. Lim."],t.s)
B.aUW=s(["Who is the notice mainly for?|Grade 5 pupils with borrowed class books|All bus drivers|Only cooks|Every shop owner|Grade 5 Book Return Day names the audience and task.","When is the return event?|Thursday at 3 p.m.|Monday at noon|Friday at 8 a.m.|Sunday at midnight|The revised notice states Thursday, 3 p.m.","Where is the event?|In the library|At the beach|On a bus|In the garden|The revised notice gives the library.","What should pupils bring?|Their borrowed class book|A pot|A kite|A bowl|The action sentence names the item.","Who receives the books?|Ms. Lim|A guard|A seller|A doctor|Give it to Ms. Lim names the receiver.","Where does Ms. Lim receive returns?|At the return desk|Behind the gate|In a pond|Under a tree|The notice gives the return desk.","Who can pupils ask for help before Thursday?|Their class teacher|Only a stranger|A bus driver|The book itself|The final sentence names the class teacher.","Why is Come soon weak?|It gives no exact day or time.|It has too many dates.|It names too many rooms.|It is a complete map.|Soon is not precise enough for an event notice.","Which revision best replaces Bring it?|Bring your borrowed class book.|Bring some stuff.|Bring whatever.|Bring it soonish.|The first revision identifies the required object.","What does revision improve most here?|Useful details for readers|The price of the books|The length of the school day|The color of the library|The revised notice answers practical questions.","Which summary gives a correct action plan?|Bring the class book to Ms. Lim in the library Thursday at 3 p.m.|Meet a driver Sunday with a pot.|Give all books to a cook Monday.|Wait until Friday to ask what to bring.|Only the first keeps the time, place, item and receiver correct."],t.s)
B.aak=new A.cg("A Better Library Notice","Q4 \xb7 informational writing, audience and revision","Draft notice: Come soon. Bring it. We will be there.\nRevised notice: Grade 5 Book Return Day is Thursday, 3 p.m., in the library. Bring your borrowed class book. Give it to Ms. Lim at the return desk. If you need help, ask your class teacher before Thursday.",B.aCu,"Writing has an audience: the people who will read it. A book-return notice must say who, what, when, where and what to do. Soon and it in the draft are vague because nothing identifies the time or item. Revision adds useful details, not just extra words.","Fix Bring it. Ask what it means. The revised notice says borrowed class book. Now readers can choose the right item.","Find the time, place and person who receives books. Explain why Come soon is not enough.","Thursday at 3 p.m.; the library; Ms. Lim at the return desk. Come soon gives no exact day or time.","Do not confuse the class teacher, who can help, with Ms. Lim, who receives returns. More words help only if they add meaning.","Revise for the reader: replace vague words with the exact information needed.",B.b2d,B.aUW)
B.azf=s([B.aa4,B.aaE,B.aa2,B.aaS,B.aaj,B.aak],t.PF)
B.aHo=s(["Explain a character\u2019s change.","Infer a theme from repeated action.","Distinguish literal and figurative meaning."],t.s)
B.aF9=s(["Before|Dano resents the longer walk.","Turning event|He helps Aling Cora carry a basket.","After|He waits each morning; the walk feels worthwhile."],t.s)
B.aPQ=s(["Why is the bridge closed?|For repairs|For a race|For a market|For a concert|During repairs explains the closure.","What does Dano first dislike?|The long walk around the creek|Every basket|The village\u2019s name|The morning sky|He complains about the detour.","Who carries heavy baskets?|Aling Cora|Dano\u2019s teacher|A guard|A seller not named|The text names Aling Cora.","What does Dano take?|One basket|The bridge|A bicycle|A lantern|He takes one basket to help.","How does he walk with her?|He matches her slow steps.|He races ahead.|He refuses to move.|He runs into the creek.|Matched her slow steps shows his pace.","Which phrase shows a repeated habit?|Each morning afterward|During repairs|Then he saw|A footbridge|Each morning indicates regular action.","Is the bridge open at the end?|No|Yes, after a week|Yes, before he helps|Yes, at noon|The bridge was still closed is explicit.","What does wasted mean in the last line?|Without useful purpose|Full of paper litter|Made of water|Too short|Dano no longer judges the time as pointless.","Which theme fits the whole story?|Helping can give meaning to a difficulty.|All repairs finish quickly.|Long walks must always be avoided.|Baskets should never be shared.|The obstacle remains, but helping changes Dano\u2019s view.","Which detail best proves a change of attitude?|He waits by her door each morning.|There is a creek.|The bridge joins two halves.|The baskets are heavy.|His new willing habit contrasts with his complaint.","Why is The bridge opened a poor summary ending?|It contradicts the stated closed bridge.|It is too short to be a sentence.|It uses no nouns.|It repeats the opening exactly.|The actual ending says still closed."],t.s)
B.aam=new A.cg("The Bridge in the Story","Q1 \xb7 narrative structure, theme and inference","A footbridge joined two halves of a village. During repairs, Dano complained about the long walk around the creek. Then he saw Aling Cora carrying heavy baskets along that path. Dano took one basket and matched her slow steps. Each morning afterward, he waited by her door. The bridge was still closed, but the walk no longer felt wasted.",B.aHo,"A theme is a message suggested by the whole story, not just a topic such as bridges. Track Dano\u2019s first attitude, the turning event and his later habit. A statement like Helping others can change how we see a difficulty fits those details. Wasted describes his judgment of time, not rubbish.","Does the repaired bridge cause the change? No: it is still closed at the end. Helping Aling Cora gives the walk meaning, so the change is in Dano\u2019s attitude.","Choose two details that show more than a one-time act of help.","Each morning afterward shows repetition. He waited by her door shows that he starts helping without being asked.","Do not say the bridge opens at the end. A theme must fit the ending as well as the opening.","Explain a theme with a character\u2019s change and the events that support it.",B.aF9,B.aPQ)
B.aKs=s(["Use conjunctions to express relationships.","Resolve unclear pronouns by naming nouns.","Recognize main and dependent clauses."],t.s)
B.aMe=s(["Reason|Because connects the first visit with the map.","Contrast|Although connects crowding with visible signs.","Condition|If visitors needed help introduces when help was given."],t.s)
B.av8=s(["What does the guide give?|A map|A ticket book|A basket|A lamp|The guide gives Lani a map.","Where is the visit?|A museum|A farm|A beach|A factory|Museum is named in the first sentence.","What does Lani do with the map?|Folds it|Burns it|Throws it in water|Sells it|Lani folds the map.","Which conjunction gives a reason?|Because|Although|If|And|Because introduces the reason for giving the map.","Which conjunction shows contrast?|Although|Because|If|And|Although contrasts crowding with visible signs.","Which word introduces a condition?|If|The|Easy|Their|If sets the condition of needing help.","Who answers questions?|An attendant|A bus driver|A cook|A child at home|The final clause names an attendant.","Which is an incomplete thought alone?|Although the hall was crowded|The signs were easy to see.|Lani folded the map.|An attendant answered.|The although clause depends on a main clause.","What is weak about she in the first sentence?|It could mean Lani or the guide.|It cannot refer to a person.|It is a past-tense verb.|It always means the map.|Two possible people make the reference unclear.","Which rewrite clearly makes Lani the new visitor?|Because Lani was visiting for the first time, the guide gave her a map.|She gave her it because she was there.|Because she she first map.|The map visited the guide.|The named reason clause resolves the ambiguity.","Which joined sentence expresses a contrast?|Although the hall was crowded, the signs were clear.|Because the hall was crowded.|If the signs.|And although because.|Only the first is complete and uses a contrast connector."],t.s)
B.aa3=new A.cg("A Clear Map for the Visitor","Q1 \xb7 grammar, reference and complex sentences","The guide gave Lani a map because she was visiting the museum for the first time. Lani thanked the guide and folded the map. Although the hall was crowded, the signs were easy to see. If visitors needed help, an attendant answered their questions.",B.aKs,"Because gives a reason. Although shows a contrast. If sets a condition. A dependent clause such as Although the hall was crowded needs a main clause to finish the thought. Pronouns can be unclear: she in the first sentence could refer to Lani or the guide. Rewrite with Lani to remove doubt.","Read Although the hall was crowded, the signs were easy to see. Crowding might make seeing hard, yet the signs stayed visible. Although expresses that contrast.","Rewrite the first sentence so Lani is clearly the first-time visitor.","Because Lani was visiting the museum for the first time, the guide gave her a map. Naming Lani in the reason clause removes the unclear she.","Do not leave Because Lani was visiting as a sentence by itself. Do not insist she is unambiguous in the original.","Choose connectors for meaning. Complete dependent clauses. Make references clear.",B.aMe,B.av8)
B.arL=s(["Identify a persuasive appeal.","Read visual and small-print information.","Separate advertising claims from evidence."],t.s)
B.au2=s(["Bandwagon|Everyone has one pressures the reader to join.","Identity appeal|Smart pupils associates buying with intelligence.","Qualification|Cap sold separately adds a practical limit."],t.s)
B.aMm=s(["What product is advertised?|A bottle|A book|A pair of shoes|A desk|BrightBottle is shown as a bottle.","What action does the ad mainly encourage?|Choosing or buying BrightBottle|Returning a library book|Planting beans|Repairing a gate|The headline directs pupils to choose the product.","Which line uses a bandwagon appeal?|Everyone has one.|Bottle sold separately from the cap.|Paid advertisement.|No survey is shown.|Everyone has one relies on supposed popularity.","Which word links the product to intelligence?|Smart|Blue|Cap|Paid|Smart pupils suggests an intelligence identity.","What does the child hold?|A shining blue bottle|A red cap|A textbook|A school gate|The visual description gives a blue bottle.","What symbol appears beside the child?|A school badge|A bus ticket|A weather map|A fish|The description includes a school badge.","What is sold separately?|The cap|The school|The child|The badge\u2019s name|The small print says the bottle is sold separately from the cap.","What evidence is missing for Everyone has one?|A survey or ownership count|A blue color|A smile|A large heading|A universal ownership claim needs relevant data.","Does the badge alone prove school approval?|No, approval needs separate confirmation.|Yes, all badges prove approval.|Yes, any blue item is approved.|No school can approve anything.|A suggestive visual is not proof of endorsement.","Which buyer question follows from the small print?|What is the total cost with the cap?|Can the child on the poster sing?|What is the badge\u2019s favorite color?|How tall is the school gate?|Separate sale makes the complete price important.","Which conclusion is supported?|The poster tries to persuade but gives no test results.|The bottle makes every child smarter.|Every pupil owns one.|The school has officially approved it.|The first describes the message without accepting unsupported claims."],t.s)
B.aas=new A.cg("Reading the Refill Advertisement","Q2 \xb7 media, persuasion and evidence",'Advertisement: SMART PUPILS CHOOSE BRIGHTBOTTLE! "Everyone has one," says the poster. A smiling child holds a shining blue bottle beside a school badge. Small print says: "Paid advertisement. Bottle sold separately from the cap." No survey or test result is shown.',B.arL,"An advertisement tries to influence a choice. Everyone has one uses a bandwagon appeal: join because others supposedly do. Smart pupils links a product to a valued identity without proving the link. A badge can suggest school approval, but the image alone does not establish approval. Read small print.","Is everyone has one proved? Look for a count or survey. None is shown. It is an unsupported broad claim, not evidence that every pupil owns the bottle.","Name one emotional appeal and one practical detail from the small print.","Smart pupils appeals to wanting to seem smart. The cap is sold separately, so a buyer should check the full cost.","A smiling child does not prove a bottle improves grades. Do not ignore a qualification just because its print is small.","Ask what a message wants you to do, how it persuades, and what evidence is missing.",B.au2,B.aMm)
B.asi=s(["Combine information from two texts.","Keep events in time order.","Write a summary without adding claims."],t.s)
B.aLH=s(["Monday|A: handle tightened; B: drips collected.","Tuesday|A: still dripping, reported; B: collection continued.","Wednesday|B: repaired, no longer dripping."],t.s)
B.b2r=s(["Where was the dripping tap?|Near Room 6|Beside the town hall|In the library roof|On a bus|Note A names Room 6.","Who wrote Note A?|The caretaker|The class pet|A driver|A shopper|The label identifies a caretaker\u2019s log.","What did the caretaker first do?|Tighten the handle|Replace the whole school|Close the garden|Count every bucket|Monday\u2019s log records tightening.","Did the tap still drip Tuesday?|Yes|No|Only in a dream|The notes say nothing about Tuesday|Note A explicitly says still dripped.","What did pupils use to catch drips?|A bowl|A paper bag|A shoe|A book|Note B says a bowl.","What was the caught water used for?|The garden|A cooking contest|Cleaning a bus|A fish tank sale|Note B names the garden.","When was the tap repaired?|Wednesday|Monday|Sunday|Friday|Note B gives Wednesday.","Which information appears only in Note B?|The completed Wednesday repair|The Monday drip|The handle tightening|The Tuesday repair request|Only the class record supplies Wednesday\u2019s outcome.","What do both notes help establish?|The tap dripped on Monday and Tuesday.|The exact liters saved|The cost of repair|The worker\u2019s full name|Both accounts cover the two dripping days.","Which claim is not supported?|The class saved exactly fifty liters.|Pupils caught drips.|The tap was reported.|Wednesday\u2019s repair stopped the drip.|Neither text provides a volume measurement.","Which summary combines both sources fairly?|A reported leak was caught for garden use until a Wednesday repair stopped it.|Tightening fixed the tap Monday.|No one reported the leak.|Pupils repaired every tap in school.|The first includes the problem, interim action and result without invention."],t.s)
B.aah=new A.cg("Two Notes about School Water","Q2 \xb7 informational comparison and summary",'Note A, caretaker\u2019s log: "Monday: tap near Room 6 dripped. I tightened its handle. Tuesday: the tap still dripped. I reported it for repair."\nNote B, class record: "On Monday and Tuesday, we used a bowl to catch clean drips for the garden. On Wednesday, the tap was repaired and no longer dripped."',B.asi,"Synthesis combines related information. Identify each source and compare its dates. Note A tells the first response and repair request. Note B adds collection and Wednesday\u2019s result. A summary should keep the shared problem and key outcome. Neither note measures how much water was saved.","Did tightening fix the tap on Monday? Note A says it still dripped Tuesday. Note B places repair on Wednesday. Together the notes show the first attempt did not fully work.","Write a two-sentence summary including the problem and result.","A tap dripped on Monday and Tuesday despite a tightened handle, so it was reported for repair. Pupils caught water for the garden until Wednesday\u2019s repair stopped the drip.","Do not claim a number of liters saved. No volume is given. Do not confuse a repair request with a completed repair.","Compare dates and roles, combine compatible details, and preserve limits.",B.aLH,B.b2r)
B.arW=s(["Identify claim, evidence and proposed action.","Recognize a limitation in an argument.","Plan clear and respectful delivery."],t.s)
B.aSQ=s(["Need|Five-day count records pupils in direct sun.","Proposal|Two movable screens offer near-term shade.","Next step|Get prices and discuss at Friday\u2019s meeting."],t.s)
B.b0B=s(["Who is addressed?|Classmates|Only shop owners|A national court|A sports team abroad|The speech opens Classmates.","What problem is described?|Little shade at noon|Too many books|A broken tap|Missing pencils|The waiting path has little shade.","How long was the class check?|Five days|One year|Two hours|Ten weeks|The speech says five-day class check.","At least how many pupils stood in sun each checked day?|Eight|Two|Four|Twenty|At least eight is the stated minimum.","What is proposed?|Two movable shade screens|Ten buses|A new lake|Closing all classes|The speaker proposes two screens.","Why use screens while waiting?|Trees take time to grow.|Screens are living trees.|The path is indoors.|All trees have vanished.|The proposal explicitly bridges the wait for trees.","What must be obtained before deciding?|A price estimate|A poetry prize|A bus license|A weather map of every country|The speaker asks for an estimate.","When should the plan be discussed?|Friday\u2019s class meeting|Sunday at a market|Next year only|At midnight|The final request names Friday\u2019s meeting.","Which evidence is relevant to the shade proposal?|The count of pupils in direct sun|A pupil\u2019s favorite song|The color of a notebook|The number of letters in Friday|The sun-exposure count supports the identified need.","Which conclusion would overreach the evidence?|Every school has this exact shade problem.|This path had pupils in sun during the check.|A cost estimate is needed.|The speaker proposes screens.|One class\u2019s short check cannot establish a universal claim.","Which delivery best suits the request?|Speak clearly and emphasize the practical next step.|Shout insults at doubters.|Race through the count silently.|Ignore the audience.|Respectful, clear delivery supports a reasoned class decision."],t.s)
B.aaY=new A.cg("A Short Speech for Shade","Q3 \xb7 persuasive writing and oral delivery","Classmates, our waiting path has little shade at noon. During our five-day class check, at least eight pupils stood in direct sun each day. I propose two movable shade screens while we wait for trees to grow. Screens cost money, so let us ask for a price estimate before deciding. Please discuss this plan at Friday\u2019s class meeting.",B.arW,"A persuasive speech gives reasons for an action. The proposal is two movable screens. The five-day check supports a local need, not a claim about every school. Acknowledging cost helps readers judge the plan. Speak at a steady pace and stress the proposal and request rather than shouting.","How does the evidence support the proposal? The count shows pupils waiting in direct sun. Screens could offer shade now, while trees take time. A price estimate is still needed.","Name one supporting detail and one question still unanswered.","At least eight pupils stood in sun each day of the check. The cost of the screens is not yet known.","Five days of one class\u2019s checks cannot prove conditions in all schools. A proposal is not an already completed project.","Make a clear claim, give relevant evidence, admit limits and ask for a practical next step.",B.aSQ,B.b0B)
B.aI6=s(["Read a simple report\u2019s question and method.","Compare results accurately.","Write a conclusion limited to the data."],t.s)
B.b2o=s(["Source|Class loan log, Monday to Friday.","Results|Stories 18; information 12; poems 6 loans.","Limit|One class, one week; loans do not measure everyone\u2019s preference."],t.s)
B.aN8=s(["What question does the report ask?|Which books had most loans this week?|Which books cost most?|Who wrote every poem?|Where is the nearest shop?|The stated question concerns weekly borrowing.","What source was counted?|The class loan log|A national survey|A shop receipt|A weather diary|The method names the class loan log.","What days were included?|Monday to Friday|Only Sunday|One month of Saturdays|Every day for a year|The method gives Monday to Friday.","Were school-library loans counted?|No|Yes, all of them|Only holiday loans|Only teacher loans|The method explicitly excludes the school library.","How many story loans were there?|18|12|6|36|The result gives 18.","Which category had 12 loans?|Information books|Stories|Poems|Maps|Information books had 12.","Which category had the fewest loans?|Poems|Stories|Information books|All tied|Six is less than twelve and eighteen.","Which section tells how the data was gathered?|Method|Title only|Greeting|Signature|Method explains the count and source.","Which conclusion matches the data?|Stories had most loans in this class this week.|Every child\u2019s favorite book is a story.|No one reads poems.|All schools have 18 story readers.|The first includes the actual population, time and measure.","Why does 18 loans not prove 18 different readers?|One person might borrow more than one book.|Logs cannot contain numbers.|Stories never have readers.|All pupils borrow exactly once.|A loan count can include repeated borrowers.","Which acknowledgment is most useful when sharing the figures?|Source: our class loan log, Monday to Friday.|I found numbers somewhere.|No source is needed.|Source: every pupil in the world.|Naming the actual source and period allows checking."],t.s)
B.aag=new A.cg("The Mini Inquiry Report","Q4 \xb7 inquiry, sources, report and revision","Question: Which class books were borrowed most this week?\nMethod: We counted entries in our class loan log from Monday to Friday. We did not count the school library.\nResult: Stories had 18 loans; information books had 12; poems had 6.\nConclusion: Stories had the most loans in our class this week. We cannot use this one week to describe every pupil\u2019s favorite books.",B.aI6,"An inquiry starts with a clear question. A method explains where information came from so readers can judge it. Results report counts; a conclusion explains what those counts support. Loans are not the same as different readers or favorite books. Name the source when you use its results.","Which category had most loans? Compare 18, 12 and 6. Eighteen is largest, so stories lead. Add our class this week to keep the conclusion within the sample.","Explain why All pupils love stories is not a fair conclusion.","The log counts loans in one class for one week, not everyone\u2019s feelings. A pupil could borrow more than one book.","Do not confuse 18 loans with 18 different pupils. Do not describe the whole school when only one class was counted.","Question, method, results and limited conclusion make a report readers can check.",B.b2o,B.aN8)
B.b0t=s([B.aam,B.aa3,B.aas,B.aah,B.aaY,B.aag],t.PF)
B.aEW=s(["Explain imagery using specific words.","Connect a speaker\u2019s observation to a value.","Draft a short original poetic comparison."],t.s)
B.aSZ=s(["Sound|Coins click places us in a waiting traveler\u2019s moment.","Action|Mother leaves a nearer seat for someone slow.","Reflection|More than bread means the speaker learns consideration."],t.s)
B.av3=s(["Where does the poem place the speaker?|At a transport stop|Inside a fishing boat|In a courtroom|On a mountain summit|The title and first line locate a stop.","What is wrapped in cloth?|Bread|Books|Fish|Shoes|The cloth is around our bread.","Which phrase creates a sound image?|Coins click softly|Gray then gold|Nearer seat|Warm and slow|Click appeals to hearing.","Which phrase most directly creates a color image?|Gray then gold|Someone slow|A small example|Our bread|Gray and gold are visual colors.","What does the mother leave for another person?|A nearer seat|All her coins|A school bag|The loaf of bread|She leaves the nearer seat for someone slow.","Who is the poem\u2019s speaker?|The child observing the mother|An all-knowing transport official|The mother speaking about her own mother|A named author outside the poem|My mother and I carry place the voice with the child.","What kind of language is folds the dawn?|Figurative comparison|A bus timetable|A literal folding instruction|A scientific definition|The phrase treats dawn like a foldable object.","Which value is supported by the seat detail?|Consideration for another traveler|Winning at any cost|Dislike of bread|Avoiding all transport|Yielding a convenient seat shows consideration.","What does more than bread suggest?|The child carries a lesson as well as food.|The bag holds exactly two loaves.|The bread is made of coins.|The school sells every seat.|The following line names a small example, a learned value.","Which interpretation uses evidence rather than a label alone?|The mother\u2019s choice teaches care because she gives up a nearer seat.|The poem is kind because every poem is kind.|The speaker is wealthy because there are coins.|The road is literally alive.|The first links a value to a specific action.","Which original line uses a meaningful poetic comparison?|The bus window frames a moving morning.|The bus is a bus.|I studied very well today.|Transport is defined as transport.|Framing a moving morning compares the view to a picture."],t.s)
B.aaJ=new A.cg("Poem at the Jeepney Stop","Q1 \xb7 Philippine poetry; imagery, voice and values","At the stop, my mother folds the dawn\ninside a cloth around our bread.\nCoins click softly in her palm;\nthe road wakes, gray then gold ahead.\nShe takes the longer line to leave\nthe nearer seat for someone slow.\nI carry more than bread to school:\na small example, warm and slow.",B.aEW,"A poem\u2019s speaker is the voice within the text, not automatically its author. Images appeal to senses: coins click suggests hearing, gray then gold suggests sight. Folds the dawn imagines morning warmth held with bread. The final lines shift from a physical object to a lesson learned through the mother\u2019s considerate action.","What is carried besides bread? The final line names an example. Link it to leaving a nearer seat for someone who moves slowly. The speaker carries a lesson in consideration.","Explain coins click and a small example. Then write one new comparison for something you notice on a local journey.","Coins click creates a sound image. A small example means the mother\u2019s quiet act teaches kindness. An original comparison could be: The bus window frames a moving morning.","Do not state the mother literally folds the time of day. A local setting alone does not establish a poem\u2019s value; show how the action and language express it.","Read images, action and reflection together to explain a poem\u2019s meaning.",B.aSZ,B.av3)
B.aGu=s(["Explain how revision adds specific imagery.","Connect form and word choice to purpose.","Revise a vague line for a chosen audience."],t.s)
B.at_=s(["Vague draft|Nice and moves give little sensory detail.","Linked image|Stitches and silver thread build a shared comparison.","Purposeful action|Leaving no wrapper shows care for the river."],t.s)
B.aLu=s(["Which word in the draft is vague praise?|Nice|River|It|Moves|Nice gives no specific reason or image.","What is the river compared to in the revision?|A silver thread|A stone wall|A cooking fire|A locked box|The second line names a silver thread.","Which verb connects with the thread image?|Stitches|Leave|Makes|Like|Stitches belongs with thread and joins places.","What does the speaker avoid leaving?|A wrapper|A tree|A house|A field|The speaker leaves no wrapper on the bank.","What places does the river pass?|House and tree|Only a stadium|A distant planet|Only a factory|Past house and tree states these details.","Which phrase suggests the river\u2019s shape?|Winding path|I like it|No wrapper|Our audience|Winding suggests bends.","Which revision process is most useful here?|Replace vague words with specific images.|Add rare words at random.|Remove every concrete detail.|Repeat nice five times.|Specific images clarify the experience.","Why read a poem aloud during revision?|To hear rhythm and phrasing|To prove every comparison is factual|To avoid checking meaning|To count only capital letters|Sound helps test line flow and emphasis.","Which new line gives a clear comparison for movement?|It bends like a ribbon past our field.|It is very extremely nice.|It moves in a moving way.|The river is a definition.|The ribbon image makes the movement and shape concrete.","How does the wrapper line contribute?|It turns appreciation into an act of care.|It proves the river makes paper.|It changes the poem into a price list.|It removes the river setting.|The speaker acts to protect the place admired.","Which claim takes the revision too literally?|The river uses a needle to sew fields.|The river connects places in the image.|The river passes houses.|The speaker values a clean bank.|Stitches is a metaphor, not literal needlework."],t.s)
B.aaI=new A.cg("Revising the River Poem","Q1 \xb7 creating and revising poetry for an audience","Draft: The river is nice. It moves. I like it.\nRevision: The river stitches fields together,\na silver thread past house and tree.\nI leave no wrapper on its bank;\nits winding path makes room for me.",B.aGu,"Poetry revision changes meaning and effect, not just spelling. Nice is vague. Stitches and thread create a linked comparison of a river joining places. The wrapper line connects appreciation with action. For classmates, familiar images can communicate more clearly than unexplained rare words. Try reading aloud to hear line breaks and rhythm.","Why is silver thread more vivid than nice? It gives a shape, color and connection. Paired with stitches, it builds one coherent image instead of unrelated decorations.","Revise It moves using one clear image. Explain your choice.","One possible line is: It bends like a ribbon past our field. Ribbon suggests a long flexible shape. This is a new comparison, not a quotation from the passage.","Do not pile up images that conflict without purpose. A river that stitches is figurative, not evidence that water sews.","Replace vague praise with concrete images and purposeful action; read aloud and revise.",B.at_,B.aLu)
B.ayS=s(["Analyze a character\u2019s choice and conflict.","Infer a communal value without stereotyping.","Explain how an ending develops a theme."],t.s)
B.aFA=s(["Wish|Belen wants the last page for a fiesta drawing.","Choice|She gives the blank page to Jun.","Resolution|An old poster lets her draw while helping him."],t.s)
B.ay8=s(["What does Belen want to draw?|The fiesta arch|A foreign airport|A new phone|A mountain map|Her plan is the town fiesta arch.","How many blank notebook pages remain?|One|Two|Ten|None|The opening says one blank page.","Why does Jun need paper?|For a message to his grandmother|To build a toy boat|To wrap food|To cover a window|His purpose is explicitly a message.","Which action suggests Belen pauses to decide?|Looking at her pencil before tearing the page|Closing a shop|Throwing all pencils away|Opening an umbrella|The look comes between the need and the choice.","What does she give Jun?|The blank page|Her whole notebook|A new poster|The fiesta arch|She tears out the one blank page.","Where does she draw later?|On an old poster\u2019s back|On Jun\u2019s message|On the school wall|On her desk|Her dialogue proposes the old poster.","Which conflict is most central?|Her wish to draw versus helping Jun|A storm versus a boat|Two teams in a final|A machine versus its maker|Her own competing purposes drive the choice.","Which detail shows resourcefulness?|Using the old poster to keep drawing|Having a pencil|Knowing the town name|Sitting in class|She finds another material instead of abandoning the task.","Which theme fits the resolution?|Creative solutions can make room for generosity and personal goals.|Helping always destroys creativity.|Only new paper can hold good art.|People never need messages.|The ending preserves both helping and drawing.","Which claim is an unsupported stereotype?|Everyone from Belen\u2019s town always makes the same choice.|Belen gives Jun paper.|Jun wants to write a message.|Belen draws later.|One fictional case cannot establish a universal cultural trait.","Which added ending would contradict the passage?|Belen never drew anything that evening.|The old poster held the new arch.|Jun had a page to use.|Faded letters remained beneath the drawing.|The stated final sentence shows she did draw."],t.s)
B.aa5=new A.cg("The Last Page of the Notebook","Q2 \xb7 Philippine prose, conflict and characterization",'Belen had one blank page left in her notebook. She planned to draw the town fiesta arch. Her classmate Jun arrived without paper for a message to his grandmother. Belen looked at her pencil, then tore out the blank page. "I can draw on the back of an old poster," she said. That evening, the arch took shape beneath faded letters.',B.ayS,"Conflict can happen within a character choosing between wishes. Belen wants to draw but sees Jun\u2019s need. The pause at her pencil suggests a decision. Characterization uses actions and dialogue, not just labels like generous. A locally set story can explore shared values without claiming every person in a culture acts the same way.","Does Belen give up drawing entirely? Check the final sentence: she draws on an old poster. Her solution supports both helping Jun and continuing her own creative work.","State Belen\u2019s inner conflict and give two details showing her response.","She chooses between keeping her last page for drawing and helping Jun write. She tears out the page and finds an old poster for her own drawing.","Do not call Jun selfish without evidence of his motives. The text shows one character\u2019s choice, not a rule about all Filipinos.","Explain conflict through wishes, choices and consequences, using action and dialogue.",B.aFA,B.ay8)
B.aXU=s(["Compare news and feature openings.","Identify attribution and missing information.","Draft an accurate lead from supplied facts."],t.s)
B.as9=s(["News lead|Shed opened Tuesday; source is the transport office.","Feature scene|Rosa\u2019s umbrella and basket make the change concrete.","Known gap|No maintenance plan has been released yet."],t.s)
B.aXd=s(["What opened?|A covered ferry waiting shed|A new ferry route|A market school|A hospital|The news lead names the shed.","When did it open?|Tuesday|Friday|Sunday|Wednesday|The opening day is Tuesday.","How many seats are stated?|Twenty|Ten|Twelve|Forty|The news item gives twenty seats.","Who announced the opening?|The transport office|Rosa\u2019s grandmother|A newspaper seller|An unnamed visitor|The source is explicitly the transport office.","What information is not yet released?|A maintenance plan|The opening day|The seat count|The shed\u2019s existence|The final news sentence states this gap.","Who appears in the feature?|Rosa|Belen|Jun|Lani|The feature centers on Rosa.","What did Rosa balance before the shed?|An umbrella and a basket|Two books|A chair and a desk|A box of lanterns|The feature gives those two items.","Which opening foregrounds who-what-when facts?|The news lead|Only the feature scene|Neither passage|A poem not supplied|The lead directly reports the public event and date.","Why begin the feature with Rosa?|To show a concrete human experience of the change|To prove every passenger agrees|To replace all factual checking|To announce a maintenance budget|Her scene illustrates an effect without a universal claim.","Which new lead is accurate?|The transport office announced a twenty-seat ferry shed opened Tuesday.|A hundred-seat shed opened Friday.|Rosa paid the entire maintenance budget.|All passengers praised the shed.|Only the first uses supplied facts.","Which inference needs more evidence?|Every passenger is satisfied with the shed.|Rosa can sit under a roof.|A covered shed exists.|The office announced it.|One feature scene cannot prove everyone\u2019s satisfaction."],t.s)
B.aaQ=new A.cg("News and Feature at the Ferry","Q3 \xb7 expository and journalistic texts","News lead: The town opened a covered ferry waiting shed on Tuesday, the transport office announced. It has twenty seats. The office has not yet released a maintenance plan.\nFeature opening: Before dawn, Rosa used to balance an umbrella and her market basket while waiting. This Tuesday, she lowered the umbrella and sat beneath the new roof.",B.aXU,"A news lead foregrounds key public facts. A feature may begin with a person\u2019s experience to give those facts human meaning. Both need accuracy. Attribution names the source: the transport office announced. A clear account also states gaps, such as a maintenance plan not yet released.","Which opening answers what and when most directly? The news lead names the shed and Tuesday immediately. The feature shows the change through Rosa\u2019s umbrella and seat.","Write a factual lead using only the location, opening day, number of seats and announced source.","The transport office announced that a twenty-seat covered waiting shed opened at the town ferry on Tuesday. This preserves all supplied details without adding a crowd count.","Do not invent an exact opening hour or a maintenance budget. Rosa is one person, not a survey of all passengers.","Match form to purpose, attribute facts, and distinguish a personal illustration from broad evidence.",B.as9,B.aXd)
B.aGL=s(["Identify a thesis and supporting reasons.","Explain cohesive links.","Revise a paragraph to keep its argument clear."],t.s)
B.aXf=s(["Central idea|A simple return system helps shared books.","Support|Loan cards track books; checks notice loose pages.","Qualification|Records still need careful handling."],t.s)
B.aRS=s(["What is the essay mainly about?|Making a shared book shelf work well|Building a road|Choosing a sport|Cooking for a class|The whole explanation concerns managing shared books.","What does a loan card record?|Title and borrower|Weather and temperature|Book price only|The school gate\u2019s color|The first support names title and borrower.","What can the record help find?|A missing book|A new building|A lost cloud|An absent bus|The text explains finding a missing book.","How often is the check proposed?|Weekly|Hourly|Every ten years|Never|A weekly check is named.","What can the check notice?|Loose pages|A ferry timetable|A food order|Rainfall|The second support concerns loose pages.","What does This record refer to?|The loan-card information|The weekly weather|A song recording|A sports score|It refers back to the recorded title and borrower.","Which connector introduces a limitation?|However|First|Second|This|However turns to what a record alone cannot do.","Which connector introduces the concluding link?|Therefore|Before|Although not used|Yesterday|Therefore signals the final inference.","Why is careful handling still needed?|Records alone cannot physically protect books.|Records make all pages unbreakable.|Checks replace readers.|Borrowers never touch books.|The qualification distinguishes tracking from physical care.","Which sentence would weaken the paragraph\u2019s focus?|My favorite snack is sweet corn.|A card should name the book clearly.|Readers can report a loose page.|The class can review the loan list.|The snack sentence does not support shared-book care.","Which outline matches the explanation?|Central idea, two supports, limitation, conclusion|Greeting, address, signature only|Climax before every character|Recipe ingredients only|The passage follows that expository structure."],t.s)
B.aaF=new A.cg("An Expository Essay about Shared Books","Q4 \xb7 academic expository essay and cohesion","A shared book shelf works best with a simple return system. First, a loan card records the title and borrower. This record helps the class find a missing book. Second, a weekly check catches loose pages before more damage occurs. However, a record alone cannot keep books safe; readers must also handle them carefully. Clear records and careful use therefore support the same goal.",B.aGL,"An expository essay explains a subject with a central idea and ordered support. The opening gives the main claim; first and second organize reasons. This record refers back to the loan card. However signals a limit. Therefore connects the explanation to its conclusion. Specific details make the explanation useful.","What does This record refer to? Look backward to a loan card records the title and borrower. The phrase links the next sentence to that information, creating cohesion.","Find the essay\u2019s central idea and explain why However is needed.","A simple return system helps a shared shelf work. However introduces a limit: records cannot replace careful handling.","Do not mistake the weekly check for a new unrelated topic. It supports care of shared books. A connector must fit the relationship, not merely sound formal.","State a central idea, support it in order, and use reference and connectors to show relationships.",B.aXf,B.aRS)
B.aSJ=s(["Identify a request letter\u2019s purpose and audience.","Explain how details make a request actionable.","Compose a polite request with practical limits."],t.s)
B.aTX=s(["Audience|Principal Cruz is asked for permission.","Request|Lunchtime reading corner; two-week trial from June 17.","Responsibility and response|Volunteers restore the area; principal gives availability and rules."],t.s)
B.aFY=s(["Who receives the letter?|Principal Cruz|Ari Santos|A ferry driver|A bookseller|The salutation names Principal Cruz.","Who signs the letter?|Ari Santos|Principal Cruz|Rosa|Belen|The signature names Ari as representative.","What does the class request?|Permission for a reading corner|A new swimming pool|Cancellation of all lessons|A bus for a trip|The opening states the request.","Where is the proposed space?|Beside Room 7|On the roof|Outside the town hall|In the kitchen|The requested corner is beside Room 7.","When would the space be used?|Lunchtime|Midnight|Only on holidays|During every exam|Lunchtime reading space specifies the time.","How long is the trial?|Two weeks|Two years|One day|Six months|The letter proposes a two-week trial.","Who would restore the corner?|Two class volunteers|Every visitor from town|The principal alone|No one|The plan assigns two volunteers.","What response is requested?|Availability and safety rules|A list of every teacher\u2019s age|A weather forecast|A recipe|The final request asks for those two practical matters.","Why include a trial period?|It makes the proposal limited and easier to evaluate.|It proves permission has been granted.|It removes all safety duties.|It hides the purpose.|A defined trial sets a manageable scope.","Which follow-up would be inaccurate before a reply?|The principal has already approved our corner.|We have requested permission.|We await the principal\u2019s response.|We proposed two volunteers.|The letter does not contain approval.","Which opening is most appropriate for a similar request?|We request permission to use Room 8 for a supervised reading session.|Give us the room now or else.|We will use any room without asking.|Rooms are things that exist.|A clear, respectful request suits the audience and purpose."],t.s)
B.aaq=new A.cg("A Letter Requesting a Reading Corner","Q4 \xb7 transactional letter of request","Dear Principal Cruz,\nOur Grade 7 class requests permission to use the unused corner beside Room 7 as a lunchtime reading space. We propose a two-week trial starting on June 17. Two class volunteers will arrange mats and restore the corner after each session. Please let us know whether the space is available and what safety rules we should follow.\nRespectfully,\nAri Santos, class representative",B.aSJ,"A request letter asks a particular reader for a specific action. The principal can consider permission; the class representative writes on behalf of the class. Place, trial length and responsibilities let the reader evaluate the plan. A polite closing cannot replace missing details. Ask about safety rather than assuming approval.","Is permission already granted? Find requests permission and Please let us know. These ask for a decision, so the letter is a proposal, not proof of approval.","List the proposed place, trial period and cleanup responsibility.","The corner beside Room 7; two weeks from June 17; two class volunteers arrange mats and restore the area each session.","Do not announce that the principal approved the plan. Do not confuse the writer\u2019s name with the intended recipient.","Name the reader, request a clear action, supply practical details, and invite a response.",B.aTX,B.aFY)
B.aVf=s([B.aaJ,B.aaI,B.aa5,B.aaQ,B.aaF,B.aaq],t.PF)
B.aYC=s(["Analyze how a choice develops theme.","Separate textual evidence from cultural assumptions.","Explain the function of a story detail."],t.s)
B.b2n=s(["Initial signal|A large name makes access uncertain.","Revision|Whoever needs a rest invites shared use.","Result|Three readers use the seat."],t.s)
B.aCs=s(["Where is the story set?|A fictional coastal town in Kenya|A documented royal court|A real named village in Japan|A ship near London|The opening explicitly calls the town fictional.","What does Amina paint?|A wooden seat|A boat|A gate|A roof|The object is a courtyard seat.","What does she first write prominently?|Her name|A bus timetable|A price|A test score|Her name is across the back.","Why do younger pupils stand nearby?|They are unsure they may sit.|They are waiting for paint lessons.|They dislike every book.|The seat is already broken.|Their uncertainty is directly stated.","What does the new line invite?|Anyone needing a rest|Only Amina\u2019s cousins|Only painters|Only adults|Whoever needs a rest is inclusive.","Where does she put her signature afterward?|In a small corner|Across every seat board|On the school roof|Nowhere in the text|She signs a small corner.","What happens by noon?|Three readers use the seat.|The seat is sold.|A storm breaks it.|All pupils leave town.|The final sentence gives three readers.","Which theme is supported?|A creator can acknowledge authorship while welcoming others.|Every gift must be anonymous.|Seats always divide communities.|Painting prevents reading.|The small signature and invitation balance authorship and sharing.","Which claim is an unsupported generalization?|All Kenyan children handle ownership this way.|Amina changes the words.|The pupils were unsure.|The seat becomes shared.|One fictional case cannot establish a national trait.","What does the signature detail contribute?|It shows sharing need not erase the maker\u2019s credit.|It proves Amina stops painting forever.|It names a historical ruler.|It gives a weather forecast.|The maker remains acknowledged without dominating the invitation.","What would be needed for fuller study of Afro-Asian literature?|Actual contextualized works by writers from the regions|Only this invented scene|A list of stereotypes|No authors or historical context|This original skill exercise does not replace a regional literary corpus."],t.s)
B.aaC=new A.cg("The Courtyard Seat","Q1 \xb7 literary elements and respectful cultural reading",'In a fictional coastal town in Kenya, Amina painted a wooden seat for the school courtyard. She wrote her name across its back. When younger pupils stood nearby, unsure whether they could sit, Amina covered her name with a new line: "A place for whoever needs a rest." She signed a small corner instead. By noon, the seat held three readers.',B.aYC,"Literary analysis connects details to meaning. The large name may suggest private ownership; the new line invites shared use. The small signature keeps Amina\u2019s authorship without excluding readers. This is an original fictional exercise, not a traditional Kenyan tale or a claim about all Kenyan people. Reading a region\u2019s literature also requires actual works by writers from that region.","Why do younger pupils hesitate? The text says they are unsure they can sit. The large name plausibly signals ownership. Changing the words removes that barrier.","Explain how the large name, new line and final readers form a sequence.","The name leaves access uncertain. The invitation makes shared use explicit. Three readers show that the seat becomes used as a shared place.","Do not label the fictional scene an authentic folktale. Do not generalize one character\u2019s action to a whole country.","Interpret choices with evidence; distinguish a text\u2019s local setting from broad cultural claims.",B.b2n,B.aCs)
B.aFh=s(["Recognize parallel forms in a list.","Build an outline from main points.","Plan pacing for an informative speech."],t.s)
B.aCR=s(["I. Collecting|Permission before recording.","II. Labeling|Who, where and when for photographs.","III. Storing|Working copy plus backup."],t.s)
B.aFu=s(["What is the speech about?|A class archive|A sports final|A cooking contest|A ferry fare|The opening names the class archive.","How many main tasks are listed?|Three|Two|Four|Six|Collecting, labeling and storing are three tasks.","What should happen before recording?|Ask permission|Publish every private detail|Delete all labels|Hide the recorder|The first supporting step asks permission.","Which details label photographs?|Who, where and when|Only the paper color|Only the price|The speaker\u2019s favorite food|The speech lists those three identifiers.","How many copies are described?|A working copy and a backup|Only one with no backup|Ten printed originals|No copies|The storage step names two roles.","Which list is parallel?|Collecting accounts, labeling photographs, storing copies|Collecting accounts, photographs, to store copies|To collect, labeling, copies|Accounts, to label, storing safely|All items in the first list are matching -ing phrases.","Which word marks the last main point?|Finally|Our|Who|One|Finally introduces storage.","Which detail belongs under collecting accounts?|Ask permission before recording.|Write dates on photographs.|Keep a backup.|Choose a new paint color.|Permission applies to recording people\u2019s accounts.","Which phrase outline matches the speech?|Collecting, labeling, storing|Painting, racing, swimming|Arriving, buying, cooking|Recording, forgetting, deleting|It preserves the three main task categories.","Which delivery would make the structure easiest to follow?|Pause between the three tasks and stress their key words.|Read all points in one breath.|Whisper only the final word.|Skip all transitions.|Pauses and emphasis help listeners recognize the outline.","Why is a backup relevant to the final main point?|It supports safer storage if the working copy is lost.|It replaces permission.|It identifies every speaker.|It changes all photographs.|A backup belongs to protecting stored information."],t.s)
B.aaA=new A.cg("An Informative Speech in Parallel","Q1 \xb7 informative speech, outline and parallel structure","Our class archive has three tasks: collecting oral accounts, labeling photographs, and storing copies safely. First, we ask permission before recording a speaker. Next, we write who, where and when for each photograph. Finally, we keep one working copy and one backup. These steps preserve information and respect the people who share it.",B.aFh,"Parallel structure puts related items in matching grammatical forms: collecting, labeling, storing. This helps listeners hear a balanced list. An outline separates main points from examples. First, next and finally guide the audience. Pause between main points and emphasize permission, labels and copies so the structure is audible.","Is collecting accounts, photographs, and to store copies parallel? No: it mixes an -ing phrase, a noun and an infinitive. Use collecting, labeling and storing.","Make a three-point phrase outline and add one detail under the first point.","I. Collecting accounts \u2014 ask permission. II. Labeling photographs. III. Storing copies. The detail belongs under the first task rather than becoming an unrelated fourth task.","An outline is not a full transcript. Parallel wording must preserve meaning, not merely force all words to end alike.","Use matched forms and a visible or audible outline to organize information.",B.aCR,B.aFu)
B.b2l=s(["Compare an initial judgment with a revised one.","Use context clues for an unfamiliar word.","Connect ideas with contrast and cause."],t.s)
B.atF=s(["Initial evidence|A later arrival enters first.","Additional evidence|Urgent-case sign and a patient needing support.","Revised response|Sami clears the doorway."],t.s)
B.aJX=s(["What does the first sign list?|Patients by arrival time|Medicine prices|School grades|Bus numbers|The first sentence gives arrival order.","What exception does the second sign describe?|Urgent cases may be seen first.|No patient may enter.|Only nurses may sit.|All late arrivals go home.|The second sign states urgent priority.","Why does Sami first complain?|A later arrival goes in first.|The clinic has no doorway.|His name is misspelled.|The nurse loses a book.|The order change triggers his complaint.","Who supports the patient?|A nurse|A guard|A bookseller|A teacher|Sami notices a nurse supporting the patient.","What clue shows the patient\u2019s difficulty?|The patient can barely stand.|The patient has a blue bag.|The patient reads quickly.|The patient tells a joke.|Barely standing suggests a need for assistance.","What does Sami reread?|The second sign|A newspaper|A recipe|A ticket|He rereads the urgent-case explanation.","What does urgent mean here?|Needing prompt attention|Finished long ago|Not important|Ready for a holiday|The exception and patient condition show urgency.","What is Sami\u2019s final action?|Clearing the doorway|Blocking the nurse|Tearing down the sign|Leaving with the patient\u2019s bag|He steps aside to clear the entrance.","Which conclusion is best supported?|New information changes Sami\u2019s response.|The nurse always favors friends.|The patient has a named disease.|Arrival order never matters.|The rereading and stepping aside show revised judgment.","Which connector best marks the contrast between complaint and stepping aside?|However|For example only|Similarly|Before sunrise|However signals the change between two responses.","Which statement exceeds the evidence?|The patient definitely has a specific named illness.|The patient needs support.|There is an urgent-case rule.|Sami first complains.|No diagnosis is provided."],t.s)
B.aa6=new A.cg("The Queue beside the Clinic","Q2 \xb7 comparison, inference and cohesive writing","Outside the clinic, a sign listed patients by arrival time. A second sign explained that urgent cases could be seen first. Sami complained when a later arrival went in. Then he noticed a nurse supporting the patient, who could barely stand. Sami read the second sign again and stepped aside to clear the doorway.",B.b2l,"Readers infer by combining clues rather than guessing from one event. Later arrival seems unfair until the urgent-case rule and the patient\u2019s condition are considered. Urgent means needing prompt attention here. A contrast connector such as however can mark a revised view. Because can explain the evidence behind that revision.","Why does Sami step aside? First he rereads the exception. He also sees a patient barely able to stand. Those clues support making room for urgent care, not favoritism.","Write two connected sentences contrasting Sami\u2019s first and later responses.","Sami first complains about the later arrival. However, he steps aside after seeing the patient\u2019s condition and rereading the urgent-care rule.","Do not diagnose the patient from these lines. Barely able to stand suggests need but does not name an illness.","Compare all relevant clues before judging; use connectors to explain a change of view.",B.atF,B.aJX)
B.aEg=s(["Identify loaded language and a false choice.","Compare visual persuasion with practical information.","Rewrite a claim using appropriate modality."],t.s)
B.aSG=s(["Loaded label|Careless attacks nonmembers without proof.","False choice|Join or do not care excludes other ways to help.","Correction|Nonmembers may volunteer too."],t.s)
B.aW_=s(["What does the poster want readers to do?|Join the club|Buy a train ticket|Return a textbook|Attend a film|Join today is its action request.","Which word labels people negatively?|Careless|Street|Today|Club|Careless attacks the character of nonmembers.","What false choice does the poster give?|Join or do not care|Choose Saturday or Sunday|Read or write|Use a bin or a tray|It treats membership as the only evidence of care.","What visual contrast is described?|Gray street versus bright green park|Two identical classrooms|Two weather maps|A blue book versus red pen|The poster contrasts those scenes.","How often is the stated cleanup opportunity?|One Saturday a month|Every hour|Every night|Once in ten years|The separate note states the schedule.","Can nonmembers volunteer?|Yes|No|Only if they own the town|Only on weekdays|The note explicitly allows nonmembers.","What does may express in the note?|Permission|A completed event|An absolute requirement|A past habit|May join and may volunteer allow participation.","Who is the likely audience?|Town residents who might volunteer|Only a remote ship\u2019s crew|Only laboratory robots|Readers of a recipe|The town-care appeal targets potential local volunteers.","Which detail undermines the poster\u2019s false choice?|Nonmembers may volunteer.|The word street appears.|One side is gray.|The poster has an exclamation mark.|Volunteering without joining is a third option.","Which rewrite is fair and accurate?|You may join the monthly cleanup without membership.|All nonmembers are careless.|You must buy the park.|Only members can care.|It preserves the actual permission without attacking others.","What evidence would be needed to prove an actual cleanup result?|Comparable observations or records before and after the activity|Only brighter drawing colors|An insult in large print|The club\u2019s name alone|Images can persuade, but measured outcomes need relevant records."],t.s)
B.aa9=new A.cg("A Poster That Blames Too Much","Q3 \xb7 propaganda, target audience and modality",'Campaign poster: "Only careless people refuse our Clean Street Club. Join today or you do not care about your town!" The poster shows a gray street on one side and a bright green park on the other. In a separate note, the club says members may join one Saturday cleanup a month. Nonmembers may also volunteer.',B.aEg,"Loaded words push feelings before evidence. Careless labels people without examining their actions. Join or you do not care presents a false choice: people can help in other ways. The note even allows nonmembers. May shows permission, unlike must, which signals obligation. A fair invitation describes an opportunity without insulting others.","Does refusing membership prove lack of care? Compare the poster with Nonmembers may also volunteer. The note provides an alternative, so the poster\u2019s either-or claim fails.","Rewrite the invitation without the insult or false choice.","You may join our monthly Saturday cleanup, with or without club membership. This states the opportunity and preserves the note\u2019s permission.","A brighter picture is not measured proof of results. Do not replace may with must unless an actual rule requires it.","Check labels, either-or pressure and images against the actual options and evidence.",B.aSG,B.aW_)
B.aKq=s(["Evaluate relevance and authority for a question.","Distinguish an official record from personal experience.","Paraphrase with acknowledgment."],t.s)
B.awB=s(["Official change|A provides the announced labels and start date.","Weak broad claim|B has no author or cited evidence.","Personal experience|C supports one pupil\u2019s reported confusion."],t.s)
B.ay_=s(["What is the report question about?|Changes in school lunch-bin labels|National election results|A ferry fare|A poem\u2019s rhyme|The question specifies this school and term.","Which source is a dated school memo?|A|B|C|None|Source A is identified as the memo.","Which source is unsigned?|B|A|C|All are stated unsigned|The online post is described as unsigned.","Which source offers personal experience?|C|A|B|None|A pupil\u2019s diary records that pupil\u2019s experience.","What does A explicitly list?|New labels and start date|Every pupil\u2019s opinion|Every school\u2019s rules|The exact amount of national waste|The memo\u2019s stated contents are limited.","What weakness is stated for B?|No source for its broad claim|It has a verified national dataset|It is a dated official memo|It names an expert author|The post gives no evidence for all schools.","How many pupils\u2019 confusion does C directly document?|One pupil\u2019s|Every pupil\u2019s|All teachers\u2019|No personal experience|The diary belongs to one pupil.","Which source best supports the school\u2019s announced start date?|A|B|C|A random unrelated advertisement|The official school memo is directly relevant.","Which sentence fairly uses C?|One pupil reported confusion with one new label.|All pupils rejected the labels.|No one understood any bin.|Every school changed at once.|The first preserves the diary\u2019s limited scope.","Which detail must not be invented from these descriptions?|The actual date and wording of the labels|That A is a memo|That C is a diary|That B is unsigned|The descriptions say such details exist but do not quote them.","Which paraphrase acknowledges its source?|The school memo records the new labels and their start date.|I know all labels everywhere changed.|The facts came from nowhere.|No source is needed for copied information.|The first uses original wording and names the source."],t.s)
B.aaD=new A.cg("Choosing Sources for a Short Report","Q4 \xb7 research, relevance and acknowledgment","Report question: How did the school\u2019s lunch-bin labels change this term?\nSource A: a dated school memo lists the new labels and the start date.\nSource B: an unsigned online post says all schools use the same labels, but gives no source.\nSource C: a pupil\u2019s diary describes being confused by one new label on the first day.",B.aKq,"A useful source matches the question and supports a specific claim. The school memo is direct evidence of the school\u2019s announced change. The diary can illustrate one pupil\u2019s experience, not all pupils\u2019 views. An unsigned unreferenced post is weak evidence for a universal claim. Name the source even when using your own words.","Which source gives the start date? The dated memo explicitly lists it. The diary may mention a first day but is not the school\u2019s official statement.","Plan one factual sentence from A and one limited experience sentence from C, without inventing the actual date or labels.","The school memo records the new labels and their start date. One pupil\u2019s diary describes confusion with one label on the first day. Neither sentence invents missing details.","The source descriptions do not supply the actual labels or date. Do not invent them. One diary is not a schoolwide survey.","Match source to claim, limit generalizations, and acknowledge borrowed information.",B.awB,B.ay_)
B.aV9=s(["Convert a quotation into accurate reported speech.","Compare active and passive focus.","Explain past perfect in a sequence of past events."],t.s)
B.ayN=s(["Direct to reported|I repaired becomes she had repaired after Ms. Tan said.","Voice shift|The caretaker repaired the clock becomes The clock was repaired by the caretaker.","Time relation|Had noticed places pupils\u2019 noticing before Tuesday\u2019s interview."],t.s)
B.aQG=s(["When did Ms. Tan repair the clock?|Monday|Tuesday night|Friday|Sunday|Her direct quotation names Monday.","When was the interview?|Tuesday|Monday before the repair|Saturday|An unspecified future year|Tuesday interview is the heading.","Who repaired the clock?|The school caretaker|The reporter|A pupil|An unnamed visitor|The notes identify the caretaker and the interview identifies Ms. Tan.",'Which is direct speech?|"I repaired the hall clock on Monday."|Ms. Tan said that she had repaired it.|The clock was repaired.|Pupils had noticed the time.|The quoted exact words are direct speech.',"What replaces I in the reporter\u2019s account of Ms. Tan\u2019s words?|She|They|We|It|She refers back to Ms. Tan rather than to the reporter.","Which construction is passive?|The clock was repaired by the caretaker.|The caretaker repaired the clock.|Pupils noticed the time.|The reporter interviewed Ms. Tan.|The affected clock is subject and was repaired is passive.","Which construction is active?|The caretaker repaired the clock.|The clock was repaired by the caretaker.|The clock was repaired on Monday.|The time was noticed by pupils.|The subject caretaker performs repaired.","What does had noticed show here?|Noticing happened before the past interview.|Noticing will happen next year.|Nobody noticed the clock.|Noticing is an order to pupils.|Past perfect marks an earlier event relative to Tuesday.","Which reported sentence preserves the quotation\u2019s meaning?|Ms. Tan said that she had repaired the hall clock on Monday.|Ms. Tan said the reporter would break the clock.|Ms. Tan said no repair occurred.|Ms. Tan said pupils repaired it Friday.|Only the first keeps the person, event and day.","What changes between the active and passive versions?|The focus and grammatical subject, not the repair event|The repair date necessarily|The identity of the caretaker necessarily|The number of repaired clocks necessarily|Both versions describe the same repair with different focus.","Which revision creates an unsupported event?|The clock was repaired again on Tuesday.|Ms. Tan reported a Monday repair.|Pupils noticed correct time before the interview.|The caretaker repaired the clock.|No second repair is stated."],t.s)
B.aaa=new A.cg("Reporting the Repaired Hall Clock","Q4 \xb7 journalistic voice, reported speech and perfect tense",'Tuesday interview:\nMs. Tan: "I repaired the hall clock on Monday."\nReporter\u2019s notes: The school caretaker repaired it. By Tuesday, pupils had already noticed the correct time.\nNews paragraph: Ms. Tan said that she had repaired the hall clock on Monday. The clock was repaired by the school caretaker. Pupils had noticed the correct time before Tuesday\u2019s interview.',B.aV9,"Direct speech presents a speaker\u2019s exact words in quotation marks. Reported speech retells them: I becomes she when the reporter refers to Ms. Tan. In this past reporting context, repaired becomes had repaired. Active voice puts the doer first; passive voice can foreground the object affected. Had noticed places noticing before another past point, Tuesday\u2019s interview.","Compare The caretaker repaired the clock with The clock was repaired by the caretaker. The event and doer remain the same. The subject changes, so the passive sentence makes the clock the topic.","Report Ms. Tan\u2019s words with Ms. Tan said that. Keep the person, event and Monday date correct.","Ms. Tan said that she had repaired the hall clock on Monday. This reports the earlier repair from a past interview viewpoint without claiming a second repair.","Do not write Ms. Tan said that I repaired unless I really means the reporter. Passive voice does not mean no one acted; the by phrase can still name the doer.","Keep facts stable when changing voice or speech form; use past perfect to clarify an earlier past event.",B.ayN,B.aQG)
B.aXH=s(["Compare personal and public writing purposes.","Select details suitable for a factual report.","Revise register without misrepresenting experience."],t.s)
B.avc=s(["Journal purpose|Explore one person\u2019s nervousness and enjoyment.","Report evidence|Thursday, Room 8, 24 attendees, two stories.","Revision rule|Attribute best rather than presenting it as universal fact."],t.s)
B.awO=s(["How does the journal writer first feel?|Nervous|Angry at Tala|Asleep|Certain nobody came|The journal explicitly says nervous.","Who asks about the story?|Tala|A cook|A driver|A principal not named|Tala asks and later leads discussion.","What does the writer call the afternoon?|The best ever|A total failure|A canceled event|An ordinary exam|That phrase appears in the journal.","How many attendees does the record list?|24|12|8|2|The event record gives 24.","How many stories were read?|Two|Four|Eight|Twenty-four|The record and report both say two stories.","Where did the event occur?|Room 8|The town hall|A ferry|The garden|The event record names Room 8.","Which text mainly explores private feelings?|The journal|The event record|Only the report\u2019s date|A loan card|The journal describes nerves and relief.","Which detail is a personal evaluation?|Best afternoon ever|Thursday|24 attendees|Two stories|Best is the writer\u2019s judgment.","Which report sentence overgeneralizes?|All 24 pupils thought it was the best afternoon ever.|The event took place Thursday.|Two stories were read.|Tala led discussion.|Only one writer\u2019s opinion is supplied.","Which attributed sentence is fair?|One participant described it as the best afternoon ever.|The afternoon was objectively best for everyone.|Nobody was nervous.|All pupils wrote the same journal.|Attribution limits the evaluation to its source.","What makes the report register appropriate?|Clear verifiable details suited to a public audience|Rare words added at random|Every sentence beginning with I feel|Removing all dates and counts|The report\u2019s purpose is to inform readers about the event."],t.s)
B.aac=new A.cg("A Journal Entry Becomes a Report","Q4 \xb7 composition, register and revision",'Journal: "I was nervous at our reading event. Then Tala asked about the story, and I relaxed. It was the best afternoon ever!"\nEvent record: Thursday, Room 8, 24 attendees, two stories read, discussion led by Tala.\nReport draft: "On Thursday, 24 pupils attended a reading event in Room 8. Two stories were read, followed by a discussion led by Tala."',B.aXH,"Register is language suited to purpose and audience. A journal may explore a writer\u2019s feelings. A public event report prioritizes verifiable details and clear attribution. Best afternoon ever is personal evaluation, not a countable result. It could appear as an attributed quotation if relevant, but should not become an unqualified fact.","Which details should lead the report? Use the event record\u2019s Thursday, Room 8 and 24 attendees. The writer\u2019s nerves matter in the journal but do not describe everyone.","Add a sentence about the writer\u2019s positive view without making it universal.","One participant described it in a journal as the best afternoon ever. This attributes the evaluation to one person instead of claiming everyone agreed.","Formal writing is not just longer words. Do not convert I relaxed into All 24 pupils relaxed.","Choose details and language for the audience; keep opinions attributed and facts checkable.",B.avc,B.awO)
B.aMR=s([B.aaC,B.aaA,B.aa6,B.aa9,B.aaD,B.aaa,B.aac],t.PF)
B.b1B=s(["Infer motives from dialogue and action.","Analyze how a detail develops self-discovery.","Distinguish a modern exercise from a historical literary source."],t.s)
B.ava=s(["Hesitation|Someone from the street avoids claiming the design.","Possible encouragement|The father leaves a sharpened pencil.","Self-discovery|The next design carries Nell\u2019s name."],t.s)
B.aLv=s(["Where is the fictional scene set?|London|Nairobi|Manila|Seoul|The opening explicitly names London.","Where does Nell hide the drawing?|Under a workshop ledger|Inside a teapot|On a roof|Behind a clock|The opening gives its hiding place.","What does her father ask about?|Who designed the window sign|The price of a boat|The time of a train|A lost key|His question concerns authorship of the sign.","How does Nell first answer?|Someone from the street|I designed it proudly|You designed it yesterday|No sign exists|Her evasive words are directly quoted.","What does the father leave?|A sharpened pencil|A rejection letter|A bus ticket|A broken ruler|He leaves a pencil beside the drawing.","What changes in the next design?|It carries Nell\u2019s name.|It has no image.|It is hidden forever.|It is signed by a stranger.|The last sentence states her name appears.","Which narrative perspective is used?|Third person focused on actions|First-person diary by Nell|Second-person instructions|A factual interview transcript|The narrator refers to Nell as she.","What can the pencil plausibly symbolize?|Encouragement to create|A measured exam score|A legal ownership transfer|A train route|Its placement beside the drawing suggests support for her work.","Which inference best fits the beginning and ending?|Nell becomes more willing to claim her work.|Nell abandons drawing completely.|Her father destroys every design.|Nobody sees the drawing.|The move from an evasive answer to a signature supports confidence.","Which claim adds unsupported dialogue?|Her father says, You will be a famous artist.|He straightens the curled edge.|Nell takes the pencil.|The next design is signed.|No such speech appears in the passage.","What is needed beyond this exercise for British-American literary study?|Actual works with author and historical context|Calling this new prose a classic|Only a city name|A list of stereotypes|An original practice scene cannot replace the prescribed literary corpus."],t.s)
B.ab_=new A.cg("The Window above the Workshop","Q1 \xb7 literary self-discovery and narrative perspective",'In this original fictional scene set in London, sixteen-year-old Nell keeps a drawing hidden under a workshop ledger. Her father asks who designed the new window sign. "Someone from the street," she says. Later, he straightens the drawing\u2019s curled edge and leaves a sharpened pencil beside it. Nell takes the pencil. The next design carries her name.',B.b1B,"A third-person narrator names Nell and describes actions without directly stating every thought. Her vague answer may suggest hesitation about claiming her work. The pencil can function as a symbol of encouragement. The signed design shows a change. This newly written exercise is not a British historical text; studying that literary tradition also requires contextualized works by its writers.","Does the father\u2019s action prove his exact words or beliefs? No words are reported. Yet straightening the drawing and leaving a pencil reasonably suggest encouragement. The inference is supported but should not become an invented quotation.","Compare Nell\u2019s first answer with her final design. Explain the change.","She first hides her authorship by saying someone from the street. Later she names herself on the design, suggesting growing confidence in her creative identity.","Do not invent a speech by the father. Do not present this original contemporary exercise as a recovered British classic.","Read dialogue, objects and later choices together; separate supported inference from invented fact.",B.ava,B.aLv)
B.aIL=s(["Compare normal and inverted word order.","Explain emphasis created by placement.","Use punctuation to clarify speech and related clauses."],t.s)
B.aIN=s(["Normal order|The last light (subject) rested (verb).","Inversion|On the stair comes first; subject follows rested.","Speech boundary|Quotation marks separate Wait from I called."],t.s)
B.aVX=s(["What rests on the stair?|The last light|A bell|A door|The speaker|The first sentence identifies the light.","What breaks the silence?|A bell|A footstep|A window|A book|Both versions name a bell.","Which phrase begins the inverted line?|On the stair|The last light|A bell|I called|The location is moved to the front.","What is the subject of the inverted line?|The last light|On the stair|Rested|Then|The light is what rests.","Which is the main verb of that line?|Rested|Stair|Last|On|Rested expresses what the subject does.","Which mark joins the related clauses in the poem?|Semicolon|Question mark|Hyphen|Apostrophe|The first poetic line ends with a semicolon.","Which words are a speech tag?|I called|Wait|The door is still open|On the stair|I called tells who speaks and how.","Which is normal word order?|The last light rested on the stair.|On the stair rested the last light.|Rested on the stair the last light.|On rested light the stair.|The subject precedes the verb in the first choice.","What effect can the inverted line create?|It brings the location into focus before the light.|It changes light into a bell.|It proves the stair is the subject.|It removes every action.|Fronting the location alters emphasis, not the basic event.","Which sentence is not inverted in the same way?|After lunch, the light faded.|On the stair rested the light.|Beside the gate stood a guard.|Under the bench lay a book.|The light still comes before faded despite the opening phrase.",'Which punctuation correctly follows this lesson\u2019s dialogue convention?|"Wait," I called.|"Wait" I called,|Wait, "I called."|"Wait I" called.|The quoted word is separated from its speech tag by a comma.'],t.s)
B.aaN=new A.cg("Turning a Line for Emphasis","Q1 \xb7 poetry, word order and punctuation",'Normal draft: The last light rested on the stair. A bell broke the silence.\nPoetic revision:\nOn the stair rested the last light;\nthen a bell broke the silence.\n"Wait," I called. "The door is still open."',B.aIL,"Normal word order often places subject before verb: the last light rested. Inversion can place a location first and the subject later: On the stair rested the last light. This directs attention toward the stair before revealing the light. The semicolon connects two related independent clauses. Quotation marks identify spoken words.","Find the subject of On the stair rested the last light. Ask what rested: the last light. On the stair is the location, not the subject, despite coming first.","Return the inverted line to normal order. Explain the comma in Wait, I called.","The last light rested on the stair. The comma sits inside the closing quotation mark before the speech tag I called, following the convention used in this lesson.","Do not call every sentence starting with a phrase inverted. After lunch, the light faded still puts subject before verb. Inversion should serve an effect, not confuse readers.","Identify subject and verb before naming inversion; connect word order and punctuation to meaning.",B.aIN,B.aVX)
B.aSW=s(["Infer relationships through dialogue and stage directions.","Explain subtext without inventing backstory.","Choose delivery consistent with evidence."],t.s)
B.aD1=s(["Setting|Stage and rehearsal clock establish the immediate situation.","Action|Leo clears the chair and offers the script.","Response|Mira takes it and proposes beginning."],t.s)
B.aCN=s(["Where is the scene set?|A school stage|A bus repair shop|A courtroom|A market|The opening stage direction states the setting.","What time does the clock show?|4:10|3:00|6:30|12:00|The direction gives 4:10.","What has Leo placed on the chair?|His bag|A clock|A ladder|A lamp|He moves his bag off it.","Why does Mira say she was delayed?|The bus broke down.|She forgot every line.|She lost the school.|She refused to attend.|Her dialogue gives the bus failure.","How much rehearsal remains according to Leo?|One scene|A whole week|No scene|Ten plays|Leo says one scene left.","What does Leo offer?|The script|A bus ticket|A meal|A clock|The stage direction names the script.","Which text is a stage direction?|He offers her the script.|The bus broke down.|You kept my chair.|Then let us begin.|The bracketed action instructs performance rather than spoken dialogue.","What does Mira do before her last line?|Takes the script|Leaves the room|Moves the clock|Tears the chair|Taking it is the direction before her line.","Which interpretation best fits Leo\u2019s actions?|He makes space for Mira to join.|He physically prevents her from sitting.|He ends the rehearsal immediately.|He destroys her script.|Clearing the chair and offering the script support welcome.","Which tone fits Mira\u2019s last action and line?|Willing and purposeful|Refusing to participate|Completely asleep|Talking to nobody about a meal|Taking the script and proposing action show readiness.","Which claim is not established by the scene?|Mira and Leo have fought for years.|A bus delay is reported.|One scene remains.|A script is offered.|No long-term relationship history is supplied."],t.s)
B.aao=new A.cg("A Scene at the Rehearsal Door","Q2 \xb7 drama, subtext and performance","[A school stage. A rehearsal clock shows 4:10.]\nMIRA: You kept my chair.\nLEO [moving his bag off the chair]: I thought you might come.\nMIRA: The bus broke down.\nLEO: We have one scene left. [He offers her the script.]\nMIRA [taking it]: Then let us begin.",B.aSW,"Drama communicates through speech and performance. Stage directions show action, setting or delivery. Subtext is meaning suggested rather than openly stated. Keeping a chair and offering a script suggest Leo expects or welcomes Mira, though no speech says I forgive you. Actors should choose tone from the scene\u2019s evidence.","Is Leo refusing Mira a place? He moves his bag and offers the script. Those actions support inclusion, not refusal, even if an actor gives his line a tired tone.","Choose a tone for Then let us begin and support it with one action.","A willing, purposeful tone fits. Mira takes the script before speaking, showing readiness to join the rehearsal.","Do not invent a long feud as a fact. The scene allows performance choices, but they must fit the given actions.","Use speech and stage directions together to infer subtext and justify performance choices.",B.aD1,B.aCN)
B.aML=s(["Identify a qualified claim and evidence.","Respond to a counterargument.","Evaluate the scope of a poll."],t.s)
B.aZP=s(["Claim|Provide a quiet corner, not silence everywhere.","Evidence|19 of 30 class-poll respondents support the option.","Response to concern|Trial two tables for one week and review seating."],t.s)
B.b0G=s(["What is the proposal?|A quiet lunch-room corner|Silence in every school space|Closing lunch service|A new sports field|The opening limits the plan to a corner.","How many responded to the poll?|30|19|49|100|The fraction is 19 of 30 respondents.","How many respondents wanted the option?|19|30|11|2|Nineteen is the stated supporting count.","What concern is raised?|Reduced group seating|More library books|A broken bus|Too many gardens|The counterargument concerns seating space.","How many tables are proposed for the trial?|Two|Ten|One hundred|None|The plan uses two tables.","How long is the trial?|One week|One year|One afternoon only|Ten terms|The passage states one week.","What will be reviewed?|Seating and comments|Only wall color|Every pupil\u2019s exam score|The price of textbooks|The plan calls for seating and comments.","What limit does the writer acknowledge?|Respondents may not represent every pupil.|No one responded.|Every school voted.|All pupils rejected the plan.|The final sentence states the representation limit.","Which claim overstates the poll?|The entire school wants a quiet corner.|Nineteen respondents wanted the option.|Thirty pupils responded.|The poll was voluntary.|The data is from class respondents, not the entire school.","Which reply fairly addresses the objection?|Let us test two tables and review seating after a week.|Anyone concerned is selfish.|Seating never matters.|We will ignore all comments.|A trial evaluates the actual practical concern.","Which statement confuses proposal with result?|The trial has already proved successful.|A one-week trial is proposed.|A review is planned.|Some pupils worry about seating.|The passage reports no completed trial or outcome."],t.s)
B.aaP=new A.cg("A Proposal for a Quieter Lunch Room","Q3 \xb7 persuasive speech, evidence and counterargument","Our lunch room needs a quiet corner, not a rule of silence for everyone. In a voluntary class poll, 19 of 30 respondents wanted a place to talk softly or read. Some pupils worry that a quiet corner will reduce group seating. We propose a one-week trial using two tables, then a review of seating and comments. The poll reflects respondents, not necessarily every pupil in school.",B.aML,"A persuasive proposal should make a specific, defensible claim. Here the claim is a quiet corner, not universal silence. A counterargument raises a concern about seating. The trial and review respond practically. A voluntary poll may omit people who did not respond, so its limits matter when interpreting support.","Does 19 of 30 prove the whole school supports the plan? No. The denominator is respondents in a class poll. Keep that population in any summary of the evidence.","State the seating objection and explain the proposed response.","The objection is less space for group seating. A two-table, one-week trial with a seating review tests whether that concern becomes a serious problem.","Do not turn a trial proposal into an established success. Do not label all dissenters selfish; answer their concern.","Persuade with a precise claim, relevant evidence, a fair objection and an assessable response.",B.aZP,B.b0G)
B.aAa=s(["Explain a one-act scene\u2019s concentrated conflict and resolution.","Identify gerund and infinitive functions.","Plan voice and movement that clarify a scene."],t.s)
B.ayC=s(["Conflict|A silent printer leaves no invitations before the meeting.","Purpose and verbal|To inform everyone names the goal, an infinitive phrase.","Resolution|Inez reads while Pao writes the details on the board."],t.s)
B.aKy=s(["Where does the action occur?|The school print room|A bus station|A river bank|A theater lobby|The opening direction states the print room.","How much time remains before the meeting?|Ten minutes|One hour|Two days|One week|The stage direction gives ten minutes.","What is the immediate obstacle?|The printer is silent and there are no copies.|The school has no pupils.|The board has vanished.|Every invitation has already been delivered.|The opening and Inez\u2019s line establish the failed printing task.","What alternative does Pao suggest?|Write details on the board|Cancel all future meetings|Print without any machine|Hide the list|Pao offers a handwritten-list-to-board solution.","What does Inez identify as the true goal?|To inform everyone|To use only the printer|To avoid the meeting|To win a prize|She distinguishes the communication goal from the tool.","What is the main verb in Printing the invitations was my task?|Was|Printing|Invitations|Task|The gerund phrase is subject; was is the main verb.","What is Printing the invitations in that sentence?|A gerund phrase functioning as a noun|A future command|An adjective describing Pao|A preposition|It names the task and serves as the sentence\u2019s subject.","Which phrase is an infinitive phrase?|To inform everyone|Printing the invitations|A handwritten list|The bell|To plus the base verb inform introduces the infinitive.","Which detail shows concentrated one-scene action?|The same room and ten-minute deadline frame the problem and solution.|The characters travel through several countries.|A year passes between every line.|A new narrator changes the location.|The scene remains continuous and tightly focused.","Which performance choice supports Pao\u2019s final instruction?|Point toward the board while clearly proposing the shared task.|Pretend to leave the room forever.|Whisper with his back to Inez and the board without a purpose.|Ignore the list and recite a recipe.|The gesture and clear voice make the new plan understandable.","Why is printing not a gerund in Inez is printing?|It belongs to the progressive verb phrase is printing.|Every -ing form is always a noun.|It names a place.|The sentence has no verb.|The auxiliary is combines with printing as the main predicate."],t.s)
B.aaf=new A.cg("One Act in the Print Room","Q3 \xb7 one-act drama, verbals and performance","[One continuous scene in the school print room. A printer is silent. Ten minutes remain before a class meeting.]\nINEZ: Printing the invitations was my task. I have no copies.\nPAO [holding a handwritten list]: We can write the meeting details on the board.\nINEZ: To inform everyone is the goal, not to use this machine.\nPAO: Then read the details while I write.\n[Inez reads. Pao writes. The bell rings as they check the final line.]",B.aAa,"A one-act play can concentrate action in one short dramatic unit. Here a failed printer creates a time-limited problem and the board provides a resolution. A verbal uses a verb form in another role. Printing the invitations functions as a noun phrase naming Inez\u2019s task: it is a gerund phrase. To inform everyone is an infinitive phrase naming the purpose that matters.","Is Printing a present-tense main verb in the first line? No. The main verb is was. Printing the invitations names what the task was, so the whole phrase functions as a noun. Compare Inez is printing, where is printing is the verb phrase.","State the scene\u2019s obstacle and solution. Plan one performance choice for Pao\u2019s final line.","The silent printer prevents copies, but the pair puts details on a board. Pao can point toward the board and speak Then read clearly to signal the new shared plan.","Not every -ing word is a gerund. Check its role in the sentence. Do not add a scene change when explaining this passage\u2019s single continuous scene.","Explain dramatic structure through an obstacle, choice and outcome; classify verbals by what their phrases do.",B.ayC,B.aKy)
B.aEt=s(["Distinguish prejudice from evidence-based judgment.","Evaluate the scope of a review.","Write a supported critical comment."],t.s)
B.aZp=s(["Weak reasoning|A substitutes a stereotype for performance details.","Specific evidence|B names opening pauses and back-row audibility.","Limit|No survey establishes the whole audience\u2019s experience."],t.s)
B.ayt=s(["What does Review A criticize?|The school play|A novel|A ferry|A garden|A calls the school play weak.","What reason does A give?|A general claim about student actors|A measured sound test|A line-by-line script analysis|A counted audience response|A relies on never do serious work.","Which scene was hard to hear in B?|The final scene|Only the opening|Every scene according to all viewers|A scene not performed|B specifies the final scene.","Where was B listening from?|The back row|Backstage|The front desk of a library|Outside the town|B gives the back-row position.","What strength does B identify?|Clear pauses in the opening|A perfect national survey|A new stage roof|A free meal|The opening pauses are the stated strength.","What improvement is suggested?|A second microphone check|Removing every actor|Canceling all school plays|Buying library shelves|B suggests checking the microphone.","Was an audience survey taken?|No|Yes, of everyone|Yes, only of actors|Yes, nationwide|The record explicitly says none was taken.","Which word makes A\u2019s claim sweeping?|Never|School|Play|Work|Never treats all student work as identical.","Why is B\u2019s reasoning stronger?|It gives specific observations and a qualified suggestion.|It insults more people.|It uses no evidence.|It proves every audience member agreed.|Specific scene and position details support a limited evaluation.","Which conclusion goes beyond B\u2019s evidence?|Every audience member heard nothing.|B struggled to hear the final scene.|B noticed clear opening pauses.|A sound check might help.|One back-row observation cannot establish every listener\u2019s experience.","Which new critical sentence is supported and balanced?|The opening pauses were clear, but the ending was hard to hear from the back row.|All students are incapable of acting.|No one enjoyed anything.|The microphone was definitely broken in every scene.|It keeps both the strength and the limited criticism."],t.s)
B.aa_=new A.cg("Two Reviews of One Performance","Q4 \xb7 evaluating reasoning, bias and evidence",'Review A: "The school play was weak because student actors never do serious work."\nReview B: "The final scene was difficult to hear from the back row. However, the two lead actors used clear pauses in the opening. A second microphone check may improve the ending."\nRecord: Both reviewers attended the same performance; no audience survey was taken.',B.aEt,"A critical judgment needs specific evidence and a reasonable link between evidence and conclusion. Review A dismisses actors as a group rather than examining the play. Review B names a scene, listening position and observed strength, then offers a tentative improvement. Even B does not prove what the whole audience heard or thought.","Why is A\u2019s reason weak? Never do serious work is a sweeping assumption about students. It supplies no detail about acting, delivery or staging in this performance.","Write a balanced comment using one strength and one limited criticism from B.","The leads\u2019 opening pauses were clear, but the ending was hard for this reviewer to hear from the back row. This preserves both observations and the listening position.","Do not infer that every seat had poor sound. A reviewer\u2019s location matters. A suggested microphone check is not a proven diagnosis of every sound problem.","Judge the work with specific observations, not group stereotypes; qualify conclusions by the evidence.",B.aZp,B.ayt)
B.azC=s(["Trace conflict across acts in a longer play plan.","Distinguish obligation from recommendation in modals.","Compare direct and reported speech without changing the evidence."],t.s)
B.aVr=s(["Act I|Damage introduces a problem; Lea observes the panel.","Act II|An unsupported accusation raises social conflict.","Act III|A record explains rain entry; the class corrects the rumor."],t.s)
B.axU=s(["What kind of document is supplied?|A proposed play outline|A completed two-hour script|A transcript of an actual performance|A national repair law|The heading explicitly labels it an outline.","What is damaged in Act I?|A mural|A library card|A bus|A fountain|The initial event is mural damage.","What does Lea say she saw?|A loose roof panel|New pupils damaging the wall|A named thief|A completed repair|Her quotation reports a panel, not a culprit.","Who is blamed by the rumor?|New pupils|The director|A librarian|A nurse|Act II names the rumor\u2019s targets.","How does the class respond in Act II?|Investigates instead of accusing|Immediately punishes the pupils|Destroys all records|Cancels the school year|The outline explicitly chooses investigation.","What evidence appears in Act III?|A record showing rain entered through the roof|A confession by the new pupils|A survey of every school|A drawing proving all rumors true|The repair record supplies the resolution evidence.","What does must express in the note?|A strong requirement|A completed past action|A weak possibility only|A quotation mark|Must distinguish sets a requirement for performers.","What does should express in the note?|A recommendation|Proof the discussion already occurred|A personal pronoun|An unconditional historical fact|Should discuss recommends a follow-up action.","Which reported statement preserves Lea\u2019s evidence?|Lea said that she saw a loose panel before the storm.|Lea said the new pupils damaged the mural.|Lea confessed to breaking the roof.|Lea said no panel existed.|Only the first keeps her observation without adding an accusation.","Which conflict arc matches the proposed acts?|Damage, rumor and investigation, evidence and correction|Evidence, unrelated holiday, no ending|Correction, damage, accusation with no record|A sequence of three unrelated recipes|The first follows the outline\u2019s development and resolution.","What remains necessary before calling this a full-length production?|A developed script, rehearsals and a sustained performance|Only a longer title|Declaring the outline already performed|Removing every character|An outline alone does not satisfy the full performance task."],t.s)
B.aaW=new A.cg("Planning a Three-Act School Play","Q4 \xb7 full-length play structure and language choices",'Proposed play outline:\nAct I: A school mural is damaged. Lea says, "I saw a loose roof panel above the wall."\nAct II: A rumor blames new pupils. The class investigates instead of accusing them. Lea reports that she saw the loose panel before the storm.\nAct III: A repair record shows rain entered through the roof. The class corrects the rumor and requests professional repairs.\nDirector\u2019s note: Performers must distinguish accusation from evidence. We should discuss the rumor\u2019s harm after the play.',B.azC,"A full-length play develops characters and conflict across a sustained structure. An outline is a plan, not a completed full-length script or performance. Here Act I introduces damage, Act II complicates it through rumor, and Act III resolves the evidence question. Must signals a strong requirement; should gives a recommendation. Reported speech preserves what was observed, not the rumor.","What does Lea\u2019s observation establish? She saw a loose panel. It is not proof that new pupils caused the damage. The later repair record provides the account of rain entry that resolves this outline\u2019s investigation.","Explain must and should in the director\u2019s note. Then identify the turning point that resolves the accusation.","Must makes the evidence distinction a requirement for performers. Should recommends a discussion afterward. The repair record in Act III provides evidence of rain entry, prompting correction of the rumor.","Do not call three short outline paragraphs a full completed play. Do not turn Lea\u2019s observation into a quotation accusing the new pupils.","A longer play needs developed acts and performance; an outline can model their conflict arc while language choices keep evidence and responsibility clear.",B.aVr,B.axU)
B.aWv=s(["Build a qualified claim from evidence.","Organize reasons and limitations.","Paraphrase source notes with acknowledgment."],t.s)
B.aJk=s(["Support|Meeting record: three pupils missed a message-only change.","Limitation|Classroom check: visible board, irregular updates.","Qualified claim|Supplement messages if a named updater maintains the calendar."],t.s)
B.axH=s(["What question is being considered?|Whether to keep a printed class event calendar|Whether to close the school|Who won a national race|How to write every poem|The stated question concerns a printed calendar.","Which source records missed changes?|The class meeting record|A national census|A food receipt|A weather diary|Source note 1 comes from the meeting record.","How many pupils missed the change?|Three|Thirty|Every pupil|None|The note gives exactly three.","How was the change originally sent?|Only through a group message|Only on radio|Only by letter to every home|Only by a printed calendar|The first note specifies message-only delivery.","What is a strength of the noticeboard?|It is visible from the door.|It updates itself.|It reaches every home.|It never needs paper.|Source 2 gives the visibility detail.","What is its weakness?|Paper is not updated regularly.|It has no surface.|It is under water.|No one can see it from anywhere.|Irregular updating is the stated problem.","What does the draft propose?|A calendar that supplements messages|Replacing every source with rumors|Ending all notices|A national ban on phones|The claim says supplement, not replace.","What condition makes the proposal more practical?|A named volunteer updates it after changes.|Nobody checks dates.|All old notices stay forever.|The calendar has no writer.|The if clause assigns maintenance.","Which paraphrase accurately credits source 1?|The class meeting record reports three pupils missed a message-only date change.|Most pupils never read any message.|Everyone missed every event.|The record proves paper always works.|The first preserves count, event and source.","Which outline best fits the evidence?|Claim, missed-message evidence, outdated-paper limit, updater response|Claim, insult, unrelated poem, signature|Only a list of dates with no reasoning|An ending that denies both sources|The first uses support and responds to the actual weakness.","Which statement wrongly treats a proposal as a result?|The new calendar has already prevented all missed dates.|A volunteer could keep it current.|The claim is conditional.|The board is visible.|No implemented calendar outcome is supplied."],t.s)
B.aa8=new A.cg("From Evidence Notes to an Essay","Q4 \xb7 argument organization, paraphrase and citation","Question: Should our class keep a printed event calendar?\nSource note 1, class meeting record: three pupils missed a date change sent only through a group message.\nSource note 2, classroom check: a noticeboard is visible from the door, but the paper is not updated regularly.\nDraft claim: A printed calendar could supplement messages if a named volunteer updates it after each change.",B.aWv,"An argument is stronger when its claim fits both support and limits. Missed messages suggest a second channel may help. An outdated noticeboard warns that printing alone is insufficient. The if clause responds to that problem. Paraphrasing changes wording without changing scope; it still requires a clear source acknowledgment.","Why include a named updater? Source 2 shows the weakness of paper left unchanged. Assigning responsibility addresses that weakness instead of pretending paper is automatically reliable.","Plan an essay with a claim, one supporting reason, a limitation and a response.","Claim: supplement messages with a calendar. Reason: three pupils missed a message-only change. Limit: paper can become outdated. Response: assign a volunteer to update it.","Do not change three pupils to most pupils. Do not claim the calendar has already solved the problem; the draft proposes it.","Use evidence for a qualified claim, address the strongest relevant limit, and credit sources.",B.aJk,B.axH)
B.axG=s([B.ab_,B.aaN,B.aao,B.aaP,B.aaf,B.aa_,B.aaW,B.aa8],t.PF)
B.aBt=s(["Explain how objects and actions develop a theme.","Distinguish a supported reading from invented context.","Relate a literary concern to an ethical question."],t.s)
B.aHl=s(["Old envelope|Address plus a child\u2019s drawing links the traveler to a destination.","Translator\u2019s choice|Reading slowly and offering a card combines help with participation.","New card|The traveler\u2019s own written copy can suggest agency."],t.s)
B.b1r=s(["Where is the scene set?|An unnamed border station|A named embassy in Paris|A Philippine classroom|A ship identified by name|The opening leaves the location unnamed.","What does the traveler place on the desk?|A creased letter|A passport explicitly described|A wallet|A timetable|The stated object is a creased letter.","What does the clerk ask for?|The destination|The traveler\u2019s favorite poem|A school grade|The cost of a house|The clerk asks for the destination.","What drawing appears on the envelope?|A child\u2019s house drawing|A map of a named country|A portrait of the clerk|A train engine|The traveler points to a house drawn by a child.","How does the translator read the address?|Aloud and slowly|Silently and never again|As a joke|Only backward|The text states aloud, slowly.","What new object does she offer?|A blank card|A ticket|A phone|A coin|She slides over a blank card.","What does the traveler carry away?|The old envelope and new card|Only the clerk\u2019s desk|No written material|A named legal permit|The final line names both items.","Which fact is deliberately unspecified?|The traveler\u2019s nationality|The existence of a letter|The card\u2019s blank state at first|The house drawing|No nationality is stated.","Which theme is supported by the translator\u2019s request to write?|Help can also build a person\u2019s agency.|All borders are identical.|Letters have no value.|Every traveler must be a translator.|Inviting participation adds a reusable personal tool.","Which symbolic interpretation uses textual evidence?|The new card may represent a step toward independent navigation.|The card proves a specific citizenship.|The drawing identifies a real country.|The clerk secretly owns the traveler\u2019s house.|The first uses the copying action and qualifies the interpretation.","What is needed for broader world-literature alignment beyond this original exercise?|Contextualized literary works from multiple traditions|Calling one unnamed scene universal history|Inventing an author\u2019s nationality|Removing all literary context|A single original skill passage cannot replace that corpus."],t.s)
B.aad=new A.cg("The Translator at the Border","Q1 \xb7 literary elements, symbolism and human concerns",'At an unnamed border station, a traveler placed a creased letter on the translator\u2019s desk. The clerk asked for the destination. The traveler pointed to a child\u2019s drawing of a house on the envelope. The translator read the address aloud, slowly, then slid over a blank card. "Write it once more for yourself," she said. The traveler left holding both the old envelope and the new card.',B.aBt,"A symbol gains meaning through its use in a text. The old envelope carries an existing connection; the new card may suggest greater independence in navigating an unfamiliar place. This original fiction leaves country, nationality and migration status unspecified. Do not supply them as facts. World-literature study also needs actual contextualized works from different literary traditions.","Does the translator merely give a destination? She also offers a card and asks the traveler to write. This action supports a theme of assistance that builds agency, not just one-time dependence.","Compare the old envelope and new card. Support one possible symbolic reading.","The envelope preserves the address and a child\u2019s image of home. The new card can represent a tool the traveler creates for personal use. Both remain with the traveler.","Do not identify a real country or legal status from an unnamed border. A possible symbol is an interpretation, not a hidden fact that the narrator has confirmed.","Connect literary objects to actions and patterns; qualify interpretations where context is deliberately limited.",B.aHl,B.b1r)
B.aFc=s(["Distinguish intensive and reflexive uses.","Match pronouns to their antecedents.","Explain ethical limits when presenting results."],t.s)
B.aZ6=s(["Intensive|I myself checked emphasizes the speaker.","Reflexive|Volunteers taught themselves returns the action to the subject.","Ethical scope|No names; results describe this class only."],t.s)
B.aMn=s(["Who checked the totals?|The speaker|An unnamed national office|Every respondent separately|A newspaper editor|I myself checked identifies the speaker.","What kind of pronoun use is myself in I myself checked?|Intensive|Reflexive object|Relative|Interrogative|It adds removable emphasis to I.","What happens if myself is removed from I myself checked?|The core action still makes sense.|The subject disappears.|The sentence becomes a question.|The totals become names.|I checked remains a complete statement.","What kind of use is themselves in taught themselves?|Reflexive|Intensive emphasis only|Demonstrative|Interrogative|The action returns to the volunteers as learners.","Who does themselves refer to?|The volunteers|The totals|The chart\u2019s bars|All classes nationwide|Volunteers is the plural antecedent.","Were names recorded?|No|Yes, all names|Only teachers\u2019 names|Only surnames|The speech explicitly says no names were recorded.","What population do the results describe?|This class only|Every school|Every household|The whole country|The speaker gives an explicit scope limit.","What will the speaker do after an error?|Correct the error personally|Blame respondents|Publish private names|Hide all figures|I will correct myself commits to responsibility.","Which sentence correctly uses an intensive pronoun?|The volunteers themselves checked the chart.|Themselves checked the chart.|Myself is the chart.|Himself volunteers checked.|The pronoun emphasizes the stated subject.","Which use is reflexive rather than intensive?|I will correct myself.|I myself checked.|The volunteers themselves checked.|The teacher herself checked.|Correct myself makes the speaker the object of the action.","Which presentation choice violates the stated ethics?|Naming a respondent in the speech|Keeping names private|Stating the class-only scope|Correcting an error|The speech explicitly prohibits identifying respondents."],t.s)
B.aaT=new A.cg("Pronouns in an Ethical Speech","Q1 \xb7 reflexive and intensive pronouns; speaking ethics","I myself checked the class survey totals before presenting them. The volunteers taught themselves to read the chart. We did not record names, and no speaker should identify a respondent. Our results describe this class only. If I make an error, I will correct myself rather than blame the respondents.",B.aFc,"An intensive pronoun adds emphasis: I myself checked can lose myself without changing the basic sentence. A reflexive pronoun refers an action back to its subject: taught themselves means volunteers taught their own group. Correct myself similarly makes the speaker the object. Ethical speaking also protects identities and states scope.","Test myself in I myself checked. Remove it: I checked still gives the same core action. It is intensive. In I will correct myself, removing myself leaves the object unstated and changes the meaning: it is reflexive.","Classify themselves in the second sentence and explain one privacy rule in the speech.","Themselves is reflexive: volunteers teach the same people named as the subject. No speaker should identify a respondent because the survey does not record names.","Do not use myself as a fancy replacement for I in Myself checked the totals. Emphasis and reflexive action are different jobs even when the form is identical.","Identify a pronoun\u2019s job from its sentence; present evidence accurately and protect respondents.",B.aZ6,B.aMn)
B.aUF=s(["Identify claim, evidence and warrant.","Evaluate a counterclaim respectfully.","Distinguish a prudent recommendation from an unsupported solution."],t.s)
B.aDP=s(["Claim|Assess step-free stage access before the assembly.","Evidence and warrant|Three public-route steps may limit independent access.","Qualified action|A professional assesses safe options and cost."],t.s)
B.aNw=s(["What action is requested?|A professional assessment of step-free access|A pupil-built structure immediately|Cancellation of every assembly|A ban on all stairs|The opening and ending request assessment.","What did the walkthrough observe?|Three steps on the only public route|A certified lift|Five approved ramps|No stage|Three steps are the stated evidence.","What is the counterclaim?|Carrying a participant up is enough.|No assembly will occur.|All routes are already step-free.|Only professionals may attend.|The third sentence presents that alternative.","Which concerns answer the counterclaim?|Safety and independence|Only paint color|Only applause volume|Only ticket sales|The rebuttal names both concerns.","Who should assess options?|A qualified professional|Pupils improvising alone|A random anonymous poster|Nobody|The request explicitly names qualified assessment.","What should the assessment include?|Options and costs|Only a slogan|Every pupil\u2019s private history|Only a poem|Options and costs are the stated tasks.","What does the writer explicitly reject?|An improvised pupil-built structure|All access planning|Every professional opinion|Any assembly|The final clause excludes improvisation by pupils.","Which connector introduces the rebuttal?|However|Before|Only|Next|However marks the response to the counterclaim.","Which warrant links the steps to assessment?|A stepped-only route can restrict independent access.|All pupils enjoy drawing.|Paint makes every route accessible.|Costs never matter.|The first explains why the observation is relevant.","Which conclusion overstates the passage?|A particular ramp design is already proven safe.|An assessment is requested.|Three steps were observed.|Independence is a concern.|No technical design or safety validation is supplied.","Which response engages the counterclaim respectfully?|Carrying may raise safety and independence concerns, so assess alternatives.|Anyone suggesting carrying is evil.|No one may question the writer.|Ignore all practical concerns.|The first critiques the option with reasons rather than attacking people."],t.s)
B.aau=new A.cg("An Argument for an Accessible Stage","Q2 \xb7 argument, counterclaim and persuasive technique","The school should assess a step-free route to the stage before the next assembly. In our accessibility walkthrough, the only public route had three steps. Some argue that carrying a participant up is enough. However, that approach can compromise safety and independence. We ask a qualified professional to assess options and costs; we do not propose an improvised structure built by pupils.",B.aUF,"A claim says what should be accepted or done. Evidence supports it; a warrant explains why the evidence matters. Three steps matter because some participants cannot independently use stairs. The counterclaim offers carrying, while the rebuttal raises safety and agency. The recommendation is a professional assessment, not technical construction advice.","What bridges the observation to the recommendation? A stepped-only public route can limit access. A qualified assessment can evaluate safe alternatives. That reasoning supports assessment without claiming an untested design is safe.","State the counterclaim and one reason the writer rejects it.","The counterclaim says carrying a participant is enough. The writer objects that it may compromise safety and independence, so a safe access option should be assessed.","Do not turn this language lesson into instructions for building a ramp. No measurements, design approval or complete accessibility audit are provided.","Connect claim and evidence through an explicit reason, address alternatives, and respect the limits of expertise.",B.aDP,B.aNw)
B.aAX=s(["Apply two stated critical perspectives.","Support interpretations with details.","Recognize what a reading emphasizes or leaves unresolved."],t.s)
B.aE6=s(["Formalist pattern|Fence and inside-outside contrasts recur.","Social access|Tickets determine entry; children remain outside.","Unresolved tension|A kind bench helps but leaves the entry rule unchanged."],t.s)
B.b_y=s(["Who unlocks the garden?|Ivo|The children|A ticket seller not named|The narrator|Ivo is the caretaker.","Who may enter under the rule?|Ticket holders|Everyone without conditions|Only children|Only bench makers|The opening states ticket holders.","What do children watch?|The fountain|A film screen|A sports race|A classroom|They watch the fountain beyond the fence.","When does Ivo place the bench?|During his lunch break|At a stated midnight|During a holiday not mentioned|After the garden disappears|The text specifies his own lunch break.","Where is the bench?|Outside the fence in shade|Inside the fountain|On the ticket desk|Under the manager\u2019s bed|The bench is outside the fence.","How does the manager respond?|Praises kindness but keeps the rule|Removes all tickets immediately|Destroys the bench|Invites everyone in|Both praise and unchanged rule are stated.","Which detail suits a formalist focus?|The repeated inside-outside boundary|An invented author biography|An unstated national law|A fabricated ticket price|Formalist analysis attends to patterns in the text.","Which question suits a social-class lens?|How does paid entry shape access?|How many syllables are in caretaker only?|What color is an unspecified wall?|What is Ivo\u2019s unknown birth date?|The lens examines social and economic access.","Which interpretation preserves the ending\u2019s tension?|Ivo helps, but the access barrier remains.|The bench gives everyone garden entry.|The manager abolishes tickets.|The children never receive any help.|The bench is useful while entry remains restricted.","Which claim requires evidence not supplied?|The author based Ivo on a real relative.|The fence is a boundary.|The ticket rule remains.|The manager praises Ivo.|No author biography or real-life model is provided.","What is the strongest comparative use of the two lenses?|Explain both the boundary imagery and the unequal access it frames.|Choose one label and ignore the passage.|Treat every interpretation as equally proved.|Invent a new ending for each lens.|The first combines distinct questions while staying with textual evidence."],t.s)
B.aaH=new A.cg("Reading the Locked Garden through Two Lenses","Q3 \xb7 critical approaches and textual evidence","Every morning, caretaker Ivo unlocked a garden only for ticket holders. Beyond the fence, children watched the fountain. Ivo used his own lunch break to place a bench outside the fence in its shade. The manager praised his kindness but kept the ticket rule. The children rested on the bench; the garden remained closed to them.",B.aAX,"A formalist reading examines patterns within the text: locked and unlocked, inside and outside, repeated fence imagery. A social-class lens asks how money and rules shape access. Neither lens permits invented facts about an author or country. The ending holds two truths: Ivo offers real help, and the barrier remains.","From a social-class lens, what matters about ticket holders? Payment controls access. The bench helps people outside but does not change the rule, so kindness and structural exclusion coexist.","Give one formal pattern and one access question raised by the ending.","The fence and inside-outside contrast repeat a boundary pattern. An access question is: who can enter when tickets remain required? The passage does not say whether the rule will later change.","Do not reduce Ivo to wholly good or wholly useless. Acknowledge his help and its limits. Do not invent the author\u2019s biography to explain the rule.","A critical lens focuses questions; evidence constrains answers and can preserve unresolved tensions.",B.aE6,B.b_y)
B.aZz=s(["Explain a special-occasion speech\u2019s audience and purpose.","Analyze how modifiers make references precise.","Choose ethical and effective oral delivery."],t.s)
B.aP2=s(["Occasion|New reading-circle members are welcomed.","Modifier and anecdote|Who rarely spoke makes the pupil\u2019s later leadership significant.","Inclusive invitation|Your questions too connects the past example with the audience."],t.s)
B.aw4=s(["What occasion does the speech mark?|Welcoming new reading-circle members|Announcing a research sample|Selling a microphone|Closing a courtroom|The first sentence welcomes new members.","What did the pupil do at the end of last year?|Led the final discussion|Closed the library|Won a named national prize|Left without ever speaking|The anecdote states that the pupil led discussion.","What was her first contribution?|A question|A perfect answer|A complaint about every member|A purchase|The speech contrasts a question with a perfect answer.","Which word modifies listeners?|Patient|Today|Welcome|Wrong|Patient describes the listeners\u2019 manner.","What does who rarely spoke modify?|Pupil|Room|Year|Answer|The relative clause identifies a feature of the pupil.","What does your refer to in your questions too?|The new members being addressed|Only the former pupil|The speech paper|The room itself|The speaker directly includes the audience.","What is the main purpose?|Build belonging and invite participation|Prove a sales claim|Give repair instructions|Report a national census|The welcome and final invitation serve inclusion.","Which phrase most directly includes the new audience?|Your questions too|Last year|The final discussion|A perfect answer|Your and too extend the earlier example to listeners.","Why is the relative clause important to the anecdote?|It highlights the contrast between earlier quietness and later leadership.|It proves every pupil is identical.|It names the speaker\u2019s hometown.|It supplies a research sample size.|The modifier gives the change its significance.","Which delivery best supports the purpose?|A warm pace and emphasis on the inclusive invitation|Mocking hesitant speakers|Demanding everyone disclose a private fear|Rushing so no phrase is clear|Inclusive, respectful delivery matches the welcome.","Which conclusion overgeneralizes the anecdote?|Every quiet pupil will certainly lead a discussion.|One formerly quiet pupil led a discussion.|Listeners helped a question become conversation.|The speech welcomes questions.|One example cannot guarantee the same outcome for everyone."],t.s)
B.aaB=new A.cg("A Speech to Welcome New Readers","Q3 \xb7 special-occasion speech and structures of modification","Welcome, new members of our reading circle. Last year, a pupil who rarely spoke led our final discussion. Her first contribution was a question, not a perfect answer. The patient listeners in this room helped that question grow into a conversation. Today, we welcome your questions too. Let us begin with one another, not with the fear of being wrong.",B.aZz,"A welcome speech marks an occasion and builds belonging. It differs from a research report or a product advertisement. The relative clause who rarely spoke modifies pupil and makes the anecdote meaningful. Patient modifies listeners. These details support the message that a group can make room for a hesitant voice. Warm pacing and eye contact can support welcome without pressuring anyone to reveal a private experience.","Why include who rarely spoke? Remove it and the pupil\u2019s later leadership loses part of its contrast. The modifier explains why the change illustrates the circle\u2019s welcoming role.","Identify the occasion, one supporting anecdote and one phrase to emphasize in delivery.","The occasion is welcoming new reading-circle members. A once-quiet pupil later led discussion. Emphasizing your questions too directly includes new members.","Do not claim every quiet pupil will become a leader. The anecdote illustrates a possibility, not a universal guarantee. A warm speech need not force participation.","Fit a special-occasion speech to its audience; use specific modifying language and inclusive delivery to make the purpose clear.",B.aP2,B.aw4)
B.aGV=s(["Identify population, response count and measure.","Evaluate sampling and time limits.","Write a conclusion proportional to evidence."],t.s)
B.aCS=s(["Sample|80 invited; 50 optional anonymous responses.","Results|Walk 22; public 18; private 10.","Scope|One rainy Monday; walking leads but is below half of respondents."],t.s)
B.b2b=s(["What morning does the study describe?|One rainy Monday|Every dry Friday|A full school year|Every holiday|The question limits it to one rainy Monday.","How many pupils were offered the form?|80|50|22|18|The method gives 80 invited pupils.","How many responded?|50|80|30|10|Fifty completed responses form the dataset.","How many reported walking?|22|18|10|50|The result gives 22.","Which category had 18 responses?|Public transport|Walking|Private vehicle|Cycling as a separate category|Public transport is listed with 18.","Was response compulsory?|No, it was optional.|Yes, all 80 answered.|Only walkers were required.|Only dry-day travelers answered.|The method says optional.","Which category is largest?|Walking|Public transport|Private vehicle|All are tied|Twenty-two exceeds eighteen and ten.","Do 22 walkers form a majority of 50?|No|Yes, exactly half|Yes, all respondents|Yes, because any largest group is a majority|Twenty-two is less than 25, half of 50.","Which conclusion is proportional to the data?|Walking led among respondents that rainy morning.|All pupils usually walk.|Every nonrespondent uses a car.|Rain never affects travel.|The first preserves sample and time limits.","Which limitation concerns missing people?|Nonrespondents may travel differently.|The chart has three categories.|Monday has six letters.|Walking is listed first.|Optional nonresponse can affect representativeness.","What additional evidence would help describe usual travel?|Repeated observations across varied days with better response coverage|Renaming the current chart|A larger title|Removing the method section|Broader time and response coverage better match a usual-travel question."],t.s)
B.aaM=new A.cg("Checking a School Travel Study","Q4 \xb7 research question, method and defensible conclusion","Question: How did Grade 10 respondents travel to school on one rainy Monday?\nMethod: An anonymous optional form was offered to 80 pupils; 50 responded. Each chose one main travel mode for that morning.\nResults: 22 walked, 18 used public transport, and 10 used a private vehicle.\nDraft conclusion: Walking was the most reported mode among respondents that morning. The results may not represent nonrespondents or dry days.",B.aGV,"Research reports should make methods and limitations visible. Eighty pupils were invited, but the data comes from fifty respondents. Optional response may introduce nonresponse bias if others travel differently. One rainy morning is not a typical year. Most reported category does not mean a majority: 22 is less than half of 50.","Can we say most respondents walked? Walking is the largest category, but 22 of 50 is 44 percent, below 50 percent. Use most reported mode, not a majority walked.","Rewrite All Grade 10 pupils usually walk using the study\u2019s actual scope.","Walking was the largest reported travel category among the 50 respondents on that rainy Monday. This does not describe all pupils or their usual travel.","Do not use 80 as the respondent denominator. Do not equate a plurality with a majority or one day with a habit.","Name who responded, what was measured and when; distinguish the largest category from a majority.",B.aCS,B.b2b)
B.aQ1=s(["Synthesize compatible findings from two sources.","Paraphrase without changing scope or meaning.","Acknowledge sources and distinguish evidence from recommendation."],t.s)
B.atr=s(["Use evidence|A: 36 after-hours returns in four weeks.","Cost and condition|B: ten sorting minutes; no observed trial damage.","Synthesis|Access benefit plus workload, without a permanent safety claim."],t.s)
B.ayH=s(["How long was the trial?|Four weeks|One day|A full year|Ten years|Source A states four weeks.","How many books were collected after closing?|36|10|4|50|The library log gives 36 books.","When did the second box extend returns?|After the main desk closed|Only before sunrise by rule|Only during lessons|Only on holidays|After-hours use is the stated benefit.","Which source describes sorting time?|The librarian\u2019s note|Only a weather report|An anonymous advertisement|A student poem|Source B records staff workload.","How much extra sorting time was needed each morning?|Ten minutes|One hour|Four weeks|No time|Source B gives ten extra minutes.","What damage was observed during the trial?|None|Damage to every book|Exactly ten broken covers|Thirty-six torn pages|Source B states no damage was found.","What relationship does but express in the draft?|Benefit contrasted with added work|Two unrelated dates|A guaranteed future|A definition of library|But joins access benefit and sorting cost.","Does 36 books prove 36 different borrowers?|No|Yes, necessarily|Yes, because boxes count people|Only if books have covers|A person may return more than one book.","Which synthesis misrepresents the evidence?|The box is permanently safe and effortless.|It enabled after-hours returns but added sorting.|No damage was observed during four weeks.|The librarian noted extra sorting time.|Permanent safety overreaches, and effortless contradicts the workload.","Which paraphrase properly acknowledges evidence?|The school library log records 36 after-hours returns in a four-week trial.|The box helped, according to nobody.|These are my original measured results.|No source is needed because I changed words.|The first credits the source and preserves count and period.","Which recommendation is appropriately qualified?|Consider extending the trial while tracking workload and damage.|Guarantee the box can never harm a book.|Remove every staff member because sorting is free.|Declare all future returns measured already.|The first treats extension as a proposal and gathers missing longer-term evidence."],t.s)
B.aaK=new A.cg("Integrating Sources without Copying","Q4 \xb7 research writing, paraphrase and synthesis","Source A, school library log: During a four-week trial, a second return box collected 36 books after the main desk closed.\nSource B, librarian\u2019s note: Staff needed an extra ten minutes each morning to sort the second box; no damage was found during the trial.\nStudent draft: The trial provided an after-hours return option, but it also added morning sorting time.",B.aQ1,"Synthesis makes a relationship between sources explicit. A describes use; B adds workload and observed condition. The draft combines a benefit and a cost with but. A paraphrase uses a new structure and accurate wording, yet still credits the source. No damage during four weeks does not prove damage is impossible forever.","Is The box is perfectly safe and effortless a fair synthesis? No. Effortless contradicts ten extra minutes, and perfectly safe exceeds the short no-damage observation. Preserve both the cost and the time limit.","Write a two-sentence synthesis that credits both sources and includes a limitation.","The school library log records 36 after-hours returns during a four-week second-box trial. The librarian\u2019s note reports ten extra sorting minutes each morning and no damage in that period, but longer-term outcomes remain unknown.","Changing a few words without credit is not enough. Do not change 36 books to 36 different pupils or trial evidence to a permanent guarantee.","Combine sources through a clear relationship, retain their limits, and credit ideas as well as quotations.",B.atr,B.ayH)
B.aMA=s([B.aad,B.aaT,B.aau,B.aaH,B.aaB,B.aaM,B.aaK],t.PF)
B.LD=new A.ao(B.wi,[B.auA,B.aKX,B.aXh,B.asq,B.azf,B.b0t,B.aVf,B.aMR,B.axG,B.aMA],A.aC("ao<j,V<cg>>"))
