s($,"ceF","bRS",()=>{var q=t.s,p=t.N
return A.a([A.cQ(A.a(["Name shapes by their sides and corners.","Count and compare numbers to 100.","Use position words through tenth."],q),"Do not call a turned square a different shape. Start counting positions at the end named in the question.","How many sides has a triangle?|3|2;4;5|Trace the three straight edges.\nWhich shape has four equal sides and four square corners?|Square|Triangle;Circle;Pentagon|All four sides match and each corner is square.\nWhat comes just after 69?|70|68;60;79|One more than 69 is 70.\nWhat is one less than 40?|39|41;30;49|Move one step back from 40.\nWhich is greater: 17 or 12?|17|12;They are equal;Neither is a number|Seventeen comes after twelve when counting.\nPut 9, 3, 12 in order from least.|3, 9, 12|12, 9, 3;9, 3, 12;3, 12, 9|Start with three, then nine, then twelve.\nFrom the left, a row is cat, dog, hen. Which is second?|Dog|Cat;Hen;None|The dog follows the first animal.\nHow many are ten groups of ten?|100|10;20;90|Count tens ten times to reach one hundred.\nTwo matching triangles join along a square's diagonal. What whole do they make?|A square|A circle;A line;A single triangle|The diagonal splits the square into two matching triangles.\nWhat number is 8 tens and 2 ones?|82|28;80;12|Eighty and two make eighty-two.\nA learner is ninth in line. One learner stands just behind. What position is that learner?|Tenth|Eighth;First;Ninth|The position after ninth is tenth.\n","Cora is third. Count Ana first and Ben second. Eighteen is greater: it is three steps after fifteen.","A triangle has three straight sides and three corners. A square has four equal sides and four square corners. A rectangle has four square corners. Turning a shape does not change its name. Two matching triangles can make a square. Count each object once. Ten groups of ten make 100. On a number line, numbers grow as you move right. First, second, and third tell positions, not how many.",A.F(["Triangle","3 sides and 3 corners, even when turned.","Square","4 equal sides and 4 square corners.","Number line","16, 17, 18, 19, 20: each step adds one.","Position","A, B, C: B is second from the left."],p,p),"Use sides and corners to name shapes. Count once per object. A position tells a place in a row.","Shapes and Numbers to 100","A row from the left is Ana, Ben, Cora. Who is third? Which is greater, 18 or 15?","Count 3 groups of ten and 4 loose shells. First count the groups: 10, 20, 30. Next count four more: 31, 32, 33, 34. Write 34. Check by counting every shell once."),A.cQ(A.a(["Split a number into two parts.","Add by counting on to 20.","Find a missing part in an addition story."],q),"Count on after the starting number, not including it as the first added step. Zero is not one extra object.","What is 4 + 3?|7|6;8;1|Count five, six, seven after four.\nWhat is 9 + 0?|9|0;10;90|Zero adds no objects.\nWhat is 6 + 6?|12|11;13;0|Two groups of six contain twelve.\nWhich has the same total as 3 + 8?|8 + 3|8 - 3;3 + 3;8 + 8|Changing the order of the parts keeps the total.\nWhat is missing in 5 + __ = 9?|4|3;5;9|Five needs four more to reach nine.\nWhat is 10 + 7?|17|7;16;18|Ten and seven ones make seventeen.\nWhich two parts make 10?|6 and 4|6 and 3;5 and 4;8 and 3|Six plus four reaches ten.\nWhat is 7 + 8?|15|14;16;1|Use 7 + 3 + 5 to make fifteen.\nNine shells and five more make how many?|14|13;15;4|Nine plus one makes ten and four remain.\nWhat is missing in __ + 6 = 18?|12|11;13;24|Twelve and six make eighteen.\nLia has 8 red and 4 blue buttons. She adds 3 green buttons. Total?|15|12;14;16|First 8 + 4 = 12, then 12 + 3 = 15.\n","Six and four make ten birds. Seven needs three more to make ten, so the missing part is three.","Addition puts groups together. In 6 + 3, keep six in mind and count three more: seven, eight, nine. A number can have different parts: 8 is 5 and 3 or 4 and 4. Adding zero leaves the group unchanged. Swapping the two groups does not change how many there are.",A.F(["Part","7 red beads","Other part","5 blue beads","Whole","7 + 5 = 12 beads","Make ten","7 + 3 + 2 = 10 + 2"],p,p),"Put together, count on, or make ten. Both parts must be counted.","Putting Together to 20","There are 6 birds. Four more land. How many now? What part is missing in 7 + __ = 10?","Add 8 + 5. First notice eight needs two to make ten. Split five into two and three. Add 8 + 2 = 10. Add the three left over: 13. Check by counting five steps after eight."),A.cQ(A.a(["Measure with equal small units.","Split two-digit numbers into tens and ones.","Add without exchanging tens."],q),"Different-sized clips cannot be compared fairly. In 43, the four means forty, not four.","What value does 6 have in 64?|60|6;4;64|Six is in the tens place.\nWhat number is 5 tens and 8 ones?|58|85;13;50|Fifty plus eight is fifty-eight.\nWhat is 24 + 13?|37|36;47;11|Two tens plus one ten and four plus three ones make 37.\nWhat comes after 35 when counting by fives?|40|36;45;30|Add another five.\nWhat comes after 76 when counting by twos?|78|77;80;74|Two more than 76 is 78.\nWhich gives a fair length measurement?|Equal clips with no gaps|Mixed clips;Clips with gaps;Clips piled up|Repeated equal units must touch end to end.\nA ribbon is 8 cubes long. A string is 5 of the same cubes long. Difference?|3 cubes|13 cubes;5 cubes;8 cubes|Eight minus five leaves three units.\nWhat is 42 + 7?|49|47;59;35|Keep four tens and add two plus seven ones.\nWhich number is largest?|91|19;81;89|Nine tens exceed eight tens or one ten.\nWhat is 63 + 24?|87|77;89;39|Six tens plus two tens and three plus four ones make 87.\nTwo sticks measure 3 and 5 equal blocks. End to end they measure what?|8 blocks|2 blocks;15 blocks;5 blocks|Join the lengths: three plus five equals eight.\n","The book is two clips longer because 6 - 4 = 2. Add 50 + 20 = 70 and 1 + 6 = 7 to get 77.","To measure a pencil with clips, use clips of the same size. Place them end to end with no gaps or overlaps. A longer object uses more of the same unit. In 43, four tens mean forty and three ones mean three. Add tens to tens and ones to ones. Counting by twos, fives, or tens is a quick way to count equal groups.",A.F(["Pencil","[clip][clip][clip][clip] = 4 equal clips long","43","4 tens + 3 ones","43 + 25","6 tens + 8 ones = 68","Count fives","5, 10, 15, 20"],p,p),"Measure with one repeated unit. Keep tens and ones in their own places when adding.","Measuring and Adding Tens and Ones","A book is 6 clips long and a card is 4 of the same clips long. How much longer is the book? Add 51 + 26.","Add 32 + 14. Split into 30 + 2 and 10 + 4. Join the tens: 40. Join the ones: 6. Put them together: 46. Check that the ones total less than ten, so no exchange is needed."),A.cQ(A.a(["Subtract by taking away.","Subtract tens and ones without exchanging.","Find a missing number and check with addition."],q),"Do not reverse the numbers just to subtract a larger digit. These examples need no exchange. An equals sign means both sides name the same amount.","What is 9 - 4?|5|4;6;13|Remove four from nine and five remain.\nWhat is 18 - 8?|10|8;26;9|Eight ones leave one ten.\nWhat is 67 - 25?|42|52;44;92|Six minus two tens and seven minus five ones give 42.\nWhat is 45 - 3?|42|48;15;43|Remove three ones from five ones.\nWhat is missing in 6 + __ = 14?|8|7;9;20|Six and eight make fourteen.\nWhat is missing in 15 - __ = 9?|6|5;7;24|Nine left plus six removed rebuilds fifteen.\nWhich equals 8 - 3?|9 - 4|8 + 3;9 - 3;7 - 3|Both expressions equal five.\nHow can you check 36 - 12 = 24?|24 + 12 = 36|36 + 12 = 24;24 - 12 = 36;36 - 24 = 24|Add the part left and the part taken away.\nA tray has 16 eggs. Five are used. How many remain?|11|10;12;21|Sixteen minus five is eleven.\nWhat is 89 - 46?|43|33;45;53|Eight minus four tens and nine minus six ones leave 43.\nBen has 19 cards and Ana has 12. How many more has Ben?|7|31;6;8|Subtract twelve from nineteen to compare the groups.\n","Seven tens minus three tens is four tens. Six ones minus two ones is four ones: 44. Nine needs seven more to make sixteen.","Subtraction can tell how many are left or how far apart two groups are. Take three from eight to leave five. For 58 - 24, take two tens from five tens and four ones from eight ones. The answer is three tens and four ones. Addition can check subtraction: the part left plus the part removed makes the starting whole.",A.F(["Whole","58 = 5 tens and 8 ones","Remove","24 = 2 tens and 4 ones","Left","34 = 3 tens and 4 ones","Check","34 + 24 = 58"],p,p),"Whole minus part gives the other part. Add the two parts to check.","Taking Away and Finding Parts","Find 76 - 32. Then find the missing part in 9 + __ = 16.","There are 17 mangoes and 6 are sold. Start at seventeen. Take away six: 17 - 6 = 11. Check eleven left and six sold make seventeen. Answer: eleven mangoes."),A.cQ(A.a(["Read a picture chart with one picture per object.","Make a total and compare categories.","Find and repeat a pattern unit."],q),"A picture means one here, not five or ten. Do not treat a repeating pattern as a growing number pattern.","One picture means one vote. A row has 6 pictures. Votes?|6|1;5;12|Count one vote for each picture.\nRed has 4 votes and blue has 3. Total?|7|1;6;8|Add four and three votes.\nCats have 2 votes and dogs have 5. Which has more?|Dogs|Cats;Equal;Cannot compare|Five is greater than two.\nFive votes versus two votes differ by how many?|3|7;2;5|Subtract two from five.\nWhat follows A B A B A?|B|A;C;D|The repeating unit is A B.\nWhat follows clap tap tap clap tap tap?|Clap|Tap;Jump;Silence|A new clap tap tap unit begins.\nWhich is the repeating unit of 1 3 3 1 3 3?|1 3 3|1 3;3 1;1 1|The three-term block repeats exactly.\nWhat is a fair way to record a class fruit vote?|One answer per learner|Count one learner twice;Guess every answer;Skip quiet learners|Each learner should contribute once.\nA chart has 7 sun pictures and 4 rain pictures. Total days?|11|3;10;12|Seven plus four equals eleven.\nExtend square circle triangle square circle __.|Triangle|Square;Circle;Rectangle|The third shape completes the repeating group.\nAna says 3 pictures always mean 6 votes. The key says one picture is one vote. Correct total?|3 votes|6 votes;9 votes;1 vote|Use this chart's key rather than guessing a scale.\n","Dogs are more common by two because 5 - 3 = 2. The number pattern repeats 2, 4, so the next two terms are 2, 4.","Ask the same question to each person and record one answer each. A picture chart can show one picture for each vote. Count the pictures in each row. Keep rows lined up so they are easy to compare. A repeating pattern has a small part that repeats in the same order, such as red, blue, red, blue. The repeating part is red, blue.",A.F(["Fruit survey","One star means one vote.","Banana row","* * * * = 4 votes","Mango row","* * = 2 votes","Pattern","A B B / A B B / A B B"],p,p),"Read the picture key, count each row, and compare. Find the smallest repeating part before extending a pattern.","Picture Data and Repeating Patterns","A chart has 3 cat pictures and 5 dog pictures, one picture per pet. Which pet is more common? Extend 2, 4, 2, 4.","Four learners choose banana and two choose mango. Count all six votes. Compare four with two: banana has two more votes. For A B B A B B, mark groups of three. The next letter starts a new group, so it is A."),A.cQ(A.a(["Recognize equal halves and quarters.","Compare half and quarter of the same whole.","Follow clockwise and counterclockwise turns."],q),"Four unequal pieces are not four quarters. Compare fractions only when the wholes are the same size.","One of two equal parts is called what?|A half|A quarter;A whole;A corner|Two equal parts split one whole into halves.\nOne of four equal parts is called what?|A quarter|A half;Two wholes;A side|Four equal parts make quarters.\nHow many halves make a whole?|2|1;3;4|Join the two equal halves.\nHow many quarters make a whole?|4|2;3;8|Four equal quarters rebuild the whole.\nOf the same loaf, which is more: half or quarter?|Half|Quarter;Equal;Neither|Half contains two quarters.\nTwo quarters of a whole equal what?|One half|One quarter;Two wholes;One whole|Two of the four equal pieces are half.\nAn arrow points up. Quarter turn clockwise points where?|Right|Left;Down;Up|Follow a clock hand from up toward right.\nAn arrow points up. Quarter turn counterclockwise points where?|Left|Right;Down;Up|Turn against the clock hands toward left.\nAn arrow points down. Half turn points where?|Up|Down;Left;Right|Half a turn points in the opposite direction.\nThree quarters plus one quarter make what?|One whole|One half;Two quarters;Two wholes|All four equal pieces are present.\nWhich cut makes halves?|Two equal-sized pieces|One tiny and one large piece;Three equal pieces;Four unequal pieces|Halves require exactly two equal parts.\n","Four equal quarters make one whole. A half turn points the arrow the opposite way, so right becomes left.","A half is one of two equal parts. A quarter is one of four equal parts. Equal parts must be the same size. For the same whole, a half is larger than a quarter. Two quarters make a half. Turning changes which way an object faces. Clockwise turns follow clock hands. A quarter turn is one of four equal turns around. Two quarter turns make a half turn.",A.F(["Whole strip","[1/4][1/4][1/4][1/4]","Half strip","Two quarters = 1/2","Quarter turn clockwise","Up becomes right","Half turn","Up becomes down"],p,p),"Two halves or four quarters make a whole. A half turn faces the opposite direction.","Halves Quarters and Turns","How many quarters make one whole? An arrow points right. Where does a half turn point it?","A sandwich is cut into four equal pieces. One piece is one quarter. Two pieces are two quarters, which make a half. Check that all four pieces rebuild the whole. If an arrow points up, one clockwise quarter turn makes it point right."),A.cQ(A.a(["Read peso amounts to 100.","Combine coins and bills.","Solve one-step buying and change problems."],q),"Change is money paid minus price, not their sum. Count values rather than the number of coins.","Two \u20b15 coins are worth what?|\u20b110|\u20b15;\u20b12;\u20b125|Five plus five makes ten pesos.\nA \u20b120 bill and a \u20b110 coin total what?|\u20b130|\u20b110;\u20b120;\u20b140|Twenty plus ten equals thirty.\nWhich is worth more?|\u20b150 bill|\u20b120 bill;\u20b110 coin;\u20b15 coin|Fifty exceeds twenty, ten, and five.\nWhat is \u20b123 + \u20b114?|\u20b137|\u20b136;\u20b147;\u20b19|Add two tens and one ten, then three and four ones.\nPay \u20b120 for a \u20b112 item. Change?|\u20b18|\u20b132;\u20b112;\u20b110|Twenty minus twelve leaves eight.\nHow many \u20b110 coins make \u20b150?|5|4;6;10|Count ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty.\nWhich equals \u20b140?|Two \u20b120 bills|Two \u20b110 coins;One \u20b150 bill;Four \u20b15 coins|Twenty plus twenty makes forty.\nWhat is \u20b170 - \u20b120?|\u20b150|\u20b190;\u20b140;\u20b160|Remove two tens from seven tens.\nA snack costs \u20b124 and juice costs \u20b115. Total?|\u20b139|\u20b19;\u20b138;\u20b149|Twenty-four plus fifteen is thirty-nine.\nPay \u20b150 for a \u20b128 book. Change?|\u20b122|\u20b178;\u20b132;\u20b128|Twenty-eight plus twenty-two rebuilds fifty.\nWhich pays \u20b1100 exactly?|Two \u20b150 bills|One \u20b150 bill;Three \u20b120 bills;Five \u20b110 coins|Fifty plus fifty equals one hundred.\n","\u20b150 - \u20b135 = \u20b115 change. Three twenties make \u20b160, so they pay exactly.","The peso sign is \u20b1. A coin or bill has a written value. Its value is not its physical size. We can make one amount in different ways: two \u20b110 coins have the same value as a \u20b120 bill. Add prices to find the total. Subtract the price from the money paid to find change.",A.F(["\u20b120","\u20b110 + \u20b110","\u20b150 + \u20b120","\u20b170 altogether","Pay \u20b150 for \u20b130","\u20b150 - \u20b130 = \u20b120 change"],p,p),"Different coins and bills can make equal amounts. Add prices and subtract to find change.","Peso Coins Bills and Change","You pay \u20b150 for a \u20b135 snack. How much change? Can three \u20b120 bills pay for a \u20b160 item exactly?","A pen costs \u20b112 and a pad costs \u20b115. First join the tens: \u20b120. Then join the ones: \u20b17. The total is \u20b127. Check 12 + 15 = 27. The total is less than \u20b1100."),A.cQ(A.a(["Read hour, half-hour, and quarter-hour times.","Put days and months in order.","Solve short time and calendar problems."],q),"The long hand at 3 means fifteen minutes, not three minutes. A month is not always exactly four weeks.","At 5:00, where is the long hand?|12|5;6;3|An o\u2019clock time has zero minutes.\nHalf past 7 is what time?|7:30|7:00;7:15;7:45|Half an hour is thirty minutes.\nQuarter past 8 is what time?|8:15|8:30;8:45;9:15|A quarter hour is fifteen minutes.\nQuarter to 6 is what time?|5:45|6:15;6:45;5:15|Fifteen minutes before six is 5:45.\nHow many days are in one week?|7|5;6;12|A week runs through seven named days.\nWhich day comes after Friday?|Saturday|Thursday;Sunday;Monday|Saturday follows Friday.\nWhich month comes after March?|April|February;May;January|The month order is January, February, March, April.\nHow many months are in one year?|12|7;10;30|There are twelve named months.\nA lesson starts at 9:00 and lasts one hour. End time?|10:00|9:30;8:00;11:00|Move forward one whole hour.\nIf the calendar shows the 10th is Monday, what day is the 12th?|Wednesday|Tuesday;Thursday;Sunday|The 11th is Tuesday and the 12th is Wednesday.\nWhat is half an hour after 11:30?|12:00|11:00;11:45;12:30|Thirty more minutes complete the hour.\n","A quarter hour after 4:00 is 4:15. The next day after Tuesday is Wednesday.","The short hand tells the hour. The long hand tells minutes. At an o\u2019clock time the long hand points to 12. At half past it points to 6. At quarter past it points to 3, and at quarter to it points to 9. There are seven days in a week and twelve months in a year. A calendar places each date under its day of the week.",A.F(["3:00","Short hand at 3; long hand at 12.","3:15","Quarter past 3; long hand at 3.","3:30","Half past 3; short hand between 3 and 4.","3:45","Quarter to 4; long hand at 9."],p,p),"Read both hands. Use the calendar to check dates, weekdays, and month order.","Clock Time and Calendar Days","What time is a quarter hour after 4:00? If today is Tuesday, what day is tomorrow?","A game begins at 2:00 and lasts half an hour. First read the hour as two. A half hour moves the long hand halfway around to six. The end time is 2:30. Check the hour hand is between two and three.")],t.LQ)})
s($,"ceG","bRT",()=>{var q=t.s,p=t.N
return A.a([A.cQ(A.a(["Recognize a constant difference.","Find an arithmetic nth term and missing means.","Calculate finite arithmetic sums."],q),"Use n - 1 differences for the nth term. A sum is not the same as the last term.","Common difference of 6, 11, 16, 21?|5|6;4;11|Subtract consecutive terms.\nNext term of 20, 17, 14?|11|12;10;17|Subtract three again.\nNth term of 3, 7, 11, ...?|4n - 1|4n + 3;3n + 4;n + 4|Use three plus n minus one times four.\nTenth term if a\u2081 = 2 and d = 3?|29|32;27;30|Two plus nine threes equals twenty-nine.\nFirst five terms 2,4,6,8,10 sum to what?|30|20;25;50|Five times first plus last divided by two is thirty.\nArithmetic mean between 8 and 18?|13|10;12;26|Average the endpoints.\nHow many gaps separate term 1 from term 12?|11|12;13;10|There is one fewer gap than terms.\nWhich sequence is not arithmetic?|2,4,8,16|2,5,8,11;10,8,6,4;7,7,7,7|Its differences double rather than stay constant.\nFifteenth term of 4,9,14,...?|74|79;69;75|Four plus fourteen times five equals seventy-four.\nSum of first 20 positive integers?|210|200;190;400|Use twenty times one plus twenty divided by two.\nWhich term of 5,8,11,... equals 50?|16th|15th;17th;18th|Solve five plus three times n minus one equals fifty.\n","There are four equal gaps, so d = (20 - 4)/4 = 4. The means are eight, twelve, sixteen. Sum = 5(4 + 20)/2 = 60.","A sequence is an ordered list. Arithmetic sequences have a constant difference d. Starting with a\u2081, term n is a\u2099 = a\u2081 + (n - 1)d because there are n - 1 steps. Pairing first and last terms gives equal pair sums, so S\u2099 = n(a\u2081 + a\u2099)/2. Arithmetic means inserted between endpoints divide the total change into equal steps.",A.F(["Sequence","5, 9, 13, 17, ...","Difference","d = 4","Nth term","a\u2099 = 5 + 4(n - 1) = 4n + 1","First five sum","5(5 + 21)/2 = 65"],p,p),"Count gaps, find a constant difference, and pair endpoints for a finite sum.","Arithmetic Sequences and Finite Sums","Insert three arithmetic means between 4 and 20. Find the sum of the resulting five terms.","Find the twentieth term and sum of 7, 10, 13, .... Difference is three. a\u2082\u2080 = 7 + 19 \xd7 3 = 64. S\u2082\u2080 = 20(7 + 64)/2 = 710. Check the first and last sum to seventy-one and there are ten pairs."),A.cQ(A.a(["Find and apply a common ratio.","Calculate finite geometric sums.","Determine when an infinite geometric sum exists."],q),"A constant ratio is not a constant difference. Do not use the infinite-sum formula when |r| \u2265 1.","Common ratio of 4,12,36,...?|3|8;4;12|Divide each term by the previous term.\nNext term of 81,27,9,...?|3|0;6;1|Multiply by one third.\nFifth term of 2,4,8,...?|32|16;64;10|Use two times two to the fourth.\nCommon ratio of 3,-6,12,-24,...?|-2|2;-3;3|Each term is negative two times the previous.\nSum 1 + 2 + 4 + 8.|15|8;16;14|Add the four terms.\nDoes 1 + 2 + 4 + ... have a finite infinite sum?|No|Yes, it is -1;Yes, it is 2;Yes, it is 0|Its ratio two has magnitude greater than one.\nInfinite sum 4 + 2 + 1 + ...?|8|4;6;16|Use four divided by one minus one half.\nWhen does an infinite geometric series converge?|When the magnitude of r is less than 1|When r > 1;For every r;Only when r = 1|The terms then shrink enough for partial sums to approach a limit.\nSeventh term of 3,6,12,...?|192|96;384;21|Three times two to the sixth equals 192.\nSum of first four terms of 5,15,45,...?|200|180;135;205|The terms are five, fifteen, forty-five, and 135.\nInfinite sum 9 - 3 + 1 - 1/3 + ...?|27/4|6;9/4;27/2|The ratio is negative one third, so divide nine by four thirds.\n","Term six is 5 \xd7 2\u2075 = 160. The infinite series has ratio one half, so its sum is 6/(1 - 1/2) = 12.","A geometric sequence multiplies by a constant ratio r. Its nth term is a\u2099 = a\u2081r\u207f\u207b\xb9. For r \u2260 1, the first n terms sum to S\u2099 = a\u2081(1 - r\u207f)/(1 - r). For r = 1, use na\u2081. An infinite geometric series has a finite sum only when |r| < 1, giving S = a\u2081/(1 - r). Alternating signs are possible when r is negative.",A.F(["Sequence","3, 6, 12, 24, ...","Ratio","r = 2","Term","a\u2099 = 3 \xd7 2\u207f\u207b\xb9","Convergent sum","8 + 4 + 2 + ... = 8/(1 - 1/2) = 16"],p,p),"Identify the ratio, count multiplication steps, and check convergence before summing forever.","Geometric Sequences and Series","Find term six of 5, 10, 20, .... Find 6 + 3 + 1.5 + ... continued without end.","Find the first five terms sum of 2, 6, 18, .... Here a\u2081 = 2 and r = 3. S\u2085 = 2(1 - 3\u2075)/(1 - 3) = 242. Check by listing 2 + 6 + 18 + 54 + 162 = 242. An infinite sum does not converge because the ratio is greater than one."),A.cQ(A.a(["Divide polynomials and include missing powers.","Use the remainder and factor theorems.","Find candidate rational roots and verify factors."],q),"For x + 2 use c = -2. A remainder must have lower degree than the divisor. A listed rational candidate is not yet a root.","Remainder on dividing x\xb2 + 3x + 2 by x - 1?|6|0;2;3|Evaluate the polynomial at one.\nWhich value tests the factor x + 4?|-4|4;0;1/4|Write the divisor as x minus negative four.\nIs x - 2 a factor of x\xb2 - 4?|Yes|No;Only if x is positive;Only after rounding|The value at two is zero.\nDivide x\xb2 - 9 by x - 3.|Quotient x + 3, remainder 0|Quotient x - 3, remainder 0;Quotient x, remainder -9;Quotient 1, remainder 0|The dividend is a difference of squares.\nWhich coefficient list represents x\xb3 - 2x + 5?|1, 0, -2, 5|1, -2, 5;1, 2, 5;0, 1, -2, 5|Include zero for the missing x-squared term.\nIf P(5) = 0, which is a factor?|x - 5|x + 5;x;5x + 1|The factor theorem connects the root five with x minus five.\nPossible rational roots of monic x\xb2 - 5x + 6 come from divisors of what?|6|5;0;11|For a monic polynomial the denominator candidates are one.\nFor division by a linear polynomial, a remainder is what?|A constant|Always quadratic;Always the quotient;Higher degree than the dividend|Its degree must be less than one.\nRemainder of 2x\xb3 - x + 4 divided by x + 1?|3|-3;5;7|Evaluate at negative one: negative two plus one plus four.\nFactor x\xb3 - 6x\xb2 + 11x - 6 after finding roots 1,2,3.|(x - 1)(x - 2)(x - 3)|(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3);x(x - 1)(x - 2);(x - 1)\xb3|Each root contributes its corresponding linear factor.\nQuotient and remainder of x\xb2 + 1 divided by x - 1?|x + 1, remainder 2|x - 1, remainder 0;x + 1, remainder 0;x, remainder 1|Rebuild x\xb2 + 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1) + 2.\n","Evaluate at two: 8 - 8 + 1 = 1. Evaluate the second polynomial at three: 9 - 15 + 6 = 0, so x - 3 is a factor.","Polynomial division finds quotient and remainder so dividend = divisor \xd7 quotient + remainder. Insert zero coefficients for missing powers. Dividing P(x) by x - c leaves remainder P(c). Therefore x - c is a factor exactly when P(c) = 0. For integer coefficients, a rational root in lowest form p/q must have p dividing the constant and q dividing the leading coefficient. These are candidates, not guaranteed roots.",A.F(["Dividend","x\xb3 - 6x\xb2 + 11x - 6","Test x = 1","1 - 6 + 11 - 6 = 0","Division by x - 1","Quotient x\xb2 - 5x + 6; remainder 0","Complete factors","(x - 1)(x - 2)(x - 3)"],p,p),"Divide accurately, evaluate to check remainders, and use zero remainders to establish factors.","Polynomial Division and Root Theorems","Find the remainder when x\xb3 - 4x + 1 is divided by x - 2. Is x - 3 a factor of x\xb2 - 5x + 6?","Divide 2x\xb3 + 3x\xb2 - 5x + 6 by x + 2. Synthetic value is -2. Bring down 2, multiply and add to get -1, then -3, then remainder 12. Quotient is 2x\xb2 - x - 3. Check P(-2) = 12, so x + 2 is not a factor."),A.cQ(A.a(["Use degree and leading coefficient for end behaviour.","Relate root multiplicity to touching or crossing.","Connect factors with intercepts and a context."],q),"A graph may have fewer real roots than its degree. A repeated root is not a separate x-intercept at a new position.","Degree of 5x\u2074 - 2x + 7?|4|5;2;7|The highest nonzero power is four.\nRight-end behaviour of a polynomial with positive leading coefficient?|Rises|Falls;Always zero;Always horizontal|The positive highest-power term dominates for large positive x.\nBoth ends of y = x\u2074 rise or fall?|Rise|Fall;Left rises and right falls;Left falls and right rises|Even degree and positive leading coefficient make both ends rise.\nY-intercept of y = x\xb3 - 4x + 6?|6|-4;1;0|Set x equal to zero.\nRoot 2 in (x - 2)\xb2 has what multiplicity?|2|1;3;4|The factor is squared.\nAt an even-multiplicity real root, a polynomial graph usually does what?|Touches without crossing|Crosses;Has a gap;Becomes a vertical line|Its sign does not change across that root.\nZeros of (x + 4)(x - 1)?|-4 and 1|4 and -1;4 and 1;-4 and -1|Set each factor equal to zero.\nMaximum possible turning points for a cubic?|2|3;4;1|Degree minus one is the upper bound.\nEnd behaviour of y = -2x\xb3 + x?|Left rises, right falls|Both rise;Both fall;Left falls, right rises|Odd degree gives opposite ends and the leading sign is negative.\nY-intercept of (x - 2)(x + 3)(x - 1)?|6|-6;0;3|At zero multiply negative two, three, and negative one.\nHow many distinct x-intercepts has (x - 1)\xb2(x + 2)\xb3?|2|5;3;1|The two distinct roots are one and negative two.\n","It is a positive quadratic, with zeros three and negative one. At zero its value is negative three. Both ends rise.","A polynomial function has nonnegative integer powers and real coefficients. Far from zero, its leading term controls end behaviour. Even degree has both ends in the same direction; odd degree has opposite directions. A positive leading coefficient makes the right end rise. A root of odd multiplicity crosses the x-axis; one of even multiplicity touches and turns. The y-intercept is the constant term. Degree n allows at most n real roots and n - 1 turning points.",A.F(["Function","P(x) = (x + 1)(x - 2)\xb2","Zeros","-1 crosses; 2 touches","Y-intercept","P(0) = 4","Ends","Positive cubic: left down, right up"],p,p),"Read leading term, factors, multiplicities, and intercepts before plotting selected points.","Polynomial Functions and Graph Behaviour","For y = (x - 3)(x + 1), find degree, zeros, y-intercept, and end directions.","Sketch y = -(x - 1)\xb2(x + 2). It is cubic with negative leading coefficient, so left end rises and right end falls. The root one has multiplicity two, so the graph touches there. It crosses at negative two. At x = 0, y = -2, locating the y-intercept."),B.C6,A.cQ(A.a(["Use chord and arc angle relationships.","Apply tangent and intersecting-segment theorems.","Distinguish interior and exterior circle angles."],q),"The secant product uses external times whole, not external times internal. Interior chord angles use a sum; exterior angles use a difference.","Radius and tangent at contact meet at what angle?|90\xb0|45\xb0;180\xb0;60\xb0|A tangent is perpendicular to the contact radius.\nTwo tangents from one external point have lengths 7 and x. Find x.|7|14;3.5;49|Their tangent segments are equal.\nAn inscribed angle intercepts a 100\xb0 arc. Angle?|50\xb0|100\xb0;200\xb0;25\xb0|Take half the intercepted arc.\nIntersecting chords intercept opposite arcs 80\xb0 and 120\xb0. Angle?|100\xb0|20\xb0;200\xb0;40\xb0|Take half their sum.\nExterior secants intercept far arc 160\xb0 and near arc 60\xb0. Angle?|50\xb0|110\xb0;100\xb0;80\xb0|Take half their difference.\nChord pieces 3 and 10 match pieces 5 and x. Find x.|6|15;2;8|Thirty equals five x.\nA tangent t and secant external 4, whole 9 satisfy t = what?|6|36;13;5|The square of t is thirty-six.\nExternal part is 3 and internal part 7. Whole secant length?|10|7;4;21|The whole includes both parts.\nSecants have external/whole pairs 2,18 and 3,x. Find x.|12|6;9;27|Two times eighteen equals three x.\nA tangent radius is 5 and exterior-point distance to centre is 13. Tangent length?|12|8;18;\u221a194|The right triangle gives t\xb2 = 169 - 25 = 144.\nEqual chords in the same circle subtend what kind of minor arcs?|Equal arcs|Always semicircles;Unequal arcs;Always 90\xb0 arcs|Equal chords correspond to equal central angles and arcs.\n","Use 2 \xd7 12 = 3x, so x = 8. The exterior angle is half of 140 - 60, or forty degrees.","An inscribed angle is half its intercepted arc. An angle formed by intersecting chords inside a circle is half the sum of intercepted opposite arcs. An exterior angle formed by secants or tangents is half the difference of the far and near arcs. A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at contact. Tangent segments from one exterior point are equal. Intersecting chord pieces satisfy a \xd7 b = c \xd7 d; an external tangent t and secant satisfy t\xb2 = external part \xd7 whole secant.",A.F(["Inside chords","Angle = (arc1 + arc2)/2","Outside secants","Angle = (far arc - near arc)/2","Chord products","3 \xd7 8 = 4 \xd7 6","Tangent-secant","t\xb2 = 4 \xd7 (4 + 5) = 36"],p,p),"Draw the point location and label whole versus partial segments before selecting a theorem.","Chords Tangents Secants and Circle Theorems","Intersecting chord pieces are 2 and 12 on one chord, 3 and x on the other. Find x. Far and near arcs for an exterior angle are 140\xb0 and 60\xb0. Find the angle.","Two secants from an exterior point have external and whole lengths 3 and 12 on the first, 4 and x on the second. Use 3 \xd7 12 = 4x, so x = 9. The inside portion on the second is 9 - 4 = 5. Check whole lengths exceed their external portions."),A.cQ(A.a(["Find distances and midpoints on the Cartesian plane.","Write a circle equation from centre and radius.","Complete squares to recover a centre and radius."],q),"The right side is radius squared, not radius. Inside a square the centre signs are reversed. Average coordinates separately.","Distance from (0,0) to (3,4)?|5|7;25;1|Square the differences and take the square root.\nMidpoint of (2,4) and (8,10)?|(5,7)|(6,6);(10,14);(3,3)|Average the x coordinates and y coordinates.\nCentre of (x - 2)\xb2 + (y + 5)\xb2 = 16?|(2,-5)|(-2,5);(2,5);(-2,-5)|Read h and k from the shifted squares.\nRadius of x\xb2 + y\xb2 = 49?|7|49;14;\u221a7|The right side is r squared.\nCircle centre (0,0), radius 3 has equation what?|x\xb2 + y\xb2 = 9|x\xb2 + y\xb2 = 3;x + y = 9;x\xb2 - y\xb2 = 9|Use the distance squared from the origin.\nWhich point lies on x\xb2 + y\xb2 = 25?|(3,4)|(2,4);(5,5);(1,1)|Nine plus sixteen equals twenty-five.\nMidpoint of (-4,2) and (2,-6)?|(-1,-2)|(-2,-4);(1,2);(-3,4)|Average negative four and two, then two and negative six.\nDistance between (1,1) and (1,8)?|7|9;6;49|Only the vertical coordinate changes.\nCircle centre (-3,2), radius 4 has equation what?|(x + 3)\xb2 + (y - 2)\xb2 = 16|(x - 3)\xb2 + (y + 2)\xb2 = 16;(x + 3)\xb2 + (y - 2)\xb2 = 4;(x - 3)\xb2 + (y - 2)\xb2 = 16|Use opposite signs inside squares and square the radius.\nCentre of x\xb2 + y\xb2 + 4x - 6y - 3 = 0?|(-2,3)|(2,-3);(-4,6);(4,-6)|Complete squares to get (x + 2)\xb2 + (y - 3)\xb2 = 16.\nRadius of x\xb2 + y\xb2 - 8x + 2y + 8 = 0?|3|9;4;\u221a17|Complete squares: (x - 4)\xb2 + (y + 1)\xb2 = 9.\n","The equation is (x + 1)\xb2 + (y - 4)\xb2 = 9. The midpoint is (2,3).","Distance between (x\u2081,y\u2081) and (x\u2082,y\u2082) follows from a right triangle: d = \u221a[(x\u2082 - x\u2081)\xb2 + (y\u2082 - y\u2081)\xb2]. A midpoint averages each coordinate. A circle contains points at one fixed distance r from its centre (h,k), giving (x - h)\xb2 + (y - k)\xb2 = r\xb2. In expanded form, complete both squares and balance any added constants to recover the centre and radius.",A.F(["Points","A(1,2), B(7,10)","Distance","\u221a(6\xb2 + 8\xb2) = 10","Midpoint","((1+7)/2, (2+10)/2) = (4,6)","Circle","Centre (3,-2), radius 5: (x - 3)\xb2 + (y + 2)\xb2 = 25"],p,p),"Distance builds the circle equation. Complete both squares and preserve equality.","Coordinate Distance and Circle Equations","Write the circle with centre (-1,4) and radius 3. Find the midpoint of (-2,1) and (6,5).","Find the centre and radius of x\xb2 + y\xb2 - 6x + 4y - 12 = 0. Move the constant: x\xb2 - 6x + y\xb2 + 4y = 12. Add nine and four on both sides: (x - 3)\xb2 + (y + 2)\xb2 = 25. Thus centre is (3,-2) and radius five."),B.Cx,A.cQ(A.a(["Sort observations and locate quartiles.","Interpret deciles and percentiles.","State the positional convention being used."],q),"Sort first. Do not read the percentile number itself as the data value. With tiny samples, some extreme locators fall outside the data; this lesson avoids those cases.","What must be done before locating quartiles?|Sort the data|Multiply all values;Remove the largest;Divide each score by 100|Positions refer to ordered observations.\nWhich quartile is the median?|Q2|Q1;Q3;Q4|The second quartile divides data in half.\nP50 is equivalent to what?|Median|Maximum;Minimum;Range|The fiftieth percentile is the central position.\nHow many parts do deciles describe?|10|4;100;2|Deciles divide the distribution into ten shares.\nD7 is which percentile?|P70|P7;P75;P30|Seven tenths equals seventy percent.\nFor n = 9, the P30 locator using k(n+1)/100 is what?|3|2.7;30;9|Multiply thirty hundredths by ten.\nModel data Q1 under the stated convention?|25|20;30;10|Interpolate halfway between the second and third observations.\nModel data Q3 under the stated convention?|75|70;80;90|The locator is 7.5, halfway between seventy and eighty.\nIn 5,10,15,20,25,30,35,39,45, what is P60 under the stated rule?|30|25;35;60|The sixth ordered value is thirty.\nBeing at the 90th percentile means what?|High relative standing in that group|Exactly 90% correct always;A raw score of 90 always;Higher than everyone|Percentiles describe rank relative to a distribution.\nWhy state the percentile convention?|Different valid locator rules can give different results|Only one rule exists in all software;It changes the data;It makes sorting unnecessary|Small-sample percentile methods can interpolate differently.\n","D4 = P40 has locator four, giving forty. P80 has locator eight, giving eighty. An 80th-percentile score is a relative standing: about eighty percent of observations are at or below it, with ties and convention affecting exact counts.","Quartiles divide ordered data into four parts, deciles into ten, and percentiles into one hundred. The median is Q2 and P50. This lesson uses locator L = k(n + 1)/100 for Pk, with linear interpolation between neighbouring observations when L is fractional; Q1 uses k = 25 and Q3 uses k = 75. Other software conventions can differ, especially for small samples, so state the rule. A percentile is a position in a distribution, not a percent score on a test.",A.F(["Ordered data","10,20,30,40,50,60,70,80,90","Q1 locator","(9 + 1)/4 = 2.5 \u2192 25","Q2","5th value = 50","Q3 locator","3(9 + 1)/4 = 7.5 \u2192 75"],p,p),"State the convention, calculate the locator, interpolate if needed, and interpret relative standing rather than test percent.","Measures of Position in Ungrouped Data","Using the model data, find D4 and P80. What does an 80th-percentile score describe?","Find P60 of 2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16,18. There are nine observations. The locator is 0.60 \xd7 10 = 6. The sixth observation is twelve. Thus P60 is twelve under this convention. For Q1 the locator is 2.5, halfway between four and six, giving five."),A.cQ(A.a(["Locate a percentile class using cumulative frequencies.","Estimate a grouped percentile with class boundaries.","Plan and interpret an ethical small statistical study."],q),"Use class boundaries rather than lower stated limits when the formula requires boundaries. Do not expose identifiable learner scores or infer cause from association alone.","What is cumulative frequency?|Running total of frequencies|Only the largest count;Class width;Mean of boundaries|Each class adds to all earlier counts.\nFor counts 3,5,2, the cumulative counts are what?|3,8,10|3,5,2;3,15,30;0,3,5|Add frequencies successively.\nFor integer class 10\u201319, conventional continuous lower boundary is what?|9.5|10;19;19.5|A half-unit adjustment separates adjacent whole-number classes.\nFor class 10\u201319 with boundaries 9.5 and 19.5, width is what?|10|9;19;9.5|Subtract lower boundary from upper boundary.\nIn the model, which is the median class?|10\u201319|0\u20139;20\u201329;None|The fifth observation lies in cumulative positions three through eight.\nIn the model, cf before the median class equals what?|2|6;8;10|Only the first class precedes it.\nWhy is a grouped percentile usually an estimate?|Exact within-class values are unknown|Frequencies cannot be counted;All data are wrong;The total is missing always|Interpolation makes an assumption about unseen within-class positions.\nWhat should come first in a statistical study?|A clear question and target population|A desired conclusion;A decorative graph;Deleting unusual values|The question guides what data should be collected.\nTarget is P75 in N = 40 grouped observations. Target count?|30|75;10;40|Compute 0.75 times forty.\nL = 19.5, target = 15, cf = 10, f = 10, w = 10. Percentile estimate?|24.5|29.5;20;15|Add half a class width to the lower boundary.\nTwo measured variables rise together. What can a small observational study conclude safely?|They are associated in the sample|One certainly causes the other;Both are identical;No limitations matter|Association alone does not establish cause.\n","Target count is five, in 10\u201319. Use 9.5 + [(5 - 4)/4] \xd7 10 = 12.0. One class may not represent the school, so generalization is limited.","Grouped data lose exact individual values. For a grouped percentile estimate use Pk = L + [(kN/100 - cf)/f]w, where L is the lower class boundary, cf the cumulative frequency before the class, f the class frequency, and w its width. This assumes values spread evenly inside the class and is an estimate. A statistical investigation asks a clear question, identifies a population and sample, obtains consent, records data, selects suitable summaries, and reports limits without claiming causation from a simple association.",A.F(["Classes and counts","0\u20139: 2; 10\u201319: 6; 20\u201329: 2; N = 10","Cumulative counts","2, 8, 10","Median class","10\u201319 because N/2 = 5 lies after 2 and at or before 8","Estimate inputs","L = 9.5, cf = 2, f = 6, w = 10"],p,p),"Locate with cumulative counts, interpolate transparently, and report sampling and grouping limitations.","Grouped Positions and Statistical Investigation","For classes 0\u20139: 4, 10\u201319: 4, 20\u201329: 2, estimate P50 with the same boundary convention. What limitation follows from surveying only one class about the whole school?","Estimate the grouped median from the model. The target count is five. The median class is 10\u201319 with lower boundary 9.5. Compute 9.5 + [(5 - 2)/6] \xd7 10 = 14.5. Report this as an estimate; grouped data do not reveal the exact median of original observations.")],t.LQ)})
s($,"ceH","bRU",()=>{var q=t.s,p=t.N
return A.a([B.Ct,B.Cs,B.Cg,B.C1,A.cQ(A.a(["Share objects into equal groups.","Check division with multiplication.","Name one equal part of a whole."],q),"Equal sharing requires the same number in each group. A fraction needs equal-sized parts, not just any pieces.","What is 12 \xf7 2?|6|5;10;24|Two groups of six rebuild twelve.\nWhat is 15 \xf7 5?|3|2;5;10|Five threes make fifteen.\nTwenty-four stones in groups of four make how many groups?|6|4;8;20|Six groups of four use all twenty-four.\nWhich checks 16 \xf7 4 = 4?|4 \xd7 4 = 16|16 + 4 = 4;4 - 4 = 16;16 \xd7 4 = 4|Multiply the group count by its size.\nOne of three equal parts is what fraction?|1/3|3/1;1/2;3/3|Three equal parts make the whole and one is chosen.\nIn 1/6, what does 6 tell us?|Six equal parts make the whole|Six parts are chosen;The whole has one part;Each part is six wholes|The denominator counts equal parts in one whole.\nOf the same cake, which piece is larger?|1/2|1/4;1/6;1/8|Dividing into fewer equal parts gives a larger piece.\nIs one of four unequal pieces necessarily 1/4?|No, parts must be equal|Yes, any four pieces work;Yes, if it is smallest;Yes, if it is largest|Quarters require four equal shares.\nThirty seeds shared equally among five pots give how many per pot?|6|5;25;35|Five times six equals thirty.\nWhat is 40 \xf7 10?|4|5;10;30|Four groups of ten make forty.\nOne strip is cut into ten equal pieces. One piece is what fraction?|1/10|10/1;1/5;10/10|One out of ten equal parts is one tenth.\n","Each cup gets six shells since 3 \xd7 6 = 18. One of eight equal parts is 1/8.","Division shares a total fairly. Twelve seeds shared among three pots give four seeds to each pot. Write 12 \xf7 3 = 4. Check by making three groups of four. A unit fraction names one equal part: 1/3 is one of three equal parts. The bottom number tells how many equal parts make the whole. More equal parts make each part smaller when the whole stays the same.",A.F(["12 seeds","[4] [4] [4]","Division","12 \xf7 3 = 4 in each group","Check","3 \xd7 4 = 12","One third","[chosen][ ][ ]: one of three equal parts"],p,p),"Total \xf7 groups gives the number in each group. One equal part is a unit fraction.","Sharing Equally and Unit Fractions","Share 18 shells among 3 cups. Then name one equal part of a whole cut into eight parts.","Share 20 beads among five children. Give each child one bead at a time. After four rounds all twenty beads are used. Each child has four. Check 5 \xd7 4 = 20. If a strip is cut into five equal parts, one part is 1/5 of the strip."),A.cQ(A.a(["Tell straight lines from curved lines.","Sort flat shapes by sides and corners.","Extend number patterns using a rule."],q),"A tilted square is still a square. Do not change a pattern rule halfway through.","Which line bends?|Curved line|Straight line;Square side;Ruler edge|A curve changes direction along its length.\nHow many corners has a triangle?|3|2;4;0|Its three sides meet at three corners.\nWhich shape has no corners?|Circle|Triangle;Square;Rectangle|The circle has a continuous curved edge.\nHow many square corners has a rectangle?|4|3;2;1|Each of its four corners is a right angle.\nWhat follows 6, 9, 12?|15|13;14;18|Add three each time.\nWhat follows 20, 18, 16?|14|12;15;18|Subtract two each time.\nWhat follows 10, 20, 30?|40|31;35;50|The rule adds ten.\nTwo equal squares joined side by side make which outline?|Rectangle|Circle;Triangle;Pentagon|The outside has four square corners and opposite sides equal.\nA square is turned on a corner. Its name is now what?|Square|Triangle;Circle;Not a shape|Turning preserves its sides and corners.\nFill the gap: 7, 14, __, 28.|21|20;22;24|Add seven at each step.\nWhich rule fits 25, 20, 15, 10?|Subtract 5|Add 5;Subtract 10;Add 10|Each neighbour is five smaller.\n","The numbers decrease by two, so twelve comes next. A rectangle has four corners.","A straight line does not bend. A curved line bends. A square has four equal sides and four square corners. A rectangle has four square corners. A triangle has three sides. A circle has a curved edge and no corners. Shapes can be joined to make a bigger shape. A growing pattern follows a change, such as adding three each time: 3, 6, 9, 12.",A.F(["Triangle","3 straight sides; 3 corners","Circle","Curved edge; no corners","Growing rule","4 \u2192 8 \u2192 12 \u2192 16: add 4","Joined parts","Two matching small squares make one rectangle"],p,p),"Look at sides, corners, and curves. Find a rule that fits every shown pattern step.","Lines Shapes and Growing Patterns","What follows 18, 16, 14? How many corners does a rectangle have?","Extend 5, 10, 15. First compare neighbours: each grows by five. Add five to fifteen to get twenty. Check the same rule works for every step. Draw a triangle and trace its three straight sides."),B.Ce,A.cQ(A.a(["Choose centimetres, metres, grams, kilograms, and litres.","Measure from zero on a ruler.","Count square tiles covering a surface."],q),"Area counts covering tiles, not just the tiles along the edge. Kilograms measure mass, not length.","Which unit fits the length of a pencil?|Centimetres|Kilograms;Litres;Hours|A pencil has a short length.\nWhich unit fits the mass of a sack of rice?|Kilograms|Metres;Litres;Minutes|A sack is weighed using mass units.\nWhich unit fits water held by a jug?|Litres|Grams;Centimetres;Days|Capacity tells how much liquid fits.\nHow many centimetres equal one metre?|100|10;1000;60|A metre is one hundred centimetres.\nA line starts at 0 and ends at 7 cm. Length?|7 cm|6 cm;8 cm;0 cm|Subtract zero from seven.\nTwo rows of four square tiles cover what area?|8 square units|6 square units;4 square units;2 square units|Count four tiles in each of two rows.\nWhich tiling measures area fairly?|Equal tiles with no gaps or overlaps|Overlapping tiles;Different-sized tiles;Only corner tiles|Each equal tile covers one square unit.\nWhich is heavier: 2 kg or 1 kg?|2 kg|1 kg;Equal;Cannot compare|Two kilograms exceed one kilogram.\nA strip is 9 cm long. Cut off 3 cm. Length left?|6 cm|12 cm;3 cm;9 cm|Nine minus three equals six.\nThree rows of five tiles cover what area?|15 square units|8 square units;10 square units;20 square units|Five plus five plus five equals fifteen.\nTwo one-litre bottles hold how much altogether?|2 litres|1 litre;3 litres;20 litres|Add one litre and one litre.\n","Two rows of five cover ten square tiles. Centimetres fit a short pencil; kilometres are for much longer distances.","A centimetre is a short length. A metre is 100 centimetres. Start at the zero mark of a ruler, not its edge. We use grams for light objects and kilograms for heavier ones. A litre measures how much a container holds. Area tells how much flat space is covered. Cover it with equal square tiles without gaps or overlaps, then count the tiles.",A.F(["Length","0 to 8 cm on a ruler means 8 cm","Mass","1 kg = 1000 g","Capacity","1 litre holds 1000 millilitres","Area","2 rows \xd7 3 square tiles = 6 square units"],p,p),"Choose a unit that matches the object. Area is the count of equal square units covering a surface.","Standard Measures and Tile Area","A card covers 2 rows of 5 square tiles. What is its area? Which is better for the length of a pencil: centimetres or kilometres?","A mat is covered by three rows of four equal square tiles. Count four, eight, twelve. Its area is twelve square tiles. Check no tiles overlap and no spaces are left. A metre-long strip equals one hundred centimetres."),A.cQ(A.a(["Collect and count responses in a table.","Read a picture key.","Describe certain, possible, and impossible events."],q),"Always read the key. Possible does not mean guaranteed, and impossible does not mean merely unlikely.","Each star means 2 votes. Three stars mean how many?|6|3;5;9|Three groups of two total six.\nEach picture means 5 books. Two pictures mean how many?|10|2;5;7|Two fives make ten.\nA table lists 4 cats and 7 dogs. Total pets?|11|3;10;12|Four plus seven equals eleven.\nWhich is more: 8 votes or 6 votes?|8 votes|6 votes;Equal;Neither|Eight is two more than six.\nA bag contains only blue beads. Drawing blue is what?|Certain|Impossible;Possible but not certain;Unrelated|Every bead available is blue.\nA bag contains only red beads. Drawing yellow is what?|Impossible|Certain;Guaranteed;Already happened|No yellow bead can be drawn.\nA bag has red and blue beads. Drawing red is what?|Possible but not certain|Impossible;Certain;Never possible|There are red beads but a blue bead could be drawn.\nHow should a class survey count each learner?|Once|Twice;Only if tall;Not at all|One response per learner keeps the count accurate.\nFour symbols worth 3 each represent how many?|12|7;4;9|Four groups of three total twelve.\nA chart shows 10 walkers and 6 riders. Difference?|4|16;6;10|Subtract six from ten.\nA box has 3 circles and 2 squares, no triangles. Which event is impossible?|Drawing a triangle|Drawing a circle;Drawing a square;Drawing a shape|The box contains no triangle.\n","Green is impossible because no green counter is in the bag. Five pairs make ten votes.","Record each answer once in a table. A picture key tells how much each symbol stands for. If one star means two votes, three stars mean six votes. A chart makes groups easy to compare. An event is certain if it must happen, impossible if it cannot happen, and possible if it may or may not happen. These words describe a situation, not a wish.",A.F(["Key","Each star = 2 votes","Bus row","* * * = 6 votes","Walk row","* * = 4 votes","Bag","Only red counters: red is certain; blue is impossible"],p,p),"Use the key to count data. Use the contents of the situation to judge chance.","Tables Picture Charts and Chance","A bag has red and blue counters only. Can a green counter be drawn? A row has five symbols worth two each. How many votes?","A chart shows four stars for bananas and two stars for mangoes. Each star means two votes. Multiply 4 \xd7 2 = 8 and 2 \xd7 2 = 4. Bananas have four more votes. Check the total is twelve votes, not six.")],t.LQ)})
s($,"ceI","bRV",()=>{var q=t.s,p=t.N
return A.a([A.cQ(A.a(["Read thousands, hundreds, tens, and ones.","Round and compare four-digit numbers.","Add and subtract whole numbers and peso amounts."],q),"A zero still holds a place. Rounding gives an estimate, not the exact value. Ordinal words such as twentieth tell position.","What is the value of 7 in 7,305?|7000|700;70;7|Seven is in the thousands place.\nWhich equals 5,082?|5000 + 80 + 2|500 + 80 + 2;5000 + 800 + 2;5000 + 8 + 2|The hundreds digit is zero.\nWhich number is greater?|6341|6314;6143;6134|Compare thousands, then hundreds, then tens.\nRound 2,763 to the nearest hundred.|2800|2700;2760;3000|The tens digit six rounds the hundreds up.\nWhat is 1,245 + 2,314?|3559|3459;3569;1069|Add each matching place without exchange.\nWhat is 4,000 - 1,250?|2750|3250;2850;5250|Subtracting 1000 then 250 leaves 2750.\nWhat is \u20b1245 + \u20b1125?|\u20b1370|\u20b1360;\u20b1120;\u20b1380|Five plus five ones exchange for a ten.\nPay \u20b11000 for \u20b1675. Change?|\u20b1325|\u20b1425;\u20b11675;\u20b1375|The price plus 325 rebuilds 1000.\nWhat is 2,786 + 1,459?|4245|4145;4235;4345|Regroup ones, tens, and hundreds as needed.\nWhat is 6,003 - 2,578?|3425|3525;3435;4525|Exchange across the zeros before subtracting.\nWhich ordinal names position 100?|Hundredth|Hundred;Tenth;Thousandth|The ending marks a place in an ordered list.\n","The tens digit is four, so 6,348 rounds to 6,300. Change is \u20b1500 - \u20b1350 = \u20b1150.","In 4,276 the four means four thousand. Compare numbers from the largest place first. To round to the nearest hundred, look at the tens: five or more rounds up. Adding joins equal places. Subtracting may exchange one hundred for ten tens. For money in whole pesos, use the same place-value rules and write the peso sign.",A.F(["4,276","4,000 + 200 + 70 + 6","Nearest hundred","4,276 \u2192 4,300","Sum","2,346 + 1,527 = 3,873","Change","\u20b1500 - \u20b1275 = \u20b1225"],p,p),"Read each place, align equal places, and use addition to check subtraction.","Numbers Money and Operations to 10000","Round 6,348 to the nearest hundred. A \u20b1350 bag is paid for with \u20b1500. Find the change.","Find 3,402 - 1,785. Exchange through the zero: the top becomes 2 thousands, 13 hundreds, 9 tens, 12 ones. Subtract each place to get 1,617. Check 1,617 + 1,785 = 3,402."),B.Ck,B.Cj,B.Ci,A.cQ(A.a(["Recognize parallel, intersecting, and perpendicular lines.","Find a line of symmetry.","Extend a pattern and complete an equation."],q),"Lines that cross are not always perpendicular. A fold is a symmetry line only if both halves match exactly.","Which lines never meet on a flat plane?|Parallel lines|Perpendicular lines;All intersecting lines;Crossed lines|Parallel lines remain a constant distance apart.\nLines meeting at a square corner are what?|Perpendicular|Parallel;Curved;Unconnected|The angle is a right angle.\nHow many straight symmetry lines has a non-square rectangle?|2|1;3;4|Horizontal and vertical midpoint folds match, but diagonals do not.\nHow many symmetry lines has a square?|4|1;2;3|Two midpoint folds and two diagonal folds match.\nWhat follows 8, 16, 24?|32|30;28;40|Add eight each time.\nWhat follows 50, 45, 40?|35|30;39;45|Subtract five each time.\nWhat completes 6 \xd7 __ = 42?|7|6;8;9|Six sevens equal forty-two.\nWhat completes 36 \xf7 __ = 4?|9|8;6;12|Four groups of nine make thirty-six.\nWhich completes 9 + 7 = __ + 6?|10|9;11;16|Both sides must equal sixteen.\nTwo lines cross at 60 degrees. Are they perpendicular?|No|Yes;Only if long;Only if tilted|Perpendicular lines meet at ninety degrees.\nFill 4, 9, 14, __, 24.|19|18;20;21|The change is plus five at every step.\n","Yes, the two halves match. The pattern subtracts five each time, so twenty comes next.","Parallel straight lines stay the same distance apart and do not meet. Intersecting lines meet. Perpendicular lines meet at a square corner. A line of symmetry splits a shape into two matching mirror halves. Patterns may grow or shrink by a steady amount. An equals sign tells us both sides have the same value.",A.F(["Parallel","Rail tracks on a straight section do not meet.","Perpendicular","An upright line meets a horizontal line at 90 degrees.","Square symmetry","Fold through the middle: matching halves overlap.","Equation","3 \xd7 4 = 6 + 6 = 12"],p,p),"Use square corners to test perpendicular lines, matching halves for symmetry, and one consistent rule for a pattern.","Lines Symmetry and Number Patterns","A rectangle has a fold joining midpoints of opposite long sides. Is it a symmetry line? What follows 35, 30, 25?","Complete 4 \xd7 __ = 28. First ask which group size gives twenty-eight in four groups. Seven works because 7 + 7 + 7 + 7 = 28. Check 4 \xd7 7 = 28. For 6, 12, 18, add six to get twenty-four."),B.Cv,A.cQ(A.a(["Convert common length, mass, and capacity units.","Convert hours, minutes, and seconds.","Find elapsed time and solve measurement stories."],q),"Clock units use sixty, not one hundred. Smaller unit names produce larger counts for the same amount.","How many centimetres are in 5 m?|500|50;5000;5|Multiply five by one hundred.\nHow many metres are in 2 km?|2000|200;20;2|Each kilometre contains one thousand metres.\nHow many grams are in 3 kg?|3000|300;30;3|Three groups of one thousand make three thousand.\nHow many millilitres equal 1 L?|1000|100;10;60|A litre has one thousand millilitres.\nHow many minutes equal 2 hours?|120|100;200;60|Two times sixty is one hundred twenty.\nHow many seconds equal 3 minutes?|180|30;300;120|Three times sixty is one hundred eighty.\nElapsed time from 4:10 to 4:50?|40 minutes|30 minutes;50 minutes;60 minutes|Subtract ten from fifty within the same hour.\nElapsed time from 9:30 to 10:15?|45 minutes|85 minutes;30 minutes;75 minutes|Thirty minutes to ten plus fifteen more.\nWhich is longer: 150 cm or 1 m?|150 cm|1 m;Equal;Cannot compare|One metre is 100 cm, which is less than 150.\nA 2 L jug pours out 500 mL. How much remains?|1500 mL|2500 mL;500 mL;1000 mL|Change two litres to 2000 mL, then subtract 500.\nA film runs from 2:45 to 4:00. Duration?|1 hour 15 minutes|1 hour 45 minutes;2 hours;45 minutes|Fifteen minutes reach three, then sixty reach four.\n","Four groups of 100 make 400 cm. From 1:20 to 2:00 is forty minutes, then five more: forty-five minutes.","One metre is 100 centimetres. One kilometre is 1000 metres. One kilogram is 1000 grams, and one litre is 1000 millilitres. To change to smaller units, multiply by how many small units fit in one large unit. One hour is 60 minutes and one minute is 60 seconds. Count forward through the clock to find how long something lasts.",A.F(["Length","3 m = 300 cm","Mass","2 kg = 2000 g","Capacity","2 L = 2000 mL","Clock jump","2:40 \u2192 3:00 = 20 min; \u2192 3:15 = 15 min"],p,p),"Use the correct conversion fact. Count forward in easy jumps to measure elapsed time.","Changing Units and Elapsed Time","How many centimetres are in 4 m? A game runs from 1:20 to 2:05. How long is it?","A trip begins at 8:45 and ends at 10:10. From 8:45 to 9:00 is fifteen minutes. From 9:00 to 10:00 is sixty minutes. Add ten more minutes to reach 10:10. Total: 85 minutes, or one hour twenty-five minutes."),B.C2,A.cQ(A.a(["List possible outcomes of a simple experiment.","Use likely, unlikely, certain, and impossible.","Compare recorded outcomes without promising the next result."],q),"More likely is not certain. Compare equal-sized spinner parts, not colours alone.","A bag contains only red counters. Drawing red is what?|Certain|Impossible;Unlikely;Less likely than blue|Every available counter is red.\nA bag contains no green counters. Drawing green is what?|Impossible|Certain;Likely;Guaranteed|There is no green counter to select.\nA fair coin can land on which faces?|Heads or tails|Only heads;Only tails;Red or blue|These are the two coin faces.\nA bag has 8 red and 2 blue counters. Which is more likely?|Red|Blue;Equal;Green|More counters are red.\nA bag has 1 red and 9 blue counters. Drawing red is what?|Unlikely but possible|Impossible;Certain;More likely than blue|A red counter exists but is rare in the bag.\nA spinner has 2 red and 2 blue equal parts. The colours are what?|Equally likely|Red certain;Blue impossible;Red more likely|Both colours occupy the same number of equal parts.\nWhat should be recorded after each draw?|The actual colour drawn|The wished-for colour;Only red results;A guessed future result|Data must describe what happened.\nThree heads in a row force the next fair toss to be tails. True?|No|Yes;Only in the morning;Only with a large coin|Previous tosses do not force the next face.\nTen trials give 6 red and 4 blue results. Which occurred more?|Red|Blue;Equal;Neither|Six recorded red results exceed four blue results.\nA six-face die has faces 1 to 6. Which result is impossible?|7|1;3;6|Seven is not printed on any face.\nA spinner has 5 equal yellow parts and 1 green. Which statement is right?|Green may happen|Yellow must happen;Green cannot happen;Both colours are certain|One green part makes green possible, although less likely.\n","White is certain and black impossible. Tails is not forced next; either coin side can still land up.","An experiment is an action with an uncertain result, such as drawing a counter without looking. The possible outcomes depend on what is in the bag. With nine red counters and one blue counter, red is more likely but blue is still possible. Record each result in a tally. Past results describe what happened; they do not force the next result.",A.F(["Bag","9 red + 1 blue = 10 counters","More likely","Red: nine available choices","Less likely","Blue: one available choice","Impossible","Green: no green counters"],p,p),"List what can happen, compare available outcomes, and record actual results honestly.","Experiments and Likelihood","A bag has only five white counters. Describe drawing white and drawing black. If a coin lands heads three times, must the next toss be tails?","A spinner has four equal parts: three yellow and one green. Count the yellow parts, then green. Yellow is more likely because it has more equal parts. Green can still happen. Blue is impossible because no part is blue.")],t.LQ)})
s($,"ceJ","bRW",()=>{var q=t.s,p=t.N
return A.a([A.cQ(A.a(["Read and compare six-digit numbers.","Round to the nearest hundred thousand.","Add and subtract large numbers with regrouping."],q),"Round using the digit immediately to the right of the target place. Keep zeros as place holders.","Value of 8 in 381,406?|80000|8000;800;8|Eight is in the ten-thousands place.\nWhich equals 700,052?|700000 + 50 + 2|700000 + 500 + 2;70000 + 50 + 2;700000 + 5 + 2|Only the hundred-thousands, tens, and ones are nonzero.\nWhich is larger: 456,789 or 456,798?|456798|456789;They are equal;Both exceed a million|The first differing place is the tens.\nRound 349,999 to the nearest hundred thousand.|300000|400000;350000;340000|The ten-thousands digit four rounds down.\nRound 650,000 to the nearest hundred thousand.|700000|600000;650000;1000000|Five in the next place rounds up.\nWhat is 125,300 + 243,200?|368500|358500;378500;368050|Add matching places.\nWhat is 600,000 - 250,000?|350000|450000;850000;250000|Subtract 250 thousands from 600 thousands.\nEstimate 392,000 + 207,000 by rounding each to hundred thousands.|600000|500000;700000;599000|Round to 400,000 and 200,000.\nWhat is 498,765 + 123,456?|622221|612221;622121;632221|Regroup each place from right to left.\nWhat is 700,002 - 356,789?|343213|353213;443213;343223|Exchanges across zeros preserve the starting value.\nWhich checks 820,000 - 315,200 = 504,800?|504800 + 315200 = 820000|820000 + 315200 = 504800;504800 - 315200 = 820000;315200 + 820000 = 504800|The difference plus the removed amount must rebuild the whole.\n","The ten-thousands digit is five, so round up to 800,000. The sum is 377,920 after exchanging twelve tens for one hundred and two tens.","Each place is ten times the place to its right. In 642,305 the six means 600,000. Compare from the left until digits differ. To round to the nearest hundred thousand, inspect the ten-thousands digit. Five or more rounds up. To add or subtract, align the places and exchange groups of ten when needed. Rounding first gives a useful check on the size of an answer.",A.F(["642,305","600,000 + 40,000 + 2,000 + 300 + 5","Round","642,305 \u2192 600,000","Estimate","386,000 + 218,000 \u2248 400,000 + 200,000","Exact","386,000 + 218,000 = 604,000"],p,p),"Use place value for comparing, rounding, and regrouping. Check exact calculations against estimates.","Whole Numbers to One Million","Round 750,100 to the nearest hundred thousand. Add 245,670 + 132,250.","Find 502,010 - 187,625. Exchange across the zero places to subtract ones, tens, hundreds, and thousands. The result is 314,385. Check by addition: 314,385 + 187,625 = 502,010. Rounded values give about 500,000 - 200,000 = 300,000, so the exact answer is reasonable."),A.cQ(A.a(["Classify triangles and quadrilaterals.","Measure an angle with a protractor.","Find perimeters including joined shapes."],q),"Do not count an internal shared edge as boundary. A protractor has two scales: begin at the aligned zero.","A triangle has three equal sides. Name it.|Equilateral|Scalene;Right only;Quadrilateral|Equilateral means all three sides equal.\nA 90\xb0 angle is what kind?|Right|Acute;Obtuse;Straight|A right angle measures ninety degrees.\nA 35\xb0 angle is what kind?|Acute|Right;Obtuse;Straight|It is smaller than ninety degrees.\nA 125\xb0 angle is what kind?|Obtuse|Acute;Right;Full|It lies between ninety and one hundred eighty degrees.\nHow many sides has a quadrilateral?|4|3;5;6|The name describes a four-sided polygon.\nWhich statement about a square is true?|It is also a rectangle|It has three sides;It has no right angles;Its opposite sides are not parallel|A square meets every rectangle condition.\nPerimeter of sides 3, 5, 4, 6 cm?|18 cm|12 cm;24 cm;30 cm|Add the four boundary lengths.\nWhere should a protractor centre go?|At the angle vertex|At the ray end;Anywhere on the page;Outside both rays|The centre must sit where the two rays meet.\nA 7 cm by 3 cm rectangle has perimeter what?|20 cm|21 cm;10 cm;14 cm|Add 7 + 3 + 7 + 3.\nTwo squares of side 4 cm share one full side. Outside perimeter?|24 cm|32 cm;16 cm;28 cm|The total 32 loses two copies of the shared 4 cm edge.\nAn isosceles triangle has sides 5, 5, 6 cm. Perimeter?|16 cm|11 cm;25 cm;30 cm|Add the three lengths, including both equal sides.\n","Add all outside sides: 4 + 6 + 5 + 7 = 22 cm. An angle of 110\xb0 is obtuse.","An equilateral triangle has three equal sides; an isosceles triangle has at least two. A right triangle has a 90-degree angle. A quadrilateral has four sides. A parallelogram has two pairs of parallel opposite sides. A rectangle has four right angles and a square also has four equal sides. Put a protractor centre on the corner, align zero with one ray, and read the scale beginning at that zero. Perimeter is the length around the outside.",A.F(["Acute angle","Less than 90\xb0","Obtuse angle","Greater than 90\xb0 but less than 180\xb0","Joined rectangles","Perimeter = P1 + P2 - 2 \xd7 shared edge","Square family","A square is also a rectangle and a parallelogram"],p,p),"Classify using sides and angles. Perimeter adds only the outside lengths.","Triangles Quadrilaterals and Boundary Length","Find the perimeter of a quadrilateral with sides 4, 6, 5, and 7 cm. Is a 110\xb0 angle acute or obtuse?","A 6 cm by 4 cm rectangle joins a 3 cm by 4 cm rectangle along their 4 cm sides. Their perimeters are 20 and 14 cm. The shared edge is inside, counted twice. Subtract 8: 20 + 14 - 8 = 26 cm. Check the outside is a 9 cm by 4 cm rectangle."),B.Cp,A.cQ(A.a(["Convert length, mass, and capacity units.","Convert clock units using sixty.","Solve elapsed-time and measurement problems."],q),"Multiplying when converting to larger units makes the count too big. Months are not all thirty days.","How many metres are in 6 km?|6000|600;60;6|Multiply six by one thousand.\nHow many centimetres are in 9 m?|900|90;9000;9|Each metre contains one hundred centimetres.\nHow many grams are in 2 kg 300 g?|2300|2030;230;2003|Two thousand plus three hundred equals 2300.\nHow many milligrams are in 5 g?|5000|500;50;5|A gram contains one thousand milligrams.\nHow many litres equal 3000 mL?|3|30;300;3000|Divide by one thousand.\nHow many minutes equal 3 hours?|180|300;120;90|Three times sixty is 180.\nHow many hours equal 2 days?|48|24;12;20|Two times twenty-four equals forty-eight.\nHow many days equal 3 weeks?|21|30;14;24|Each week contains seven days.\nElapsed time from 1:45 to 3:20?|95 minutes|75 minutes;135 minutes;85 minutes|Fifteen plus sixty plus twenty equals ninety-five.\nA 2 m ribbon loses 35 cm. Length left?|165 cm|235 cm;135 cm;65 cm|Change two metres to 200 cm before subtracting.\nWhich gives exact days in a particular month?|Check that month's calendar|Always use 28;Always use 30;Always use four weeks|Month lengths vary.\n","Four kilograms are 4000 g, so the total is 4250 g. Ten minutes reach 9:00 and twenty-five more reach 9:25: thirty-five minutes.","Use a conversion fact to keep the amount unchanged. One kilometre is 1000 metres; one metre is 100 centimetres. One kilogram is 1000 grams, one gram is 1000 milligrams, and one litre is 1000 millilitres. Smaller units require a larger count. Clock time uses sixty seconds per minute and sixty minutes per hour. Days have 24 hours and weeks have seven days. Months have different lengths; use an actual calendar rather than assuming four weeks per month.",A.F(["Length","250 cm \xf7 100 = 2.5 m","Mass","3 g \xd7 1000 = 3000 mg","Capacity","1500 mL = 1 L 500 mL","Time","1 h 20 min = 60 + 20 = 80 min"],p,p),"Write the conversion fact, keep the same amount, and use clock jumps for time intervals.","Converting Measures and Reading Time Intervals","Convert 4 kg 250 g to grams. How long is 8:50 to 9:25?","A bus leaves at 10:35 and arrives at 12:10. Count twenty-five minutes to 11:00, sixty to 12:00, and ten to 12:10. Total 95 minutes. Divide into sixty plus thirty-five: one hour thirty-five minutes."),A.cQ(A.a(["Change improper fractions and mixed numbers.","Use common-sized parts to add or subtract.","Regroup a whole when subtracting mixed numbers."],q),"Do not add denominators. Use the same whole and same-sized parts before combining.","What is 2/9 + 4/9?|6/9|6/18;2/9;8/9|Six ninth-size parts result.\nWhat is 7/8 - 3/8?|1/2|4/0;10/8;3/8|Four eighths simplify to one half.\nWhat is 1/2 + 1/4?|3/4|2/6;1/6;1/8|Rename half as two quarters.\nWhat is 5/6 - 1/3?|1/2|4/3;2/3;1/3|Five sixths minus two sixths equals three sixths.\nWrite 9/4 as a mixed number.|2 1/4|1 1/4;2 3/4;4 1/2|Eight quarters make two wholes with one quarter left.\nWrite 2 2/3 as an improper fraction.|8/3|4/3;6/3;7/3|Two wholes are six thirds, plus two thirds.\nWhat is 1 - 2/5?|3/5|2/5;1/5;3/4|Five fifths minus two fifths leaves three.\nWhat is 1 1/4 + 2 2/4?|3 3/4|3 3/8;2 3/4;4 1/4|Add wholes and equal fourth parts.\nWhat is 2 1/5 - 4/5?|1 2/5|2 3/5;1 3/5;1 1/5|Exchange one whole to get 1 6/5 before subtracting.\nWhat is 2/3 + 3/4?|1 5/12|5/7;5/12;1 1/12|Eight twelfths plus nine twelfths gives seventeen twelfths.\nA jug holds 1 1/2 L. Use 3/4 L. Amount left?|3/4 L|1/4 L;2 1/4 L;1 L|One and a half is six quarters; subtract three quarters.\n","Two fifths are four tenths, so the sum is 5/10 = 1/2. One whole is eight eighths; subtract three to leave 5/8.","The denominator names a part size. Add or subtract counts of the same size without changing that size. For different denominators, first rename using equal fractions. Three quarters and one half become three quarters and two quarters. An improper fraction has at least one whole: 7/4 = 1 3/4. When subtracting 2 1/4 - 3/4, exchange one whole for four quarters.",A.F(["Same parts","2/7 + 3/7 = 5/7","Rename","1/2 = 2/4","Add","3/4 + 1/2 = 5/4 = 1 1/4","Exchange","2 1/4 = 1 5/4"],p,p),"Rename equal amounts, combine part counts, then simplify and check.","Adding and Subtracting Fraction Amounts","Find 2/5 + 1/10. Then find 1 - 3/8.","Find 3 1/3 - 1 5/6. First rename one third as two sixths: 3 2/6. Exchange one whole: 2 8/6. Subtract 1 5/6 to get 1 3/6. Simplify to 1 1/2. Check 1 1/2 + 1 5/6 = 3 1/3."),B.Cf,B.Cu,B.Cn,A.cQ(A.a(["Read a time table and a single line graph.","Find increases and decreases from data.","Describe a number rule and balance number sentences."],q),"A higher point means a larger value, not always a larger increase. An equals sign is not a command to write the next calculation.","The horizontal axis is days. What belongs on the vertical height axis?|Centimetres|Learner names;Days again;Fruit names|The measured quantity is height.\nDay 1 is 12 cm and day 2 is 15 cm. Increase?|3 cm|27 cm;2 cm;15 cm|Subtract twelve from fifteen.\nDay 2 is 15 cm and day 3 is 14 cm. Change?|Decrease of 1 cm|Increase of 1 cm;Decrease of 29 cm;No change|Fourteen is one less than fifteen.\nWhich day in the model has the greatest height?|Day 4|Day 1;Day 2;Day 3|Eighteen is the largest listed height.\nWhat follows 3, 7, 11, 15?|19|18;20;21|Add four at every step.\nWhat is missing in 8 + __ = 13?|5|4;6;21|Eight needs five to make thirteen.\nComplete 9 + 4 = 4 + __.|9|4;5;13|Swapping addends keeps the same total.\nComplete 3 \xd7 6 = 2 \xd7 __.|9|8;6;18|Both sides must equal eighteen.\nHeight rises from 17 cm to 25 cm. How much growth?|8 cm|42 cm;7 cm;9 cm|Twenty-five minus seventeen equals eight.\nThe rule is subtract 6. What follows 31, 25, 19?|13|12;14;15|Nineteen minus six is thirteen.\nWhy read a graph's scale before comparing points?|Each step may stand for several units|Each step always means one;Axes have no units;Lines determine exact future values|The marked scale determines each point's value.\n","Day 3 to day 4 rises from fourteen to eighteen, or four centimetres. The blank is seven because multiplication can swap factors.","A line graph shows how an amount changes over ordered times. Label the horizontal axis with time and the vertical axis with the measured amount and unit. Plot each table pair before joining neighbouring points. Read the scale, not just how steep the picture looks. A number pattern follows a rule. In a number sentence, both sides of the equals sign must have the same value.",A.F(["Table","Day 1: 12 cm; day 2: 15 cm; day 3: 14 cm; day 4: 18 cm","Horizontal axis","Days 1 to 4, equally spaced","Vertical axis","Plant height in centimetres","Rule","4, 9, 14, 19: add 5"],p,p),"Read axes and scales, compare values by subtraction, and keep both sides of an equation equal.","Line Graphs Rules and Number Sentences","Use the table in the model. What is the largest day-to-day rise? Complete 7 \xd7 4 = 4 \xd7 __.","The plant grows from 12 cm on day 1 to 18 cm on day 4. Subtract 18 - 12 = 6 cm overall. From day 2 to day 3 it drops by 1 cm. For 4 + __ = 6 + 3, first find the right side is nine, so the blank is five."),B.C3],t.LQ)})
s($,"ceK","bRX",()=>{var q=t.s,p=t.N
return A.a([A.cQ(A.a(["Read numbers through ten million.","Test divisibility using number properties.","Use common factors and multiples in problems."],q),"Divisible does not mean merely smaller. Factors divide a number; multiples are repeated copies of it.","Value of 6 in 6,254,013?|6000000|600000;60000;6000|Six is in the millions place.\nWhich number is divisible by 2?|438|435;437;439|Its last digit eight is even.\nWhich number is divisible by 5?|725|722;723;724|The final digit is five.\nIs 312 divisible by 3?|Yes, digit sum is 6|No, it ends in 2;No, it has three digits;Yes, all numbers are|Three plus one plus two is divisible by three.\nWhich is a factor of 36?|9|5;7;10|Nine times four equals thirty-six.\nGreatest common factor of 12 and 20?|4|2;6;12|Four is their largest shared factor.\nLeast common positive multiple of 3 and 4?|12|7;9;24|Twelve is the first positive multiple shared by both.\nWhich number is prime?|17|15;21;27|Only one and seventeen divide it exactly.\nHow many identical packs can use all 16 red and 24 blue beads at most?|8|4;12;16|The greatest common factor is eight.\nTwo bells ring every 6 and 10 minutes. After ringing together, when next together?|30 minutes|16 minutes;20 minutes;60 minutes|Thirty is the least common positive multiple.\nRound 8,650,000 to the nearest million.|9000000|8000000;8600000;8650000|The hundred-thousands digit six rounds up.\n","Its digit sum is twelve, so it is divisible by three. It ends in five, so it is divisible by five. The first shared multiple is twenty.","In 7,406,025, the seven means seven million. Compare large numbers at their first different place. A number is divisible by another if division leaves no remainder. An even last digit tests divisibility by two; a last digit zero or five tests five. A digit sum divisible by three tests three. Factor pairs multiply to a number. The greatest common factor makes the largest equal groups; the least common multiple finds the first shared repeating total.",A.F(["7,406,025","7,000,000 + 400,000 + 6,000 + 20 + 5","246 \xf7 3","2 + 4 + 6 = 12, divisible by 3","GCF of 18 and 24","Shared factors 1, 2, 3, 6; greatest 6","LCM of 6 and 8","First common positive multiple 24"],p,p),"Use place value, divisibility tests, and exact factor or multiple lists to support a decision.","Large Numbers and Divisibility","Is 435 divisible by three and five? Find the first shared positive multiple of four and ten.","Pack 18 red and 24 blue beads into the greatest number of identical packs without leftovers. List common factors of 18 and 24: 1, 2, 3, 6. Choose six packs. Each has three red and four blue beads. Check six threes are eighteen and six fours are twenty-four."),B.Cr,B.Ch,B.C7,A.cQ(A.a(["Compare quantities using ordered ratios.","Find an equivalent ratio and a missing proportion.","Connect percent with fractions and decimals."],q),"Do not reverse ratio order. The whole for percent must be identified before calculating a part.","There are 3 red and 5 blue beads. Red-to-blue ratio?|3:5|5:3;3:8;5:8|Keep the named order.\nIn that group, red-to-total ratio is what?|3:8|3:5;5:3;8:3|The total is three plus five.\nWhich ratio equals 2:7?|6:21|4:7;2:14;7:2|Both terms were multiplied by three.\nComplete 3/4 = __/20.|15|12;16;17|Multiply numerator and denominator by five.\nWhat fraction is 50% in simplest form?|1/2|1/5;5/1;1/50|Fifty out of one hundred reduces to half.\nWrite 0.3 as a percent.|30%|3%;0.3%;300%|Three tenths equals thirty hundredths.\nWhat is 10% of 80?|8|10;70;800|One tenth of eighty is eight.\nWhat is 25% of 60?|15|25;30;20|One quarter of sixty is fifteen.\nA ratio of flour to sugar is 4:1. For 12 cups flour, sugar needed?|3 cups|4 cups;8 cups;48 cups|The flour part is tripled, so triple the sugar.\nWhat is 60% of 150?|90|60;75;100|Multiply 0.6 by 150.\nIn 40 equal squares, 10 are shaded. Percent shaded?|25%|10%;40%;75%|Ten out of forty is one quarter.\n","Multiply both parts by three: 3:12, so twelve cups water. Three quarters is 75/100, or 75%.","A ratio keeps an order: two red beads for three blue is red to blue 2:3. It is not two out of three beads overall; there are five beads. Multiply both terms by the same number to scale a ratio. A proportion states two ratios are equal. Percent means out of one hundred. Thus 25% is 25/100 = 1/4 = 0.25.",A.F(["Mixture","2 red : 3 blue; total 5 parts","Double batch","4 red : 6 blue","Proportion","2/3 = 8/12","Percent grid","25 shaded squares out of 100 = 25%"],p,p),"Scale both ratio terms equally. Percent is a fraction with denominator one hundred.","Ratios Proportions and Percent Models","For a 1:4 syrup-to-water ratio, how much water goes with 3 cups syrup? Write 3/4 as a percent.","A recipe uses 3 cups of rice for 6 people. To serve 10 people, first find 3 \xf7 6 = 1/2 cup per person. Multiply by ten to get five cups. Check 3/6 and 5/10 both equal one half. For 20% of 150, take 20/100 \xd7 150 = 30."),A.cQ(A.a(["Classify polygons by sides.","Relate radius, diameter, and circumference.","Identify faces and nets of simple solids."],q),"Equal sides alone do not guarantee a regular polygon. Radius is half the diameter. A circle is not a polygon.","A polygon with five sides is what?|Pentagon|Hexagon;Octagon;Triangle|Penta names five sides.\nA polygon with six sides is what?|Hexagon|Pentagon;Octagon;Quadrilateral|Hexa names six sides.\nWhat makes a polygon regular?|Equal sides and equal angles|Only equal sides;Only one right angle;Any closed curve|Both conditions are required.\nWhich is not a polygon?|Circle|Triangle;Square;Pentagon|Its boundary is curved.\nA circle has radius 4 cm. Diameter?|8 cm|2 cm;4 cm;16 cm|Double the radius.\nA circle has diameter 18 cm. Radius?|9 cm|18 cm;36 cm;6 cm|Take half the diameter.\nUsing \u03c0 = 3.14, circumference for diameter 10 cm?|31.4 cm|314 cm;15.7 cm;10 cm|Multiply pi by diameter.\nHow many square faces has a cube?|6|4;8;12|A cube has six equal square faces.\nA cube net is made of what flat shapes?|Squares|Circles;Only triangles;Only semicircles|Each cube face is a square.\nUsing \u03c0 = 22/7, circumference for radius 14 cm?|88 cm|44 cm;28 cm;616 cm|Use 2 \xd7 22/7 \xd7 14.\nA regular pentagon has side 6 cm. Perimeter?|30 cm|11 cm;24 cm;36 cm|Five equal sides total five times six.\n","An eight-sided polygon is an octagon. Double five to get diameter ten centimetres. A cube has six square faces.","A polygon is closed and made of straight sides. A regular polygon has all sides equal and all angles equal. Five sides make a pentagon and six make a hexagon. A circle has no straight sides. A radius joins its centre to its edge. A diameter crosses the centre and equals two radii. Circumference is the distance around: C = \u03c0d. A net folds from a flat arrangement into the faces of a solid.",A.F(["Regular hexagon","6 equal sides and 6 equal angles","Circle radius","Centre to edge: r","Circle diameter","Edge through centre to edge: d = 2r","Cube net","6 square faces joined so they can fold without overlap"],p,p),"Classify polygons using sides and angles. Label the circle centre, radius, and diameter before calculating.","Polygons Circles and Solid Models","Name a polygon with eight sides. Find the diameter of a circle with radius 5 cm. How many square faces does a cube have?","A circular hoop has radius 7 cm. Double it to get diameter 14 cm. Use \u03c0 = 22/7: C = 22/7 \xd7 14 = 44 cm. Check this is a length around the hoop, not an area."),A.cQ(A.a(["Distinguish area from circumference.","Calculate circle area using radius.","Read and compare Celsius temperatures."],q),"Do not use \u03c0d for area or write a length unit for a surface. A warmer temperature does not mean more degrees between every tick.","Which formula gives circle area?|\u03c0r\xb2|2\u03c0r;\u03c0d;r + d|The surface has area pi times radius squared.\nWhat radius belongs to diameter 12 cm?|6 cm|12 cm;24 cm;3 cm|Radius is half the diameter.\nUsing \u03c0 = 3.14, area for radius 1 cm?|3.14 cm\xb2|6.28 cm\xb2;1 cm\xb2;31.4 cm\xb2|Square one and multiply by pi.\nUsing \u03c0 = 3.14, area for radius 3 cm?|28.26 cm\xb2|18.84 cm\xb2;9.42 cm\xb2;9 cm\xb2|Three squared is nine, then multiply by 3.14.\nWhich unit fits the area of a circular garden?|Square metres|Metres;Litres;Degrees Celsius|Area measures two-dimensional coverage.\nTemperature rises from 21\xb0C to 28\xb0C. Rise?|7\xb0C|49\xb0C;6\xb0C;8\xb0C|Subtract the starting value from the ending value.\nTemperature falls from 32\xb0C to 27\xb0C. Fall?|5\xb0C|59\xb0C;4\xb0C;6\xb0C|Thirty-two minus twenty-seven is five.\nTicks read 10, 15, 20. Each step means what?|5\xb0C|1\xb0C;2\xb0C;10\xb0C|Neighbouring values differ by five.\nUsing \u03c0 = 22/7, area for radius 7 m?|154 m\xb2|44 m\xb2;49 m\xb2;308 m\xb2|Multiply 49 by 22/7.\nA circle's diameter doubles. How does its area change?|Becomes four times as large|Doubles;Halves;Stays equal|The radius doubles and its square multiplies by four.\nA temperature of 25\xb0C rises by 6\xb0C. New reading?|31\xb0C|19\xb0C;30\xb0C;150\xb0C|Add the rise to the initial reading.\n","A = 3.14 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 = 12.56 cm\xb2. The temperature falls by four degrees Celsius.","Area measures flat space in square units. Cut a circle into many thin sectors and arrange them alternately: the shape approaches a rectangle with height r and base \u03c0r. So A = \u03c0r\xb2. Use radius, not diameter. A thermometer scale measures temperature in degrees Celsius. Read the value at the liquid level and the value of each marked step. A temperature rise is ending minus starting temperature.",A.F(["Circle area","A = \u03c0 \xd7 r \xd7 r","Radius 3 cm","A = 9\u03c0 cm\xb2","Thermometer steps","20, 22, 24, 26: each step is 2\xb0C","Temperature rise","29\xb0C - 24\xb0C = 5\xb0C"],p,p),"Area uses \u03c0r\xb2 and square units. Read thermometer tick values before comparing temperatures.","Circle Area and Temperature","Using \u03c0 = 3.14, find the area of a circle with radius 2 cm. A thermometer drops from 30\xb0C to 26\xb0C. Find the fall.","A round mat has diameter 10 m. First halve the diameter: r = 5 m. Square it: 25 m\xb2. Multiply by 3.14: area = 78.5 m\xb2. Check the unit is square metres. If temperature rises from 23\xb0C to 31\xb0C, the rise is 8\xb0C."),B.Cd,A.cQ(A.a(["Read two data series on one time graph.","Compare changes using a legend.","Estimate chance from recorded experiments."],q),"Do not compare points from different days by accident. Experimental frequency need not equal the theoretical chance exactly.","What identifies the two lines in a double-line graph?|Legend|Title alone;Page number;Date alone|The legend maps line styles to groups.\nIn the model, how many books does A read Tuesday?|10|8;9;6|Read A at Tuesday.\nIn the model, how many more books does A read than B Monday?|2|1;3;14|Eight minus six equals two.\nHow much does B increase from Monday to Tuesday?|3 books|2 books;1 book;15 books|Nine minus six equals three.\nAn event occurs 6 times in 20 trials. Relative frequency?|3/10|6/14;1/6;20/6|Divide six by twenty and simplify.\nHeads occurs 15 times in 30 tosses. Observed percent?|50%|15%;30%;100%|Fifteen is half of thirty.\nDoes 5 heads in a row force tails next?|No|Yes;Only with 10 trials;Only on a small coin|Past independent tosses do not force the next outcome.\nWhat should a graph's vertical axis include?|Quantity and units|Only a colour;Only a date;Only the word graph|Units explain the measured values.\nRed occurs 18 times in 60 trials. Observed probability?|3/10|1/18;18/42;3/5|Eighteen divided by sixty reduces to three tenths.\nA line rises from 14 to 23 books. Increase?|9 books|37 books;8 books;10 books|Subtract fourteen from twenty-three.\nWhy might experimental and theoretical probabilities differ?|Trials vary by chance|One must be a counting error;Theory always equals every trial;Graphs change the bag|Finite trial results can vary without changing theoretical chance.\n","Class B increased by three rather than two. The observed fraction for six is 8/48 = 1/6.","A double-line graph uses one time axis and two labelled lines. A legend tells which line belongs to each group. Compare values at the same time and use the vertical scale. In an experiment, relative frequency is the number of times an event occurs divided by the total trials. It describes the observed results. More trials can improve an estimate, but they do not guarantee a particular next result.",A.F(["Monday","Class A: 8 books; class B: 6 books","Tuesday","Class A: 10 books; class B: 9 books","Legend","Solid line = A; dotted line = B","Experiment","12 heads in 20 tosses: 12/20 = 0.6"],p,p),"Read the legend and axes. Use event count divided by trials for an experimental estimate.","Line Graphs and Experimental Probability","Which class had the larger Monday-to-Tuesday increase? A die shows six in 8 of 48 rolls. Find its observed fraction.","On Tuesday, A reads ten books and B reads nine. Their difference is one book. A increased by two since Monday; B increased by three. In forty spinner trials, green occurs eighteen times. The experimental chance is 18/40 = 9/20 = 45%.")],t.LQ)})
s($,"ceL","bRY",()=>{var q="10/40 = 25% = 90\xb0",p=t.s,o=t.N
return A.a([A.cQ(A.a(["Multiply and divide fractions using equal amounts.","Compute decimal products and quotients.","Choose operations from a measurement story."],p),"Dividing by a number smaller than one can make an answer larger. Do not shift only one decimal in division.","What is 2/3 \xd7 3/4?|1/2|2/7;5/7;3/2|Six twelfths reduce to one half.\nWhat is 3/5 \xf7 1/5?|3|3/25;1/3;5|Three fifth-size groups fit in three fifths.\nWhat is 2 \xf7 2/3?|3|4/3;1/3;2/3|Two times three halves equals three.\nWhat is 1 1/2 + 2 3/4?|4 1/4|3 1/4;4 3/4;3 3/4|One half is two quarters; the fraction total is five quarters.\nWhat is 1.4 \xd7 0.2?|0.28|2.8;28;0.028|Fourteen tenths times two tenths gives twenty-eight hundredths.\nWhat is 6.3 \xf7 0.7?|9|0.9;90;4.41|Scale both by ten to divide 63 by 7.\nWhat is 4.05 + 0.8?|4.85|4.13;4.75;4.058|Align decimal places and add 4.05 + 0.80.\nWhat is 5 - 2.75?|2.25|3.25;2.75;7.75|Exchange through 5.00 before subtracting.\nHow many 3/4 L servings fit in 6 L?|8|4;6;9|Six times four thirds is eight.\nA cloth costs \u20b112.50 per metre. Cost for 2.4 m?|\u20b130|\u20b125;\u20b135;\u20b1300|Multiply 12.5 by 2.4 to obtain thirty.\nWhat is 3 1/3 - 1 5/6?|1 1/2|2 1/2;1 1/6;2 1/6|Rename as 3 2/6, exchange one whole, then subtract.\n","Cancel the common five to get 3/6 = 1/2. Scale both decimals by ten: 72 \xf7 9 = 8.","Multiplying a fraction by a fraction can find part of a part: half of three quarters is three eighths. Dividing by a fraction asks how many groups of that size fit. Two divided by one quarter is eight quarter-size groups. Multiplying by the reciprocal gives the same count. Decimal multiplication uses place value: 1.2 \xd7 0.3 = 0.36. For decimal division, multiply both numbers by the same power of ten so the divisor becomes whole.",A.F(["Part of a part","1/2 \xd7 3/4 = 3/8","Group count","2 \xf7 1/4 = 8","Scale both","4.8 \xf7 0.6 = 48 \xf7 6 = 8","Decimal product","12 tenths \xd7 3 tenths = 36 hundredths"],o,o),"Keep amounts equal when renaming, select the operation from the story, and check by multiplication.","Fraction and Decimal Problem Solving","Find 3/5 \xd7 5/6. Then find 7.2 \xf7 0.9.","A 2 1/4 m ribbon is cut into 3/8 m pieces. Change 2 1/4 to 9/4. Compute 9/4 \xf7 3/8 = 9/4 \xd7 8/3 = 6. Check six pieces of 3/8 m total 18/8 m = 2 1/4 m."),B.t9,B.ta,B.tb,B.Cl,A.cQ(A.a(["Describe prisms, pyramids, cylinders, cones, and spheres.","Match faces to a solid net.","Calculate surface area from exposed faces."],p),"Surface area uses square units; volume uses cubic units. An open box does not include a missing lid.","Which solid has six equal square faces?|Cube|Cone;Sphere;Cylinder|A cube has six congruent square faces.\nWhich solid has one base and triangular faces meeting at a point?|Pyramid|Prism;Sphere;Cylinder|Its side faces meet at the apex.\nWhich solid has two circular bases?|Cylinder|Cone;Sphere;Cube|The two circles are parallel and equal.\nWhich solid has no flat faces?|Sphere|Cube;Cylinder;Pyramid|A sphere has only a curved surface.\nArea of one cube face when the side is 3 cm?|9 cm\xb2|6 cm\xb2;3 cm\xb2;27 cm\xb2|Square the side length.\nSurface area of a closed cube of side 3 cm?|54 cm\xb2|27 cm\xb2;18 cm\xb2;9 cm\xb2|Six faces each have area nine.\nWhich unit fits surface area?|cm\xb2|cm\xb3;cm;kg|Surface area covers faces with squares.\nWhat does a net show?|Unfolded surface faces|Only the inside volume;Only one edge;Only a point|Its connected faces can fold into a solid.\nSurface area of a closed 4 by 3 by 2 cm prism?|52 cm\xb2|24 cm\xb2;26 cm\xb2;48 cm\xb2|Use 2(12 + 8 + 6).\nA cube of side 5 cm has no top. Area of its five faces?|125 cm\xb2|150 cm\xb2;25 cm\xb2;100 cm\xb2|Five faces each cover twenty-five square centimetres.\nA square pyramid has base side 6 cm and slant height 4 cm. Surface area?|84 cm\xb2|60 cm\xb2;96 cm\xb2;144 cm\xb2|Base area is 36 and four triangles each have area 12.\n","The face pairs give 2(24 + 18 + 12) = 108 cm\xb2. A cone has one circular base and a pointed top.","A prism has two matching parallel bases. A pyramid has one base and triangular faces meeting at a point. A cylinder has two circular bases and a curved surface; a cone has one circular base and a curved surface. A sphere has only a curved surface. A net unfolds a solid surface. For a closed rectangular prism, two faces have area lw, two lh, and two wh. Its surface area is 2(lw + lh + wh).",A.F(["Box dimensions","l = 5 cm, w = 3 cm, h = 2 cm","Base pair","2 \xd7 5 \xd7 3 = 30 cm\xb2","Side pairs","2 \xd7 5 \xd7 2 + 2 \xd7 3 \xd7 2 = 32 cm\xb2","Total surface","30 + 32 = 62 cm\xb2"],o,o),"Unfold and label the faces before adding their areas. Count only surfaces that actually exist.","Solids Nets and Surface Area","Find the surface area of a closed 6 cm by 4 cm by 3 cm box. Which solid has one circular base and one pointed top?","Find the surface area of a closed cube of side 4 cm. Each face is a square with area sixteen square centimetres. There are six faces. Multiply 6 \xd7 16 = 96 cm\xb2. Check all six faces are counted once."),A.cQ(A.a(["Find volumes of cylinders, pyramids, cones, and spheres.","Keep radius and height labels clear.","Subtract meter readings to find consumption."],p),"Use perpendicular height, not a sloping side. Meter readings are cumulative; the later reading is not itself the period usage.","Volume of a prism with base area 20 cm\xb2 and height 4 cm?|80 cm\xb3|24 cm\xb3;40 cm\xb3;5 cm\xb3|Multiply base area by height.\nVolume of a pyramid with base area 30 cm\xb2 and height 6 cm?|60 cm\xb3|180 cm\xb3;36 cm\xb3;90 cm\xb3|Take one third of thirty times six.\nUsing \u03c0 = 3.14, cylinder radius 2 cm and height 5 cm has volume what?|62.8 cm\xb3|31.4 cm\xb3;20 cm\xb3;125.6 cm\xb3|Use 3.14 \xd7 4 \xd7 5.\nA cone has the same base and height as a 90 cm\xb3 cylinder. Cone volume?|30 cm\xb3|90 cm\xb3;270 cm\xb3;60 cm\xb3|A cone occupies one third of the matching cylinder.\nWhich formula gives sphere volume?|4\u03c0r\xb3/3|\u03c0r\xb2;2\u03c0r;\u03c0r\xb2h|A sphere uses the cube of its radius.\nWater readings are 120 and 138 m\xb3. Consumption?|18 m\xb3|258 m\xb3;138 m\xb3;12 m\xb3|Subtract the previous total from the new total.\nElectric readings are 2080 and 2145 kWh. Consumption?|65 kWh|4225 kWh;55 kWh;2145 kWh|The difference measures usage during the period.\nAt \u20b110 per kWh, 25 kWh costs what before other fees?|\u20b1250|\u20b125;\u20b135;\u20b12500|Multiply twenty-five units by ten pesos each.\nUsing \u03c0 = 22/7, cylinder radius 7 cm and height 3 cm volume?|462 cm\xb3|154 cm\xb3;132 cm\xb3;147 cm\xb3|Multiply 22/7 by 49 and by 3.\nA sphere has radius 3 cm. Exact volume?|36\u03c0 cm\xb3|12\u03c0 cm\xb3;9\u03c0 cm\xb3;108\u03c0 cm\xb3|Use 4/3 \xd7 \u03c0 \xd7 27.\nReadings rise from 742 to 761 m\xb3. At \u20b120 per m\xb3, simple usage cost?|\u20b1380|\u20b1400;\u20b119;\u20b11503|Nineteen cubic metres at twenty pesos each cost 380.\n","48 \xd7 9 \xf7 3 = 144 cm\xb3. Water usage is 526 - 508 = 18 m\xb3.","Volume counts cubic units inside a solid. A prism or cylinder has volume base area \xd7 perpendicular height. A pyramid or cone has one third the volume of a prism or cylinder with the same base area and height. A sphere has V = 4\u03c0r\xb3/3. Water and electricity meters record running totals. Consumption over a period is the later reading minus the earlier reading; multiply usage by a stated unit price to estimate a simple bill.",A.F(["Cylinder","V = \u03c0r\xb2h","Cone or pyramid","V = base area \xd7 h \xf7 3","Sphere","V = 4\u03c0r\xb3 \xf7 3","Water meter","Later 246 m\xb3 - earlier 231 m\xb3 = 15 m\xb3 used"],o,o),"Identify the solid, label dimensions, calculate in cubic units, and subtract cumulative meter readings.","Volume and Household Meter Readings","A pyramid has base area 48 cm\xb2 and height 9 cm. Find volume. Water readings rise from 508 to 526 m\xb3. Find usage.","A cone has radius 3 cm and height 10 cm. Its matching cylinder holds \u03c0 \xd7 9 \xd7 10 = 90\u03c0 cm\xb3. Take one third: 30\u03c0 cm\xb3, or 94.2 cm\xb3 using \u03c0 = 3.14. For readings 1250 and 1318 kWh, usage is 68 kWh."),B.Cq,A.cQ(A.a(["Relate circle sectors to fractions of a total.","Count favourable and total outcomes.","Distinguish theoretical chance from observed frequency."],p),"Count all outcomes in the denominator, not only the unfavourable ones. A whole pie represents one total, not several unrelated totals.","How many degrees make a whole pie graph?|360|100;180;90|A full turn measures 360 degrees.\nWhat angle represents half the data?|180\xb0|90\xb0;45\xb0;360\xb0|Half of 360 is 180.\nWhat percent is one quarter of a pie?|25%|20%;40%;75%|One quarter is twenty-five out of a hundred.\nChance of heads on a fair coin?|1/2|1;0;1/3|One of two equally likely faces is heads.\nChance of an even number on a fair die labelled 1 to 6?|1/2|1/6;2/3;1|Faces two, four, and six are three of six.\nA bag has 3 green and 7 red equal counters. Chance of green?|3/10|3/7;7/10;1/3|Three favourable counters out of ten total.\nChance of rolling 8 on a normal six-face die?|0|1;1/6;1/8|No face shows eight.\nChance of rolling less than 7 on a normal six-face die?|1|0;1/7;5/6|All six faces are favourable.\nA category is 30% of 60 learners. How many learners?|18|20;30;12|Multiply sixty by three tenths.\nWhat angle represents 20% of a pie graph?|72\xb0|20\xb0;36\xb0;90\xb0|One fifth of 360 is seventy-two.\nIn 50 trials, red occurs 22 times. Observed probability?|11/25|22/28;1/22;1/2|Use event count over all trials: 22/50.\n","The favourable faces are five and six: 2/6 = 1/3. A quarter of 360 degrees is ninety degrees.","A pie graph divides a full 360-degree circle into shares of a total. A quarter share uses ninety degrees. Label every category and state the total. For equally likely outcomes, theoretical probability is favourable outcomes divided by all outcomes. For a fair die, three of six faces are even, giving one half. Experimental probability instead uses actual recorded trials, which may differ from theory.",A.F(["Class total","40 learners","Walkers","20/40 = 50% = 180\xb0","Riders",q,"Other",q],o,o),"Fractions, percents, and sector angles describe the same shares. Probability counts favourable out of all equally likely outcomes.","Pie Graphs and Equally Likely Outcomes","What is the chance of rolling a number greater than four on a fair six-face die? What angle represents 25% of a pie graph?","A bag contains 4 red and 6 blue equal counters. The total is ten. The chance of red is 4/10 = 2/5 = 40%. A pie sector for red is 40/100 \xd7 360 = 144 degrees. Check the blue sector is 216 degrees and they total 360.")],t.LQ)})
s($,"ceM","bRZ",()=>{var q=t.s,p=t.N
return A.a([A.cQ(A.a(["Classify regular, irregular, convex, and non-convex polygons.","Relate adjacent interior and exterior angles.","Calculate angle sums and side counts."],q),"The interior sum is not 360\xb0 for every polygon. The division 360/n gives exterior, not interior, angles.","Interior angle sum of a hexagon?|720\xb0|360\xb0;540\xb0;1080\xb0|Six minus two gives four triangles of 180 degrees.\nEach exterior angle of a regular pentagon?|72\xb0|108\xb0;60\xb0;120\xb0|Divide a full turn by five.\nEach interior angle of a regular octagon?|135\xb0|45\xb0;120\xb0;140\xb0|Subtract its 45-degree exterior angle from 180.\nA regular polygon has exterior angle 30\xb0. Sides?|12|6;10;15|360 divided by 30 equals twelve.\nA regular polygon has ten sides. Interior sum?|1440\xb0|1800\xb0;1080\xb0;360\xb0|Eight triangles give 1440 degrees.\nAdjacent interior and exterior angles total what?|180\xb0|90\xb0;270\xb0;360\xb0|They form a straight angle.\nWhat makes a polygon non-convex?|An interior angle greater than 180\xb0|Any unequal side;All sides equal;Exactly five sides|A reflex inward corner makes a simple polygon non-convex.\nIs every equal-sided polygon regular?|No, its angles must also be equal|Yes always;Only if red;Only if rotated|Equal angles and equal sides are both needed.\nA regular hexagon has side 7 cm. Perimeter?|42 cm|49 cm;35 cm;14 cm|Six equal sides total six times seven.\nA polygon's interior sum is 900\xb0. How many sides?|7|5;6;8|Solve (n - 2)180 = 900 to get n = 7.\nInterior angle is 156\xb0 in a regular polygon. Side count?|15|12;24;30|Exterior is 24 degrees, and 360 divided by 24 is fifteen.\n","The pentagon sum is 540\xb0, so each angle is 108\xb0. An octagon exterior angle is 360 \xf7 8 = 45\xb0.","A polygon is a simple closed figure of straight segments. Regular polygons have equal sides and equal interior angles. A convex polygon has every interior angle less than 180\xb0; a non-convex polygon has an inward corner greater than 180\xb0. Draw diagonals from one vertex of a convex n-sided polygon to form n - 2 triangles. Its interior sum is (n - 2) \xd7 180\xb0. One exterior angle at each vertex, taken in the same direction, sums to 360\xb0. An interior angle and its adjacent exterior angle sum to 180\xb0.",A.F(["Pentagon","5 sides; interior sum = 3 \xd7 180\xb0 = 540\xb0","Regular hexagon","Interior 120\xb0; exterior 60\xb0","Regular octagon","Interior 135\xb0; exterior 45\xb0","Side count","n = 360\xb0 \xf7 regular exterior angle"],p,p),"Use triangle partitions for interior sums and one full turn for exterior sums.","Polygon Angles and Classification","Find each interior angle of a regular pentagon. What exterior angle belongs to a regular octagon?","A regular polygon has exterior angle 36\xb0. Divide 360 by 36 to obtain ten sides. Its adjacent interior angle is 180 - 36 = 144\xb0. Check its interior sum: 10 \xd7 144 = 1440\xb0, equal to (10 - 2) \xd7 180."),B.ta,B.t9,B.C5,A.cQ(A.a(["Find square roots of perfect squares and cube roots of perfect cubes.","Identify rational and irrational numbers.","Locate an irrational root between integers."],q),"\u221a36 means six, not plus or minus six; x\xb2 = 36 has two solutions. A long decimal is not automatically irrational.","What is \u221a81?|9|8;18;\xb19|The principal square root is nonnegative and nine squared is 81.\nWhat is \u221b125?|5|25;3;15|Five cubed equals 125.\nWhat is \u221b(-27)?|-3|3;-9;9|Negative three cubed is negative twenty-seven.\nWhich number is irrational?|\u221a3|0.75;-4;2/7|Three is not a perfect square.\nWhich subset contains -5 but not 1/2?|Integers|Whole numbers;Natural numbers only;Irrational numbers|Integers include negative whole-number values but not fractional halves.\nBetween which integers is \u221a20?|4 and 5|3 and 4;5 and 6;2 and 3|Sixteen is less than twenty and twenty less than twenty-five.\nIs 0.333... with threes repeating rational?|Yes, it equals 1/3|No, every endless decimal is irrational;No, it has no value;Only if rounded|Repeating decimals can be written as integer ratios.\nWhat is \u221a0?|0|1;Undefined;-1|Zero squared is zero.\nBetween which integers is \u221a70?|8 and 9|7 and 8;9 and 10;6 and 7|Sixty-four and eighty-one bound seventy.\nWhich statement is true?|Every integer is rational|Every rational is an integer;Every real is rational;Every irrational is whole|An integer can be written over denominator one.\nWhat is \u221a144 + \u221b8?|14|10;16;20|Twelve plus two equals fourteen.\n","Eleven squared is 121 and four cubed is 64. Since 25 < 30 < 36, \u221a30 lies between five and six.","The principal square root of a nonnegative number is nonnegative: \u221a49 = 7. A cube root reverses cubing, so \u221b(-8) = -2. An integer ratio with nonzero denominator is rational. Terminating and repeating decimals are rational. An irrational number cannot be written as such a ratio; \u221a2 is an example. Whole numbers fit inside integers, integers inside rational numbers, and rational numbers inside real numbers. Irrational numbers are real but not rational.",A.F(["Square model","An 8 by 8 square has area 64, so \u221a64 = 8","Cube model","A 3 by 3 by 3 cube has volume 27, so \u221b27 = 3","Bounds","9 < 11 < 16, so 3 < \u221a11 < 4","Subsets","Whole \u2282 integers \u2282 rational \u2282 real"],p,p),"Use known squares and cubes to reverse powers and bound roots. Classify by exact number properties.","Roots and Real Number Subsets","Find \u221a121 and \u221b64. Between which integers does \u221a30 lie?","Classify \u221a50 and locate it. Seven squared is 49 and eight squared is 64. Thus \u221a50 is between seven and eight. Fifty is not a perfect square, so its root is irrational. It is still a real number."),A.cQ(A.a(["Convert within SI and across stated measurement systems.","Derive cylinder volume from circular layers.","Compare pyramid volume with a matching prism."],q),"Do not use diameter as radius. A pyramid slant height is not its perpendicular height. Cubic conversions cube the length factor.","Convert 3.2 km to metres.|3200 m|320 m;32 m;0.0032 m|Multiply by one thousand.\nConvert 4500 g to kilograms.|4.5 kg|45 kg;450 kg;0.45 kg|Divide by one thousand.\nUsing 1 inch = 2.54 cm, convert 5 inches.|12.7 cm|7.54 cm;25.4 cm;2 cm|Multiply five by 2.54.\nUsing 1 foot = 12 inches, convert 3 feet.|36 inches|15 inches;4 inches;24 inches|Three groups of twelve equal thirty-six.\nA cylinder has radius 3 cm and height 4 cm. Exact volume?|36\u03c0 cm\xb3|12\u03c0 cm\xb3;24\u03c0 cm\xb3;9\u03c0 cm\xb3|Square three, multiply by four and pi.\nA rectangular pyramid has base 5 by 6 cm and height 9 cm. Volume?|90 cm\xb3|270 cm\xb3;45 cm\xb3;180 cm\xb3|Multiply base area thirty by nine and divide by three.\nA pyramid and prism share base and height. Pyramid volume is what fraction of prism volume?|1/3|1/2;2/3;3|The volume comparison gives one third.\nWhat height is used in a pyramid volume formula?|Perpendicular height|Any sloping edge;Base diagonal;Perimeter|Height measures the perpendicular distance to the base plane.\nConvert 1 m\xb3 to cm\xb3.|1000000 cm\xb3|100 cm\xb3;1000 cm\xb3;10000 cm\xb3|Cube the factor one hundred.\nA cylinder diameter is 10 cm and height 2 cm. Exact volume?|50\u03c0 cm\xb3|200\u03c0 cm\xb3;20\u03c0 cm\xb3;100\u03c0 cm\xb3|The radius is five, so volume is pi times twenty-five times two.\nA square pyramid has volume 48 cm\xb3 and base area 16 cm\xb2. Height?|9 cm|3 cm;6 cm;12 cm|Solve 48 = 16h/3 to obtain nine.\n","Cylinder volume is 3.14 \xd7 4 \xd7 8 = 100.48 cm\xb3. Four inches equal 10.16 cm.","A conversion changes units without changing an amount. Multiply by a ratio equal to one, such as 100 cm / 1 m. For different systems use the given fact, such as 1 inch = 2.54 cm. A cylinder stacks equal circular layers; V = \u03c0r\xb2h. A rectangular pyramid holds one third of the volume of a prism with the same base and perpendicular height: V = lwh/3.",A.F(["SI conversion","2.5 m \xd7 100 cm/m = 250 cm","Across systems","10 inches \xd7 2.54 cm/inch = 25.4 cm","Cylinder","Base area \u03c0r\xb2, height h","Pyramid","Base area lw; perpendicular height h; V = lwh/3"],p,p),"Label dimensions and units first. Cylinder volume stacks bases; pyramid volume takes one third of a matching prism.","Unit Conversions Cylinders and Pyramids","Using \u03c0 = 3.14, find the volume of a cylinder of radius 2 cm and height 8 cm. Convert 4 inches using 2.54 cm per inch.","A square pyramid has base side 6 cm and height 10 cm. Base area is 36 cm\xb2. The matching prism volume is 360 cm\xb3. Divide by three: pyramid volume is 120 cm\xb3. Check cubic rather than square units."),A.cQ(A.a(["Describe well-defined sets and subsets.","Find unions and intersections.","Use Venn regions without double-counting."],q),"Union does not count repeated members twice. A complement depends on the named universal set.","For A = {1,2,3} and B = {3,4}, what is A \u2229 B?|{3}|{1,2,3,4};{1,2};{4}|Three is the only shared element.\nFor A = {1,2} and B = {2,3}, what is A \u222a B?|{1,2,3}|{2};{1,2,2,3};{1,3}|Union includes either set without duplicate members.\nWhich is a subset of {2,4,6}?|{2,6}|{2,5};{1};{8}|Every listed element must belong to the larger set.\nHow many elements are in {a,b,c}?|3|1;2;6|Count each distinct member once.\nWhat does an empty set contain?|No elements|Zero as its only element;Every number;One blank element|Having no members differs from containing the number zero.\nIn a Venn diagram, what is the overlap?|Intersection|Union only;Universal boundary;Complement of both|It contains members shared by both sets.\nU = {1,2,3,4}, A = {2,4}. Complement of A?|{1,3}|{2,4};{1,2,3,4};{5}|Use members of U outside A.\nTwo disjoint sets have what intersection?|Empty set|Their union;Both sets;The universe|Disjoint sets share no members.\nThere are 12 in A, 9 in B, and 4 in both. How many in the union?|17|21;25;5|Add twelve and nine, then subtract the double-counted four.\nOut of 25 learners, 20 are in A \u222a B. How many in neither?|5|20;45;0|Subtract the union from the universal total.\nA has 15 members and 7 are also in B. How many in A only?|8|22;7;15|Remove the overlap from A.\n","Only two is shared, so the intersection is {2}. The universal elements outside A are {1, 3, 5}.","A set is a collection with clear membership. Write its elements inside braces. A subset has every element in the larger set. The intersection A \u2229 B contains shared elements. The union A \u222a B contains elements in either set, counting shared elements only once. In a Venn diagram the overlap is the intersection. A universal set gives the full collection being considered, so a complement means outside the set but still inside that universe.",A.F(["A","{1, 2, 3, 4}","B","{3, 4, 5}","Overlap A \u2229 B","{3, 4}","Either A \u222a B","{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}"],p,p),"Place shared members in the overlap first. Subsets preserve membership, not size alone.","Sets Subsets and Venn Regions","Let U = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, A = {2, 4, 6}, B = {1, 2, 3}. Find A \u2229 B and the complement of A.","In a class of thirty, eighteen play chess, fourteen play badminton, and six play both. Chess only is 18 - 6 = 12; badminton only is 14 - 6 = 8. The union is 12 + 6 + 8 = 26. Four play neither. Check all four regions total thirty."),A.cQ(A.a(["Distinguish a population from a sample.","Organize values in frequency and stem-and-leaf displays.","Choose and interpret bar, line, and pie graphs."],q),"A large biased sample can still mislead. Read graph scales: a cut-off axis can exaggerate differences.","What is a sample?|A selected part of a population|The entire population always;Only the largest value;A graph title|It is a subset studied to learn about the whole.\nWhich school travel survey is less biased?|Random learners from all grades|Only cyclists;Only one friendship group;Only late arrivals|Broader random selection avoids restricting travel types.\nFrequency of 4 in 4, 2, 4, 3, 4?|3|2;4;5|The value four appears three times.\nWhich graph fits monthly rainfall over one year?|Line graph|Unlabelled picture;Venn diagram;Only a shape net|Ordered time changes suit a line graph.\nWhich graph compares favourite fruit categories?|Bar graph|Number line only;Triangle diagram;Clock face|Separate bars compare category counts.\nWhat must pie-graph sectors total?|100%|50%;200%;360%|All categories together make the full data total.\nWith stem 2 and leaf 5 meaning 25, what do stem 3 and leaf 7 mean?|37|3.7;73;10|Stem is tens and leaf is ones.\nModel data median for 12,14,14,18,21,23?|16|14;18;17|Average the two middle values fourteen and eighteen.\nFrequencies are 5, 8, 7. Total observations?|20|15;56;13|Add all category frequencies.\nA graph axis begins at 95 rather than 0. What should a reader do?|Check whether the scale exaggerates differences|Ignore labels;Assume all values are under five;Treat bars as exact percentages|The truncated baseline can change visual impressions.\nWhat graph keeps each original two-digit value?|Stem-and-leaf plot|Pie chart alone;Bar chart with only totals;Unlabelled line|Each leaf preserves one observed number.\n","The stem two has two leaves, so two values are at least twenty. A line graph is suitable for ordered daily temperatures.","A population is the whole group of interest. A sample is a smaller group used to learn about it. Random selection helps avoid choosing only convenient or similar people. A frequency counts how often a value appears. Bar graphs compare categories, line graphs show change over ordered time, and pie graphs show shares of one total. A stem-and-leaf plot keeps original numbers by separating tens and ones; always provide its key.",A.F(["Data","12, 14, 14, 18, 21, 23","Stem 1","Leaves 2, 4, 4, 8","Stem 2","Leaves 1, 3","Key","1 | 2 means 12, not 1.2"],p,p),"Select a sample that represents the population. Count every observation and choose a graph that matches the question.","Sampling Frequency Tables and Graph Choice","In the model, how many values are at least twenty? Which graph fits daily temperature over a week?","For 2, 3, 2, 4, 3, 2, count frequency: two occurs three times, three twice, four once. Sum frequencies to get six, matching the data count. To estimate travel choices of the whole school, randomly sample learners across grades instead of asking only a cycling club."),B.tb,B.Cm,B.C8,A.cQ(A.a(["Build equations from bar models.","Evaluate expressions by substitution.","Rearrange a formula using equality."],q),"Apply an operation to both sides, not just one term. Substitution requires multiplication signs or clear brackets.","Translate five more than twice n.|2n + 5|5n + 2;2(n + 5);n + 7|Twice the number is formed before adding five.\nEvaluate 3x + 4 for x = 2.|10|14;9;6|Substitute two: six plus four equals ten.\nSolve x + 8 = 19.|11|27;10;12|Subtract eight from both sides.\nSolve 5x = 35.|7|30;40;5|Divide both sides by five.\nSolve 2x + 3 = 17.|7|10;8;5|Subtract three, then divide fourteen by two.\nIn 6a + 9, which is the variable?|a|6;9;15|The letter names a changeable or unknown value.\nFrom A = lw, make w the subject for l \u2260 0.|w = A/l|w = Al;w = A - l;w = l/A|Divide both sides by length.\nFrom P = 2l + 2w, make l the subject.|l = (P - 2w)/2|l = P - w;l = P/2 + w;l = 2P - w|Subtract twice width, then divide by two.\nSolve 6x - 5 = 25.|5|4;6;30|Add five to get thirty, then divide by six.\nAn item costs \u20b115 each plus \u20b110 delivery. Total \u20b185. Number of items?|5|6;7;4|Solve 15n + 10 = 85.\nGiven C = 2\u03c0r, what is r?|C/(2\u03c0)|2\u03c0C;C - 2\u03c0;2C/\u03c0|Divide both sides by the nonzero factor two pi.\n","Subtract seven to obtain 4x = 24, then divide by four: x = 6. Multiply v = d/t by t to obtain d = vt, with t nonzero in the starting formula.","A variable stands for a value that may be unknown or change. A constant is a fixed value. Three equal boxes and two loose beads totalling seventeen make 3x + 2 = 17. Equality stays true when the same operation is applied to both sides. A formula connects named quantities. From P = 2l + 2w, subtract 2w and divide by two to make l the subject: l = (P - 2w)/2.",A.F(["Bar model","[x][x][x] + 2 = 17","Remove 2","[x][x][x] = 15","Equal groups","x = 15 \xf7 3 = 5","Formula","A = bh \u2192 h = A/b when b \u2260 0"],p,p),"Translate the relationship, keep equality balanced, and check the value in its original context.","Formulas and Nonnegative Unknowns","Solve 4x + 7 = 31. Rearrange v = d/t to make d the subject.","A taxi charges a fixed \u20b140 plus \u20b112 per kilometre. A \u20b1100 trip gives 40 + 12d = 100. Subtract forty to get 12d = 60. Divide by twelve: d = 5 km. Check 40 + 12 \xd7 5 = 100 and the distance is nonnegative."),A.cQ(A.a(["Record repeated experimental outcomes.","Represent outcomes in tables.","Use relative frequency without assuming certainty."],q),"Do not discard unwanted results. State whether counters are replaced because this changes later available outcomes.","One repetition of an experiment is called what?|Trial|Population;Variable only;Average|A trial is one performed experiment.\nHeads occurs 14 times in 25 tosses. Relative frequency?|14/25|14/11;25/14;1/14|Use heads count divided by all tosses.\nBlue occurs 9 times in 30 draws. Observed percent?|30%|9%;21%;90%|Nine thirtieths equals three tenths.\nWhich faces are prime on a die labelled 1 to 6?|2, 3, 5|1, 2, 3;2, 4, 6;1, 3, 5|One is not prime and four and six are composite.\nFrequencies are 6 heads and 4 tails. Trial total?|10|2;24;6|Add every outcome count.\nWhat should a frequency table include?|Every trial outcome|Only successful trials;Only expected outcomes;Only the first trial|Complete recording prevents biased frequencies.\nDoes a fair coin remember previous tosses?|No|Yes;Only after ten tosses;Only after heads|Independent tosses do not retain prior outcomes.\nAn event occurs zero times in 10 trials. Is it necessarily impossible?|No|Yes;Only on a die;Always certain|A possible event may fail to appear in a small set of trials.\nAn event occurs 32 times in 80 trials. Relative frequency?|0.4|0.32;0.8;2.5|Divide 32 by 80.\nWhat does drawing with replacement mean?|Return the object before the next draw|Remove it permanently;Change its colour;Always choose another bag|Returning it resets the contents.\nA table lists frequencies 8, 12, and 5. What percent belongs to frequency 5?|20%|5%;25%;50%|There are twenty-five trials, and five twenty-fifths is twenty percent.\n","21/60 = 7/20 = 35%. One trial is one draw followed by returning the counter, so the bag contents reset.","An experiment can have different outcomes, such as heads and tails in a coin toss. A trial is one repetition. Define the event before collecting data, then record every result. Relative frequency is event count divided by trials. It estimates how often an event has occurred. More repetitions may stabilize an estimate, but earlier results do not force the next independent result.",A.F(["Trials","20 coin tosses","Heads","12 tallies \u2192 frequency 12","Tails","8 tallies \u2192 frequency 8","Relative frequency","Heads 12/20 = 0.6; tails 8/20 = 0.4"],p,p),"Define, repeat, record, and divide event count by total trials. Describe the data without promising future outcomes.","Experimental Outcomes and Relative Frequency","Red occurs 21 times in 60 draws with replacement. Find the relative frequency. What counts as one trial?","A die is rolled forty times. A prime face appears eighteen times. The prime faces on a normal die are two, three, and five. The observed relative frequency is 18/40 = 0.45. Report this as 45% of these trials, not a guarantee of the next forty trials."),A.cQ(A.a(["Write large and small numbers in scientific notation.","Multiply and divide scientific notation.","Align powers before addition or subtraction."],q),"Do not add exponents when adding numbers. A negative exponent is not a negative quantity.","Write 63000 in scientific notation.|6.3 \xd7 10\u2074|63 \xd7 10\xb3;6.3 \xd7 10\xb3;0.63 \xd7 10\u2075|Move the decimal four places while keeping the coefficient between one and ten.\nWrite 0.0042 in scientific notation.|4.2 \xd7 10\u207b\xb3|4.2 \xd7 10\xb3;42 \xd7 10\u207b\xb3;4.2 \xd7 10\u207b\xb2|The decimal shifts three places from the coefficient.\nWhat is 7 \xd7 10\xb3 in ordinary notation?|7000|700;0.007;70000|Ten cubed is one thousand.\nWhat is 3 \xd7 10\u207b\xb2?|0.03|0.3;300;0.003|Divide three by one hundred.\nMultiply (2 \xd7 10\u2074)(3 \xd7 10\xb2).|6 \xd7 10\u2076|6 \xd7 10\u2078;5 \xd7 10\u2076;6 \xd7 10\xb2|Multiply coefficients and add exponents.\nDivide 9 \xd7 10\u2078 by 3 \xd7 10\xb2.|3 \xd7 10\u2076|3 \xd7 10\u2074;6 \xd7 10\u2076;3 \xd7 10\xb9\u2070|Nine divided by three is three and eight minus two is six.\nNormalize 24 \xd7 10\u2075.|2.4 \xd7 10\u2076|2.4 \xd7 10\u2074;24 \xd7 10\u2076;0.24 \xd7 10\u2075|Reducing the coefficient by ten requires raising the power by one.\nAdd 2 \xd7 10\xb3 + 5 \xd7 10\xb3.|7 \xd7 10\xb3|7 \xd7 10\u2076;10 \xd7 10\xb3;7 \xd7 10\u2070|The shared power remains unchanged.\nAdd 3 \xd7 10\u2075 + 2 \xd7 10\u2074.|3.2 \xd7 10\u2075|5 \xd7 10\u2079;5 \xd7 10\u2075;3.02 \xd7 10\u2075|Rewrite the second term as 0.2 \xd7 10\u2075.\nMultiply (6 \xd7 10\u207b\xb3)(2 \xd7 10\xb2).|1.2 \xd7 10\u2070|1.2 \xd7 10\u207b\xb9;12 \xd7 10\u2075;8 \xd7 10\u207b\xb9|The initial product is 12 \xd7 10\u207b\xb9, equal to 1.2.\nSubtract 8 \xd7 10\u2076 - 3 \xd7 10\u2075.|7.7 \xd7 10\u2076|5 \xd7 10\xb9;5 \xd7 10\u2076;7.97 \xd7 10\u2076|Rewrite three hundred thousand as 0.3 million.\n","0.00056 = 5.6 \xd7 10\u207b\u2074. The product is 20 \xd7 10\u2075 = 2 \xd7 10\u2076 after normalizing.","Scientific notation is a \xd7 10\u207f with 1 \u2264 a < 10 and integer n. Positive exponents make large numbers and negative exponents make small ones. In products multiply coefficients and add exponents; in quotients divide coefficients and subtract exponents. For sums, first express both numbers with the same power of ten. Renormalize the coefficient if it is outside the required interval.",A.F(["Large","4500000 = 4.5 \xd7 10\u2076","Small","0.00072 = 7.2 \xd7 10\u207b\u2074","Product","(3 \xd7 10\u2074)(2 \xd7 10\xb3) = 6 \xd7 10\u2077","Sum","4 \xd7 10\u2075 + 3 \xd7 10\u2074 = 4.3 \xd7 10\u2075"],p,p),"Keep one nonzero digit before the decimal. Choose the exponent rule that matches the operation.","Scientific Notation and Powers of Ten","Write 0.00056 in scientific notation. Multiply (4 \xd7 10\xb3)(5 \xd7 10\xb2).","Divide 8.4 \xd7 10\u2077 by 2 \xd7 10\xb3. Divide coefficients: 8.4/2 = 4.2. Subtract exponents: 7 - 3 = 4. Answer 4.2 \xd7 10\u2074. Check 42,000 \xd7 2,000 = 84,000,000.")],t.LQ)})
s($,"ceN","bS_",()=>{var q=t.s,p=t.N
return A.a([A.cQ(A.a(["Extract a greatest common monomial factor.","Factor differences of squares and trinomials.","Recognize cube patterns and verify by expansion."],q),"A sum of squares is not a difference of squares. Stop only when each factor is fully factored over the intended number system.","Factor 4x + 8.|4(x + 2)|4(x + 8);x(4 + 8);8(x + 4)|Four is common to both terms.\nFactor x\xb2 - 16.|(x - 4)(x + 4)|(x - 4)\xb2;(x - 8)(x + 2);x(x - 16)|Use the difference of two squares.\nFactor x\xb2 + 5x + 6.|(x + 2)(x + 3)|(x + 1)(x + 6);(x - 2)(x - 3);(x + 5)(x + 1)|Two and three sum to five and multiply to six.\nFactor x\xb2 - 7x + 12.|(x - 3)(x - 4)|(x + 3)(x + 4);(x - 2)(x - 6);(x - 1)(x - 12)|Negative three and negative four sum to negative seven.\nFactor x\xb2 + 10x + 25.|(x + 5)\xb2|(x - 5)\xb2;(x + 10)(x + 5);x(x + 25)|The middle term is twice x times five.\nFactor 6x\xb2 + 12x.|6x(x + 2)|6(x + 2);x(6x + 2);12x(x + 1)|Both terms contain six x.\nFactor x\xb3 - 1.|(x - 1)(x\xb2 + x + 1)|(x - 1)\xb3;(x + 1)(x\xb2 - x + 1);x(x\xb2 - 1)|Use the difference of cubes identity.\nWhich expression expands to x\xb2 - 9?|(x - 3)(x + 3)|(x - 3)\xb2;(x + 3)\xb2;(x - 9)(x + 1)|The middle terms cancel and the constant is negative nine.\nFactor 2x\xb2 + 9x + 4.|(2x + 1)(x + 4)|(2x + 4)(x + 1);(2x - 1)(x - 4);(x + 2)(x + 4)|Expansion gives 2x\xb2 + 8x + x + 4.\nFactor 5x\xb2 - 45 completely.|5(x - 3)(x + 3)|5(x - 9)(x + 9);(5x - 3)(x + 3);5(x - 3)\xb2|Take out five, then factor x\xb2 - 9.\nFactor x\xb3 + 8.|(x + 2)(x\xb2 - 2x + 4)|(x + 2)\xb3;(x - 2)(x\xb2 + 2x + 4);(x + 8)(x\xb2 - x + 1)|Apply the sum of cubes with b equal to two.\n","Extract three, then use a difference of squares: 3(x - 2)(x + 2). The trinomial is the perfect square (x - 3)\xb2.","Factoring rewrites a sum as a product. First extract the greatest common factor. A difference of squares a\xb2 - b\xb2 factors as (a - b)(a + b). In x\xb2 + bx + c, seek two integers with sum b and product c. For ax\xb2 + bx + c, splitting the middle term can enable grouping. Cube identities are a\xb3 + b\xb3 = (a + b)(a\xb2 - ab + b\xb2) and a\xb3 - b\xb3 = (a - b)(a\xb2 + ab + b\xb2). Always expand to verify.",A.F(["Common factor","6x\xb2 + 9x = 3x(2x + 3)","Difference of squares","x\xb2 - 25 = (x - 5)(x + 5)","Trinomial","x\xb2 + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4)","Cube difference","x\xb3 - 8 = (x - 2)(x\xb2 + 2x + 4)"],p,p),"Take out the common factor first, identify a pattern, and verify by multiplication.","Factoring Polynomial Forms","Factor 3x\xb2 - 12 completely and factor x\xb2 - 6x + 9.","Factor 2x\xb2 + 7x + 3. Multiply the first and last coefficients: six. Choose six and one, which sum to seven. Split: 2x\xb2 + 6x + x + 3. Group: 2x(x + 3) + 1(x + 3). Factor: (2x + 1)(x + 3). Expand to check."),A.cQ(A.a(["State restrictions from original denominators.","Simplify products and quotients by factoring.","Add rational expressions using a common denominator."],q),"Cancelling does not restore an excluded input. For (x + 2)/x you cannot cancel the x term through addition.","What input is excluded from 1/(x - 4)?|4|-4;0;1|The denominator becomes zero at four.\nSimplify 8x/(4x), x \u2260 0.|2|2x;4;1/2|Cancel the whole common factor four x.\nSimplify (x\xb2 - 1)/(x - 1), x \u2260 1.|x + 1|x - 1;x;1|Factor the numerator as (x - 1)(x + 1).\nAdd 2/x + 5/x for x \u2260 0.|7/x|7/(2x);10/x;3/x|Add numerators with the same denominator.\nSimplify (x + 3)/x for x \u2260 0.|1 + 3/x|3;4;x + 3|Split the quotient into x/x and 3/x.\nMultiply (2/x)(3/5), x \u2260 0.|6/(5x)|5/(3x);6x/5;5/(6x)|Multiply numerators and denominators.\nDivide (4/x) by (2/x), x \u2260 0.|2|2/x;8/x\xb2;1/2|Multiply by x/2 and cancel.\nWhat inputs are excluded from 1/[(x - 2)(x + 5)]?|2 and -5|-2 and 5;2 and 5;0 only|Each denominator factor must remain nonzero.\nAdd 1/x + 1/(3x), x \u2260 0.|4/(3x)|2/(4x);2/(3x);4/x|Rewrite the first fraction as 3/(3x).\nSimplify (x\xb2 - 25)/(x + 5), x \u2260 -5.|x - 5|x + 5;x\xb2 - 5;1|Factor and cancel x + 5.\nAfter cancelling x - 2 from a fraction originally undefined at 2, is x = 2 now allowed?|No|Yes always;Only if positive;Only if an integer|The original domain restriction remains.\n","Cancel the common factor 3x to get 2x, with x \u2260 0. Like denominators allow adding numerators: 5/x, again x \u2260 0.","A rational algebraic expression is a quotient of polynomials with a nonzero denominator. Record excluded values before cancelling. Cancel common factors, never separate terms in a sum. For addition, rename using a common denominator. For division, multiply by the reciprocal and require the original divisor to be defined and nonzero.",A.F(["Original","(x\xb2 - 9)/(x - 3), x \u2260 3","Factored","(x - 3)(x + 3)/(x - 3)","Simplified","x + 3, still x \u2260 3","Addition","1/x + 1/(2x) = 3/(2x), x \u2260 0"],p,p),"Restrict, factor, cancel factors, and retain every original restriction.","Rational Algebraic Expressions","Simplify 6x\xb2/(3x), then add 2/x + 3/x. State restrictions.","Simplify (x\xb2 - 4)/(x\xb2 + x - 6). Factor to (x - 2)(x + 2)/[(x + 3)(x - 2)]. Original exclusions are x \u2260 -3, 2. Cancel the common factor x - 2 to get (x + 2)/(x + 3), retaining both exclusions."),B.Cc,B.C4,A.cQ(A.a(["Test ordered pairs in two-variable inequalities.","Choose dashed or solid boundaries.","Find the overlap in a system of inequalities."],q),"Do not shade automatically above every line. Rewrite or test a point. The solution to a system is intersection, not union.","Boundary for y < 3x + 2 should be what?|Dashed|Solid;A circle;No boundary|Strict inequality excludes equality.\nBoundary for y \u2265 x - 1 should be what?|Solid|Dashed;Invisible;Only a dot|Equality belongs to the solution.\nDoes (1,4) satisfy y > 2x?|Yes|No;Only on the boundary;Only if x is negative|Four is greater than two.\nDoes (3,1) satisfy x + y \u2264 3?|No|Yes;Always;Only on an axis|Three plus one is four, exceeding three.\nFor y \u2264 5, which side is shaded?|Below including the line|Above only;Only the line;Neither side|All y values at most five are included.\nThe solution to two simultaneous inequalities is what?|Their overlapping region|Their union always;Only both boundary lines;Only the origin|Each selected point must satisfy both.\nDoes (0,0) satisfy x + y < 8?|Yes|No;Only if 8 is negative;Only on the boundary|Zero is less than eight.\nWhat does x \u2265 0 describe?|On or right of the vertical axis|Only above the horizontal axis;Left only;Only the horizontal axis|The x coordinate must be nonnegative.\nDoes (2,5) satisfy y \u2265 x + 1 and y \u2264 6?|Yes|No, first fails;No, second fails;No, both fail|Five is at least three and at most six.\nNonnegative x,y satisfy x + y \u2264 10. Which point is excluded?|(7,5)|(2,3);(0,10);(4,4)|Its coordinate sum twelve exceeds ten.\nWhat region satisfies y > 2 and y < 2?|No points|All points;The line y = 2;The first quadrant|No number is both greater and less than two.\n","Three is less than four, so (2,3) works. The boundary is dashed. The second system is a horizontal strip between y = 1 and y = 4, with both edges included.","A linear inequality in two variables describes a region, not just a line. Graph its boundary equation first. Use a solid line for \u2264 or \u2265 because equality is included; use a dashed line for < or >. Test a point not on the boundary and shade the side that makes the inequality true. A system requires all inequalities, so its solution is the overlapping region.",A.F(["Boundary","y = 2x + 1","Inequality","y > 2x + 1: dashed line, shade above","Test point","(0, 0): 0 > 1 is false, so do not shade its side","System","x \u2265 0 and y \u2265 0: first quadrant including both axes"],p,p),"Boundary, inclusion, test point, shading, then overlap: each step has a meaning.","Graphing Linear Inequalities and Regions","Does (2,3) satisfy y < 2x? Should its boundary be solid? Describe the overlap of y \u2265 1 and y \u2264 4.","Graph x + y \u2264 6. The boundary passes through (6,0) and (0,6). Draw it solid because equality is allowed. Test (0,0): zero is at most six, so shade the side containing the origin. If x \u2265 0 and y \u2265 0 are also required, keep only the first-quadrant triangular region."),B.C9,A.cQ(A.a(["Distinguish definitions, postulates, and theorems.","Use SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, or right-triangle hypotenuse-leg.","Match corresponding parts in a proof."],q),"SSA is not generally a congruence test. Keep corresponding vertices in order when writing a congruence statement.","Which is an undefined basic term in Euclidean geometry?|Point|Isosceles triangle;Perpendicular bisector;Rectangle|Point is a starting term rather than defined using simpler geometry terms.\nA statement accepted as a starting assumption is what?|Postulate|Proved theorem;Numerical answer;Counterexample|A postulate is accepted without proof within the system.\nA statement supported by proof is what?|Theorem|Guess only;Undefined term;Measurement error|A theorem follows from accepted facts and reasoning.\nThree matching side pairs establish which criterion?|SSS|AAA;SSA always;Only parallelism|Side-side-side fixes size and shape.\nTwo sides and their included angle establish what?|SAS|SSA always;AAA;Only AAS|The angle must lie between the two known sides.\nDoes AAA establish congruence?|No, only similarity|Yes always;Only for large triangles;Only for rotated triangles|Angles do not fix side lengths.\nIf \u25b3ABC \u2245 \u25b3PQR, which corresponds to BC?|QR|PQ;PR;QP|Vertex order pairs B with Q and C with R.\nWhy does a shared side equal itself?|Reflexive property|Angle addition;Parallel postulate;Area formula|Any segment has its own length.\nTwo right triangles share equal hypotenuse and one equal leg. Congruent?|Yes, hypotenuse-leg|No criterion exists;Only if equilateral;Only if upright|The right angle and given sides fix the remaining leg.\nIf AB=DE, \u2220A=\u2220D, \u2220B=\u2220E, which criterion applies?|ASA|SSS;SSA;AAA only|The known side lies between the known angles.\nAfter proving \u25b3ABC \u2245 \u25b3ADC with the stated order, which angle equals \u2220BAC?|\u2220DAC|\u2220ADC;\u2220ACD;\u2220ABC|Corresponding A angles match under that vertex order.\n","SAS applies because the angle is between the known sides. AAA gives matching shape but not necessarily size, so it proves similarity, not congruence.","Geometry begins with undefined terms such as point, line, and plane. A definition gives a term meaning. A postulate is accepted as a starting statement; a theorem requires proof. Congruent triangles have all corresponding sides and angles equal. SSS uses three matching sides; SAS uses two sides and their included angle; ASA uses two angles and the included side. AAS also works. For right triangles, a matching hypotenuse and leg are sufficient.",A.F(["Correspondence","\u25b3ABC \u2245 \u25b3DEF means A\u2194D, B\u2194E, C\u2194F","SAS","AB=DE, AC=DF, \u2220A=\u2220D","SSS","AB=DE, BC=EF, AC=DF","After congruence","Corresponding parts, such as \u2220B and \u2220E, are equal"],p,p),"Give a reason for each fact, establish a valid congruence condition, then use corresponding parts.","Geometric Reasoning and Triangle Congruence","Two triangles have sides 5 and 7 with the included angle 40\xb0 in each. Which criterion applies? Is AAA enough for congruence?","Triangles ABC and ADC share AC. Suppose AB = AD and BC = DC. The shared side AC equals itself by reflexivity. The three pairs of sides match, so the triangles are congruent by SSS. Therefore corresponding angles ABC and ADC are equal."),A.cQ(A.a(["Test whether three lengths form a triangle.","Relate side lengths to opposite angles.","Use angle pairs from a transversal of parallel lines."],q),"Equality fails the triangle side test: the figure would flatten. Equal angle rules require known or proved parallel lines.","Can sides 2, 3, 6 form a triangle?|No|Yes;Only if right;Only if tilted|Two plus three is less than six.\nCan sides 5, 6, 8 form a triangle?|Yes|No;Only if equilateral;Only if parallel|The smallest pair sums to eleven, greater than eight.\nTwo sides are 4 and 9. Third side x must satisfy what?|5 < x < 13|5 \u2264 x \u2264 13;x > 13;x < 5|Use the difference and sum as strict bounds.\nWhich side faces the largest angle?|Longest side|Shortest side;Any side equally;Only the base|Larger opposite sides correspond to larger angles.\nRemote interior angles 40\xb0 and 70\xb0 give exterior angle what?|110\xb0|30\xb0;70\xb0;180\xb0|Add the two remote interior angles.\nParallel-line corresponding angle to 58\xb0 equals what?|58\xb0|122\xb0;32\xb0;180\xb0|Corresponding angles are equal for parallel lines.\nParallel-line same-side interior partner of 58\xb0 equals what?|122\xb0|58\xb0;32\xb0;238\xb0|They sum to one hundred eighty degrees.\nParallel-line alternate interior angles are what?|Equal|Always right;Always complementary;Always obtuse|Parallel lines create equal alternate interior pairs.\nSides 7, 10, x form a triangle. Which x is possible?|8|3;17;20|Eight lies strictly between three and seventeen.\nA triangle has angles 30\xb0, 60\xb0, 90\xb0. Which opposite side is longest?|Side opposite 90\xb0|Side opposite 30\xb0;Side opposite 60\xb0;All equal|The largest angle faces the longest side.\nTwo sides of each of two triangles match. Their included angles are 50\xb0 and 70\xb0. Which third side is longer?|The side opposite 70\xb0|The side opposite 50\xb0;They must be equal;No comparison is possible|The hinge theorem gives the larger third side to the larger included angle.\n","No, because four plus seven is eleven, less than twelve. The exterior angle is 35 + 65 = 100\xb0.","The sum of any two triangle sides must exceed the third. The longest side lies opposite the largest angle. An exterior triangle angle equals the sum of the two remote interior angles, so it exceeds each alone. When a transversal crosses parallel lines, corresponding angles and alternate interior angles are equal; same-side interior angles sum to 180\xb0. These relationships can also prove lines parallel.",A.F(["Side test","3 + 4 > 5, so 3,4,5 can form a triangle","Failed test","2 + 3 = 5, so 2,3,5 cannot","Parallel lines","Alternate interior angles: 65\xb0 and 65\xb0","Same-side interior","65\xb0 + 115\xb0 = 180\xb0"],p,p),"Use strict side bounds and clearly identify the angle pair before applying a parallel-line rule.","Triangle Inequalities and Parallel Lines","Can 4, 7, 12 form a triangle? Two remote interior angles are 35\xb0 and 65\xb0. Find their exterior angle.","Two triangle sides are 6 and 9 cm. Let the third side be x. The triangle inequality gives x > 9 - 6 = 3 and x < 9 + 6 = 15. So 3 < x < 15. If a parallel-line corresponding angle is 72\xb0, its matching angle is also 72\xb0 and its adjacent straight-line angle is 108\xb0."),A.cQ(A.a(["List equally likely outcomes.","Find event probabilities and complements.","Compare theoretical and experimental probabilities."],q),"HH, mixed, TT are not three equally likely outcomes: mixed has two ordered outcomes. Do not count one as a prime.","How many ordered outcomes have two coin tosses?|4|2;3;6|The space is HH, HT, TH, TT.\nProbability of HH on two fair coin tosses?|1/4|1/2;1/3;3/4|One of four equally likely outcomes is HH.\nProbability of exactly one head in two fair tosses?|1/2|1/4;3/4;1|HT and TH are two of four.\nProbability of at least one head in two fair tosses?|3/4|1/4;1/2;1|Only TT has no head.\nProbability of a number greater than 4 on a fair die?|1/3|1/6;1/2;2/3|Five and six are two of six.\nIf P(A) = 0.3, what is P(not A)?|0.7|0.3;1.3;0|Complements total one.\nProbability of an impossible event?|0|1;1/2;-1|No sample-space outcomes are favourable.\nProbability of a certain event?|1|0;2;1/2|Every outcome is favourable.\nA bag has 5 red and 3 blue equal counters. Chance of blue?|3/8|3/5;5/8;1/3|Three blue counters out of eight total.\nProbability of not rolling a 6 on a fair die?|5/6|1/6;1/5;0|Five faces belong to the complement.\nHeads occurs 16 times in 40 tosses. Experimental probability?|2/5|1/2;3/5;1/16|Divide sixteen by forty, regardless of the theoretical half.\n","TT is one of four equally likely outcomes, so two tails has probability 1/4. Its complement, at least one head, has probability 3/4.","A sample space lists every possible outcome. An event is a selected subset. With equally likely outcomes, P(event) is event size divided by sample-space size. The complement includes all outcomes not in the event, so its probability is 1 - P(event). Use a table or tree to avoid missing outcomes in two-stage experiments. Observed trial frequencies need not exactly equal theoretical probabilities.",A.F(["Two coins","HH, HT, TH, TT","Exactly one head","HT and TH: 2 of 4 outcomes","At least one head","HH, HT, TH: 3 of 4","No heads","TT: complement of at least one head"],p,p),"List the full space, identify the event, count consistently, and check with a complement.","Sample Spaces and Simple Probability","Toss two fair coins. Find the probability of two tails and of at least one head.","Roll a fair die. The event prime is {2,3,5}, so its probability is 3/6 = 1/2. Its complement is {1,4,6}, also 1/2. One is not prime. In twenty actual rolls the prime count may be other than ten.")],t.LQ)})
s($,"ceO","bS0",()=>{var q=t.s,p=t.N
return A.a([B.Cb,B.Cw,A.cQ(A.a(["Solve a quadratic inequality using sign intervals.","Exclude zero denominators in rational equations.","Check transformed solutions in original equations."],q),"Do not keep a root that makes an original denominator zero. An inequality solution is often an interval, not just its endpoints.","Solve x\xb2 - 1 < 0.|-1 < x < 1|x < -1 or x > 1;x = \xb11;All real x|The upward parabola is below zero between its roots.\nSolve x\xb2 - 9 \u2265 0.|x \u2264 -3 or x \u2265 3|-3 \u2264 x \u2264 3;x = 0;No real x|It is nonnegative outside and at the roots.\nWhich value is excluded from 2/(x + 4)?|-4|4;2;0|Negative four makes the denominator zero.\nSolve 2/x = 1/5.|10|5;2;0|Cross-multiplication gives x = ten, with x nonzero.\nSolve 4/(x - 1) = 2.|3|1;2;-1|Four equals two times x minus one, so x is three.\nDoes x = 2 satisfy (x\xb2 - 4)/(x - 2) = 4?|No, it is undefined|Yes, after cancellation;Yes, because both sides are four;Only if rounded|The original denominator is zero.\nSolve (x - 2)(x - 6) < 0.|2 < x < 6|x < 2 or x > 6;x = 2 or 6;All x|The factors have opposite signs between the roots.\nWhat should be done before clearing denominators?|Record forbidden inputs|Discard all negative numbers;Square every term;Assume denominator is one|Original domain restrictions must survive the transformation.\nSolve 5/(x + 2) = 1.|3|-2;5;7|Five equals x plus two.\nSolve x\xb2 + 1 < 0 over the reals.|No solution|All real x;x < -1;x > 1|A real square is at least zero, so adding one is positive.\nSolve (x - 1)(x + 2) \u2264 0.|-2 \u2264 x \u2264 1|x \u2264 -2 or x \u2265 1;-2 < x < 1 only;All x|The product is nonpositive between the roots, including both zeros.\n","The upward quadratic is nonnegative outside its roots: x \u2264 -2 or x \u2265 2. Exclude -1 in the rational equation; its solution x = 2 is allowed.","A quadratic inequality asks where a quadratic is positive or negative. Factor, mark its zeros, and test the intervals between them. Strict inequalities exclude zero points; non-strict inequalities may include them. A rational equation contains variable denominators. List forbidden values first, then multiply by a common denominator to solve. Clearing denominators can produce a candidate that is forbidden in the original equation.",A.F(["Quadratic","(x - 2)(x - 5)","Sign intervals","Positive for x < 2; negative for 2 < x < 5; positive for x > 5","Restriction","1/(x - 3) requires x \u2260 3","Equation","2/x = 1/3 \u2192 x = 6, x \u2260 0"],p,p),"Use signs for inequality regions, restrictions for rational equations, and substitution to verify candidates.","Quadratic Inequalities and Rational Equations","Solve x\xb2 - 4 \u2265 0. Solve 3/(x + 1) = 1 with its restriction.","Solve (x - 1)/(x - 2) = 2/(x - 2). Exclude x = 2. Multiply by x - 2 to get x - 1 = 2, hence x = 3. Substitute into the original: 2/1 = 2/1, so three is valid. For x\xb2 - 9 < 0, roots -3 and 3 bound the negative interval: -3 < x < 3."),A.cQ(A.a(["Read vertex form and its transformations.","Find intercepts and the axis of symmetry.","Use a quadratic model with a sensible domain."],q),"In (x - h), the vertex coordinate is h, not -h. A projectile height model should not use negative time unless its context allows it.","Vertex of y = (x - 4)\xb2 + 2?|(4,2)|(-4,2);(4,-2);(2,4)|Vertex form directly gives h and k.\nAxis of y = (x + 3)\xb2 - 1?|x = -3|x = 3;y = -1;y = 3|The x coordinate of the vertex is negative three.\nDoes y = -x\xb2 + 5 have a maximum or minimum?|Maximum|Minimum;Neither;Both at every point|A negative leading coefficient opens downward.\nY-intercept of y = 2x\xb2 - 3x + 7?|7|2;-3;0|Set x equal to zero.\nZeros of y = x\xb2 - 16?|-4 and 4|4 only;-8 and 8;-16 and 16|Factor into x minus four and x plus four.\nMinimum value of y = 3(x - 2)\xb2 - 6?|-6|2;3;6|The squared term cannot be negative.\nVertex of y = x\xb2 - 4x + 3?|(2,-1)|(-2,3);(4,3);(2,1)|Complete the square to get (x - 2)\xb2 - 1.\nWhich graph opens upward?|y = 2x\xb2|y = -x\xb2;y = -3x\xb2;y = -0.5x\xb2|The leading coefficient is positive.\nFor y = (x - 1)\xb2, what is y when x = 4?|9|3;16;5|The square of four minus one is nine.\nHeight h = -t\xb2 + 6t has its maximum at which t?|3|0;6;9|Complete the square to get -(t - 3)\xb2 + 9.\nWhich x values give the same y for axis x = 2?|0 and 4|0 and 2;1 and 4;2 and 3|They lie equal distances on opposite sides of the axis.\n","Vertex (-1,8) is a maximum because the coefficient is negative. Set (x + 1)\xb2 = 4; zeros are x = -3 and x = 1.","A quadratic function y = ax\xb2 + bx + c, a \u2260 0, graphs as a parabola. Vertex form y = a(x - h)\xb2 + k has vertex (h,k) and axis x = h. Positive a opens upward with a minimum; negative a opens downward with a maximum. Zeros are x-intercepts, while c is the y-intercept in standard form. A real problem can limit which x values make sense.",A.F(["Function","y = (x - 2)\xb2 - 9","Vertex","(2, -9), minimum","Axis","x = 2","Intercepts","x = -1 or 5; y-intercept -5"],p,p),"Read vertex, opening, symmetry, and intercepts as connected features of the same quadratic.","Quadratic Functions and Parabola Models","State vertex and maximum or minimum of y = -2(x + 1)\xb2 + 8. Find its zeros.","For y = x\xb2 - 6x + 5, complete the square: y = (x - 3)\xb2 - 4. The vertex is (3,-4) and the axis is x = 3. Factor x\xb2 - 6x + 5 = (x - 1)(x - 5), giving zeros one and five. The y-intercept is five. Plot symmetric pairs around x = 3."),A.cQ(A.a(["Distinguish four variation structures.","Find a constant of variation.","Predict a new value using a model."],q),"A straight line with a nonzero intercept is not direct variation. Inverse variation keeps a product, not a ratio, constant.","Which equation shows direct variation?|y = 5x|y = x + 5;y = 5/x;y = x\xb2 + 5|The ratio y/x stays constant at five.\nWhich equation shows inverse variation?|y = 12/x|y = 12x;y = x + 12;y = 12 - x|The product xy stays twelve.\nFor y = kx, x = 3 and y = 18. Find k.|6|15;21;54|Divide eighteen by three.\nFor y = 4x, find y when x = 7.|28|11;3;49|Multiply four by seven.\nFor y = k/x, x = 5 and y = 8. Find k.|40|13;3;1.6|Multiply x and y.\nFor y = 40/x, find y when x = 10.|4|400;30;50|Divide forty by ten.\nWhich expresses y jointly proportional to x and z?|y = kxz|y = kx/z;y = k/(xz);y = k + x + z|Both direct factors multiply the constant.\nIf y = 2xz, x = 3, z = 4, find y.|24|9;12;18|Multiply two times three times four.\nIf y = 6x/z, x = 5, z = 3, find y.|10|90;14;2|Thirty divided by three equals ten.\nEight workers finish in 9 days. Under inverse variation, twelve workers need how long?|6 days|13.5 days;3 days;12 days|The product is seventy-two worker-days, divided by twelve.\nIf y varies directly with x and inversely with z, doubling both x and z does what to y?|Leaves it unchanged|Doubles it;Halves it;Quadruples it|The two scale factors cancel in kx/z.\n","Use y = kxz. Since 24 = k \xd7 2 \xd7 3, k = 4. The new value is 4 \xd7 5 \xd7 2 = 40.","Direct variation has y = kx, so y/x is constant. Inverse variation has y = k/x, so xy is constant. Joint variation combines direct factors, such as y = kxz. Combined variation mixes direct and inverse factors, such as y = kx/z. Use known data to find k before substituting new values. The equations describe the assumed relationship, not every real-world situation.",A.F(["Direct","y = 3x: doubling x doubles y","Inverse","y = 24/x: doubling x halves y","Joint","A = kbh","Combined","y = kx/z"],p,p),"Choose the relationship, solve for k, then use and check the new values.","Direct Inverse Joint and Combined Variation","y varies jointly with x and z. When x = 2, z = 3, y = 24. Find y for x = 5 and z = 2.","A job takes six equally productive workers ten days. Assume time varies inversely with workers. Set t = k/w. Then k = 6 \xd7 10 = 60 worker-days. For twelve workers, t = 60/12 = 5 days. Check the worker-day product remains sixty."),B.Co,A.cQ(A.a(["Use side and angle properties of parallelograms.","Apply diagonal bisection.","Distinguish rectangles, rhombi, and squares."],q),"Do not assume every parallelogram has right angles or equal diagonals. Bisecting means halving each diagonal, not making the two diagonals equal.","Opposite sides of a parallelogram are what?|Equal and parallel|Always perpendicular;Always unequal;Always curved|Both pairs are parallel and have equal lengths.\nAdjacent angles in a parallelogram total what?|180\xb0|90\xb0;270\xb0;360\xb0|Parallel sides make them supplementary.\nOne angle is 75\xb0. Its opposite angle is what?|75\xb0|105\xb0;90\xb0;150\xb0|Opposite angles are equal.\nOne angle is 75\xb0. An adjacent angle is what?|105\xb0|75\xb0;15\xb0;285\xb0|Subtract seventy-five from 180.\nDiagonals of every parallelogram do what?|Bisect each other|Are equal;Are perpendicular;Bisect all angles|Each divides the other into equal halves.\nWhich special parallelogram always has equal diagonals?|Rectangle|Any rhombus;Any slanted parallelogram;Any kite|Rectangle diagonals have equal lengths.\nWhich special parallelogram always has perpendicular diagonals?|Rhombus|Any rectangle;Any parallelogram;Any trapezoid|All rhombi have perpendicular diagonals.\nWhich figure is both a rectangle and a rhombus?|Square|Any kite;Any trapezoid;Any triangle|A square has right angles and four equal sides.\nIf a parallelogram diagonal is 18 cm, each half is what?|9 cm|18 cm;36 cm;6 cm|Its diagonals bisect each other.\nOpposite sides 2x + 5 and 4x - 1 are equal. Find x.|3|2;4;6|Solve six equals two x.\nA rectangle diagonal is 13 cm. What is the other diagonal?|13 cm|6.5 cm;26 cm;Cannot determine|Rectangle diagonals are equal.\n","Set 3x + 2 = 5x - 6, giving x = 4 and length fourteen. A rhombus need not have equal diagonals; a square does.","A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel. Opposite sides and angles are equal, adjacent angles are supplementary, and diagonals bisect each other. A rectangle is a parallelogram with four right angles and equal diagonals. A rhombus has four equal sides and perpendicular diagonals. A square has both sets of properties. A diagonal divides a parallelogram into two congruent triangles, supporting the side and angle results.",A.F(["ABCD in order","AB \u2225 CD and BC \u2225 AD","Sides","AB = CD; BC = AD","Angles","\u2220A = \u2220C; \u2220A + \u2220B = 180\xb0","Diagonals meet at O","AO = OC and BO = OD"],p,p),"Use the properties guaranteed by the named figure, and no stronger properties without proof.","Parallelogram Properties and Proofs","Opposite sides have lengths 3x + 2 and 5x - 6. Find x and the side length. Must a rhombus have equal diagonals?","In parallelogram ABCD, angle A is 68\xb0. Its adjacent angle B is 180 - 68 = 112\xb0. Opposite angle C is 68\xb0 and D is 112\xb0. If AO = 7 cm, diagonal AC is 14 cm because O is its midpoint."),A.cQ(A.a(["Use the triangle midsegment theorem.","Find trapezoid midsegment lengths.","Apply kite diagonal properties."],q),"A triangle midsegment is half the third side, while a trapezoid midsegment averages two bases. Do not assume both kite diagonals bisect each other.","Triangle base is 18 cm. Parallel midsegment length?|9 cm|18 cm;36 cm;6 cm|A triangle midsegment is half the third side.\nTriangle midsegment is 11 cm. Parallel side length?|22 cm|5.5 cm;11 cm;33 cm|Double the midsegment.\nTrapezoid bases are 6 and 14 cm. Midsegment?|10 cm|8 cm;20 cm;7 cm|Average the two bases.\nTrapezoid bases are 9 and 15 cm. Midsegment?|12 cm|6 cm;24 cm;13 cm|Their sum twenty-four divided by two is twelve.\nWhat is true of an isosceles trapezoid's legs?|They are equal|They are parallel;They are perpendicular;They are its diagonals|Isosceles specifies equal nonparallel sides.\nKite diagonals meet at what angle?|90\xb0|60\xb0;45\xb0;180\xb0|Kite diagonals are perpendicular.\nArea of a kite with diagonals 6 and 12 cm?|36 cm\xb2|72 cm\xb2;18 cm\xb2;24 cm\xb2|Multiply diagonal lengths and divide by two.\nDoes every kite have both diagonals bisecting each other?|No|Yes;Only if rotated;Only if drawn large|Only the symmetry diagonal is guaranteed to bisect the other.\nA trapezoid midsegment is 17 cm and one base is 12 cm. Other base?|22 cm|29 cm;5 cm;34 cm|Twice seventeen minus twelve is twenty-two.\nA triangle midpoint segment parallels a 25 cm side. Length?|12.5 cm|50 cm;25 cm;10 cm|Half of twenty-five is twelve and a half.\nAn isosceles trapezoid has one lower base angle 65\xb0. Other lower base angle?|65\xb0|115\xb0;90\xb0;130\xb0|Angles on the same base are equal.\n","The parallel triangle side is twice six, or twelve centimetres. Kite area is 10 \xd7 8 \xf7 2 = 40 cm\xb2.","A segment joining midpoints of two triangle sides is parallel to the third side and half its length. A trapezoid midsegment joins the leg midpoints; its length is the average of the two parallel bases. This lesson uses trapezoid for a quadrilateral with exactly one parallel pair. In an isosceles trapezoid, legs and base angles match. A kite has two pairs of adjacent equal sides; its diagonals are perpendicular, and the symmetry diagonal bisects the other.",A.F(["Triangle","Base 14 cm \u2192 midpoint segment 7 cm","Trapezoid","Bases 8 and 14 cm \u2192 midsegment 11 cm","Kite diagonals","Perpendicular; area = d1d2/2","Isosceles trapezoid","Matching legs and equal angles on each base"],p,p),"Mark midpoints and parallel bases. Use the theorem that matches the actual figure.","Trapezoids Kites and Triangle Midsegments","A triangle midsegment is 6 cm. Find the parallel side. A kite has perpendicular diagonals 10 and 8 cm. Find area.","A trapezoid midsegment is 12 cm and one base is 9 cm. Write 12 = (9 + b)/2. Multiply by two to get 24 = 9 + b. Subtract nine: the other base is 15 cm. Check their average is twelve."),B.Cy,B.Ca,A.cQ(A.a(["Model elevation and depression with horizontal reference lines.","Apply the sine rule to known opposite pairs.","Apply the cosine rule to SAS or SSS data."],q),"Angles of elevation begin at the horizontal, not vertical. Opposite sides must match their angles in the sine rule. Include observer eye height when given.","An angle of elevation is measured from what?|Horizontal upward|Vertical upward;Ground distance alone;A circle centre|It starts at the observer's horizontal line.\nAn angle of depression is measured in which direction?|Downward from horizontal|Upward from vertical;Along a wall only;Around a full circle|The line of sight falls below horizontal.\nFor a ground-level observer 8 m from a tree at 45\xb0 elevation, height?|8 m|4 m;16 m;8\u221a2 m|Height equals distance times tan 45 degrees.\nWhich ratio is the sine rule?|a/sin A = b/sin B|a/cos A = b/cos B;a + A = b + B;a sin A = b sin B|Match each side with its opposite angle.\nWhich data directly suit the cosine rule?|Two sides and included angle|Only one side;Only two angles;Only one angle|SAS determines the third side through cosine.\nSides 3 and 4 include a 90\xb0 angle. Third side?|5|7;1;25|Cosine ninety is zero, giving the Pythagorean result.\nIf A = 30\xb0, B = 90\xb0, a = 5, find b.|10|5;2.5;5\u221a3|Five divided by sin thirty gives ten.\nIf a = b in a triangle, what follows?|A = B|A + B = 90\xb0 always;C = 90\xb0 always;A = 2B|Equal sides face equal angles.\nSides 6 and 6 include 60\xb0. Third side?|6|12;3;6\u221a2|The cosine rule gives 36 + 36 - 36 = 36.\nEye height 1.5 m, horizontal distance 10 m, elevation 45\xb0. Total height?|11.5 m|10 m;8.5 m;15 m|Add eye height to the ten-metre rise.\nWhy check both angles for some sine-rule SSA problems?|A sine value can match two supplementary angles|Sine is always negative;All triangles are right;Side lengths can be negative|Both an acute and an obtuse candidate may fit unless other data rule one out.\n","tan 45\xb0 = 1, so the tree is ten metres high in this ground-level model. By the sine rule b = 4 \xd7 sin 90\xb0 / sin 30\xb0 = 8 cm.","An angle of elevation is measured upward from a horizontal line; depression is downward. Draw a right-triangle model before choosing a trig ratio. For any triangle, a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C with opposite labels matched. This is the sine rule. The cosine rule c\xb2 = a\xb2 + b\xb2 - 2ab cos C works with two sides and their included angle or three sides. A sine-rule SSA situation can have two solutions, so check geometric possibilities.",A.F(["Elevation","Observer horizontal \u2192 angle \u03b8 \u2192 line of sight upward","Right triangle","height / horizontal distance = tan \u03b8","Opposite pairs","a opposite A, b opposite B, c opposite C","Cosine rule","Included angle C lies between a and b"],p,p),"Draw and label first. Use right-triangle ratios, sine rule, or cosine rule according to the given information.","Elevation Depression and Oblique Triangles","A tree is 10 m away horizontally and the elevation to its top is 45\xb0 from ground level. Find height. In a triangle A = 30\xb0, B = 90\xb0, a = 4 cm. Find b.","Two sides are 5 m and 8 m with included angle 60\xb0. Use the cosine rule: c\xb2 = 25 + 64 - 2 \xd7 5 \xd7 8 \xd7 1/2 = 49. Thus c = 7 m. Check seven is between |8 - 5| and 8 + 5.")],t.LQ)})
